// 3, 4, 5 and 6 ask (the pe1-cp … pe6-cp cards of data/qb-aamc-fl.js), plus the cards (cpt-st-*) that twin the daily
// quiz's Sample Test and QBank-pack questions. TODAY's C/P flashcard step uses only these cards (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js).
// Same ask, same setup: `twinOf` names the question(s) each card mirrors, asked the same way with the same kind of
// choices; a passage question's card carries a short twin passage (`passage`, 3–6 sentences), a stand-alone's has none.
// `from` names the spec items (peX-cp:Q#n) the card teaches; `aamc`, `disc` and `skill` come from a source's spec line.
// Original wording, numbers and compounds only: no AAMC passage, question, number or answer choice is reproduced.
// Format: {id, s, disc, aamc, skill, from, twinOf, passage?, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, elim, rule, fast, exp, how, teach}
// (tools/test-cp-test-cards.cjs checks all of it).
const CP_TEST_CARDS = [
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-01a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"chromatography polarity order","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:1#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:1"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"Which reference compound shown will have the smallest Rf?","choices":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran","Hexane-1,5-diol","Octane"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanone","tetrahydropyran","hexane-1-5-diol","n-octane"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Polar phases barely hold nonpolar compounds","c":[3]},{"f":"Ketones and ethers can't donate H-bonds","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Water-coated paper is a polar phase, so look for O–H groups. Only one compound has O–H donors (two of them); the ketone and ether only accept, and the alkane does neither."},"exp":"The bound water is a **polar stationary phase** and toluene is a nonpolar mobile phase. Whichever compound forms the **strongest hydrogen bonds** with the water lags most and travels the **shortest distance** (smallest Rf). Hexane-1,5-diol carries **a pair of O–H groups** that both donate and accept H-bonds; cyclohexanone and tetrahydropyran only accept, and octane does neither. Spot size only reflects how much was loaded.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound held most strongly by the polar stationary phase.","know":"• A polar stationary phase holds compounds that hydrogen-bond with it; they move least (small Rf).\n• O–H and N–H donate H-bonds; C=O and ether O only accept.\n• Spot size depends on the amount loaded, not on Rf.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says water bound to the cellulose is the stationary phase: a polar phase.","hl":"water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase"},{"text":"The alkane has no polar group at all, so it rides with toluene near the front.","strike":["Octane"]},{"text":"The ketone and the ether can only accept H-bonds; neither has an O–H.","strike":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran"]},{"text":"Two O–H groups give the strongest hold on the water. Answer: hexane-1,5-diol."}],"trap":"Linking spot size to distance, or picking the ketone for its strong C=O dipole: on a water phase, O–H donors hold hardest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase","note":"polar phase"},{"t":"nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase","note":"nonpolar mobile phase"},{"t":"hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water","note":"held back"},{"t":"the spots differed in size","note":"amount, not distance"}]},{"text":"**Rank H-bonding** with the bound water.","calc":["two O–H: donor and acceptor","C=O or ether O: acceptor only","alkane: neither"]},{"text":"**Most H-bonding = shortest distance = smallest Rf.**"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a polar stationary phase, the compound with the most O–H/N–H donors moves least; spot size only shows how much was loaded."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-02a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"experimental evidence logic","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:2"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"According to the passage, what does the team's conclusion assume?","choices":["Those reactants existed then.","The ester formed in high yield.","The products were optically pure.","Heat speeds up every reaction."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Low-yield reactions still happen","c":[1]},{"f":"Racemic products still count as formed","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lab result = 'could happen'; 'did happen' needs the same inputs back then. Pick the choice saying the ingredients existed on early Earth; yield, purity and all-reactions claims are irrelevant."},"exp":"A lab result shows what **can** happen. To conclude it **did** happen on the early Earth, the **starting materials and conditions** (here N-acetylglycine, ethanol, acidic water and clay) must have existed then. Yield, purity and a claim about all reactions do not bear on that.","how":{"ask":"Find the unstated premise that must be true for the lab result to support the historical claim.","know":"• **Assumption**: a premise the conclusion needs but does not state; if it were false, the conclusion would fail.\n• A lab model of a past event supports the claim only if its **inputs and conditions** existed at that time.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the conclusion, about the primitive Earth; sentence 1 shows the evidence is a lab run.","hl":"such condensations took place on the primitive Earth"},{"text":"Test each choice: if it were false, would the argument fail?","calc":["reactants absent then → the lab run says nothing about then → fails","low yield → some ester still formed → stands"]},{"text":"Yield, optical purity and all reactions are not needed for the conclusion.","strike":["The ester formed in high yield.","The products were optically pure.","Heat speeds up every reaction."]},{"text":"Answer: the reactants must have been present on the early Earth."}],"trap":"Choosing a detail about the experiment's quality; the gap is between the lab and the early Earth."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"on the primitive Earth","note":"the conclusion"},{"t":"a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol","note":"the evidence: a lab run"},{"t":"assume","note":"find the assumption"}]},{"text":"**Split conclusion from evidence.** The evidence is a lab result; the conclusion is about the past."},{"text":"**Bridge the gap.** For the lab pool to stand in for the early Earth, the same ingredients had to be there."},{"text":"**Negate to test.** If those reactants were absent, the argument collapses, so that is the assumption.","calc":["negate the choice → does the conclusion fail?"]},{"text":"**Also:** an assumption is the unstated premise whose denial would break the conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lab model supports a claim about the past only if its starting materials and conditions existed then."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-03a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"Rf from TLC distances","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:3#1","pe1-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:46#1","pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:3"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"What is the retention factor (Rf) of the ester on the silica plate?","choices":["0.36","0.40","0.47","2.5"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Rf is never above 1","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Shift both readings down by 1: 3.2 over 8. Halve twice: 0.8 over 2 = 0.40. Anything above 1 (2.5) is out instantly."},"exp":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance, both from the baseline.** Spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm. Front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm. Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**.","how":{"ask":"Measure both distances from the baseline, then divide.","know":"**Rf = distance moved by the compound ÷ distance moved by the solvent front**, both measured from the **baseline** where the sample was spotted. Rf is between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the readings from the plate's edge, but the sample started at the baseline.","hl":"the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge","fig":"cpt-tlc-plate"},{"text":"Subtract the baseline from both readings.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = **3.2 cm**","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = **8.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**"]},{"text":"0.47 skips the baseline (4.2 ÷ 9.0), 0.36 subtracts it from only the spot (3.2 ÷ 9.0), and 2.5 is inverted (8.0 ÷ 3.2).","strike":["0.47","0.36","2.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40."}],"trap":"Dividing the raw readings (4.2 ÷ 9.0 = 0.47): both distances must start at the baseline."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"where the sample started"},{"t":"9.0 cm","note":"solvent front, from the edge"},{"t":"4.2 cm","note":"spot, from the edge"}]},{"text":"**Start at the baseline.** The race starts where the sample was spotted, not at the plate's edge.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Divide spot by front.**","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40"]},{"text":"**Also:** on silica, the compound with the strongest polar groups (O–H or N–H first) has the smallest Rf."},{"text":"**Also:** a low Rf means strong binding to the stationary phase; a high Rf means the compound prefers the mobile phase."},{"text":"**Also:** only N–H, O–H and F–H donate hydrogen bonds; C=O and ether oxygens can only accept them."},{"text":"**Also:** in chromatography, Rf is characteristic of the compound under fixed conditions; spot size only reflects how much was applied."},{"text":"**Also:** every Rf lies between 0 and 1; a larger value means the ratio is inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** sugar-based column materials (polysaccharides such as dextran, agarose and cellulose) are covered in –OH groups, so they interact with proteins mainly by hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** in a binding pocket, sort each ligand atom by what it carries. An oxygen with lone pairs but no H of its own (a ketone or amide C=O, a carboxylate –COO⁻, an ether O) can only receive an H-bond, for instance from arginine's N–H; an O–H or N–H on the ligand can also give one; a C–H does neither. In acetic acid, drawn here, the C=O oxygen is a pure acceptor and the O–H is a donor.","fig":"acetic-acid","calc":["C=O oxygen: lone pairs, no H → acceptor only","O–H: H on O plus lone pairs → donor and acceptor","C–H: no lone pair, no polar H → neither"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline), and it always falls between 0 and 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-04e","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"E1 alcohol dehydration","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:4#1","pe1-cp:4#3","pe1-cp:4#4","pe1-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:4"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"Which statement about the pentan-2-ol dehydration is NOT correct?","fig":"cpt-pentan-2-ol","choices":["The reaction proceeds through a carbocation.","One molecule of water is lost.","Tertiary alcohols react faster.","Only the trans alkene forms."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Secondary alcohols dehydrate via carbocations","c":[0]},{"f":"Tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"A planar carbocation can lose H⁺ toward either face, so E1 is never stereospecific. A choice claiming a single geometry is the false one."},"exp":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration is **E1**: the –OH is protonated, **one water** leaves and a **planar carbocation** forms; **tertiary** alcohols react most easily because they give the most stable cation. Because the cation is planar, the C–H can break on either side, so **both cis and trans** pent-2-ene form, as sentence 3 reports. The claim that only the trans alkene forms is the false statement.","how":{"ask":"Find the statement that contradicts the E1 mechanism or the passage.","know":"• E1: protonate the –OH, lose one H₂O, form a planar carbocation, lose H⁺.\n• Ease of dehydration: 3° > 2° > 1°.\n• A planar cation gives cis and trans alkenes, so the reaction is not stereospecific.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 names the planar carbocation and the single water lost: both statements are true.","hl":"lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation","strike":["The reaction proceeds through a carbocation.","One molecule of water is lost."]},{"text":"The tertiary alcohol reacts at a lower temperature: true.","hl":"reacted at a much lower temperature","strike":["Tertiary alcohols react faster."]},{"text":"Both cis and trans pent-2-ene were collected, so a trans-only claim is false. Answer: only the trans alkene forms.","hl":"both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene"}],"trap":"Treating the major (trans) alkene as the only one: a planar cation gives both isomers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"lost one water molecule","note":"one H₂O"},{"t":"planar carbocation","note":"E1; either face"},{"t":"both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene","note":"not stereospecific"},{"t":"NOT correct","note":"three are true"}]},{"text":"**Check each statement** against E1 and the passage; cross off the true ones.","calc":["carbocation: true","one H₂O: true","3° fastest: true","a single geometry: false"]},{"text":"**Also:** in E1, the C=C always forms between the cationic carbon and a carbon directly bonded to it."},{"text":"**Also:** a direct E1 alkene always has the former C–OH carbon in its C=C."},{"text":"**Also:** E1 through a planar carbocation gives both E and Z alkenes, with the more stable E usually major."},{"text":"**Also:** tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily because they form the most stable carbocation; primary alcohols dehydrate slowest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in E1 dehydration a planar carbocation forms, one water is lost, 3° alcohols react fastest, and both cis and trans alkenes form."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-05a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"peptide bond = amide + water","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:27#3","pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:5"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P1","passage":"To test whether condensation reactions could have run in hot, acidic volcanic pools on the early Earth, a team heated N-acetylglycine with ethanol in dilute acid over a clay at 85 °C. One product formed with loss of a water molecule; its IR spectrum kept the amide C=O band, added one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹ and showed no new N–H stretch. The team also heated pentan-2-ol in the same acidic water: it lost one water molecule through a planar carbocation on C2 and gave pent-1-ene plus both cis- and trans-pent-2-ene, while the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol reacted at a much lower temperature. The team concluded that such condensations took place on the primitive Earth. The crude mixture and four reference compounds were then run by paper chromatography, in which water bound to the cellulose –OH groups is the stationary phase and nonpolar toluene is the mobile phase. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water lag behind the solvent front, and the spots differed in size because different amounts were loaded. On a silica TLC check of the ester, the baseline was 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge; measured from that edge, the solvent front reached 9.0 cm and the spot center 4.2 cm.","q":"Which functional group links the two former reactants in the product shown?","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","choices":["Ester","Amide","Ether","Carboxylic acid"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ethanol has no nitrogen","c":[1]},{"f":"Carboxylic acid groups are chain ends","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name a carbonyl by its neighbors: C=O next to O–R is ester, next to N is amide, next to OH is acid, no C=O is ether. Here C=O touches O–CH2CH3, so ester."},"exp":"A carbonyl carbon bonded to an **–O–R** group is an **ester**, C(=O)–O–C: here an ethyl ester. The other carbonyl in the molecule is bonded to N, so that one is the **amide**. An ether has C–O–C with no carbonyl, and a carboxylic acid has C(=O)–O**H**.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom on the new carbonyl carbon, then name the linkage it makes.","know":"• Name a carbonyl group by its other neighbor on the C=O carbon.\n• C(=O)–**O–C** → **ester**; C(=O)–**N** → **amide** (the peptide bond); C(=O)–**OH** → carboxylic acid.\n• **Ether**: C–O–C with no C=O on either carbon.\n• Carboxyl + alcohol → ester; carboxyl + amine → amide.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the acid was heated with ethanol, an alcohol: the new bond joins the carboxyl carbon to its oxygen.","hl":"with ethanol","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃","C=O carbon + O–R → **ester**"]},{"text":"Sentence 2: the amide C=O band was already there and no new N–H appears, so the new link is not an amide.","hl":"showed no new N–H stretch","strike":["Amide"]},{"text":"An ether has no carbonyl next to its oxygen, and an acid's oxygen carries H, not an ethyl group.","strike":["Ether","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: an ester."}],"trap":"Calling every new carbonyl link an amide because amino acids are involved: check whether the carbonyl carbon is bonded to N or to O–R."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"with ethanol","note":"an alcohol partner"},{"t":"with loss of a water molecule","note":"condensation"},{"t":"one new C=O stretch","note":"a new carbonyl link"},{"t":"no new N–H stretch","note":"not a new amide"}]},{"text":"**Name a carbonyl by its other neighbor.**","calc":["C(=O)–O–R → ester","C(=O)–N → amide (peptide bond)","C(=O)–OH → carboxylic acid","C–O–C, no C=O → ether"]},{"text":"**Read the product.** The old carbonyl sits on N; the new one sits on O–ethyl.","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["CH₃C(=O)–NH– → amide (old)","–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃ → ester (new)"]},{"text":"**Also:** forming a peptide (amide) bond is a condensation that releases one H₂O, 18 amu, so a dipeptide's mass is the sum of its two amino acids minus 18."},{"text":"**Also:** a peptide bond is an amide, C(=O)–N, made when a carboxyl group condenses with an amino group and releases water; carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester instead.","calc":["R–COOH + H₂N–R′ → R–CO–NH–R′ + H₂O","R–COOH + HO–R′ → R–CO–O–R′ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the new link by the partner: carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester (C(=O)–O–R); carboxyl + amine gives an amide, the peptide bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-06a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"SN2 steric rate order","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:6"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Of the chlorides shown, the one that reacts fastest with the iodide is:","choices":["Chlorocyclopentane","1-Chloropentane","2-Chloro-2-methylbutane","1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"SN2 slows from primary to tertiary","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Concerted and second order means SN2: pick the least crowded carbon. An unbranched CH₂–Cl with an unbranched neighbor wins."},"exp":"A one-step reaction that is first order in both reactants is **SN2**, so the rate depends on **steric crowding at the carbon bearing Cl**. 1-Chloropentane is primary with no branching nearby, so iodide reaches its back side most easily and NaCl precipitates first. Chlorocyclopentane is secondary (slower), 2-chloro-2-methylbutane is tertiary (no SN2), and 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane is primary but its quaternary neighbor blocks the back side.","how":{"ask":"Identify the mechanism, then pick the least hindered carbon.","know":"• Concerted + second order = SN2.\n• SN2 rate: methyl > 1° > 2°; 3° does not react.\n• Branching on the next carbon (neopentyl-type) also blocks backside attack.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step, first order in each reactant: SN2.","hl":"one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"A tertiary carbon is too crowded for backside attack.","strike":["2-Chloro-2-methylbutane"]},{"text":"Primary, but the neighboring carbon carries two methyls and an ethyl: blocked.","strike":["1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"]},{"text":"A secondary ring carbon is slower than an open primary one.","strike":["Chlorocyclopentane"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-chloropentane."}],"trap":"Assuming every primary chloride is fast: check the next carbon for bulky branches."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"one concerted step","note":"SN2"},{"t":"first order in each reactant","note":"bimolecular"},{"t":"NaCl does not","note":"precipitate = reaction"}]},{"text":"**Classify each carbon bearing Cl.**","calc":["1-chloropentane: 1°, open","chlorocyclopentane: 2°","2-chloro-2-methylbutane: 3°","1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane: 1°, crowded neighbor"]},{"text":"**Least crowded reacts fastest** and clouds the solution first."},{"text":"**Also:** SN2 slows with crowding at the carbon being attacked (methyl > 1° > 2°, no 3°), not at the leaving group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 slows with crowding at the attacked carbon and its neighbor: methyl > 1° > 2°, and 3° does not react."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-07b","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"carbocation rearrangement","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:7#2","pe1-cp:7#1","pe1-cp:7#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:7"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Which product of the HBr reaction could form only by way of a rearranged carbocation?","choices":["3-Bromo-2-methylpentane","2-Bromo-2-methylpentane","4-Methylpent-2-ene","2-Methylpent-2-ene"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Br at original carbon: no shift","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mark C3, where the –OH was. Both alkenes include C3 in the C=C, so they can form directly; only a bromide with Br off C3 needs a hydride shift."},"exp":"The first carbocation forms on **C3**, the carbon that lost the –OH. Capture there gives 3-bromo-2-methylpentane, and losing H from C2 or C4 gives 2-methylpent-2-ene or 4-methylpent-2-ene: all **direct**. Moving a hydride from C2 (a **1,2-shift**) puts the + charge on the **tertiary** C2; only that cation gives **2-bromo-2-methylpentane**, with Br on a carbon that never held the –OH.","how":{"ask":"Find the product whose Br position needs the carbocation to move first.","know":"• Direct products keep Br on, or a C=C touching, the carbon that held the –OH.\n• A 1,2-hydride or methyl shift moves the + charge to a more substituted neighbor.\n• Br on that neighbor means a rearrangement happened.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the first cation sits on C3, the carbon that held the –OH.","hl":"giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH"},{"text":"Br on C3 is direct capture of that cation.","strike":["3-Bromo-2-methylpentane"]},{"text":"Both alkenes have C3 in their C=C, so each forms by losing an H next to the C3 cation.","strike":["4-Methylpent-2-ene","2-Methylpent-2-ene"]},{"text":"A hydride shift from C2 makes a 3° cation on C2; Br there needs that shift. Answer: 2-bromo-2-methylpentane."}],"trap":"Picking the most substituted alkene: 2-methylpent-2-ene can form directly from the C3 cation, so it does not prove a shift."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH","note":"start here"},{"t":"this cation could rearrange","note":"look for a shift"},{"t":"a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes","note":"sort four products"},{"t":"only by way of a rearranged carbocation","note":"the ask"}]},{"text":"**Sort the products** by where the new group sits.","calc":["Br on C3 → direct","C=C at C2–C3 or C3–C4 → direct","Br on C2 (3°) → needs a hydride shift"]},{"text":"**Also:** when the neighboring carbon has no H, a methyl moves instead, as in 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol, drawn here: its 2,3-dimethyl products need a methyl shift.","fig":"cpt-33-dimethylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"**Also:** a carbocation shifts H or CH₃ from a neighboring carbon whenever that makes a more stable cation."},{"text":"**Also:** a new group on a carbon that never held the leaving group is the fingerprint of a carbocation rearrangement."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a product with Br (or another group) on a carbon that never held the OH can only come from a shifted carbocation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-09b","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"SN2 inversion of stereocenter","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:9#1","pe1-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:9"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P2","passage":"A teaching lab studied how alcohols become alkyl halides and how those halides react. First, 2-methylpentan-3-ol was heated with concentrated HBr; the protonated –OH left as water, giving a carbocation on C3, the carbon that held the –OH, and this cation could rearrange before it captured bromide or lost a proton. The isolated products were a pair of bromides and a pair of alkenes. Next, four alkyl chlorides were each added to sodium iodide in acetone at 50 °C, where iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step and the rate law is first order in each reactant. NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl does not, so the first cloudiness marks the fastest chloride. A crude sample of 1-chloropentane, made from pentan-1-ol, was analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column at 105 °C; it held pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and the chloride (107 g/mol). Each compound spends less time in the column's liquid coating the more easily it evaporates. Finally, (R)-2-chlorobutane was stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone; doubling either reactant doubled the rate, and the thiolate attacks carbon readily but is only weakly basic.","q":"Which compound is the major product of the thiolate reaction?","choices":["(S)-2-(Methylthio)butane","(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Second order → SN2 → inversion. SCH₃ takes Cl's place as the top-priority group, so the label flips from R to S."},"exp":"Doubling either reactant doubles the rate: **second order, SN2**. Thiolate attacks from the back and the stereocenter **inverts**. S outranks ethyl and methyl just as Cl did, so the priority order is unchanged and the inverted carbon is **(S)**: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane. The new group stays on C2, and a weakly basic thiolate gives little alkene.","how":{"ask":"Decide SN1 or SN2 from the rate data, then name the product and its configuration.","know":"• Rate depends on both reactants → SN2 → inversion.\n• After inversion, re-rank priorities: the label flips only if the new group holds the same rank as the leaving group.\n• SN2 keeps the new group on the same carbon.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 says doubling either reactant doubled the rate: SN2.","hl":"doubling either reactant doubled the rate","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Inversion rules out keeping R; the group stays on C2; a weak base gives little alkene.","strike":["(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"]},{"text":"S ranks first just as Cl did, so inversion flips R to S. Answer: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane.","calc":["before: Cl > ethyl > methyl > H","after: SCH₃ > ethyl > methyl > H"]}],"trap":"Assuming the label never changes: here it flips only because S takes Cl's top rank."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"(R)-2-chlorobutane","note":"start R"},{"t":"doubling either reactant doubled the rate","note":"SN2"},{"t":"only weakly basic","note":"little E2"}]},{"text":"**Invert, then re-rank.**","calc":["backside attack → inversion","SCH₃ keeps Cl's top rank → R becomes S"]},{"text":"**Also:** CIP priority is set by the first atom's atomic number; go farther out only to break a tie."},{"text":"**Also:** after a substitution, the product keeps the substrate's carbon chain and the new group's position; only the group's name changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 inverts the stereocenter; re-rank priorities with the new group before naming R or S, and keep the group on the same carbon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Fischer esterification oxygen origin","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:10#1","pe1-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:10#3","pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:10","sample-cp:38"],"q":"Ethanol whose oxygen is ¹⁸O is esterified with acetic acid under acid catalysis. In which product is the ¹⁸O found?","fig":["cpt-ethanol","cpt-acetic-acid"],"choices":["In the ester","In the water","In both products","In neither product"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Water's oxygen comes from the acid","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Atoms are conserved in reactions","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fischer esterification: acid loses its OH, alcohol keeps its O. So the alcohol's labeled oxygen becomes the ester's bridging O, never the water."},"exp":"In Fischer esterification the **alcohol's oxygen is the nucleophile**: it attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon and stays bonded to it, becoming the ester's bridging –O–. The water that leaves takes an oxygen from the **acid**. So the ¹⁸O ends up **in the ester**.","how":{"ask":"Find which oxygen attacks and which one leaves, then follow the labeled one.","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O (acid catalyst).\n• The **alcohol O attacks** the carbonyl carbon and is kept; **water leaves from the acid** side.\n• So the ester's C–O–R′ oxygen comes from the alcohol, and the water's O from the acid.","steps":[{"text":"The labeled O is ethanol's, the nucleophile.","hl":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","calc":["CH₃CH₂–¹⁸OH attacks the C=O carbon"]},{"text":"The ethyl–¹⁸O bond never breaks, and one ¹⁸O can't be in two places or vanish.","strike":["In both products","In neither product"]},{"text":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen.","strike":["In the water"]},{"text":"Answer: in the ester, as its bridging oxygen."}],"trap":"Thinking the alcohol loses its OH as water, as in an SN1 reaction: in esterification the acid loses the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"esterified with acetic acid","note":"Fischer"},{"t":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","note":"the nucleophile's O"}]},{"text":"**Activate.** H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic."},{"text":"**Attack and leave.**","calc":["EtO–H attacks C → tetrahedral intermediate","acid's –OH → –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in Fischer esterification the ester's bridging oxygen comes from the alcohol, its C=O oxygen from the acid, and the water from the acid."},{"text":"**Also:** an H on O or N exchanges rapidly with solvent water (faster with acid or base), so it cannot keep a label, so it cannot show which bond broke."},{"text":"**Also:** an H bonded to carbon, such as an aromatic ring C–H or a CH₃ hydrogen, is far too weakly acidic to swap with solvent protons, so a ³H or D placed there stays put even after hours in water. Only H on O, N or S trades places with the solvent.","calc":["O–H, N–H, S–H: swap with H₂O → label lost","C–H (aromatic or alkyl): no swap → label kept"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in Fischer esterification the alcohol keeps its oxygen as the ester's –O–, and the acid loses an oxygen as water; follow a label by asking which O attacks and which leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-11a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"thin lens equation","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:11#1","pe1-cp:11#2","pe1-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:11"],"q":"A camera images a slide 20 cm from its lens onto a sensor 4.0 cm behind the lens. What is the lens's power?","choices":["+30 D","+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"In metres: 1/0.20 = 5 and 1/0.040 = 25. Add them: 30 D. A camera or eye lens making a real image is always +."},"exp":"**Power P = 1/f (f in metres) = 1/o + 1/i.** o = 0.20 m and i = 0.040 m, so P = 5 + 25 = **+30 D**. The sensor plays the retina's role: the image distance is the lens-to-sensor distance.","how":{"ask":"Find 1/f in m⁻¹ from the two distances.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• Power (D) = 1/f with f in metres.\n• Image distance = lens to screen (or retina).","steps":[{"text":"Convert both distances to metres.","hl":"4.0 cm behind the lens","calc":["o = 0.20 m","i = 0.040 m"]},{"text":"Add the reciprocals.","calc":["P = 1/0.20 + 1/0.040 = 5 + 25 = 30 D"]},{"text":"Centimetres give 0.30; subtracting gives 20; dropping the 1/o term gives 25.","strike":["+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"]},{"text":"Answer: +30 D."}],"trap":"Leaving the distances in cm (0.30 D): diopters are inverse metres."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"20 cm from its lens","note":"object distance"},{"t":"4.0 cm behind the lens","note":"image distance"}]},{"text":"**Metres, then reciprocals.**","calc":["1/0.20 m = 5 D","1/0.040 m = 25 D"]},{"text":"**Add** for a real image on a screen, and keep the sign positive."},{"text":"**Also:** power in diopters = 1 ÷ (focal length in metres); converging lenses are +, diverging lenses are −."},{"text":"**Also:** in any eye problem, the image distance is the lens-to-retina distance; the object distance is lens-to-object."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** power in diopters = 1/o + 1/i with both distances in metres; a lens forming a real image on a screen has positive power."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"catalyst lowers Ea, not ΔG/K","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:12#1","pe1-cp:12#3","pe1-cp:12#2","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:12"],"q":"The plot shows free energy along the reaction path for peptide hydrolysis, without and with a protease (kJ/mol). The plot supports the conclusion that:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide hydrolysis with and without the protease","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]},"choices":["the protease lowers Ea by 40 kJ/mol.","the protease lowers ΔG° by 40 kJ/mol.","the protease lowers Ea to 40 kJ/mol.","the protease makes ΔG° positive."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Catalysts don't change ΔG°","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The endpoints never move with a catalyst, so strike any ΔG° change. Then subtract the two peaks: 90 − 50."},"exp":"Read the plot: reactants at 0, products at −30, uncatalyzed peak at 90 and catalyzed peak at 50 kJ/mol. **Ea = peak − reactants**: 90 without and 50 with the protease, so the enzyme **lowers Ea by 40**. **ΔG° = products − reactants = −30** on both curves: an enzyme stabilizes the **transition state** and leaves ΔG° and the equilibrium unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea and ΔG° from each curve and compare them.","know":"• Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy.\n• ΔG° = product energy − reactant energy.\n• A catalyst lowers the transition state only; ΔG° and K stay the same.","steps":[{"text":"Read the endpoints: the same reactants and products on both curves.","calc":["ΔG° = −30 − 0 = −30 kJ/mol on both curves"],"strike":["the protease lowers ΔG° by 40 kJ/mol.","the protease makes ΔG° positive."]},{"text":"Read the peaks.","calc":["Ea without = 90","Ea with = 50","drop = 90 − 50 = 40"]},{"text":"The new barrier is 50, not the size of the drop.","strike":["the protease lowers Ea to 40 kJ/mol."]},{"text":"Answer: the protease lowers Ea by 40 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading the drop as the new barrier, or thinking the catalyst also changes ΔG°."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **read the plot and the question**.","marks":[{"t":"without and with a protease","note":"two curves"},{"t":"supports the conclusion that","note":"compare Ea and ΔG°"}]},{"text":"**Measure two gaps on each curve.**","calc":["Ea: peak − start","ΔG°: end − start"]},{"text":"**A catalyst lowers only the peak.** It stabilizes the transition state, so the barrier falls while the start and end stay put."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalyst speeds both directions equally, so it shortens the time to equilibrium but leaves K, ΔG° and the equilibrium yield unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** catalysis needs the transition state stabilized more than the substrate; extra substrate binding alone raises the barrier."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − reactants and ΔG° = products − reactants; an enzyme lowers only the peak, never ΔG° or K."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"catalyst phase and regeneration","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:50"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"Which outcome is most likely for the hydrogenation product, and why?","choices":["The product is pure, since Pd is filtered off","The product is contaminated, since Pd stays in solution","The yield rises, since the catalyst increases K","The yield falls, since the catalyst is consumed"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":5,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Pd/C is an insoluble solid","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask what phase the catalyst is in. An insoluble powder in a liquid is caught by a filter, so the product leaves clean."},"exp":"Pd on charcoal is a **heterogeneous** catalyst: an insoluble solid in a liquid reaction. Filtration removes it, so the product is **free of catalyst**, and the recovered solid can be reused because catalysts are **regenerated**. A dissolved (homogeneous) catalyst that cannot be separated would stay in the product. No catalyst shifts the equilibrium or the yield; it only shortens the time to reach equilibrium.","how":{"ask":"Decide the catalyst's phase, then whether filtration can separate it.","know":"• Heterogeneous (solid) catalysts are filtered off; homogeneous (dissolved) ones stay with the product.\n• Catalysts are regenerated, not consumed.\n• Catalysts change the rate, not K or the equilibrium yield.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the catalyst is an insoluble powder, a different phase from the solution.","hl":"a black powder that does not dissolve"},{"text":"A filter catches it, so it does not contaminate the product.","hl":"passed through a filter","strike":["The product is contaminated, since Pd stays in solution"]},{"text":"Catalysts neither move the equilibrium nor get used up.","strike":["The yield rises, since the catalyst increases K","The yield falls, since the catalyst is consumed"]},{"text":"Answer: the product is pure, since the solid Pd is filtered off."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal catalyst always ends up in the product: only a dissolved one does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a black powder that does not dissolve","note":"heterogeneous"},{"t":"passed through a filter","note":"separated"},{"t":"reused in the next batch","note":"not consumed"}]},{"text":"**Phase decides separation.**","calc":["solid in liquid → filter → clean product","dissolved → same phase → stays in product"]},{"text":"**Also:** an inseparable homogeneous catalyst contaminates the product; a catalyst never shifts the equilibrium."},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme is a catalyst too. A lipase making an ester (acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water) only brings the mixture to equilibrium sooner; doubling the enzyme leaves K and the equilibrium amount of ester unchanged. Changing concentrations (adding acid or alcohol, taking water out) shifts the equilibrium position; only a temperature change alters K.","calc":["2× enzyme → equilibrium reached faster","K and [ester] at equilibrium: unchanged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a solid catalyst is filtered off and a dissolved one stays with the product; neither is consumed, and neither changes K or the yield."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-13a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"common-ion effect","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:13#1","pe1-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:13#3","pe1-cp:13#4"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:13"],"q":"A 0.20 M solution of formic acid is prepared. Which of the following changes would lower the percent ionization of the formic acid?","choices":["Adding nitric acid","Adding pure water","Adding sodium hydroxide","Adding sodium bicarbonate"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Bases raise weak-acid ionization","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Dilution raises percent ionization","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common-ion rule: adding the ion already produced (H+ from a strong acid) pushes the weak acid back left. Bases and water pull it right, so nitric acid."},"exp":"HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻. Nitric acid is a **strong acid**, so it floods the solution with **H⁺, an ion already in the equilibrium**. By **Le Châtelier** the equilibrium shifts **left** and a smaller fraction of formic acid ionizes (the **common-ion effect**). Water dilutes the acid, and the two bases remove H⁺; all three raise ionization.","how":{"ask":"For each added substance, decide whether the formic acid equilibrium shifts left (less ionized) or right (more ionized).","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] at equilibrium ÷ starting [HA] × 100.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product pushes the equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product pulls it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: a strong acid adds H⁺, a product of HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, so HA ionizes less.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium for the weak acid in the stem.","hl":"0.20 M solution of formic acid","calc":["HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻   (Ka ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴)","alone: x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁵) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ M","% = 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **3.0%**"]},{"text":"Nitric acid is a strong acid: it adds H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts left. With 0.050 M HNO₃ present:","calc":["x ≈ Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 ÷ 0.050","x = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","% = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **0.36%**  (down from 3.0%)"]},{"text":"Water dilutes the acid, and a diluted weak acid ionizes to a larger fraction.","strike":["Adding pure water"]},{"text":"Both bases remove H⁺ (OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O; HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃), so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Adding sodium hydroxide","Adding sodium bicarbonate"]},{"text":"Answer: adding nitric acid."}],"trap":"Choosing a base because it \"neutralizes\" the acid: removing H⁺ pulls the equilibrium to the right, so the weak acid ionizes more, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid","note":"weak acid: HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻"},{"t":"lower the percent ionization","note":"shift the equilibrium left"}]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** is the fraction of the acid molecules that have given up their H⁺.","calc":["% ionization = [A⁻] ÷ [HA]₀ × 100"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a product and the equilibrium moves back toward the reactant. H⁺ is a product, so an extra source of H⁺ makes less HA ionize.","calc":["add H⁺ → shift left → % ionization down","remove H⁺ → shift right → % ionization up"]},{"text":"**Also:** diluting a weak acid lowers [H⁺] but raises its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Also:** an electron-withdrawing substituent (such as Cl) near the acidic group makes the acid stronger, so it ionizes more."},{"text":"**Also:** a drop of indicator is too small an amount to change an acid's equilibrium or its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a strong acid (a common ion, H⁺) lowers a weak acid's percent ionization; adding a base raises it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Ohm's law V = IR","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:14"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"Between the two supply settings, the voltage across the enzyme electrode:","choices":["rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V.","falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V.","stays at 0.6 V.","rises from 600 V to 1800 V."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Fixed R: more current, more voltage","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The current triples (15 → 45 mA) at fixed R, so V triples. 40 Ω × 0.015 A = 0.6 V, then × 3 = 1.8 V."},"exp":"By **V = IR** at a fixed 40 Ω: V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = **0.6 V** and V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = **1.8 V**. Tripling the current triples the voltage, because **V is proportional to I** when R is constant.","how":{"ask":"Apply V = IR at each setting with the same R.","know":"• V = IR.\n• At constant R, V is proportional to I.\n• 1 mA = 10⁻³ A.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says R stays at 40 Ω while the current changes: R is fixed.","hl":"keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates"},{"text":"Convert and multiply.","calc":["V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = 0.6 V","V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = 1.8 V"]},{"text":"Unconverted mA gives hundreds of volts; a fall reverses V ∝ I; a constant voltage ignores the change in current.","strike":["rises from 600 V to 1800 V.","falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V.","stays at 0.6 V."]},{"text":"Answer: rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V."}],"trap":"Thinking constant resistance means constant voltage: at fixed R, V follows I."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"40 Ω","note":"R fixed"},{"t":"15 mA","note":"low setting"},{"t":"45 mA","note":"high setting"}]},{"text":"**Same R, so V follows I.**","calc":["V = IR","3 × I → 3 × V"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant R, V = IR rises in proportion to I; convert mA to A before multiplying."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"index of refraction n = c/v","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:15#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:15"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"What is the light guide's index of refraction, and what happens to the light's wavelength inside it?","choices":["1.5; the wavelength decreases","1.5; the wavelength increases","0.67; the wavelength decreases","0.67; the wavelength increases"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Refractive index is never below 1","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"n can never be below 1, which kills two choices. Slower light at a fixed frequency packs its waves closer together: shorter λ."},"exp":"**n = c/v** = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**. The frequency does not change at the boundary and **λ = v/f**, so the wavelength shrinks by the same factor: λ in the plastic = λ in vacuum ÷ 1.5. Any index below 1 is impossible.","how":{"ask":"Divide c by v, then use λ = v/f with f fixed.","know":"• n = c/v, and n ≥ 1 always.\n• Frequency is unchanged crossing a boundary.\n• λ = v/f, so λ falls by the factor n.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the two speeds: c in vacuum and v in the plastic.","hl":"inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s","calc":["n = c/v = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**"]},{"text":"An index below 1 is impossible.","strike":["0.67; the wavelength decreases","0.67; the wavelength increases"]},{"text":"f fixed and v smaller → λ smaller.","hl":"does not change on entering the guide","strike":["1.5; the wavelength increases"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.5; the wavelength decreases."}],"trap":"Writing v/c instead of c/v, or thinking slower light stretches its waves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two speeds and what stays fixed**.","marks":[{"t":"300,000 km/s","note":"c"},{"t":"200,000 km/s","note":"v in the plastic"},{"t":"does not change","note":"f fixed"}]},{"text":"**Fast over slow.** The bigger speed goes on top, so n comes out at least 1.","calc":["300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"**Same f, slower v, shorter λ.**","calc":["λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c/v ≥ 1, and with f fixed the wavelength inside a material is the vacuum wavelength ÷ n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"photon energy E = hf = hc/λ","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:16"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"Compared with LED Y, the quantity that is doubled for LED X is the:","choices":["energy of each photon.","photon arrival rate.","speed of the photons.","wavelength of the light."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Light's speed in vacuum never changes","c":[2]},{"f":"Higher frequency means shorter wavelength","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy, so double f means double energy per photon. Speed stays c, λ halves, and equal intensity means fewer photons."},"exp":"A photon's energy is set by its frequency: **E = hf**. Doubling f **doubles the energy of each photon**. The wavelength halves (λ = c/f), the speed stays c, and since both beams have the same intensity, X needs only **only half the photons each second** to carry the same power.","how":{"ask":"Which property of a single photon depends on frequency, and what does equal intensity then force on the photon count?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**: one photon's energy is proportional to its frequency.\n• **c = fλ**: higher f means shorter λ; light speed in air stays c ≈ 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.\n• **Intensity** = (photons per second) × (energy per photon) ÷ area.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says X's frequency is twice Y's; apply E = hf to both.","hl":"LED Y at half that frequency","calc":["E_Y = hf","E_X = h(2f) = **2E_Y**","e.g. f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz: E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","2f = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ Hz: E = 6.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy rises in proportion to frequency (E = hf)","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"Equal intensity with twice the energy per photon means half the photons each second, so the arrival rate halves.","hl":"equal intensity","calc":["I = (photons/s) × E ÷ A","same I, 2E → photons/s × ½"],"strike":["photon arrival rate."]},{"text":"Light speed in air is c at every frequency, and c = fλ means doubling f halves λ.","calc":["λ = c ÷ f → λ_X = λ_Y ÷ 2"],"strike":["speed of the photons.","wavelength of the light."]},{"text":"Answer: the energy of each photon."}],"trap":"Thinking higher frequency means more photons arriving. Frequency sets each photon's energy; at the same intensity a higher frequency actually means fewer photons per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same and what differs**.","marks":[{"t":"equal intensity","note":"same energy per second"},{"t":"LED Y at half that frequency","note":"X has twice f"},{"t":"is doubled","note":"the ask"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in photons.** Each photon's energy is set by its frequency alone.","calc":["E = hf = hc/λ","2f → 2E"]},{"text":"**Intensity belongs to the beam**: energy per photon times photons per second, per area.","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A","fixed I and 2E → ½ the photons/s"]},{"text":"**See it:** photon energy is a straight line through zero against frequency.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"E = hf: doubling the frequency doubles the photon energy","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** at one fixed color, intensity is proportional to the number of photons per second; speed, wavelength and polarization don't set it."},{"text":"**Also:** one photon has an energy, a frequency and a wavelength, linked by E = hf = hc/λ. Intensity and amplitude belong to the beam (how many photons arrive each second), so a photon with twice the energy has twice the f and half the λ, but that tells you nothing about amplitude.","calc":["one photon: E, f, λ","the whole beam: photons per second → intensity, amplitude"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf, so photon energy rises in step with frequency (and λ falls); the photon rate sets intensity at a fixed color, and speed stays c."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-17a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"photon energy E = hf = hc/λ","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:17","pe6-cp:51"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P3","passage":"A point-of-care glucose meter pairs an enzyme electrode with an optical check. The enzyme-coated electrode keeps a resistance of 40 Ω while it operates; its adjustable supply drives 15 mA through it at a low setting and 45 mA at a higher one. For the optical check, light from an LED reaches the sample along a plastic light guide. In vacuum this light travels at 300,000 km/s, but inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s, and its frequency, set by the LED, does not change on entering the guide. The meter's indicator dye fluoresces only when a single photon brings it at least 3.5 eV. LED X emits at 750 THz and LED Y at half that frequency; Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Photodetectors show that the two LEDs deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre.","q":"How much energy does each photon from LED X carry, and is it enough to make the dye fluoresce?","choices":["3.1 eV; no","3.1 eV; yes","0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"4 × 7.5 = 30 and 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹, so about 3 eV. That is below 3.5 eV, so one photon cannot do it."},"exp":"**E = hf** with **tera = 10¹²**: f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(7.5 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) ≈ **3.1 eV**. That is **less** than the 3.5 eV the photoinitiator needs, so a single photon cannot start the curing, however bright the lamp.","how":{"ask":"Convert THz to Hz, compute E = hf, then compare E with the threshold.","know":"• E = hf.\n• tera = 10¹², giga = 10⁹.\n• One photon either carries enough energy or it does not; a brighter lamp only adds photons.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives LED X at 750 THz; convert to hertz.","hl":"LED X emits at 750 THz","calc":["750 THz = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵)(7.5 × 10¹⁴)","4.14 × 7.5 ≈ 31; 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"Giga instead of tera gives 0.0031 eV; dividing h by f gives 10⁻³⁰.","strike":["0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"]},{"text":"Compare: 3.1 eV is below the 3.5-eV threshold.","hl":"at least 3.5 eV","strike":["3.1 eV; yes"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.1 eV; no."}],"trap":"Getting E right but flipping the comparison, or thinking a brighter lamp makes each photon stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the threshold**.","marks":[{"t":"750 THz","note":"f of X"},{"t":"at least 3.5 eV","note":"threshold, one photon"},{"t":"4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s","note":"h in eV"}]},{"text":"**Tera means 10¹².** Write f in scientific notation, then multiply.","calc":["f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz","E = hf ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"**Compare one photon with the threshold.** Brightness changes how many photons arrive, never the energy of each."},{"text":"**Also:** E = hf covers radio waves too; only the prefix changes. Mega = 10⁶, so an FM station at 100 MHz emits photons at 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz, each carrying millions of times less energy than a visible photon.","calc":["E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁶ J","green light (5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz): ≈ 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","FM-band photons (88–108 MHz): about 6–7 × 10⁻²⁶ J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf with f in hertz (THz × 10¹²); compare one photon's energy with the threshold, since brightness never makes a single photon stronger."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-51a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-18a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"use a given formula","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:18"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 35 L of fluid in 7.0 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"Which equation gives this patient's tidal volume from the passage's quantities?","choices":["V_T = V_A/f + V_D","V_T = V_A/f − V_D","V_T = f × V_A + V_D","V_T = (V_A + V_D)/f"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Divide by f to undo ×f","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Undo the outside first: divide by f, leaving V_T − V_D = V_A/f. Then undo the subtraction: add V_D."},"exp":"Rearrange V_A = f(V_T − V_D): divide both sides by f, then add V_D, so **V_T = V_A/f + V_D**. With her numbers: 4.2 ÷ 14 + 0.15 = 0.45 L, a normal-sized breath.","how":{"ask":"Isolate V_T in the given formula by undoing each operation in reverse order.","know":"• Undo operations in reverse order: divide by the factor outside the bracket, then add what was subtracted.\n• Check units and size: a resting breath is about half a litre.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the formula.","hl":"V_A = f × (V_T − V_D)"},{"text":"Divide both sides by f.","calc":["V_A/f = V_T − V_D"]},{"text":"Add V_D to both sides.","calc":["V_T = **V_A/f + V_D**","check: 4.2 ÷ 14 + 0.15 = 0.45 L"]},{"text":"Subtracting V_D takes it away twice; multiplying by f undoes nothing; dividing the sum by f puts V_D inside the division.","strike":["V_T = V_A/f − V_D","V_T = f × V_A + V_D","V_T = (V_A + V_D)/f"]},{"text":"Answer: V_T = V_A/f + V_D."}],"trap":"Keeping the minus sign: V_D was subtracted inside the bracket, so it must be added back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **name every symbol** in the formula.","marks":[{"t":"f is the breathing rate","note":"f"},{"t":"V_T the tidal volume","note":"the unknown"},{"t":"V_D the dead-space volume","note":"V_D"}]},{"text":"**Undo the outside first.** Divide both sides by f.","calc":["V_T − V_D = V_A ÷ f"]},{"text":"**Then undo the inside.** Add V_D to both sides.","calc":["V_T = V_A ÷ f + V_D"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a fluid column, h = P/(ρg): atmospheric pressure can push water up an evacuated tube about 10 m.","calc":["h = P/(ρg)","1 atm ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; water: 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) ≈ 10 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a question gives a formula, name each variable and plug the values in. In a blood pressure written systolic/diastolic, the diastolic is the lower number.","calc":["given pulse pressure = P_s − P_d","120/76 mmHg: P_s = 120, P_d = 76","pulse pressure = 120 − 76 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same plug-in works for a formula you have never met, such as a gyromagnetic ratio γ = 2πf/B. Match each symbol to its value in the passage (f, frequency; B, field), keep the units the choices use (MHz stays MHz for an answer in MHz/T), and leave 2π standing as a factor when every choice carries it.","calc":["¹H at 400 MHz in a 9.4 T field","γ = 2π × 400 MHz ÷ 9.4 T ≈ 42.6 × 2π MHz/T"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to isolate a variable inside a bracket, divide by the outside factor first, then undo the operation inside; check that the answer's size makes sense."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-19d","s":"Fluids","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"pressure units mmHg ↔ atm","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:19#4","pe1-cp:19#1","pe1-cp:19#2","pe1-cp:19#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:19"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 35 L of fluid in 7.0 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"Approximately what percentage of atmospheric pressure is the patient's pulse pressure?","choices":["5.8%","16%","9.7%","59%"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"No blood pressure nears 0.5 atm","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. 38 mmHg is 5% of 760, so 44 is a bit more: about 5.8%."},"exp":"**Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic** = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. **1 atm = 760 mmHg**, so 44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ **5.8%**. 16% and 9.7% use one reading alone, and 59% divides by the diastolic value.","how":{"ask":"Subtract to get the pulse pressure, then divide by 760 mmHg.","know":"• Blood pressure is systolic/diastolic; a typical adult is about 120/80 mmHg.\n• Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic.\n• 1 atm = 760 mmHg; % = value ÷ 760 × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the reading and the definition: subtract diastolic from systolic.","hl":"118/74 mmHg","calc":["pulse = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"Divide by 760 and multiply by 100.","calc":["44 ÷ 760 = 0.058","× 100 = **5.8%**"]},{"text":"16% and 9.7% skip the subtraction; 59% divides by the diastolic pressure.","strike":["16%","9.7%","59%"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.8%."}],"trap":"Dividing one reading by 760 instead of the difference between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the definition**.","marks":[{"t":"118/74 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"systolic minus diastolic","note":"pulse pressure"}]},{"text":"**Subtract, then compare with 760 mmHg.**","calc":["44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ 5.8%"]},{"text":"**Also:** a typical healthy adult's blood pressure is about 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."},{"text":"**Also:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic."},{"text":"**Also:** 1 atm = 760 mmHg; multiply atm by 760 to get mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic; percent of 1 atm = (mmHg ÷ 760) × 100."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-20b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"half-life decay","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:20#2","pe1-cp:20#1","pe1-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:20"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 35 L of fluid in 7.0 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"Assuming the trend continues, what percentage of the tracer will have decayed 35 hours after preparation, and what activity will remain?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Tracer activity after preparation","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":15,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Activity (Bq)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,640],[5,320],[10,160],[15,80]]}]},"choices":["99.2%; 5 Bq","0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The graph halves every 5 h, so 35 h is 7 halvings: 640 → 5 Bq, and only 1 part in 128 (about 0.8%) is left, so about 99.2% has decayed."},"exp":"The activity halves every **5 h** (640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq), so the half-life is 5 h. At 35 h, **n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7** half-lives: remaining = (½)⁷ = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%, so **about 99.2% has decayed**, and the activity is 640 ÷ 128 = **5 Bq**.","how":{"ask":"Find the half-life from the graph, count half-lives, then compute the decayed fraction and the activity.","know":"• n = time ÷ half-life.\n• remaining = (½)ⁿ; decayed = 1 − remaining.\n• Activity tracks how many undecayed nuclei are left.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the activity was read every 5 hours; the graph halves at each reading, so t½ = 5 h.","hl":"measured every 5 hours after preparation","calc":["640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq","t½ = 5 h"]},{"text":"Count half-lives and compute.","hl":"35 hours","calc":["n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7","remaining = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%","decayed ≈ 99.2%","activity = 640 ÷ 128 = 5 Bq"]},{"text":"0.8% is what remains; 98.4% stops at six half-lives; 75% is the 10-h reading.","strike":["0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"]},{"text":"Answer: 99.2% decayed; 5 Bq."}],"trap":"Reporting the fraction remaining as the fraction decayed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"every 5 hours","note":"time step"},{"t":"35 hours","note":"target time"},{"t":"first-order kinetics","note":"constant half-life"}]},{"text":"**Half-life from the graph**, then count.","calc":["t½ = 5 h","n = 35 ÷ 5 = 7"]},{"text":"**Decayed = 1 − remaining.**","calc":["1 − 1/128 ≈ 99.2%"]},{"text":"**Also:** fraction decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ, where n = time ÷ half-life."},{"text":"**Also:** to find the starting amount, run it backward: double once per half-life, so original = remaining × 2ⁿ.","calc":["n = 18 days ÷ 6 days = 3","original = 7 mg × 2³ = 7 × 8 = 56 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** activity (decays per second, in Bq or Ci) is proportional to the number of nuclei left, so it falls on the same schedule: A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ, with n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.","calc":["t½ = 8.0 days, t = 24 days → n = 3","A = 600 Bq × (½)³ = 600 ÷ 8 = 75 Bq"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = t ÷ t½, remaining = (½)ⁿ, decayed = 1 − remaining, and activity falls by the same factor as the nuclei."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-21b","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"energy = power × time","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:21#2","pe1-cp:21#1","pe1-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:21","sample-cp:56"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P4","passage":"A patient with kidney failure is assessed before her first dialysis session. Her blood pressure reads 118/74 mmHg, and her pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. Alveolar ventilation, the fresh air reaching the alveoli each minute, is V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T the tidal volume and V_D the dead-space volume of the airways. She breathes 14 times per minute, with a dead space of 0.15 L and an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min. For a kidney scan she receives a radioactive tracer that decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter; its activity was measured every 5 hours after preparation, and the first four readings are graphed. During dialysis, the machine's pump moves 35 L of fluid in 7.0 min at a steady flow. The pump's power curve passes through 9 W at 0 L/min, 14 W at 2.5 L/min, 19 W at 5 L/min and 24 W at 7.5 L/min.","q":"How much work does the dialysis pump do during the 7.0 min?","choices":["8.0 kJ","133 J","9.7 kJ","3.8 kJ"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Flow = 35 ÷ 7.0 = 5 L/min → 19 W. Time = 420 s. 19 × 420 ≈ 8000 J."},"exp":"Flow = 35 L ÷ 7.0 min = **5.0 L/min**, where the curve gives **19 W**. Work = P × t = 19 W × 420 s = 7980 J ≈ **8.0 kJ**.","how":{"ask":"Find the flow, read the power at that flow, then multiply by the time in seconds.","know":"• Flow = volume ÷ time, in the axis units (L/min).\n• Read y at that x on the curve.\n• W = P × t, with t in seconds for joules.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the volume and time; turn them into a flow rate in the curve's units.","hl":"moves 35 L of fluid in 7.0 min","calc":["Q = 35 ÷ 7.0 = 5.0 L/min"]},{"text":"Sentence 7: read the power at 5.0 L/min.","hl":"19 W at 5 L/min","calc":["P = 19 W"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pump power versus flow rate","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2.5},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,9],[2.5,14],[5,19],[7.5,24],[10,29]]}]}},{"text":"Multiply by the time in seconds.","calc":["t = 7.0 × 60 = 420 s","W = 19 × 420 = 7980 J ≈ 8.0 kJ"]},{"text":"133 J keeps minutes; 3.8 kJ reads near zero flow; 9.7 kJ reads the curve at 7.0.","strike":["133 J","3.8 kJ","9.7 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.0 kJ."}],"trap":"Reading the graph at a number from the question (7.0 min), or multiplying watts by minutes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"35 L","note":"volume"},{"t":"7.0 min","note":"time"},{"t":"power curve","note":"read P at that flow"}]},{"text":"**Get the x value**, then read the curve.","calc":["35 L ÷ 7.0 min = 5.0 L/min → 19 W"]},{"text":"**Work = power × time**, in seconds.","calc":["19 W × 420 s ≈ 8.0 kJ"]},{"text":"**Also:** compute a rate in exactly the units of the graph's axis before reading the graph."},{"text":"**Also:** work = power × time, with power in watts and time in seconds, giving joules."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute the x value in the axis's units, read the curve there, then W = P × t with t in seconds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-23c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain classes","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:23#3","pe1-cp:23#1","pe1-cp:23#2","pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:23"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which residue gives migrating cells a charged group to bind?","choices":["Arg in region B","Cys in region A","Gly in region A","Cys in region B"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Glycine has no side chain","c":[2]},{"f":"Cysteine is polar but uncharged","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charged at body pH: K, R, D, E (and H partly). Gly and Cys carry no charge, and region B has no Cys, so Arg in B."},"exp":"Match each residue to its region first. Region A holds **Gly** (no side chain) and **Cys** (a thiol for disulfide cross-links), so it offers no charge. Region B holds Lys, Glu, Arg and Asp, all **charged** at body pH; **Arg** is one of them. There is no Cys in region B.","how":{"ask":"List the residues in each region, then find a charged one in the region where it actually sits.","know":"• Charged at body pH: Asp −, Glu −, Lys +, Arg +.\n• **Cys** = thiol for **disulfide cross-links**, not a charge.\n• **Gly** = no side chain.\n• A choice must name a residue that is really in the region it names.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 lists the residues of each region.","hl":"Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys","calc":["region A: Gly, Cys → cross-linking","region B: Lys, Glu, Arg, Asp → all charged"]},{"text":"Region A has no charged side chain: Gly has none, and Cys forms disulfides.","strike":["Cys in region A","Gly in region A"]},{"text":"Cys does not appear in region B, so that pairing is wrong.","hl":"Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp","strike":["Cys in region B"]},{"text":"Answer: Arg in region B."}],"trap":"Choosing cysteine because its sulfur looks reactive: it belongs to the cross-linking region and gives a covalent link between strands, not a charge for cells."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cross-linking region A","note":"Cys, Gly"},{"t":"cell-binding region B","note":"charged residues"},{"t":"electrostatic contacts","note":"need a charge"}]},{"text":"**Sort by job.** Cys makes S–S cross-links; Gly is a spacer; charged residues bind cells.","fig":["cysteine","glycine"]},{"text":"**Check the charged ones.**","calc":["Lys +, Arg +","Glu −, Asp −"],"fig":["arginine","glutamate"]},{"text":"**Check the location too.** A right residue in the wrong region is a wrong answer."},{"text":"**Also:** for an electrostatic contact, choose a side chain charged opposite to the partner: Lys or Arg for a − site, Asp or Glu for a + site."},{"text":"**Also:** glycine has no side chain (R = H), so it adds no charge or functional group to a binding site."},{"text":"**Also:** a binding pocket can name two contacts at once. A phosphate (−) makes a salt bridge only with a + side chain (Lys or Arg), and a flat aromatic ring π-stacks with an aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp or His). The right pair satisfies both: Asp or Glu would repel the phosphate, and Ser or Ala cannot stack.","calc":["phosphate (−) → Lys⁺ or Arg⁺","aromatic ring → Phe, Tyr, Trp or His","pair: one from each list, e.g. Lys + Trp"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each residue to its labeled region and its job: Cys cross-links, Gly adds nothing, and Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg give charges; for a phosphate in a ring pocket, pair a + residue (Lys/Arg) with an aromatic one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-25c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"ionization state at pH (pH vs pKa)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:25#3","pe1-cp:25#1","pe1-cp:25#2","pe3-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:31#3","pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:25","sample-cp:17"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P5","passage":"A designed peptide is spun into fibers for a wound-dressing gel. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands, and its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH. A Ser–Met linker joins region B to an assembly region of six glutamates, whose side-chain pKa is about 4.3. Samples equilibrated at different pH values were tested for the percentage of peptide found in fibers, and the results are graphed. When the wet gel is left in air, links form between neighboring strands and the gel turns stiff. Soaking the stiff gel in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes it soft again. In the wound, a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to the peptide: mass spectrometry places it on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol, and replacing that residue with alanine prevents the labeling.","q":"Which explanation is most consistent with the fiber data in the graph?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide found in fibers after equilibration","x":{"label":"pH","min":2,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"In fibers (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Fibers","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[3,90],[4,65],[5,20],[6,5],[7,2],[8,2]]}]},"choices":["Protonated glutamates no longer repel","Deprotonated glutamates attract each other","Protonated glutamates form ion pairs","Deprotonated glutamates shed bound water"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Low pH protonates carboxyl groups","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Neutral –COOH can't form ion pairs","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fibers appear below about pH 4, near the glutamate pKa. Below it COO⁻ becomes neutral COOH, so the strands stop repelling and pack."},"exp":"Fibers form **below pH ≈ 4.3**, the glutamate side-chain pKa. Under that pH, the side chains are **protonated and neutral (–COOH)**, so the strands **no longer repel** and can pack into fibers. Above it they are **–COO⁻**, all negative, and like charges push the strands apart. A neutral –COOH has no charge to make an ion pair.","how":{"ask":"Link the pH where fibers form to the side chain's pKa and charge.","know":"• Acidic side chain: pH < pKa → –COOH (0); pH > pKa → –COO⁻ (−1).\n• Like charges repel and keep strands apart; neutral strands can pack.\n• A salt bridge needs opposite charges.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the glutamate pKa; the graph (sentence 4) shows fibers only at pH below it.","hl":"side-chain pKa is about 4.3"},{"text":"Above the pKa every glutamate is −1: they repel rather than attract, and they hold water more tightly.","strike":["Deprotonated glutamates attract each other","Deprotonated glutamates shed bound water"]},{"text":"Below the pKa the –COOH is neutral, so it has no charge to pair.","strike":["Protonated glutamates form ion pairs"]},{"text":"Answer: protonated glutamates no longer repel."}],"trap":"Reaching for ion pairs whenever charge matters: a protonated –COOH is neutral and has no charge to pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"six glutamates","note":"acidic side chains"},{"t":"pKa is about 4.3","note":"charge switch"},{"t":"percentage of peptide found in fibers","note":"read the graph"}]},{"text":"**Charge on each side of the pKa.**","calc":["pH < 4.3: –COOH, 0","pH > 4.3: –COO⁻, −1"]},{"text":"**Neutral strands pack; charged strands repel.**"},{"text":"**Also:** a group grafted onto a residue sets its charge the same way: an added –COOH (pKa ≈ 4) is ionized at pH 7, so the residue becomes acidic; an added amine (pKa ≈ 9–10) is protonated, so it becomes basic."},{"text":"**Also:** a side chain's charge changes only when the pH moves past its pKa; acids go 0 → −1, bases go +1 → 0 as pH rises."},{"text":"**Also:** charged side chains repel and keep peptides apart; when the pH neutralizes them, the strands can pack together."},{"text":"**Also:** at a fixed pH, lowering a group's pKa means less of it is protonated, so a structure held together by the protonated form (such as C·C⁺ pairs in an i-motif) is less stable; compare pK values to see which change lowered it.","calc":["pKa 6.5 → 6.0 at pH 6.5: 50% → 24% protonated → less stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same pKa-versus-pH test works for a group added to a residue. A grafted –CH₂NH₃⁺ with pKa 9.5 sits about 2 units above pH 7.4, so it stays protonated (+1) and makes the side chain basic.","calc":["9.5 − 7.4 = 2.1 units","[–CH₂NH₂] : [–CH₂NH₃⁺] = 10^(7.4 − 9.5) ≈ 1 : 126","≈ 99% carries +1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare the pH with each side chain's pKa: charged chains repel and stay apart, and neutralized chains can pack together."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-27a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"Pourbaix diagram reading","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:27#1","pe1-cp:27#2","pe1-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:27"],"q":"On a potential–pH diagram, the boundary between Cu²⁺ and copper metal stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5. Which species is absent from its half-reaction?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Every half-reaction transfers electrons","c":[1]},{"f":"A half-reaction contains both redox forms","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Flat line vs pH means pH has zero effect, so the half-reaction contains no H+ or OH−. Electrons and both copper forms must still be there."},"exp":"A **horizontal** boundary keeps the same potential whatever the pH, so the equilibrium **does not depend on pH**: its half-reaction, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu, contains **no H⁺ or OH⁻**. It still transfers electrons, and both copper forms appear in it.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a boundary at one fixed potential across a range of pH says about the half-reaction.","know":"• Pourbaix diagram: potential E (y) versus pH (x); each line is an equilibrium between two forms.\n• **Horizontal line**: E does not change with pH → **pH-independent** → no H⁺ or OH⁻ in the half-reaction, but electrons are transferred.","steps":[{"text":"The boundary is flat: one potential across several pH values.","hl":"stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5","calc":["E constant while pH changes → pH-independent"]},{"text":"Write the half-reaction it represents.","calc":["Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu   (E° = +0.34 V)","no H⁺, no OH⁻"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"A line set at a potential is a redox couple, so electrons must be in it; Cu²⁺ and Cu are the two forms the line separates.","strike":["The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ and OH⁻."}],"trap":"Thinking a flat line means \"no electrons\": that describes a vertical line. A flat line is a redox couple with no H⁺ or OH⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"potential–pH diagram","note":"E on y, pH on x"},{"t":"stays at +0.34 V","note":"horizontal line"},{"t":"from pH 0 to 5","note":"pH changes, E does not"}]},{"text":"**Read the slope of each boundary.** A flat line sits at one potential, whatever the pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"**Flat = pH has no say.** If changing pH does not move the line, H⁺ and OH⁻ are not in the equilibrium.","calc":["horizontal → electrons yes, H⁺/OH⁻ no"]},{"text":"**Also:** a vertical Pourbaix boundary means the equilibrium depends on pH but transfers no electrons."},{"text":"**Also:** the higher a region lies on a potential–pH diagram, the more oxidized the species stable there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a horizontal Pourbaix boundary is a redox equilibrium that does not depend on pH (no H⁺ or OH⁻ involved)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-29b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ideal gas assumptions","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:29#2","pe1-cp:29#1","pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:29"],"q":"A chemist compares 0.200 mol of neon at 127 °C and 0.820 atm with the ideal-gas model, taking R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). Which pair gives an assumption of the ideal-gas model and the volume it predicts for this sample?","choices":["Particles do not attract; 8.0 L","Particles do not attract; 4.5 L","Particles attract weakly; 8.0 L","Particles attract weakly; 4.5 L"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ideal gases have no attractions","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"nRT ≈ 0.2 × 0.082 × 400 ≈ 6.6; ÷ 0.82 ≈ 8 L. Then pick the premise with zero attractions."},"exp":"The ideal-gas model treats particles as points with **no intermolecular attractions** (and no volume of their own). Volume: V = nRT/P = (0.200)(0.0821)(400 K) ÷ 0.820 ≈ **8.0 L**. 4.5 L is 0.200 × 22.4, valid only at STP.","how":{"ask":"Pick the true premise, then compute V = nRT/P.","know":"• Ideal gas: negligible particle volume, no attractions, elastic collisions.\n• V = nRT/P with T in K.\n• 22.4 L/mol only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm).","steps":[{"text":"Convert T to kelvin.","hl":"127 °C","calc":["T = 127 + 273 = 400 K"]},{"text":"Compute V.","calc":["V = (0.200)(0.0821)(400) ÷ 0.820","= 6.57 ÷ 0.820 ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"22.4 L/mol is for STP only.","strike":["Particles do not attract; 4.5 L","Particles attract weakly; 4.5 L"]},{"text":"Ideal particles have no attractions, not weak ones.","strike":["Particles attract weakly; 8.0 L"]},{"text":"Answer: particles do not attract; 8.0 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol away from STP, or calling weak attractions ideal."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"0.200 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"127 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"0.820 atm","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Premise:** ideal particles occupy no volume and feel no attractions."},{"text":"**Volume:** V = nRT/P in kelvin.","calc":["V ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** a gas is most ideal at high T and low P, where particle volume and intermolecular forces are both negligible."},{"text":"**Also:** run it backward to count moles: at STP, n = V ÷ 22.4 L/mol, the same answer PV/RT gives. Put the volume in litres first: 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.","calc":["112 cm³ = 112 mL = 0.112 L","n = 0.112 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.0050 mol","check: n = PV/RT = (1 atm)(0.112 L) ÷ (0.0821 × 273) = 0.0050 mol ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ideal gas = point particles with no attractions; V = nRT/P in kelvin, and 22.4 L/mol only at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-30a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"color vision needs multiple pigments","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:30"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"Shown the 450-nm and 530-nm lights, the fish:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance of the fish's rod pigment","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]}]},"choices":["cannot tell them apart, since one pigment gives the same signal.","can tell them apart, since 530-nm light gives a stronger signal.","can tell them apart, since bluer photons give a stronger signal.","can tell them apart, since the brain compares two pigments."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Rods signal photons absorbed, not wavelength","c":[1,2]},{"f":"This fish has one visual pigment","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal photons absorbed by the only pigment means identical signals. With nothing to compare, the two lights look the same."},"exp":"A photoreceptor reports only **how many photons it absorbs**, not their wavelength. The intensities were set so the single pigment absorbs equal numbers from both lights, so the rods send **identical signals** and the fish cannot tell them apart. Color vision needs **a second pigment with a different peak**, so the brain can compare the two responses.","how":{"ask":"Does anything differ between the two signals once absorption is matched?","know":"• A pigment signals photons absorbed, not wavelength (univariance).\n• Color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks; the brain compares their ratio.\n• Matching absorption for the only pigment erases any difference.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the rods hold one pigment, and each rod reports only how many photons it absorbs.","hl":"a single visual pigment"},{"text":"Sentence 3: absorption was matched for the two lights, so the signals are equal whatever the photon energy.","hl":"captured equal numbers of photons per second from each","strike":["can tell them apart, since 530-nm light gives a stronger signal.","can tell them apart, since bluer photons give a stronger signal."]},{"text":"With one pigment there is no second signal to compare.","strike":["can tell them apart, since the brain compares two pigments."]},{"text":"Answer: cannot tell them apart, since one pigment gives the same signal."}],"trap":"Thinking the wavelength itself is signaled: only the count of absorbed photons is."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a single visual pigment","note":"one signal only"},{"t":"equal numbers of photons per second","note":"matched absorption"},{"t":"only how many photons","note":"no color information"}]},{"text":"**One pigment sends one number**, so matched lights look identical."},{"text":"**Add a second pigment** with another peak and the two lights give different ratios: that comparison is color vision.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two pigments, two peaks","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a single pigment reports only photons absorbed; color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks whose responses the brain compares."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain classes","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:31#1","pe1-cp:31#2","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:31"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"The amino acid that would most likely line the odorant cavity is:","fig":"limonene","choices":["Methionine","Asparagine","Glutamine","Histidine"],"choiceFigs":["methionine-sc","asparagine-sc","glutamine-sc","histidine-sc"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Asn, Gln and His are polar","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydrocarbon ligand + no water = hydrophobic lining. Only one choice has a side chain with no N–H, O–H or charge."},"exp":"Limonene is a **hydrocarbon** with no polar groups, and the cavity is **sealed from water**, so it is held by **hydrophobic (dispersion) contacts**. Methionine's side chain (–CH₂CH₂–S–CH₃) is nonpolar. Asparagine and glutamine carry polar amides, and histidine has a polar, ionizable ring; these line water-facing surfaces.","how":{"ask":"Classify the ligand and the pocket, then pick the side chain that matches.","know":"• Nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket → hydrophobic lining (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met).\n• Polar (Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr) and charged or ionizable (Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His) side chains prefer water.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the odorant is a hydrocarbon held in a cavity away from water.","hl":"sealed off from the surrounding water","fig":"limonene"},{"text":"Amide side chains are polar.","strike":["Asparagine","Glutamine"]},{"text":"Histidine's ring is polar and can carry a + charge.","strike":["Histidine"]},{"text":"Answer: methionine, whose thioether side chain is nonpolar."}],"trap":"Picking any long side chain: polar amides do not suit a hydrocarbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a small hydrocarbon odorant","note":"nonpolar guest"},{"t":"sealed off from the surrounding water","note":"water-free pocket"},{"t":"charged residues","note":"polar walls fail"}]},{"text":"**Like holds like.** A nonpolar guest is held by nonpolar side chains through dispersion contacts.","calc":["nonpolar: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met","polar: Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr","ionizable: Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His"]},{"text":"**Also:** charged or polar side chains don't fit a pocket for a nonpolar ligand; swapping in Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, Ser or Thr weakens binding."},{"text":"**Also:** when a nonpolar part of a drug sits in a water-free pocket, its main interaction is hydrophobic contact (London dispersion) with nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe, not hydrogen bonds or salt bridges."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket is lined by nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met); polar and charged side chains face water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-32a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphate label tracking (kinase/phosphatase)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:32#1","pe1-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:32"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P6","passage":"Biologists studied how a deep-sea fish senses its dim surroundings. Its rods contain a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is graphed, and each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs. In one test, a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light were adjusted so that the pigment captured equal numbers of photons per second from each. The fish also smells limonene, a small hydrocarbon odorant held by an odorant-binding protein in a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water; mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding. Males release (E)-4-chlorooct-4-ene as a mating signal, while its Z isomer is inactive. A pure sample of either isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature, and the two interconvert only under ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond. A gill parasite switches off the fish's proteins by AMPylation: its enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect this, researchers supply ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions and check only the target protein for radioactivity.","q":"The ATP label that would end up on the parasite's target protein is:","fig":"atp","choices":["[α-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[γ-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Pyrophosphate holds ATP's β, γ phosphates","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"AMP = adenosine + the α phosphate. Pyrophosphate = β + γ. Only a label in the AMP part reaches the protein."},"exp":"AMPylation transfers **AMP**, adenosine plus the **α phosphate**, to the threonine and releases **pyrophosphate (PPi)**, which holds the β and γ phosphates. So only an **α label** ends up on the protein. A kinase is different: it transfers the terminal **γ phosphate**, and the kinase itself is the catalyst, not the source.","how":{"ask":"Split ATP into the part transferred and the part released, then follow each label.","know":"• ATP = adenosine–Pα–Pβ–Pγ.\n• Kinases move Pγ; ADP is left behind.\n• AMP transfer moves adenosine–Pα; Pβ–Pγ leaves as pyrophosphate.\n• The enzyme is the catalyst, not the source of the group.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says the parasite's enzyme moves AMP and releases pyrophosphate.","hl":"attaches an AMP unit from ATP","fig":"atp"},{"text":"γ and β labels leave in the pyrophosphate.","hl":"releases pyrophosphate","strike":["[γ-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"]},{"text":"Answer: [α-³³P]ATP."}],"trap":"Reaching for γ by habit: that is the kinase answer; here AMP, with the α phosphate, is transferred."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is moved and what is released**.","marks":[{"t":"attaches an AMP unit","note":"α phosphate + adenosine"},{"t":"releases pyrophosphate","note":"β + γ leave"},{"t":"only the target protein","note":"what is counted"}]},{"text":"**Map each phosphate.**","calc":["Pα → with AMP → protein","Pβ, Pγ → PPi → released"]},{"text":"**Also:** a kinase is the catalyst; the phosphate it attaches comes from ATP's γ phosphate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the piece of ATP the enzyme transfers: γ for a kinase, α (with adenosine) for AMP transfer; labels elsewhere leave in ADP or PPi."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-34b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"hydrolysis products","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:34#2","pe1-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:34"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"The lipase is then run in a nearly water-free solvent with glycerol and excess oleic acid, and triolein builds up. Which type of reaction does the lipase catalyze under these conditions?","fig":"triacylglycerol","choices":["Hydrolysis","Esterification","Saponification","Phosphorolysis"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Saponification needs a strong base","c":[2]},{"f":"No phosphate is present","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Triolein builds up from glycerol and acid, so ester bonds are forming: the reverse of hydrolysis."},"exp":"A lipase catalyzes the **hydrolysis of ester bonds** in water, and like any catalyst it speeds both directions. With almost no water and excess oleic acid, the net reaction runs the other way: glycerol + 3 oleic acid → triolein + 3 H₂O. Ester bonds form and water is released, so the lipase now catalyzes **esterification**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which bonds are forming when triolein builds up.","know":"• Lipases catalyze ester hydrolysis: ester + H₂O → fatty acid + alcohol.\n• A catalyst speeds a reaction in both directions; the conditions set the net direction.\n• Esterification is the reverse: acid + alcohol → ester + H₂O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2: in water, the lipase cuts an ester bond, and the pH falls as fatty acid forms.","hl":"hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon","fig":"triacylglycerol"},{"text":"In the stem, triolein builds up from glycerol and oleic acid, so ester bonds are forming and water is released.","hl":"triolein builds up","calc":["glycerol + 3 oleic acid → triolein + 3 H₂O"]},{"text":"Hydrolysis would use up esters, not build them, and saponification needs a strong base, which is absent.","strike":["Hydrolysis","Saponification"]},{"text":"Phosphorolysis cleaves bonds with phosphate, and no phosphate is present.","strike":["Phosphorolysis"]},{"text":"Answer: esterification."}],"trap":"Answering hydrolysis by habit: a catalyst speeds both directions, and with little water the net reaction runs in reverse."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"nearly water-free solvent","note":"little water"},{"t":"excess oleic acid","note":"acid in excess"},{"t":"triolein builds up","note":"esters forming"}]},{"text":"**Name the bond that forms.**","calc":["acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in water this lipase cuts only the C2 ester of triolein, giving oleic acid and 1,3-dioleoylglycerol; pancreatic lipase cuts at C1 and C3 instead and leaves a 2-monoacylglycerol.","calc":["triolein + H₂O → oleic acid + 1,3-dioleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lipase catalyzes ester hydrolysis in water and esterification when water is scarce and acid is in excess; name the reaction by the bond that forms or breaks."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-35b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ionization state at pH (pH vs pKa)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:35#2","pe1-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2","pe5-cp:31#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:35","pe5-cp:31"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"By how much does the resin-binding variant's net charge change at pH 7?","choices":["It decreases by 6","It decreases by 5","It does not change","It increases by 6"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[1,2]},{"f":"Basic-to-acidic swaps lower net charge","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7: K, R = +1; D, E = −1; A, H ≈ 0. Each + to − swap is −2; three swaps = −6. Ala→His adds nothing."},"exp":"At pH 7, Lys and Arg are **+1**, Glu and Asp are **−1**, and Ala and His are **≈ 0**. Each basic-to-acidic swap goes from +1 to −1, a change of **−2**; there are three of them. Ala→His changes nothing. Total: −2 − 2 − 2 + 0 = **−6**.","how":{"ask":"For each substitution, subtract the old side-chain charge from the new one at pH 7, then add the four changes.","know":"• Charges at pH 7: Arg/Lys **+1**, Asp/Glu **−1**, His **≈ 0**, others **0**.\n• Change at each site = **new − old**.\n• Net change = sum of the site changes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 lists the four swaps.","hl":"Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His"},{"text":"Compute new − old at each site.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc","glutamate","aspartate-sc","histidine-sc"],"calc":["Lys→Glu: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Arg→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Lys→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Ala→His: 0 − 0 = **0**"]},{"text":"Add them.","calc":["Δ = −2 + (−2) + (−2) + 0 = **−6**"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives −2 − 2 − 2 + 1 = −5, but at pH 7 His is ≈ 0.","strike":["It decreases by 5"]},{"text":"Seeing three charged residues swapped for three charged residues as no change ignores the sign: each swap trades +1 for −1, a change of −2.","strike":["It does not change"]},{"text":"Positive charges are replaced by negative ones, so the net charge falls; an increase has the sign flipped.","strike":["It increases by 6"]},{"text":"Answer: it decreases by 6 (Δ = −6)."}],"trap":"Counting His as +1 at pH 7: its side-chain pKa is about 6, so at pH 7 it is mostly neutral and Ala→His adds nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"four replacements","note":"four sites"},{"t":"pH 7","note":"charges at neutral pH"},{"t":"positively charged purification resin","note":"aim: a more negative protein"}]},{"text":"**Assign charges at pH 7.**","calc":["Lys, Arg: +1","Glu, Asp: −1","Ala, His: 0"]},{"text":"**Change per site = new − old.** A + → − swap is worth 2.","calc":["(−1) − (+1) = −2"]},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7, count Arg and Lys as +1, Asp and Glu as −1, and His and everything else as 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each substitution take new charge minus old charge at the stated pH, then add all the site changes."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe5-cp:31"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-31a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-36a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"data table/graph interpretation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:36#1","pe1-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:36"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"For the loop mutants, these results support the conclusion that:","choices":["only mutant A is more stable.","both mutants are more stable.","only mutant B is more stable.","neither mutant is more stable."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Less activity kept means less stable","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Higher retained % = more heat-stable. Compare to wild type's 35: A at 70 beats it, B at 15 falls short. So only A."},"exp":"Retained activity is the measure of heat stability: more activity left means less damage. Mutant A kept **70% vs the wild type's 35%**, so it is more stable. Mutant B kept **15%**, **less** than the wild type, so it is not more stable.","how":{"ask":"Line up each mutant's retained activity against the wild type's and decide which are higher.","know":"• **% activity kept after heating**: higher → more heat-stable.\n• Compare every variant with the **wild type**, the reference.\n• A conclusion about \"both\" fails if even one variant does not fit.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the reference and each mutant's retained activity after heating; unheated activity was equal.","hl":"wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%"},{"text":"Compare each with the wild type.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Activity kept after heating","x":{"label":"Enzyme"},"y":{"label":"Activity kept (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Wild type","Mutant A","Mutant B"],"series":[{"name":"Kept","values":[35,70,15]}]},"calc":["A: 70% > 35% → **more stable**","B: 15% < 35% → **not more stable**"]},{"text":"Mutant B kept less than the wild type, so it is neither more stable alone nor along with A.","strike":["both mutants are more stable.","only mutant B is more stable."]},{"text":"Mutant A clearly kept more, so it is wrong that neither is more stable.","strike":["neither mutant is more stable."]},{"text":"Answer: only mutant A is more stable."}],"trap":"Assuming every mutant behaves alike: check each one against the wild type separately."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"55 °C","note":"heating step"},{"t":"35%","note":"wild type, the reference"},{"t":"70%","note":"mutant A"},{"t":"15%","note":"mutant B"}]},{"text":"**Heat, cool, assay.** The activity that survives measures how well the enzyme resists heat.","calc":["% kept ↑ → heat stability ↑"]},{"text":"**Subtract the reference.**","calc":["A: 70 − 35 = +35","B: 15 − 35 = −20"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep only the claim the numbers show; reject absolute words like all, fully or every when any activity remains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare each variant's retained activity with the wild type's; higher means more heat-stable, lower means less."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-37a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"[H⁺]/[OH⁻] from pH (10^−pH, ΔpH factor)","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:7#2","pe6-cp:7#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:37","pe6-cp:7"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P7","passage":"A fungal lipase is being engineered for a laundry detergent. Incubated with triolein in an unbuffered emulsion, the enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact, and the pH of the emulsion falls as the reaction runs. So that the lipase binds a positively charged purification resin more tightly, engineers made a variant with four replacements: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His; it folds normally and keeps its activity. Two other mutants, A and B, carry changes in a flexible surface loop. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 35% of its activity, mutant A kept 70% and mutant B kept 15%; unheated samples of all three had equal activity. Finally, the enzymes are tested in a rinse with a pH of 5.8 and in a wash solution with a pH of 7.8, both read at the same temperature.","q":"How does [H⁺] in the wash solution compare with that in the rinse?","choices":["1/100 as much","100 times as much","1/2 as much","1/20 as much"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Higher pH means less H⁺","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each pH unit = 10× in [H+], and higher pH = less H+. 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units → 10² = 1/100."},"exp":"Because **pH = −log[H⁺]**, one pH unit corresponds to a **tenfold** change in [H⁺]. The wash is 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units higher than the rinse, so its [H⁺] is 10² = 100 times lower, **1/100 as much**: about 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M versus 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M.","how":{"ask":"Find the pH difference, turn it into a power of ten, and decide whether a higher pH means more or less H⁺.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units = a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺].\n• **Higher pH = less H⁺**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives both pH values; find the change from rinse to wash.","hl":"a pH of 5.8","calc":["ΔpH = 7.8 − 5.8 = **2** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"a pH of 7.8","calc":["factor = 10² = **100**","pH 5.8: [H⁺] = 10^−5.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M","pH 7.8: [H⁺] = 10^−7.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ = **100**"],"strike":["1/2 as much","1/20 as much"]},{"text":"Direction: the wash has the higher pH, so it has less H⁺.","strike":["100 times as much"]},{"text":"Answer: 1/100 as much."}],"trap":"\"1/2 as much\" treats pH as linear; every pH unit is a tenfold change, so 2 units is 10 × 10 = 100."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what you must compare.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 5.8","note":"sample A"},{"t":"a pH of 7.8","note":"sample B"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).","calc":["pH 5 → 10⁻⁵ M","pH 6 → 10⁻⁶ M (10 times less)"]},{"text":"**Subtract, then raise 10 to that power.** Then set the direction: higher pH, less H⁺.","calc":["factor = 10^(ΔpH)"]},{"text":"**Also:** compare any solution with pure water (pH 7.0 at 25 °C) the same way. Each pH unit above 7 divides [H⁺] by 10, and since [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ stays fixed, [OH⁻] grows by that same factor. A fractional gap works too: 10 raised to the gap.","calc":["pH 9.0 vs water: gap 2.0 → [H⁺] 100 times lower","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻⁹ = 10⁻⁵ M, 100 times water's 10⁻⁷ M","gap 0.5 → 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a change of Δ pH units changes [H⁺] by a factor of 10^Δ, and a lower pH means more H⁺."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-07a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-38b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"sugar carbon numbering","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:38#2","pe1-cp:38#1","pe1-cp:38#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:38"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"To which carbon of glucose does phosphorylase attach the phosphate?","choices":["C-1","C-4","C-5","C-6"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Pyranose C5 carries no –OH","c":[2]},{"f":"Mutase, not phosphorylase, makes 6-phosphate","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage names phosphorylase's product: glucose 1-phosphate. The number in the name is the carbon."},"exp":"Phosphorylase makes **glucose 1-phosphate**, so the phosphate sits on **C1, the anomeric carbon**, bonded to the ring O and to an O outside the ring. C6 is the familiar position, but it belongs to glucose 6-phosphate, the later product. In glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 bonds the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH.","how":{"ask":"Take the carbon number from the compound the passage names for this enzyme.","know":"• Glucopyranose numbering: C1 anomeric; C2–C4 in the ring; C5 bonded to the ring O; C6 = CH₂OH.\n• Glucose n-phosphate → phosphate on Cn.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 names the phosphorylase product.","hl":"the product is glucose 1-phosphate","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"C-6 belongs to the later product, glucose 6-phosphate.","strike":["C-6"]},{"text":"C-4 and C-5 carry no phosphate in either compound.","strike":["C-4","C-5"]},{"text":"Answer: C-1."}],"trap":"Defaulting to C6 because glucose 6-phosphate is the familiar one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glucose 1-phosphate","note":"the phosphorylase product"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"a later product"},{"t":"pyranose form","note":"six-membered ring"}]},{"text":"**Number the ring.**","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["C1: anomeric","C2–C4: ring","C5: bonded to ring O","C6: CH₂OH"]},{"text":"**Also:** in glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 is bonded to the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH."},{"text":"**Also:** always put the phosphate where the passage says, then number the ring from C1; do not default to C6."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** glucose n-phosphate has its phosphate on Cn; number from the anomeric C1 and use the compound the passage names, not the familiar C6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"glycosidic bonds (α-1,4 / α-1,6)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:39#2","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:43"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Compared with normal glycogen of the same mass, the patients' glycogen has fewer:","choices":["nonreducing ends.","α-1,4-glycosidic bonds.","β-1,4-glycosidic bonds.","reducing ends."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Glycogen has no β linkages","c":[2]},{"f":"Each glycogen has one reducing end","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count ends: a molecule with b branch points has b + 1 nonreducing ends and still one reducing end."},"exp":"Each branch hangs from an **α-1,6 bond** made by the branching enzyme, and each one starts a new chain with its own **nonreducing end**. With few branches the glycogen has **fewer α-1,6 bonds and fewer nonreducing ends**. It still has one reducing end per molecule, more α-1,4 links in its long chains, and no β-1,4 links at all.","how":{"ask":"Trace what each branch point adds, then take most of them away.","know":"• Branch points are α-1,6 bonds; chains are α-1,4.\n• Each branch adds one nonreducing end; a molecule has one reducing end.\n• β-1,4 links are in cellulose, not glycogen.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1: the branching enzyme is missing, so few α-1,6 branch points form.","hl":"very few branch points","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"Fewer branches → fewer chain tips → fewer nonreducing ends."},{"text":"α-1,4 links do not drop, the reducing end stays at one, and glycogen has no β-1,4 links.","strike":["α-1,4-glycosidic bonds.","reducing ends.","β-1,4-glycosidic bonds."]},{"text":"Answer: nonreducing ends."}],"trap":"Thinking every bond count drops: only the branch-linked features (α-1,6 bonds, nonreducing ends) fall."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"branching enzyme is deficient","note":"fewer α-1,6 links"},{"t":"very few branch points","note":"fewer chain tips"},{"t":"of the same mass","note":"compare like with like"}]},{"text":"**Count what one branch adds.**","calc":["1 branch = 1 α-1,6 bond + 1 nonreducing end","reducing ends: always 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase forms α-1,4 bonds; the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** branch points are α-1,6 bonds, so fewer branches means fewer α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each branch point is one α-1,6 bond and one extra nonreducing end; fewer branches means fewer of both, while the single reducing end stays."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39c","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"glycosidic bonds (α-1,4 / α-1,6)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:39#3","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:39"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"How is the linkage in isomaltose best classified?","choices":["α-1,4","α-1,6","β-1,4","β-1,6"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Glycogen has no β linkages","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Opposite faces → α. Linked to the CH₂OH carbon → 1,6. A branch-point bond, like glycogen's."},"exp":"On a Haworth drawing, **C1's bond on the face opposite the CH₂OH** marks an **α** link; the same face would be β. The bridging O joins the second glucose's **CH₂OH carbon, C6**, so the bond is **α-1,6**, the branch-point bond of glycogen and amylopectin. Glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 chain bonds.","how":{"ask":"Decide α or β from the faces, then read the partner carbon's number.","know":"• Haworth: C1–O bond and CH₂OH on the same face → β; opposite faces → α.\n• C6 is the CH₂OH carbon; C4 is a ring carbon.\n• Glycogen synthase makes α-1,4; the branching enzyme makes α-1,6.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4: CH₂OH above, C1 bond below: opposite faces, so α.","hl":"points below the ring","strike":["β-1,4","β-1,6"]},{"text":"The partner is the CH₂OH carbon, C6, not a ring C4.","hl":"exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","strike":["α-1,4"]},{"text":"Answer: α-1,6."}],"trap":"Reading \"below the ring\" as β: compare C1's bond with the CH₂OH, not with up or down alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂OH group above the ring","note":"reference face"},{"t":"points below the ring","note":"opposite face → α"},{"t":"exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","note":"C6"}]},{"text":"**Compare faces** on the glucose that donates C1.","fig":["alpha-glucopyranose","beta-glucopyranose"],"calc":["same face → β","opposite faces → α"]},{"text":"**Number the partner.** A ring C4–OH gives 1,4; the CH₂OH carbon gives 1,6."},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 bonds of each chain, and the branching enzyme makes the α-1,6 branch points."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Haworth drawing, C1's bond on the same face as CH₂OH is β and on the opposite face α; the partner carbon gives 1,4 or 1,6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-40a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide components","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:40#1","pe1-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:40"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Apart from galactose, which sugar ring is part of UDP-galactose?","choices":["Ribofuranose","Deoxyribofuranose","Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Glucose and fructose are hexoses","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Deoxyribose is C₅H₁₀O₄","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"U in UDP is uridine, an RNA nucleoside, so its sugar is ribose (5-ring, furanose). DNA ones are deoxy, and dUDP isn't the carrier."},"exp":"UDP is **uridine diphosphate**. Uridine is uracil bonded to **ribose**, a five-membered (furanose) ring with –OH groups on C2′ and C3′. UDP is an RNA-type nucleotide, so its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose; glucose and fructose are not part of it.","how":{"ask":"What does UDP stand for, and which sugar is in that nucleoside?","know":"• **UDP** = uridine diphosphate; **uridine** = uracil + **ribose**.\n• Nucleotides without a \"d\" (ATP, UTP, UDP, CTP) carry **ribose**.\n• Deoxyribose appears in DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5: the molecule is galactose attached to UDP.","hl":"UDP-galactose"},{"text":"Expand the name.","fig":["uracil","ribose"],"calc":["UDP = uridine diphosphate","uridine = uracil + **ribose** (furanose)"]},{"text":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose.","strike":["Deoxyribofuranose"]},{"text":"Glucose and fructose are not part of a uridine nucleotide.","strike":["Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"]},{"text":"Answer: ribofuranose."}],"trap":"Picking glucose because UDP-sugars are usually UDP-glucose: the attached sugar varies, but the nucleotide's own sugar is always ribose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"UDP-galactose","note":"uridine diphosphate + galactose"},{"t":"C₅H₁₀O₅","note":"a pentose with all its –OH groups"},{"t":"Apart from galactose","note":"find the other sugar"}]},{"text":"**Read UDP.** Uridine diphosphate: uracil + ribose + two phosphates.","fig":"ribose","calc":["U = uridine","D = di (2 phosphates)","P = phosphate"]},{"text":"**Ribose vs deoxyribose.** Ribose has –OH on C2′ and C3′; deoxyribose lacks the 2′ –OH.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Also:** deoxyribose is found in DNA nucleotides such as dTTP; nucleotides without the \"d\" carry ribose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UDP is uridine diphosphate, and uridine contains ribose."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"phosphate label tracking (kinase/phosphatase)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:41"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P8","passage":"In Andersen disease the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient, so liver glycogen has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points; normal glycogen branches every few residues along each chain. Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from chain ends by phosphorolysis: the product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis, both mainly in the pyranose form. Digesting the branch points of normal glycogen releases isomaltose, a disaccharide of two glucose units. In its Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring and bridges to that sugar's exocyclic –CH₂– carbon. Dietary galactose reaches glycogen through UDP-galactose, which an epimerase converts to UDP-glucose; complete hydrolysis of UDP-galactose gives galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅. To trace the pathway, the glucose units are tagged with ³H, and phosphorylase itself carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines. Both ³H and ¹³¹I are β⁻ emitters: in each decay an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Labeled phosphorylase is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Compared with an untreated control, the radioactive signal in the small-molecule fraction after the phosphatase step shows that:","choices":["³²P increases and ¹³¹I is unchanged.","¹³¹I increases and ³²P is unchanged.","both ³²P and ¹³¹I increase.","neither ³²P nor ¹³¹I changes."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Phosphatases remove phosphate groups","c":[3]},{"f":"Phosphatases leave tyrosine rings intact","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphatases remove only phosphate groups. The ³²P leaves as free Pᵢ and runs with the small molecules; the ¹³¹I stays on the tyrosines."},"exp":"A phosphatase **hydrolyzes phosphate esters**, releasing the phosphate as free Pᵢ. The ³²P rides on that phosphate, so it leaves the enzyme and is recovered with the small molecules: **that ³²P signal goes up**. The ¹³¹I is bonded to the tyrosine rings, which the phosphatase does not touch, so it stays on the protein and the small-molecule ¹³¹I signal is **unchanged**.","how":{"ask":"Place each label on its chemical group, then ask which group the phosphatase frees into the small-molecule fraction.","know":"• **Phosphatase**: R–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → R–OH + Pᵢ (removes phosphate).\n• A radioactive label goes wherever its atom goes.\n• Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase takes it off.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 places the two labels on phosphorylase.","hl":"³²P in its phosphoserines and ¹³¹I on its tyrosines","calc":["³²P → phosphate groups","¹³¹I → tyrosine rings"]},{"text":"Sentence 8: the phosphatase cuts off the phosphate, and the gel then separates the enzyme from small molecules.","hl":"separated from small molecules on a gel","calc":["Ser–O–PO₃²⁻(³²P) + H₂O → Ser–OH + Pᵢ(³²P)","³²P **moves to the small molecules**; ¹³¹I **stays on the protein**"]},{"text":"Freed Pᵢ carries ³²P into the small-molecule fraction, so the signal there cannot stay the same.","strike":["neither ³²P nor ¹³¹I changes."]},{"text":"The tyrosines are untouched, so no ¹³¹I is released.","strike":["¹³¹I increases and ³²P is unchanged.","both ³²P and ¹³¹I increase."]},{"text":"Answer: ³²P increases and ¹³¹I is unchanged."}],"trap":"Reading the signal on the enzyme instead of the small-molecule fraction: the ³²P that leaves the protein shows up as a gain in the small molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"³²P in its phosphoserines","note":"label on phosphate"},{"t":"¹³¹I on its tyrosines","note":"label on the protein"},{"t":"protein phosphatase","note":"removes phosphate"},{"t":"separated from small molecules on a gel","note":"free Pᵢ goes here"}]},{"text":"**Each isotope tags one part.** Phosphorus is only in the phosphate; the iodine sits on the tyrosine ring, drawn here.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Phosphatase = phosphate off.** The phosphate leaves as free Pᵢ, a small molecule.","calc":["phospho-protein + H₂O → protein + Pᵢ"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase removes only phosphate, so a ¹⁴C or ³H label in a sugar backbone stays with the sugar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphatase removes phosphate groups, so a ³²P label on them moves from the protein to the free Pᵢ while labels elsewhere on the protein stay put."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-44c","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"polyprotic Ka order","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:44#3","pe1-cp:44#1","pe1-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:44"],"q":"Arsenic acid, H₃AsO₄, is triprotic. Which of these acids dissociates to the smallest extent when dissolved in water?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.050 M H₃AsO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":45,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1.8],[3,2],[7.5,2.4],[12,2.9],[14,3.4],[15,4.6],[16,5.9],[18,6.4],[22.5,7],[27,7.6],[29,8.1],[30,9.2],[31,10.3],[33,10.8],[37.5,11.3],[42,11.7],[45,11.8]]}]},"choices":["HAsO₄²⁻","H₂AsO₄⁻","H₃AsO₄","HCl"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"HCl is a strong acid","c":[3]},{"f":"Each successive Ka is smaller","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each H+ pulled off a more negative ion is harder. The 2− ion (HAsO4²⁻) holds its last H+ hardest, so it dissociates least; HCl is strong."},"exp":"HCl is a strong acid. Arsenic acid's three Ka values fall steeply (pKa ≈ 2.2, 7.0, 11.5) because each H⁺ comes off a more negative ion. **HAsO₄²⁻** must lose H⁺ from a 2− ion, so it has the smallest Ka and dissociates least, just as HPO₄²⁻ does in the phosphate series.","how":{"ask":"Rank the four by Ka: the strong acid first, then each successive arsenic-acid step.","know":"• A strong acid (HCl) ionizes completely.\n• Triprotic acid: H₃A → H₂A⁻ → HA²⁻ → A³⁻, with Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃.\n• The most negative species that still has an H⁺ is the weakest acid.","steps":[{"text":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely.","strike":["HCl"]},{"text":"Write the three arsenic-acid steps.","hl":"is triprotic","calc":["H₃AsO₄ ⇌ H⁺ + H₂AsO₄⁻   pKa₁ ≈ 2.2","H₂AsO₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + HAsO₄²⁻   pKa₂ ≈ 7.0","HAsO₄²⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + AsO₄³⁻   pKa₃ ≈ 11.5"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"buffer 1","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":22.5,"text":"pH 7.0 = pKa₂","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":37.5,"text":"buffer 3","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Each step is about 10⁴–10⁵ times weaker than the one before, because H⁺ leaves an ever more negative ion.","calc":["Ka₁ ≈ 6 × 10⁻³ > Ka₂ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁷ > Ka₃ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹²"],"strike":["H₃AsO₄","H₂AsO₄⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HAsO₄²⁻."}],"trap":"H₃AsO₄ is the weak acid named in the stem, but it is the strongest of the three arsenic species; its ions are weaker still."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"triprotic","note":"three H⁺ to lose"},{"t":"smallest extent","note":"weakest acid, smallest Ka"}]},{"text":"**Strong acids are out:** HCl ionizes completely, so it dissociates most."},{"text":"**Line up the arsenic species by charge:** neutral H₃AsO₄, then 1− H₂AsO₄⁻, then 2− HAsO₄²⁻. The more negative the ion, the more tightly it holds H⁺.","calc":["0 → 1− → 2−","Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** HCl and H₂SO₄ (first H⁺) are strong acids; HF, H₃PO₄ and H₂CO₃ are weak."},{"text":"**Also:** each successive Ka of a polyprotic acid is much smaller, because H⁺ is harder to remove from a more negative ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a polyprotic series, the most negative ion that still carries an H⁺ (HPO₄²⁻, HAsO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻) is the weakest acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-45a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"thin lens equation","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:45#1","pe1-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:45"],"q":"An object sits 15 cm from a converging lens (f = 12 cm). What is the ratio of the image height to the object height, and is the image upright or inverted?","choices":["4:1, inverted","4:1, upright","1:4, upright","4:5, inverted"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Object beyond f: inverted image","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Objects inside 2f give enlarged images","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so i = 60 cm. m = −60/15 = −4: four times taller, inverted."},"exp":"1/i = 1/f − 1/o = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so **i = 60 cm**, a real image. **m = −i/o = −60/15 = −4**: the image is **4.0 times** as tall as the object, and the minus sign marks it as **inverted**. An object between f and 2f always gives an enlarged, inverted real image.","how":{"ask":"Find i from the thin-lens equation, then m = −i/o.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• m = −i/o = image height ÷ object height.\n• m < 0 → inverted (real); m > 0 → upright (virtual).","steps":[{"text":"Solve for the image distance.","hl":"sits 15 cm from","calc":["1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60","i = 60 cm"]},{"text":"Magnification.","calc":["m = −60/15 = −4.0"]},{"text":"Upright ignores the sign; 0.25 is o/i; 0.80 is f/o.","strike":["4:1, upright","1:4, upright","4:5, inverted"]},{"text":"Answer: 4:1, inverted."}],"trap":"Dropping the minus sign: a real image from a single converging lens is always inverted."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"f = 12 cm","note":"focal length"},{"t":"sits 15 cm from","note":"o, between f and 2f"}]},{"text":"**Image distance first**, then the ratio.","calc":["i = 60 cm","m = −i/o = −4.0"]},{"text":"**Also:** solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| scales the height and a negative m means inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** for an object beyond 2f, find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i and use m = −i/o: the real image is inverted and smaller, shrinking as the object moves away."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i, then m = −i/o: |m| is the height ratio and a negative m means an inverted image."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-46a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"radioactive decay A/Z changes","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:46"],"q":"Phosphorus-32 decays to sulfur-32, raising Z by one at constant A, and its radiation bends toward a positive plate. The particle emitted is:","choices":["An electron","A photon","A positron","An alpha particle"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Photons carry no charge","c":[1]},{"f":"Positron and alpha emission lower Z","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Z up by one with A fixed means a neutron became a proton and threw out a negative charge; bending toward + confirms it."},"exp":"Same A and **Z up by one** means a neutron turned into a proton, and the nucleus emitted an **electron (β⁻)** plus an antineutrino. Its negative charge explains the bend **toward the positive plate**. A photon (γ) has no charge and changes neither A nor Z; a positron lowers Z; an alpha particle lowers A by 4.","how":{"ask":"Use the change in A and Z, then the charge shown by the deflection.","know":"• α: A − 4, Z − 2 (charge +2).\n• β⁻: A same, Z + 1 (an electron, −1).\n• β⁺: A same, Z − 1 (a positron, +1).\n• γ: no change in A or Z (a photon, no charge).","steps":[{"text":"Same mass number, atomic number one higher.","hl":"raising Z by one at constant A","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino"]},{"text":"A positron lowers Z; an alpha lowers A; a photon changes nothing and is not deflected.","strike":["A positron","An alpha particle","A photon"]},{"text":"Bending toward + means a negative charge. Answer: an electron."}],"trap":"Picking γ whenever A is unchanged: γ leaves Z unchanged too, and it is not deflected."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"decays to sulfur-32","note":"A same, Z + 1"},{"t":"raising Z by one","note":"n → p"},{"t":"bends toward a positive plate","note":"negative charge"}]},{"text":"**Match the change to the decay.**","calc":["A same, Z + 1 → β⁻","A same, Z − 1 → β⁺","A − 4, Z − 2 → α","no change → γ"]},{"text":"**Also:** gamma emission is the one decay that changes neither A nor Z: an excited nucleus drops to a lower state and releases a photon, which has no mass and no charge (X* → X + γ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify an emission from the change in A and Z and from its charge: β⁻ raises Z with A fixed, β⁺ lowers Z, α lowers A by 4, and γ changes neither."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"net reaction from mechanism steps","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:48#1","pe1-cp:48#2","pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3","pe1-cp:48#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:48"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"Based on the passage, what is the overall reaction of the iron-catalyzed oxidation?","choices":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂","S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","S₂O₈²⁻ + I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Catalysts and intermediates cancel out overall","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Balanced equations conserve every atom","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Add the steps and strike what shows on both sides: Fe²⁺ (catalyst) and Fe³⁺ (intermediate) cancel, leaving only persulfate, iodide and their products."},"exp":"Add the two steps and cross out anything on both sides: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ becomes **S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂**. Fe²⁺ is used and then regenerated (a catalyst), and Fe³⁺ is made and then used (an intermediate), so neither appears overall. Leaving Fe²⁺ on both sides is an unfinished sum, the second step alone keeps an intermediate, and a single I⁻ cannot balance I₂.","how":{"ask":"Add the steps, then cancel each species that appears on both sides.","know":"• **Overall reaction** = sum of the elementary steps (multiply a step if needed so shared species cancel).\n• Cancel species found on both sides: **catalysts** (used, then regenerated; a regenerated cofactor such as enzyme-bound FAD → FADH₂ → FAD cancels like a catalyst) and **intermediates** (made, then used).\n• What's left holds only the true reactants and products.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives both steps; write them and add them.","hl":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","calc":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","sum: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"Cancel 2Fe²⁺ and 2Fe³⁺, each found on both sides.","fig":"cpt-fe-cycle","calc":["cancel Fe²⁺ (catalyst)","cancel Fe³⁺ (intermediate)","**S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂**","check: I 2 = 2; charge −2 + (−2) = −4 = 2 × (−2) ✓"]},{"text":"Leaving Fe²⁺ on both sides is a sum that was never simplified.","strike":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"The second step alone keeps Fe³⁺, an intermediate, and drops persulfate.","strike":["2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂"]},{"text":"One I⁻ cannot make I₂: atoms and charge fail to balance.","strike":["S₂O₈²⁻ + I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"]},{"text":"Answer: persulfate plus two iodide ions gives two sulfate ions and I₂."}],"trap":"The sum with Fe²⁺ on both sides is balanced and looks complete, but a species on both sides must be cancelled; an overall equation never shows a catalyst."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two steps","note":"a mechanism"},{"t":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","note":"step one"},{"t":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","note":"step two"},{"t":"overall reaction","note":"sum and cancel"}]},{"text":"**A mechanism** is the list of elementary steps a reaction actually goes through. Adding them gives the overall equation."},{"text":"**Cancel like terms**, just as in algebra: a species on both sides drops out.","calc":["A + B → C","C + D → A + E","sum: B + D → E"]},{"text":"**Also:** after summing a mechanism, confirm that atoms and total charge (coefficient × charge) balance on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** add the steps, then cancel every species on both sides (catalysts and intermediates) to get the overall reaction."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-48c"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-49b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"catalyst phase and regeneration","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:49#1","pe1-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:49"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"In the sucrose hydrolysis, which best describes H⁺?","choices":["Homogeneous catalyst that is regenerated","Heterogeneous catalyst that is regenerated","Homogeneous catalyst that is used up","Heterogeneous catalyst that is used up"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Catalysts are regenerated, not consumed","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Dissolved catalysts are homogeneous","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Dissolved catalyst in a dissolved reaction → same phase → homogeneous. Same pH at the end → not consumed."},"exp":"H⁺ is **dissolved in the same aqueous phase** as the sucrose, so it is a **homogeneous** catalyst. It speeds the reaction and is **regenerated**: the pH, and so the amount of H⁺, is unchanged at the end. A solid catalyst acting on gases, like the platinum in a catalytic converter, would be heterogeneous.","how":{"ask":"Compare the catalyst's phase with the reactants', then check whether it is consumed.","know":"• Homogeneous: same phase as the reactants; heterogeneous: a different phase.\n• A catalyst is regenerated, not consumed.\n• A catalytic converter is a heterogeneous solid catalyst: it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O and reduces NOₓ to N₂.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says H⁺ mixes evenly through the solution: the same phase as the sucrose.","hl":"mix evenly through the solution","strike":["Heterogeneous catalyst that is regenerated","Heterogeneous catalyst that is used up"]},{"text":"The pH is unchanged at the end, so the H⁺ is not consumed.","hl":"the pH is the same","strike":["Homogeneous catalyst that is used up"]},{"text":"Answer: Homogeneous catalyst that is regenerated."}],"trap":"Calling every metal catalyst heterogeneous: dissolved ions share the reactants' phase."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"mix evenly through the solution","note":"one phase"},{"t":"the pH is the same","note":"H⁺ not used up"}]},{"text":"**Two questions classify any catalyst.**","calc":["same phase as reactants? → homogeneous","different phase? → heterogeneous","consumed? → never"]},{"text":"**Also:** a solid catalyst acting on gases (a catalytic converter) is a heterogeneous catalyst."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalytic converter reduces nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) to N₂ while it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst in the reactants' phase is homogeneous and one in a different phase is heterogeneous; either way it is regenerated, not used up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-51a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"surface area raises rate","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:51#1","pe1-cp:51#2","pe1-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:51"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P9","passage":"A chemistry class compared catalysts in three experiments. Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature; a little hydrochloric acid makes it fast, the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution, and when the reaction is over the pH is the same as just after the acid was added. A solid acid resin also speeds the hydrolysis: one cube of resin 1.5 cm on each side was compared with the same mass cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side, and the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. In the second experiment, a little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) sped up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate in two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stayed very small, while [I₂] rose steadily until the persulfate was used up. In the third, an alkene in ethanol was hydrogenated over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. The mixture was then passed through a filter, and the recovered catalyst was reused in the next batch.","q":"If the small resin cubes are fused back into a single cube of the same mass, how does the rate of hydrolysis change, compared with the cut pieces?","choices":["It increases, because more resin surface is exposed.","It decreases, because less resin surface is exposed.","It stays the same, because the resin mass is unchanged.","It decreases, because less resin mass is present."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Rate tracks exposed surface area","c":[2]},{"f":"Fusing keeps the resin's mass","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Rate ∝ exposed area. One 1.5-cm cube has 30 times less surface than the 0.050-cm pieces (the edge ratio), so the hydrolysis slows."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the rate rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area. The cut pieces expose 27,000 × 6(0.050)² = 405 cm²; one 1.5-cm cube of the same mass exposes only 6(1.5)² = 13.5 cm², 30 times less. So fusing them **decreases the rate**, because **less resin surface is exposed**; the mass is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Compare the exposed surface area before and after, at the same mass.","know":"• Rate ∝ exposed surface area.\n• Cutting a cube of edge L into cubes of edge l: N = (L/l)³, and the total area grows by L/l.\n• The mass of catalyst does not change.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 ties the rate to the exposed area, not to the mass.","hl":"rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area"},{"text":"Compare the areas.","calc":["cut pieces: 27,000 × 6 × 0.050² = 405 cm²","one cube: 6 × 1.5² = 13.5 cm²","405 ÷ 13.5 = 30 → area falls 30-fold"]},{"text":"Less area means a slower rate, so the rate cannot rise or stay the same.","strike":["It increases, because more resin surface is exposed.","It stays the same, because the resin mass is unchanged."]},{"text":"Fusing keeps every gram of resin, so mass is not the reason.","strike":["It decreases, because less resin mass is present."]},{"text":"Answer: it decreases, because less resin surface is exposed."}],"trap":"Thinking the same mass of catalyst must give the same rate: for a solid, only the exposed surface counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 cm on each side","note":"L"},{"t":"0.050 cm on each side","note":"l"},{"t":"rose in step with the resin's exposed surface area","note":"rate ∝ area"}]},{"text":"**Area factor = edge ratio** at fixed mass, in either direction.","calc":["N × 6l² ÷ 6L² = (L/l)³ × (l/L)² = L/l","fusing: area ÷ 30"]},{"text":"**Also:** grinding or cutting a catalyst raises its surface area but leaves its mass unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** cutting a cube into smaller cubes multiplies the total surface area by the ratio of the edge lengths."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at fixed mass, a solid catalyst's rate follows its exposed surface: cutting it up speeds the reaction, and fusing the pieces back slows it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Ohm's law V = IR","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:52#1","pe1-cp:52#2","pe1-cp:52#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:52"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"Which replacement coil would draw the largest current from the 9.0-V battery?","choices":["A 12-Ω coil","A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Fixed voltage: higher R, less current","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"I ∝ 1/R at fixed voltage, so the smallest resistance draws the most: 12 Ω. No division needed."},"exp":"By **Ohm's law, I = V/R**, a fixed battery voltage drives more current through a smaller resistance. The 12-Ω coil draws 9.0 V ÷ 12 Ω = **0.75 A**, more than the 0.30 A now.","how":{"ask":"With the battery voltage fixed, which resistance gives the biggest I = V/R?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**.\n• Fixed V → current is **inversely proportional** to resistance: lower R, more current.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the battery voltage stays constant; only R changes.","hl":"the battery's voltage stays constant","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Current now.","hl":"30-Ω coil","calc":["I = 9.0 V ÷ 30 Ω = **0.30 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the replacements.","calc":["12 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 12 = **0.75 A**","45 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 45 = 0.20 A","60 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 60 = 0.15 A","90 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 90 = 0.10 A"]},{"text":"The larger coils all lower the current below 0.30 A.","strike":["A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"]},{"text":"Answer: the 12-Ω coil."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger resistor \"uses more\" current. At fixed voltage, more resistance means less current."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"9.0-V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"30-Ω coil","note":"present R"},{"t":"largest current","note":"smallest R"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** the battery's push divided by the resistance gives the current.","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**Smaller R, bigger I.** Halve R and the current doubles.","calc":["R ↓ → I ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** a longer, thinner or hotter metal wire has more resistance; shorter, thicker or cooler has less."},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the ion concentrations a cell runs on lowers its voltage, and at fixed R that lowers the current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise the current from a fixed voltage, lower the resistance (I = V/R)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-54a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"balance an equation (atoms & charge)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:54#1","pe1-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:54"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"What is the balanced net ionic equation for the reaction in the silver nitrate solution?","choices":["Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag","Mg + Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Ag","Mg²⁺ + 2Ag → Mg + 2Ag⁺","2Mg + Ag⁺ → 2Mg²⁺ + Ag"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Net ionic equations balance charge","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Mg²⁺ cannot oxidize silver","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mg gives 2e⁻ and each Ag⁺ takes 1e⁻, so two Ag⁺ per Mg. Charge check: +2 on both sides."},"exp":"Magnesium has the **lower E°**, so it is **oxidized**: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻. Silver ions are reduced: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, which is the silver seen growing. Balance the electrons with **two Ag⁺ per Mg**: **Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag**, with a charge of +2 on each side.","how":{"ask":"Pick the oxidized metal from E°, then balance electrons and charge.","know":"• Lower E° → oxidized; higher E° → reduced.\n• Electrons lost = electrons gained.\n• Check that atoms and total charge balance.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2: magnesium has the lower E°, so magnesium metal is oxidized and silver ions are reduced.","hl":"Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V","strike":["Mg²⁺ + 2Ag → Mg + 2Ag⁺"]},{"text":"Balance electrons: Mg gives 2, each Ag⁺ takes 1.","calc":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","2Ag⁺ + 2e⁻ → 2Ag"]},{"text":"The one-to-one and two-to-one versions leave the charge unbalanced.","strike":["Mg + Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Ag","2Mg + Ag⁺ → 2Mg²⁺ + Ag"]},{"text":"Answer: Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag."}],"trap":"Balancing atoms but not charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"gray-black crystals of silver","note":"Ag⁺ reduced"},{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"Mg: lower, oxidized"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: higher, reduced"}]},{"text":"**Balance electrons, then charge.**","calc":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","2 × (Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag)","sum: Mg + 2Ag⁺ → Mg²⁺ + 2Ag"]},{"text":"**Also:** balance a redox equation by multiplying the half-reactions so the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, then check the charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** oxidize the metal with the lower E°, scale the half-reactions until electrons lost equal electrons gained, and check that charge balances."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-55a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"E°cell from reduction potentials","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-cp:55#1","pe1-cp:55#2","pe1-cp:55#3","pe6-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:55","pe1-cp:53","pe6-cp:41"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"What is the standard potential of the student's cell, and does its reaction run spontaneously?","choices":["+1.24 V, spontaneous","−0.36 V, not spontaneous","+0.36 V, spontaneous","+1.24 V, not spontaneous"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Running galvanic cells are spontaneous","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Silver gains mass, so it is the cathode. 0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V, and positive means spontaneous."},"exp":"Silver gains mass, so Ag⁺ is reduced there: silver is the **cathode**, with the higher E°. Subtract the anode's value from the cathode's: **0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V**. A **positive** E°cell means ΔG° = −nFE° < 0, so the reaction is **spontaneous**, as a working galvanic cell must be.","how":{"ask":"Identify the cathode and anode, subtract, then read the sign.","know":"• E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.\n• The cathode has the higher reduction potential (and gains mass when metal plates out).\n• E°cell > 0 → spontaneous.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says silver gains mass: it is the cathode, and iron is the anode.","hl":"the silver electrode gains mass"},{"text":"Subtract, anode from cathode.","calc":["E°cell = +0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V"],"strike":["−0.36 V, not spontaneous","+0.36 V, spontaneous"]},{"text":"Positive means spontaneous.","strike":["+1.24 V, not spontaneous"]},{"text":"Answer: +1.24 V, spontaneous."}],"trap":"Adding the potentials as written, or swapping cathode and anode."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"gains mass","note":"cathode"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag, higher"},{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"Fe, lower"}]},{"text":"**Cathode minus anode, then the sign.**","calc":["0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V","+ → spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a galvanic cell, the cathode is the half-cell with the higher standard reduction potential."},{"text":"**Also:** a positive E°cell means spontaneous as written; a negative E°cell means not spontaneous (the reverse reaction is)."},{"text":"**Also:** when E°cell and one electrode are known, solve the same formula for the other: E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell. A strongly negative result marks a metal that gives up electrons easily.","calc":["this cell backward: +0.80 − (+1.24) = −0.44 V (iron ✓)","Ag cathode, E°cell = +3.17 V: E°anode = 0.80 − 3.17 = −2.37 V (magnesium)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with the higher reduction potential as the cathode; a positive E°cell means spontaneous."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:53"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-53a","cpt-pe6-41a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Ohm's law V = IR","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:56"],"pid":"pe1-cp:P10","passage":"For a science fair, a student builds a galvanic cell and uses batteries to run small heaters. Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. To choose metals, she places strips in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration: magnesium fizzes vigorously, and silver is tested next. A magnesium strip placed in colorless silver nitrate solution grows gray-black crystals of silver, and the nitrate ions take no part. Her cell joins an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate to a silver electrode in silver nitrate through a salt bridge; as it runs, the silver electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. One heater is a 30-Ω coil run from a 9.0-V battery through short copper leads, with spare coils of other resistances on hand; the battery's voltage stays constant. Another is a thin strip with a resistance of 250 mΩ, connected across a 4.5-V supply; its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"About how much current does the thin heater strip draw?","choices":["18 A","18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Under 1 Ω, amps exceed volts","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"250 mΩ is a quarter ohm; dividing by 1/4 is multiplying by 4: 4.5 × 4 = 18 A. R under 1 Ω means I bigger than the volt number, so not mA."},"exp":"Convert first: **250 mΩ = 0.250 Ω**. Then **Ohm's law, I = V/R** = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω = **18 A**. A resistance under 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number.","how":{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide the voltage by it.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (A = V ÷ Ω).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mΩ = 0.001 Ω. Capital **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives R in milliohms; convert to ohms.","hl":"a resistance of 250 mΩ","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.250 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"4.5-V supply","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω","I = **18 A**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 4.5-V supply versus resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"I = V ÷ R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,18],[0.5,9],[0.75,6],[1,4.5],[1.5,3],[2,2.25],[3,1.5]]}]}},{"text":"18 mA divides by 250 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 56 A divides 250 by 4.5, which is R ÷ V and unconverted. 0.056 A is R ÷ V in ohms, the ratio upside down.","strike":["18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 A."}],"trap":"Treating 250 mΩ as 250 Ω gives 18 mA, a thousand times too small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"250 mΩ","note":"0.250 Ω"},{"t":"4.5-V supply","note":"V"},{"t":"how much current","note":"I = V/R"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before dividing.","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 ÷ 1000 Ω = 0.250 Ω"]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** current = voltage ÷ resistance.","calc":["I = V ÷ R = 4.5 ÷ 0.250 = 18 A"]},{"text":"**Also:** at constant resistance, V = IR makes voltage directly proportional to current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** I = V/R with R in ohms; convert mΩ by dividing by 1000 first, and expect more amps than volts when R is under 1 Ω."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-57a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"common-ion effect","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:57#1","pe1-cp:57#2","pe1-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:57"],"q":"Solid potassium iodide is dissolved in a saturated lead(II) iodide solution standing over undissolved PbI₂. The result is best described as:","choices":["solid PbI₂ forms, since Q exceeds Ksp.","solid PbI₂ forms, since Ksp drops.","more PbI₂ dissolves, since Ksp rises.","no shift occurs, since Ksp is unchanged."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ksp changes only with temperature","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Common ions shift toward precipitate","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common ion added → Q goes up → Q > Ksp → shift left → solid forms. Ksp never moves at constant temperature."},"exp":"Dissolving KI adds **I⁻, a common ion**. The ion product **Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises above Ksp**, so PbI₂(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻ **shifts left** and PbI₂ precipitates until Q = Ksp again. Ksp itself is unchanged at constant temperature.","how":{"ask":"Compare Q with Ksp after the common ion is added.","know":"• Ksp is fixed at a given T.\n• Q > Ksp → precipitation; Q < Ksp → more dissolves.\n• A common ion raises Q (Le Châtelier: adding a product shifts the equilibrium left).","steps":[{"text":"KI adds iodide, a common ion.","hl":"potassium iodide","calc":["Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises"]},{"text":"Q > Ksp, so the shift is toward the solid.","strike":["more PbI₂ dissolves, since Ksp rises.","no shift occurs, since Ksp is unchanged."]},{"text":"Ksp does not change; only Q does.","strike":["solid PbI₂ forms, since Ksp drops."]},{"text":"Answer: solid PbI₂ forms, since Q exceeds Ksp."}],"trap":"Saying Ksp drops: the constant stays put; Q is what moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"potassium iodide","note":"common ion I⁻"},{"t":"saturated lead(II) iodide solution","note":"Q = Ksp at the start"},{"t":"undissolved PbI₂","note":"solid present"}]},{"text":"**Compare Q with Ksp.**","calc":["add I⁻ → Q > Ksp → PbI₂(s) forms"]},{"text":"**Also (Le Châtelier):** adding a product, here the common ion, shifts the equilibrium left, so the sparingly soluble salt precipitates."},{"text":"**Also:** adding a common ion lowers a salt's solubility, but Ksp itself doesn't change unless temperature does."},{"text":"**Also:** a pure solid is left out of K and Q (its activity is 1), so adding or removing one (more undissolved AgCl, an ion-exchange resin, a precipitate filtered off) doesn't shift the equilibrium. Only changes in dissolved concentrations or gas pressures do.","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻: Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻], no AgCl term","more AgCl(s) → Q unchanged → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a common ion raises Q above Ksp, so the salt precipitates; Ksp itself changes only with temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-58a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-cp:58#1","pe3-cp:21#3","pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:24#1","pe6-cp:35#2","pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:58"],"q":"A recipe needs 2.22 g of calcium chloride (molar mass 111 g/mol), measured out from a 0.400 M CaCl₂ stock. What volume of stock is required?","choices":["50.0 mL","8.0 mL","20.0 mL","5.55 L"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"2.22 ÷ 111 = 0.02 mol (111 × 2 = 222). Then 0.02 ÷ 0.4 = 0.05 L = 50 mL."},"exp":"First grams to moles: 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol = **0.0200 mol**. Then **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L = 0.0500 L = **50.0 mL**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, then divide moles by molarity to get litres, then convert to mL.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass**.\n• **M = n ÷ V**, so **V = n ÷ M** (V in litres).\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Note the mass, molar mass and molarity.","hl":"2.22 g of calcium chloride","calc":["m = 2.22 g","molar mass = 111 g/mol","M = 0.400 mol/L"]},{"text":"Grams to moles.","calc":["n = 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol","n = 0.0200 mol"]},{"text":"Volume from V = n ÷ M, then mL.","calc":["V = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L","V = 0.0500 L","V = 0.0500 × 1000 = **50.0 mL**"]},{"text":"Multiplying n × M instead of dividing gives 8.0 mL, and stopping at the moles (0.0200 read as litres) gives 20.0 mL.","calc":["0.0200 × 0.400 = 0.0080 L = 8.0 mL ✗","0.0200 → 20.0 mL skips ÷ M ✗"],"strike":["8.0 mL","20.0 mL"]},{"text":"Skipping the grams-to-moles step divides grams by molarity.","calc":["2.22 ÷ 0.400 = 5.55 ✗"],"strike":["5.55 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 50.0 mL."}],"trap":"Dividing grams by molarity: molarity is moles per litre, so the mass must become moles first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"2.22 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"111 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"0.400 M","note":"mol per L"}]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.**","calc":["n = mass ÷ molar mass"]},{"text":"**Moles → litres → mL.** Rearrange M = n/V.","calc":["V = n ÷ M","mL = L × 1000"]},{"text":"**Also:** going the other way, mass = M × V(L) × molar mass. Turn both prefixes into base units before multiplying: μM × 10⁻⁶ = M and mL × 10⁻³ = L.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M; 750 mL = 0.750 L","n = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L × 0.750 L = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","mass = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 342 g/mol ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² g ≈ 10 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** when only the moles are wanted, n = M × V(L). A millimolar value must become molar first (mM ÷ 1000), and mL ÷ 1000 gives litres.","calc":["600 mM = 0.600 M; 400 mL = 0.400 L","n = 0.600 mol/L × 0.400 L = 0.240 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** to make a solution from the solid, chain the units forward: mass = M × V × molar mass, with V in litres. Change mL to L first, or the mass comes out 1000 times too big.","calc":["0.050 M CaCl₂ in 300 mL = 0.300 L","mass = 0.050 mol/L × 0.300 L × 111 g/mol ≈ 1.7 g"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a concentration given in g/L, mass = concentration (g/L) × volume (L), and no molar mass enters. A microlitre is 10⁻⁶ L (a millilitre is 10⁻³ L); mixing them up makes the mass 1000 times too big.","calc":["40 g/L protein; drop = 8.0 μL = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L","mass = 40 g/L × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L = 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ g = 0.32 mg","μL read as mL: 40 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.32 g ✗ (1000× too big)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n ÷ M gives litres; convert grams to moles first and litres to mL at the end; mass = M × V(L) × molar mass, or (g/L) × V(L) with μL = 10⁻⁶ L."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-59c","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"dimensional analysis of units","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe1-cp:59#3","pe1-cp:59#1","pe1-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:59"],"q":"An engineer reports a lab centrifuge motor's output using different unit expressions. Which of these is NOT equivalent to the watt?","choices":["V·A","Pa·m³·s⁻¹","kg·m·s⁻²","A²·Ω"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Power is IV, also I²R","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Power always carries m² (J = N·m). An expression whose base units show only one metre is a force, not a power."},"exp":"A watt is a joule per second, which in base units is **kg·m²·s⁻³**. V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s; Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = (N/m²)(m³/s) = N·m/s = J/s; A²·Ω = I²R = J/s. **kg·m·s⁻²** is a **newton**, a unit of force: it lacks the extra metre that makes a joule and the extra s⁻¹ that makes power.","how":{"ask":"Convert each expression to base units and compare with kg·m²·s⁻³.","know":"• W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³.\n• J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²; N = kg·m·s⁻².\n• V = J/C, A = C/s, Ω = V/A, Pa = N/m².","steps":[{"text":"Electrical products: V·A and A²·Ω both reduce to J/s.","calc":["V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s","A²·Ω = A·(A·Ω) = A·V = J/s"],"strike":["V·A","A²·Ω"]},{"text":"Pressure × volume per time is energy per time.","calc":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s"],"strike":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kg·m·s⁻² is a newton, a force. Answer: kg·m·s⁻²."}],"trap":"Stopping at \"it has kg, m and s\": count the powers; one metre with s⁻² makes a force."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ask**.","marks":[{"t":"NOT equivalent to the watt","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"different unit expressions","note":"reduce each to base units"}]},{"text":"**Build the ladder.**","calc":["N = kg·m·s⁻²","J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²","W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³"]},{"text":"**Also:** W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³; one more s⁻¹ than a joule means power."},{"text":"**Also:** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule (energy), not power; power needs kg·m²·s⁻³."},{"text":"**Also:** with no change in speed there is no acceleration (ΣF = 0), so the driving force equals the resisting force; then P = F·v.","calc":["constant speed: driving force = resisting force = 120 N","P = F·v = 120 N × 2.5 m/s = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** reduce every unit expression to base units: power is kg·m²·s⁻³ (J/s); kg·m²·s⁻² is energy and kg·m·s⁻² is force."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-01a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"aldol reaction product","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:1#2","pe3-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:1"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"In Experiment 2, which two chain carbons of the dione become the C=C of the ring product?","choices":["C1 and C6","C2 and C3","C4 and C5","C5 and C6"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for a 5- or 6-membered ring: only the CH₃ end can reach the far carbonyl and close six atoms. Dehydration then puts the C=C right on that new bond."},"exp":"Sentence 3 numbers the dione. Only the CH₃ carbon, **C6**, can reach a carbonyl and close a six-membered ring: its enolate bonds to **C1**, the phenyl ketone carbon. The OH left on C1 then leaves with an H from C6, so the **C=C joins C1 and C6**, conjugated with the C5 ketone, which stays a C=O.","how":{"ask":"Find which α-carbon attacks which carbonyl, then place the C=C that dehydration makes.","know":"• **Aldol bond**: the enolate's **α-carbon** (nucleophile) bonds to the **other carbonyl carbon** (electrophile).\n• **Ring size**: an intramolecular aldol closes a **five- or six-membered** ring.\n• **Dehydration**: the C=C forms between the **former α-carbon** and the **former carbonyl carbon**, conjugated with the remaining C=O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the dione's structure and its numbering: C1 is the phenyl ketone carbon, C5 the methyl ketone carbon, C6 the CH₃.","hl":"C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃","calc":["C₆H₅–C1(=O)–C2H₂–C3H₂–C4H₂–C5(=O)–C6H₃"]},{"text":"Two ketones plus base and loss of water point to an intramolecular aldol condensation, which closes a five- or six-membered ring. C6 attacking C1 makes six atoms; C2 or C4 attacking the nearer carbonyl makes only four.","calc":["C6 → C1: ring = C6, C5, C4, C3, C2, C1 = **6 atoms** ✓","C2 → C5 or C4 → C1: **4 atoms** ✗"]},{"text":"The new C6–C1 bond leaves an OH on C1, β to the C5 ketone. Dehydration removes that OH with an H from C6.","calc":["C=C: **C6 = C1**, conjugated with C5=O"]},{"text":"Check by neighbors: the phenyl starts on C1, and the product named in sentence 3 carries it on ring C3, an alkene carbon.","hl":"3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one"},{"text":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; C5 keeps its oxygen as the ring C=O, so it is never part of the C=C.","strike":["C2 and C3","C4 and C5","C5 and C6"]},{"text":"Answer: C1 and C6."}],"trap":"Putting the surviving C=O carbon (C5) into the C=C: it keeps its oxygen; the alkene forms at C1, the carbon that lost its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione","note":"two ketones, 1,5 apart"},{"t":"one ring closed and water was lost","note":"aldol, then dehydration"},{"t":"3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one","note":"six-membered enone"}]},{"text":"**Number and label.** Write C1–C6 under the chain; mark the carbonyls (C1, C5) and the α-carbons (C2, C4, C6)."},{"text":"**Make the aldol bond.** An α-carbon (as its enolate) bonds to the other carbonyl carbon; pick the pair that closes a five- or six-membered ring.","calc":["C6 + C1 → 6-membered ring"]},{"text":"**Dehydrate.** The OH on the former carbonyl carbon leaves with an H from the former α-carbon, so the C=C joins those two and is conjugated with the remaining C=O."},{"text":"**Also:** track carbons by their neighbors: the carbon that carries the phenyl keeps it, so you can find it again in the ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an intramolecular aldol condensation, the α-carbon bonds to the other carbonyl carbon, and the C=C forms between those same two carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-02a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"thermodynamic vs kinetic product","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:2"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"Which conclusion is best supported by the two Experiment 2 runs?","choices":["2-en is more stable, since it dominates at 78 °C","3-en is more stable, since it dominates at 0 °C","2-en forms faster, since it dominates at 78 °C","They are equally stable, since both isomers form"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Equilibrium favors the more stable isomer","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cold and brief = kinetic (fastest-forming); hot and long with interconversion = thermodynamic (most stable). Read stability only from the hot run."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Enone mix from the two Experiment 2 runs","x":{"label":"Conditions"},"y":{"label":"Percent of product","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["3 min, 0 °C","12 h, 78 °C"],"series":[{"name":"2-en isomer","values":[22,93]},{"name":"3-en isomer","values":[78,7]}]},"exp":"Sentence 5 says the enones **interconvert** only in the long, hot run, so only that run reaches **equilibrium**, where the **more stable** product dominates: the conjugated 2-en isomer. The 3 min, 0 °C run shows **kinetic control**: the 3-en isomer forms faster, so it has the lower Ea.","how":{"ask":"Decide which run reached equilibrium, then read stability from that run only.","know":"• **Thermodynamic control** (reversible, long time, higher T): the major product is the **more stable** one.\n• **Kinetic control** (irreversible, short time, low T): the major product is the one that **forms faster** (lower Ea).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the two enones interconvert only in the long, hot run.","hl":"where the two enones interconvert","calc":["interconversion → equilibrium → thermodynamic control"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the major product is the more stable one. The 12 h bars are mostly the 2-en isomer.","calc":["equilibrium → major = more stable = **2-en isomer**"],"graph":{"bars":[["12 h, 78 °C","2-en isomer"]]}},{"text":"The cold, brief run cannot reverse, so it shows rate: the 3-en isomer forms faster.","hl":"stopped after 3 min at 0 °C","calc":["kinetic → major = faster = 3-en isomer → lower Ea"],"graph":{"bars":[["3 min, 0 °C","3-en isomer"]]}},{"text":"The 3-en isomer wins only under kinetic control, so it is not the more stable one; and since it forms faster, it, not the 2-en isomer, has the lower Ea.","strike":["3-en is more stable, since it dominates at 0 °C","2-en forms faster, since it dominates at 78 °C"]},{"text":"Equal energies would give a near-even mix once the enones interconvert, not 93% of one.","strike":["They are equally stable, since both isomers form"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-en is more stable, since it dominates at 78 °C."}],"trap":"Reading stability from the cold, brief run: there the faster-forming product wins, whatever its stability."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"stopped after 3 min at 0 °C","note":"kinetic conditions"},{"t":"heated for 12 h at 78 °C","note":"thermodynamic conditions"},{"t":"interconvert","note":"equilibrium reached"}]},{"text":"**Sort the runs.** Interconversion means equilibrium; no interconversion means the first-formed product stays."},{"text":"**Equilibrium → stability.** The product favored at equilibrium has the lower free energy.","calc":["K = [2-en]/[3-en] > 1 → 2-en more stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** a C=C conjugated with the C=O is lower in energy, which fits the 2-en isomer winning at equilibrium."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** under equilibrium (thermodynamic) conditions the more stable product is major; under kinetic conditions the faster-forming product is major."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"aldol reaction product","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:3#1","pe3-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:3"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","choiceFigs":["cpt-3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","cpt-2-methylpent-2-enal","cpt-2-hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","cpt-3-hydroxyhexanal"],"q":"Which compound is isolated in Experiment 1?","choices":["3-Hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","2-Methylpent-2-enal","2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Aldol additions give β-hydroxy carbonyls","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Aldol dimer of an n-carbon aldehyde: 2n carbons, OH on C3, the α-carbon's substituent on C2. Propanal: 6 C, R = methyl."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says no water was lost (C₆H₁₂O₂ is two whole propanals), so this is **aldol addition**. The enolate carbon of one propanal (C2, which carries the CH₃) bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second, leaving a **β-OH**: CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO, **3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal**. The O–H band near 3400 cm⁻¹ fits; losing water would give 2-methylpent-2-enal.","how":{"ask":"Join an α-carbon of one aldehyde to the carbonyl carbon of the other, and keep the OH.","know":"• **Aldol addition** gives a **β-hydroxy carbonyl**: the old C=O becomes C–OH, two carbons from the remaining C=O.\n• **Dehydration** (heat or more base) removes that OH and an α-H, giving the **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**.\n• IR: broad ~3400 cm⁻¹ = O–H; ~1715 cm⁻¹ = C=O.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the product was isolated before any water was eliminated, and its formula is C₆H₁₂O₂: two whole propanal units.","hl":"isolated before any water was eliminated","calc":["2 C₃H₆O → C₆H₁₂O₂ (no H₂O lost)"]},{"text":"That points to aldol addition, not condensation, so the product is a β-hydroxy aldehyde. The IR bands back this up: an O–H and a C=O.","hl":"3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹"},{"text":"Base removes an α-H from C2 of propanal; that carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second propanal, and the O⁻ picks up H⁺.","calc":["CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO"]},{"text":"Name it: five-carbon chain with CHO as C1, CH₃ on C2, OH on C3.","calc":["3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (C₆H₁₂O₂ ✓)"]},{"text":"2-Methylpent-2-enal has lost water and has no O–H, so sentence 2 rules it out.","strike":["2-Methylpent-2-enal"]},{"text":"The OH must be on the β-carbon and the methyl on the α-carbon, so the swapped name is wrong; a straight six-carbon chain would need the terminal CH₃ to attack, but it is not α to the C=O.","strike":["2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."}],"trap":"Choosing the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde out of habit: it forms only after dehydration, which the passage rules out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"cold, dilute NaOH","note":"mild: aldol addition"},{"t":"before any water was eliminated","note":"no dehydration"},{"t":"C₆H₁₂O₂","note":"two whole propanals"}]},{"text":"**Find the α-carbon.** In CH₃CH₂CHO the α-carbon is the CH₂ next to C=O; its enolate is the nucleophile."},{"text":"**Join and protonate.** α-Carbon to the other C=O carbon; the O⁻ picks up H⁺, giving an OH on the β-carbon.","calc":["α-C–C(OH) next to CHO → β-hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the mixture is heated, the β-OH and an α-H leave as water, giving the conjugated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.","calc":["β-hydroxy carbonyl − H₂O → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aldol addition gives a β-hydroxy carbonyl; only dehydration turns it into the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-04a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"aldol reaction product","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:4#1","pe3-cp:4#2","pe3-cp:4#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:4"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P1","passage":"Base-promoted aldol reactions were run in three experiments. In Experiment 1, propanal was stirred with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product was isolated before any water was eliminated; it has the formula C₆H₁₂O₂ and IR bands near 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1715 cm⁻¹. In Experiment 2, 1-phenylhexane-1,5-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, whose chain carbons are numbered C1 (the phenyl ketone carbon) through C6 (the methyl carbon), was treated with KOH; one ring closed and water was lost to give 3-phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, whose C=C is conjugated with its C=O. Its isomer 3-phenylcyclohex-3-en-1-one, whose C=C lies one carbon farther from the C=O, also formed. A run stopped after 3 min at 0 °C gave mostly the 3-en isomer, while a run heated for 12 h at 78 °C, where the two enones interconvert, gave mostly the 2-en isomer. In Experiment 3, the enolate of cyclohexanone added to pent-1-en-3-one, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in a Michael addition, and an intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water closed a new six-membered enone ring. In that new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 is the alkene carbon beside it, C3 is the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 is the CH₂ bonded to C1.","q":"In Experiment 3, on which carbon of the new ring does the extra methyl group of pent-1-en-3-one end up?","choices":["C1","C2","C3","C6"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Ring ketone carbon bears no substituent","c":[0]},{"f":"Fusion alkene carbon: no free valence","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The extra methyl rides on the ethyl CH₂. That carbon becomes the enolate that closes the ring, so after dehydration it is the alkene carbon right next to the C=O."},"exp":"Sentence 6 names the acceptor, pent-1-en-3-one. **Michael**: the cyclohexanone enolate bonds to its **β-carbon** (the CH₂= end). **Aldol**: the ketone's other α-carbon, the CH₂ of the ethyl group, attacks the ring C=O and closes a six-membered ring; **dehydration** puts the C=C between that carbon and the old ring carbonyl carbon. The extra CH₃ rides along on that α-carbon, so by sentence 7's numbering it sits on **C2**.","how":{"ask":"Track the carbon that carries the extra methyl through the Michael addition, the ring-closing aldol and the dehydration.","know":"• **Michael addition**: an enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** of an α,β-unsaturated ketone.\n• **Intramolecular aldol + dehydration**: an α-carbon attacks the other C=O to close a six-membered ring, and the C=C forms between the former α-carbon and the former carbonyl carbon.\n• A group that does not react **rides along** on the atom that carried it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the acceptor: pent-1-en-3-one, whose ketone carries an ethyl group where methyl vinyl ketone has a methyl.","hl":"CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃","calc":["extra CH₃ sits on the ethyl CH₂"]},{"text":"Michael step: the enolate adds to the vinyl end, the β-carbon of the enone, so a chain hangs off the ring.","calc":["ring C–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₃"]},{"text":"Aldol step: the ethyl CH₂, the α-carbon that carries the CH₃, attacks the ring C=O; that closes a six-membered ring.","calc":["ring = old C=O carbon, old α-carbon, CH₂, CH₂, C=O, CH(CH₃) = **6 atoms**"]},{"text":"Dehydration: water leaves, and the C=C joins the CH(CH₃) carbon to the old ring carbonyl carbon. Sentence 7 calls that carbon beside the C=O C2.","hl":"C2 is the alkene carbon beside it","calc":["C1 = C=O, **C2 = C(CH₃)=**, C3 = ring fusion"]},{"text":"C1 keeps its C=O and has no spare bond; C6 comes from the vinyl end; C3 is the old ring carbonyl carbon.","strike":["C1","C6","C3"]},{"text":"Answer: C2."}],"trap":"Putting the extra group on the vinyl end: the enolate attacks the CH₂= carbon, but the methyl rides on the ethyl side, which becomes the ring-closing α-carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Michael addition","note":"enolate → β-carbon"},{"t":"intramolecular aldol reaction followed by loss of water","note":"ring closes, then C=C forms"},{"t":"pent-1-en-3-one","note":"one extra CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Michael first.** The enolate carbon bonds to the β-carbon, the far end of the C=C, so a chain hangs off the ring with its ketone three atoms out."},{"text":"**Ring closure.** The α-carbon that can reach the ring C=O to make a six-membered ring is the one that attacks; water then leaves and the C=C forms at the new bond, conjugated with the C=O."},{"text":"**Ride-along groups.** A substituent that does not react stays on its own carbon, so follow that carbon, not the substituent."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a Michael-then-aldol ring closure, the enolate hits the β-carbon, the far α-carbon closes a six-membered ring, and every extra group stays on the atom it started on."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"capacitor charge Q = CV","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:5#3","pe3-cp:5#4","pe5-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:5","pe5-cp:52"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"Once the strobe capacitor is fully charged, which of the following gives its charge and the energy it stores?","choices":["0.40 C and 3.2 J","0.40 C and 6.4 J","400 C and 3200 J","0.40 C and 0.20 J"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[2]},{"r":6,"c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mF × V = mC: 25 × 16 = 400 mC. Then U = ½QV = ½ × 0.40 C × 16 V."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says charging runs **until no current flows**, so no voltage drops across the 0.3 Ω internal resistance and the capacitor sits at the full 16 V. **Q = CV** = (25 × 10⁻³ F)(16 V) = **0.40 C**; **U = ½CV²** = ½(0.025 F)(16 V)² = **3.2 J**.","how":{"ask":"Find the voltage across the charged capacitor, then apply Q = CV and U = ½CV².","know":"• **Q = CV** and **U = ½CV² = ½QV**.\n• Fully charged: **no current**, so the internal resistance drops no voltage.\n• **1 mF = 10⁻³ F**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the capacitor charges until no current flows: with zero current, the battery's internal resistance drops no voltage.","hl":"until no current flows","calc":["V across C = 16 V (I = 0, so Ir = 0)"]},{"text":"That points to the capacitor relations, with mF converted to F.","calc":["C = 25 mF = 0.025 F","Q = CV = 0.025 × 16 = **0.40 C**"]},{"text":"Now the energy stored.","calc":["U = ½CV² = ½ × 0.025 × 256 = **3.2 J**","check: ½QV = ½ × 0.40 × 16 = 3.2 J ✓"]},{"text":"Treating 25 mF as 25 F makes both values a thousand times too large.","strike":["400 C and 3200 J"]},{"text":"Dropping the ½ doubles the energy, and ½CV leaves out a factor of V.","strike":["0.40 C and 6.4 J","0.40 C and 0.20 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40 C and 3.2 J."}],"trap":"Subtracting a voltage drop across the internal resistance: once charged, no current flows, so Ir = 0."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"25 mF capacitor","note":"C = 0.025 F"},{"t":"16 V battery","note":"full charging voltage"},{"t":"until no current flows","note":"internal resistance drops out"}]},{"text":"**No current, no drop.** A resistor drops V = IR; with I = 0, the capacitor gets the battery's whole voltage."},{"text":"**Charge, then energy.**","calc":["Q = CV","U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert prefixes first: m = 10⁻³, µ = 10⁻⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a fully charged capacitor, use the source voltage, Q = CV and U = ½CV², with C in farads."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe5-cp:52"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-52a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-06a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"Doppler shift direction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:6","sample-cp:27","sample-cp:55"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"Suppose that after passing the horn the driver stops and then backs toward it at 5.0 m/s. Compared with the horn's frequency, how does the frequency she hears change before she stops and after she reverses?","choices":["Higher before stopping; lower after reversing","Lower before stopping; higher after reversing","Equal before stopping; equal after reversing","Lower before stopping; lower after reversing"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"A moving listener still hears shifts","c":[2]},{"f":"Closing in raises the pitch","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Before stopping she has passed the horn and is moving away: lower. Backing toward it she closes in: higher."},"exp":"Sentence 4 puts a **fixed** horn beside the track, so only the listener moves. Before she stops she has already passed it and is moving **away**: f′ = f(v − v_o)/v ≈ 512 Hz, **lower** than 520 Hz. Backing toward it she **closes in**: f′ = f(v + v_o)/v ≈ 528 Hz, **higher**.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the driver and horn are separating or closing in, before she stops and after she reverses.","know":"• **Doppler effect**: closing in → higher f; separating → lower f.\n• Moving listener, still source: f′ = f(v ± v_o)/v, + toward, − away.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the horn is fixed, so only the listener moves.","hl":"a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz","calc":["source still, listener moving"]},{"text":"Before stopping she has passed the horn and is moving away from it.","calc":["f′ = 520 × (346 − 5.0)/346 ≈ 512 Hz → **lower**"]},{"text":"Backing toward the horn she runs into the crests.","calc":["f′ = 520 × (346 + 5.0)/346 ≈ 528 Hz → **higher**"]},{"text":"The reverse order, no shift at all, or a lower pitch while closing in each break the toward-higher, away-lower rule.","strike":["Higher before stopping; lower after reversing","Equal before stopping; equal after reversing","Lower before stopping; lower after reversing"]},{"text":"Answer: lower before stopping; higher after reversing."}],"trap":"Answering \"higher, then lower\" by habit: here she is already past the horn, so she moves away first and closes in second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a fixed alarm horn","note":"source at rest"},{"t":"after passing the horn","note":"moving away"},{"t":"backs toward it","note":"closing in"}]},{"text":"**Closing in or separating?** That one question sets the sign of the shift."},{"text":"**Size of the shift.**","calc":["f′ = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the size of the shift depends on her speed, not her distance; reversing at the same speed flips its sign but keeps its size."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** closing in raises the heard frequency and separating lowers it, whether the source or the listener moves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-07a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"power P = Fv","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:7"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P2","passage":"A battery locomotive that hauls ore through a straight, level mine tunnel was checked on one test run. Its warning strobe runs from a 25 mF capacitor, which is charged from the locomotive's 16 V battery (internal resistance 0.3 Ω) until no current flows. Through the main tunnel, the 8000-kg locomotive held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N. Halfway along, it passed a fixed alarm horn that sounds a steady 520 Hz, and the driver heard the horn as she approached it and again after she passed it; sound travels at 346 m/s in the tunnel air. At the end of the run the motor was switched off, and the locomotive coasted into a magnetic braking zone, where eddy currents in its steel wheels slowed it and rolling friction was negligible. In that zone its speed fell from 5.0 m/s to half that value in the first 3 s and halved again in each later 3 s interval, so it never quite stopped (Figure 1).","q":"What power must the motor deliver to hold the locomotive at its steady speed in the main tunnel?","choices":["280 W","1.4 kW","7.0 kW","40 kW"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"W = N·m/s, so multiply the resisting force (N) by the speed (m/s): 1400 × 5.0."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the speed is **steady**, so the net force is zero and the motor's driving force equals the **1400 N** of resistance. **P = Fv** = (1400 N)(5.0 m/s) = 7000 W = **7.0 kW**. The 8000 kg mass plays no part.","how":{"ask":"Find the driving force at constant speed, then multiply by the speed.","know":"• **Constant velocity** → net force zero → driving force = resisting force.\n• **P = Fv** (watts = newtons × m/s).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the locomotive holds a steady speed against a known resisting force.","hl":"held a steady 5.0 m/s while rolling resistance and air drag together opposed it with 1400 N","calc":["v = 5.0 m/s, F_resist = 1400 N"]},{"text":"Steady speed means zero net force (Newton's first law), so drive force and resistance match.","calc":["F_drive = 1400 N"]},{"text":"Power at constant speed is force times speed.","calc":["P = Fv = 1400 × 5.0 = 7000 W = **7.0 kW**"]},{"text":"Dividing force by speed gives N·s/m, not watts; 1400 N alone is a force, not a power; 8000 kg × 5.0 m/s is momentum.","strike":["280 W","1.4 kW","40 kW"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.0 kW."}],"trap":"Bringing in the mass: at constant speed no net force acts, so mass doesn't enter the power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"held a steady 5.0 m/s","note":"zero net force"},{"t":"opposed it with 1400 N","note":"resisting force"},{"t":"8000-kg","note":"distractor at steady speed"}]},{"text":"**Balance the forces.** Constant velocity → F_drive = F_resist."},{"text":"**Power.**","calc":["P = W/t = F·d/t = Fv"]},{"text":"**Also:** check units: N × m/s = J/s = W."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant speed, the power needed is the resisting force times the speed, P = Fv."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"heat of combustion → stability","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:9#1","pe3-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:9"],"q":"Four C₄H₈ isomers, W–Z, each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Their heats of combustion are W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol. Which isomer is the most thermodynamically stable?","choices":["W, since it releases the most heat","X, since it releases more heat than Y","Y, since its heat is nearest the average","Z, since it releases the least heat"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Isomers: more combustion heat, less stable","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same products, so less heat out means it started lower in energy. Smallest heat of combustion = most stable: 2701 kJ/mol, Z."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Heat released on burning one mole of each C₄H₈ isomer","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Heat released (kJ/mol)","min":2690,"max":2720,"step":10},"categories":["W","X","Y","Z"],"series":[{"name":"Heat released","values":[2717,2710,2707,2701]}]},"exp":"All four isomers end at the **same products**, so the difference in heat released is the difference in their starting enthalpies. Z releases the **least heat**, so it began lowest in energy and is the most stable; W, releasing the most, is the least stable.","how":{"ask":"Compare the heats of combustion; the smallest one marks the most stable isomer.","know":"• Isomers that burn to the **same products** can be ranked by heat of combustion: **less heat released = lower starting enthalpy = more stable**.\n• Boiling points, UV spectra and mass spectra do **not** measure thermodynamic stability.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula, same products, so all four reactions end at the same enthalpy.","hl":"each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O"},{"text":"Rank by heat released; the gap from the products is the starting enthalpy.","hl":"W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol","calc":["Z 2701 < Y 2707 < X 2710 < W 2717 kJ/mol","most stable: **Z**; Z is 2717 − 2701 = 16 kJ/mol below W"],"graph":{"bars":[["Z","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest and is least stable; X and Y lie in between.","strike":["W, since it releases the most heat","X, since it releases more heat than Y","Y, since its heat is nearest the average"]},{"text":"Answer: Z, since it releases the least heat."}],"trap":"Thinking more heat released means a 'stronger' or more stable molecule: more heat out means it started higher in energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈ isomers","note":"same formula"},{"t":"4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O","note":"same products"},{"t":"W 2717","note":"heat released, kJ/mol"},{"t":"Z 2701 kJ/mol","note":"smallest heat"}]},{"text":"**Common endpoint.** Products are identical, so each heat of combustion measures how far its isomer sits above the same floor.","calc":["ΔH_comb = H(products) − H(isomer)"]},{"text":"**Smaller drop, lower start.** The isomer giving off the smallest amount of heat starts lowest in enthalpy: most stable.","calc":["2701 kJ/mol → lowest start → Z"]},{"text":"**Also:** boiling points reflect intermolecular forces, UV absorption reflects conjugation, and mass-spec patterns reflect mass and fragmentation; none of them measures stability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among isomers, the smallest heat of combustion marks the most stable one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"exchangeable H in isotope labels","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:10"],"q":"Tritiated N-(2-hydroxyethyl)acetamide, with atoms labeled I–IV, is kept in water for two days. Only labeled atoms bearing H can hold tritium. Which site would best retain the label?","choices":["I","II","III","IV"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Carbonyl oxygens carry no hydrogen","c":[0]},{"f":"O–H and N–H exchange with water","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan for the one labeled atom that is a carbon bearing H. O–H and N–H exchange with water; C–H does not."},"fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites","exp":"Hydrogens on **O or N** are acidic enough to **exchange with water** quickly, so tritium there is washed into the solvent. Hydrogens on **carbon** do not exchange, so a label on the **CH₃ (site IV)** stays put. Site I, the C=O oxygen, has no H to label.","how":{"ask":"Classify each labeled atom by what it is and what it carries, then keep only a C–H.","know":"• **O–H and N–H** hydrogens exchange with solvent water (fast proton transfer).\n• **C–H** hydrogens, alkyl or aromatic, do not exchange under these conditions.\n• An atom with no H cannot carry a hydrogen label.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each labeled atom: I is the C=O oxygen, II the O–H, III the N–H, IV the CH₃ carbon.","hl":"atoms labeled I–IV"},{"text":"Site I bears no hydrogen, so it cannot carry tritium.","strike":["I"]},{"text":"Sites II and III are H on O and on N: they swap with water's H over two days.","strike":["II","III"]},{"text":"Answer: IV."}],"trap":"Choosing a polar O–H or N–H because it looks reactive: those are exactly the hydrogens that wash out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"kept in water for two days","note":"time for exchange"},{"t":"best retain the label","note":"non-exchangeable H"}]},{"text":"**Exchangeable H.** Any H on O or N is passed back and forth with water, so its tritium ends up in the solvent.","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** an aromatic ring C–H is just as stable as an alkyl C–H, so on a drawn ring the C–H site is the one that keeps a label."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only hydrogens on carbon keep an isotope label in water; H on O or N exchanges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-11a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ideal gas law PV = nRT","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:11#1","pe3-cp:11#2","pe3-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:11"],"q":"A balloon of volume V holds gas at pressure P and 25 °C. Using R and Avogadro's number N_A, which of the following gives the number of gas molecules in it?","choices":["PVN_A/(R × 298 K)","PV/(R × 298 K)","PVN_A/(R × 25 K)","PV/(RN_A × 298 K)"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Gas laws need kelvin","c":[2]},{"f":"Moles times N_A gives molecules","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"PV/RT is moles; mol × (molecules/mol) = molecules, so N_A multiplies. T must be 25 + 273 K."},"exp":"**n = PV/RT** gives moles, with T in **kelvin**: 25 + 273 = 298 K. Multiplying by **N_A** (molecules per mole) gives the number of molecules: **PVN_A/(R × 298 K)**.","how":{"ask":"Build the count from n = PV/RT, then convert moles to molecules, with T in kelvin.","know":"• **Ideal gas law**: n = PV/RT, with T in **kelvin** (K = °C + 273).\n• **Molecules = n × N_A**.\n• Units: (Pa·m³) / (J/(mol·K) × K) = mol.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the temperature.","hl":"25 °C","calc":["T = 25 + 273 = **298 K**"]},{"text":"Moles from the gas law.","calc":["n = PV/(R × 298 K)"]},{"text":"Molecules = moles × N_A.","calc":["N = PVN_A/(R × 298 K)"]},{"text":"25 is a Celsius value; dividing by N_A runs the conversion backwards; PV/(R × 298 K) stops at moles.","strike":["PVN_A/(R × 25 K)","PV/(RN_A × 298 K)","PV/(R × 298 K)"]},{"text":"Answer: PVN_A/(R × 298 K)."}],"trap":"Stopping at moles: PV/RT counts moles, and the question asks for molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"convert to kelvin"},{"t":"the number of gas molecules","note":"not moles: × N_A"}]},{"text":"**Kelvin first.** Gas laws use absolute temperature.","calc":["25 °C + 273 = 298 K"]},{"text":"**Moles to molecules.**","calc":["N = nN_A = PVN_A/RT"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** particles = PVN_A/RT, with T in kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-12a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"Ka·Kb = Kw (pKa + pKb = 14)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:12#1","pe3-cp:12#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:12"],"q":"H₂SO₃ has Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻² and Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴). In water, HSO₃⁻ is best classified as:","choices":["an acid, since its Ka is larger than its Kb.","a base, since its Kb is larger than its Ka.","a base, since its Ka1 is the largest constant.","neither, since its Ka and Kb are equal."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Kb(HSO₃⁻) = Kw/Ka1 = 10⁻¹⁴/1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7 × 10⁻¹³. Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is about 10⁵ times larger, so it acts as an acid."},"exp":"As an acid, HSO₃⁻ has **Ka = Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸**. As a base it becomes H₂SO₃, so its **Kb = Kw/Ka1** = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**. Ka is far larger, so HSO₃⁻ behaves as an **acid**: its Ka is larger than its Kb.","how":{"ask":"Get the ion's Ka and its Kb, then compare them.","know":"• For HA⁻ made from H₂A: **Ka(HA⁻) = Ka2**.\n• **Kb(HA⁻) = Kw/Ka1**, because its conjugate acid is H₂A.\n• Ka > Kb → acts as an acid; Kb > Ka → acts as a base.","steps":[{"text":"The ion's own acid constant is Ka2.","hl":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸"},{"text":"Its base constant comes from its conjugate acid, H₂SO₃.","hl":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻²","Kb ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**"]},{"text":"Compare: Ka2 is about 10⁵ times Kb, so its Ka is larger than its Kb.","calc":["6.3 × 10⁻⁸ ≫ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³"]},{"text":"Kw/Ka2 is not this ion's Kb; Ka1 belongs to H₂SO₃; the two constants are nowhere near equal.","strike":["a base, since its Kb is larger than its Ka.","a base, since its Ka1 is the largest constant.","neither, since its Ka and Kb are equal."]},{"text":"Answer: an acid, since its Ka is larger than its Kb."}],"trap":"Dividing Kw by Ka2 to get the ion's Kb: that gives the Kb of SO₃²⁻, not of HSO₃⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","note":"H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻"},{"t":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"the ion's own Ka"},{"t":"HSO₃⁻","note":"amphoteric ion"}]},{"text":"**Two jobs, two constants.** As an acid HSO₃⁻ uses Ka2; as a base it uses Kw divided by the Ka of its conjugate acid, Ka1.","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amphoteric ion's Ka is the constant for losing its H⁺, its Kb is Kw over the Ka of the acid it forms; the larger one decides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-13a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"limiting reagent","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:13#1","pe3-cp:13#2","pe3-cp:13#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:13"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"In the synthesis, the limiting reactant and the theoretical yield of CdS are:","choices":["Na₂S; 2.9 g of CdS.","Cd(NO₃)₂; 4.3 g of CdS.","Na₂S; 4.3 g of CdS.","Cd(NO₃)₂; 2.9 g of CdS."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Leftover reactant isn't limiting","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage already names the excess reactant (leftover Cd²⁺), so the other one limits. Yield = its moles × 144.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the filtrate still held **Cd²⁺**, so Cd(NO₃)₂ was in excess and **Na₂S limits**. Check with sentence 2: 7.09 g ÷ 236.4 = 0.0300 mol Cd(NO₃)₂; 1.56 g ÷ 78.0 = **0.0200 mol Na₂S**, and the ratio is 1:1. Yield = 0.0200 mol × 144.5 g/mol = **2.9 g CdS**.","how":{"ask":"Find which reactant runs out, then base the yield on its moles.","know":"• **Moles** = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• Divide by each coefficient: the **smallest** value is the **limiting** reactant.\n• A reactant **left over** at the end was in **excess**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the filtrate gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺, so cadmium nitrate was in excess.","hl":"it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺","calc":["Cd(NO₃)₂ in excess → Na₂S limits"]},{"text":"That is the limiting-reactant idea; confirm it with moles from sentence 2.","hl":"7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol)","calc":["Cd(NO₃)₂: 7.09 ÷ 236.4 = 0.0300 mol","Na₂S: 1.56 ÷ 78.0 = **0.0200 mol** (1:1, smaller → limiting)"]},{"text":"Base the yield on the limiting reactant.","calc":["CdS = 0.0200 mol × 144.5 g/mol = **2.9 g**"]},{"text":"Cadmium nitrate is left over, so it cannot be limiting, whatever mass is paired with it.","strike":["Cd(NO₃)₂; 4.3 g of CdS.","Cd(NO₃)₂; 2.9 g of CdS."]},{"text":"4.3 g is 0.0300 mol × 144.5: the excess reactant's moles, more than the limiting one allows.","strike":["Na₂S; 4.3 g of CdS."]},{"text":"Answer: Na₂S; 2.9 g of CdS."}],"trap":"Calling the reactant with the smaller mass limiting, or basing the yield on the excess reactant's moles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"positive test for leftover Cd²⁺","note":"Cd(NO₃)₂ in excess"},{"t":"7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol)","note":"0.0300 mol"},{"t":"1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol)","note":"0.0200 mol"}]},{"text":"**Moles, not grams.** Divide each mass by its molar mass, then by its coefficient."},{"text":"**Yield from the limiting reactant.**","calc":["mol limiting × (product coefficient ÷ its coefficient) × M(product)"]},{"text":"**Also:** leftover reactant seen after the reaction is a direct clue to which reactant was in excess."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the reactant with the fewest moles per coefficient limits the reaction and sets the theoretical yield; any reactant left over was in excess."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-15a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"electron configuration","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:15#1","pe3-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:15#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:15","sample-cp:29"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P3","passage":"Cadmium sulfide, CdS, is a yellow pigment made by mixing two colorless solutions: Cd(NO₃)₂(aq) + Na₂S(aq) → CdS(s) + 2NaNO₃(aq). A chemist dissolved 7.09 g of Cd(NO₃)₂ (236.4 g/mol) and 1.56 g of Na₂S (78.0 g/mol) in separate beakers of water and then poured them together (CdS: 144.5 g/mol). A bright yellow solid formed at once and was filtered off; the colorless filtrate still conducted electricity, since Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ stay dissolved, and it gave a positive test for leftover Cd²⁺. Cadmium (Z = 48) sits below zinc in group 12, and like zinc it forms only one ion, Cd²⁺; after the [Kr] core of 36 electrons, its 5s subshell fills before its 4d. Treating the precipitation as a single elementary step, the chemist sketched its energy diagram: the separated ions start at 15 kJ/mol, the highest point reaches 70 kJ/mol, and the solid product ends at −45 kJ/mol.","q":"The ground-state electron configuration of a neutral cadmium atom is:","choices":["[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²","[Kr]4d¹⁰5s¹","[Kr]4d⁵5s²","[Kr]4d¹⁰"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Group 12 atoms: no unpaired electrons","c":[1,2]},{"f":"5s fills before 4d","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Group 12 = d¹⁰s². Cd: 48 − 36 = 12 electrons past [Kr] = 4d¹⁰5s². The ion drops the 5s pair."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives Z = 48 and the [Kr] core of 36, so neutral Cd has **12** electrons past the core: **5s²** (filled) and **4d¹⁰** (filled), [Kr]4d¹⁰5s². The ion Cd²⁺ loses the two **5s** electrons first, leaving [Kr]4d¹⁰.","how":{"ask":"Count the electrons past the core, fill 5s and 4d, then describe each subshell.","know":"• Group 12 atoms (Zn, Cd) end in **d¹⁰s²**: both subshells filled (Zn is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²).\n• Cations lose the outer **s electrons before d** electrons (Zn²⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰).\n• \"Half-filled\" means **d⁵** or **s¹**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives cadmium's atomic number and the size of its core, and says 5s fills before 4d.","hl":"Cadmium (Z = 48)","calc":["48 − 36 = 12 electrons past [Kr]"]},{"text":"Fill in aufbau order: 5s first (2), then 4d (10).","hl":"its 5s subshell fills before its 4d","calc":["5s² + 4d¹⁰ = 12 ✓"]},{"text":"A half-filled 4d (7 in all) or a half-filled 5s (11 in all) has too few electrons; an empty 5s is the Cd²⁺ ion, not the atom.","strike":["[Kr]4d⁵5s²","[Kr]4d¹⁰5s¹","[Kr]4d¹⁰"]},{"text":"Answer: [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²."}],"trap":"Describing the ion when the question asks about the neutral atom: Cd²⁺ has an empty 5s, but the atom keeps both 5s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Cadmium (Z = 48)","note":"48 electrons"},{"t":"forms only one ion, Cd²⁺","note":"loses two electrons"},{"t":"the [Kr] core of 36 electrons","note":"subtract the core"}]},{"text":"**Count past the core.**","calc":["48 − 36 = 12 → 4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"**Also:** the ion removes the 5s electrons first, giving [Kr]4d¹⁰ with every electron paired; zinc does the same, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s² → [Ar]3d¹⁰."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** group 12 atoms are d¹⁰s² with both subshells filled; their ions lose the s pair first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"redox from oxidation-state change","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:16#1","pe3-cp:32#1","pe3-cp:32#2","pe3-cp:32#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:32"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which species is reduced in enzyme Z's overall reaction?","choices":["O₂","NADH","FMN","RNH₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"NADH to NAD⁺ is oxidation","c":[1]},{"f":"Regenerated cofactors undergo no net change","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"In a reaction that consumes NADH and molecular oxygen, NADH is the reductant and the oxygen is what ends up reduced."},"exp":"Adding the steps in sentences 2 and 3 gives NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O. **O₂** (oxidation state 0) ends up as H₂O and as the N–OH oxygen, both **−2**: it gains electrons, so it is **reduced**. NADH and the substrate's nitrogen are oxidized, and FMN is regenerated.","how":{"ask":"Write the overall reaction, then find the species whose oxidation state drops.","know":"• **Reduced** = gains electrons; its oxidation state goes **down**.\n• O in O₂ is **0**; in H₂O or an OH group it is **−2**.\n• A **regenerated** cofactor has no net change.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the reduced enzyme uses O₂, and its oxygen ends up in H₂O and in RNHOH.","hl":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","calc":["O: 0 → −2 in both products"]},{"text":"An oxidation state that drops means a gain of electrons: reduction.","calc":["O₂ gains electrons → **reduced**"]},{"text":"NADH loses a hydride to become NAD⁺, and the substrate's N goes from −3 to −1: both are oxidized.","strike":["NADH","RNH₂"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 reduces FMN, but sentence 3 reoxidizes it, so it has no net change.","strike":["FMN"]},{"text":"Answer: O₂."}],"trap":"Naming the flavin because it is reduced in the first step: it is reoxidized in the second, so overall it is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"takes a hydride and a proton from NADH","note":"NADH is oxidized"},{"t":"uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen","note":"O₂ is reduced"}]},{"text":"**Track oxidation states.**","calc":["O: 0 (O₂) → −2 (H₂O, N–OH)","N: −3 (RNH₂) → −1 (RNHOH)"]},{"text":"**Cancel the cofactor.** A flavin that is reduced and then reoxidized each turn has no net change."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the species whose oxidation state falls is reduced; check the net reaction, not a single step."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-17a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"temperature to kelvin","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:17#1","pe3-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:17"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"At the passage's gas temperature limit, what value of T must be entered in PV = nRT?","choices":["55 K","328 K","346 K","404 K"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Temperatures above freezing exceed 273 K","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor points: 32 °F = 0 °C and each °F step is 5/9 of a °C. (131 − 32) × 5/9 = 55 °C, then + 273."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the limit in **°F** and says it goes into PV = nRT, which needs **kelvin**. °F → °C: (131 − 32) × 5/9 = 55 °C. °C → K: 55 + 273 = **328 K**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the Fahrenheit limit to Celsius, then to kelvin.","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**.\n• **K = °C + 273**.\n• Gas-law temperatures are always absolute (kelvin).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the limit in Fahrenheit and says it is used in the ideal gas law.","hl":"the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F","calc":["T = 131 °F → must become kelvin"]},{"text":"Convert Fahrenheit to Celsius: subtract 32, then scale by 5/9.","calc":["(131 − 32) × 5/9 = 99 × 5/9 = 55 °C"]},{"text":"Convert Celsius to kelvin.","calc":["55 + 273 = **328 K**"]},{"text":"Stopping at Celsius gives 55; adding 273 to the °F reading gives 404; dividing 131 by 1.8 without subtracting 32 gives about 346.","strike":["55 K","404 K","346 K"]},{"text":"Answer: 328 K."}],"trap":"Adding 273 straight to a Fahrenheit number: the 273 offset belongs to the Celsius scale."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"131 °F","note":"Fahrenheit: convert twice"},{"t":"PV = nRT","note":"needs kelvin"}]},{"text":"**Fahrenheit to Celsius.**","calc":["°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9"]},{"text":"**Celsius to kelvin.**","calc":["K = °C + 273"]},{"text":"**Also:** sanity check: body temperature, 98.6 °F, is 37 °C, about 310 K."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** before any gas-law calculation, take °F to °C (subtract 32, × 5/9) and then add 273."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-20a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"uniform field between plates (E = V/d)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:20#1","pe3-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:20","sample-cp:5"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P4","passage":"A portable water purifier kills microbes with ultraviolet light from a sealed, gas-filled lamp. The lamp emits every wavelength from 280 nm to 450 nm; take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. To protect the quartz envelope, the lamp gas must never exceed 131 °F, and the design team plugs this limit into PV = nRT to find the highest gas pressure. The lamp is driven by square pulses: the voltage is held at 2.4 kV for 15 µs, then switched off for 25 µs, and the cycle repeats. The lamp's cooling air passes between two large parallel plates 8.0 mm apart, the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded, which pull charged dust out of the airflow. Away from the plate edges, the field in the gap has one strength everywhere.","q":"Away from the edges, the electric field lines in the gap are:","choices":["evenly spaced and pointing toward the lower plate.","evenly spaced and pointing toward the upper plate.","crowded near plates and pointing toward the lower plate.","curved outward and pointing toward the upper plate."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Field lines point toward lower potential","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Uniform field lines are evenly spaced","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same strength everywhere = same line spacing everywhere. Lines point downhill in potential: + plate → grounded plate."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says the field has **the same strength everywhere** away from the edges, so the lines are **parallel and evenly spaced**. Sentence 5 puts the upper plate at **+150 V** and the lower one at 0 V; field lines point from higher to lower potential, so they run **from the upper plate to the lower**.","how":{"ask":"Use the field's strength to set the spacing, then the plate potentials to set the direction.","know":"• Line **density** shows field **strength**: equal strength → equal spacing.\n• Field lines point from **+ (higher V)** to **− (lower V)**.\n• Large parallel plates give a **uniform** field away from the edges.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the field has one strength throughout the gap, away from the edges.","hl":"the field in the gap has one strength everywhere","calc":["uniform E → evenly spaced, parallel lines"]},{"text":"Direction: sentence 5 puts the upper plate at the higher potential.","hl":"the upper one at +150 V and the lower one grounded","calc":["E points high V → low V: upper → lower"]},{"text":"Pointing toward the upper plate points uphill in potential; crowding near the plates would mean a stronger field there; curved lines belong only at the edges.","strike":["evenly spaced and pointing toward the upper plate.","crowded near plates and pointing toward the lower plate.","curved outward and pointing toward the upper plate."]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced and pointing toward the lower plate."}],"trap":"Drawing the lines toward the positive plate: they start on positive charge and end on negative."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"large parallel plates","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"one strength everywhere","note":"even spacing"},{"t":"the upper one at +150 V","note":"lines start here"}]},{"text":"**Spacing = strength.** Closer lines mean a stronger field, so a uniform field has evenly spaced lines."},{"text":"**Direction.** Lines run from higher to lower potential, the way a positive charge would be pushed.","calc":["E = ΔV/d = 150 V ÷ 8.0 mm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** between parallel plates, field lines are straight, evenly spaced and point from the higher-potential plate to the lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:22#1","pe3-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:22"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","fig":["1-phenylethanol","acetophenone"],"q":"The enzyme class of the sensor's oxidase is:","choices":["oxidoreductase, since it removes two H atoms.","transferase, since it passes H to O₂.","lyase, since it forms a C=O bond.","isomerase, since the molecular formula is unchanged."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Alcohol to ketone changes the formula","c":[3]},{"f":"Lyases cleave bonds without oxidation","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count H: alcohol → ketone loses 2 H, which end up on O₂. Losing hydrogen is oxidation, so the class is the redox one."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the enzyme turns the alcohol into a ketone by **removing two hydrogens**, and sentence 3 says they end up reducing O₂ to H₂O₂. An enzyme that catalyzes an **oxidation–reduction** is an **oxidoreductase**, just like glucose oxidase in a glucose meter.","how":{"ask":"Compare substrate and product, decide what kind of change it is, and match it to one of the six classes.","know":"• **Oxidoreductase**: oxidation–reduction (H removed, O added, electrons moved).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (methyl, phosphate) from one molecule to another.\n• **Hydrolase**: splits a bond with water.\n• **Lyase**: makes or breaks a double bond or ring by adding or removing a group, without hydrolysis or redox.\n• **Isomerase**: rearranges atoms within one molecule.\n• **Ligase**: joins two molecules, using ATP.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the oxidase strips two hydrogen atoms from the alcohol to make the ketone.","hl":"by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons","calc":["C₈H₁₀O → C₈H₈O + 2 H"]},{"text":"Losing hydrogen (electrons) is oxidation, and sentence 3 says O₂ is reduced to H₂O₂: a redox reaction.","hl":"passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂"},{"text":"The formula changed, so it is not an isomerase.","strike":["isomerase, since the molecular formula is unchanged."]},{"text":"Removing H and reducing O₂ is oxidation–reduction, not a group transfer or a lyase addition.","strike":["transferase, since it passes H to O₂.","lyase, since it forms a C=O bond."]},{"text":"Answer: oxidoreductase, since it removes two H atoms."}],"trap":"Calling it a transferase because hydrogens move to O₂: moving electrons with hydrogens is redox; transferases move whole groups such as methyl or phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"removing two hydrogen atoms","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"passes them to O₂","note":"O₂ is reduced"},{"t":"no other substrate is consumed","note":"no group donor"}]},{"text":"**Six classes, six kinds of change.**","calc":["redox → oxidoreductase","group moved → transferase","water splits a bond → hydrolase","double bond made or broken → lyase","atoms rearranged → isomerase","two joined with ATP → ligase"]},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme that moves a methyl group from SAM onto a substrate is a transferase; one that then oxidizes that methyl with O₂ is an oxidoreductase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** decide what changed (redox, group transfer, hydrolysis, double bond, rearrangement, joining), then name the class."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-23a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Faraday: moles from charge","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:23#1","pe3-cp:23#2","pe3-cp:23#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:23"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","q":"What mass of 1-phenylethanol was oxidized during the field reading?","choices":["31 µg","61 µg","122 µg","61 mg"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Q/F = 0.0965/96,500 = 1.0 µmol e⁻ → 0.50 µmol alcohol (2 e⁻ each) → × 122 µg/µmol."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the charge; sentence 4 gives **two electrons per alcohol**. mol e⁻ = Q/F = 0.0965 C ÷ 96,500 C/mol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol. mol alcohol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol. Mass = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol × 122 g/mol = 6.1 × 10⁻⁵ g = **61 µg**.","how":{"ask":"Turn the charge into moles of electrons, then into moles of alcohol, then into mass.","know":"• **mol e⁻ = Q/F** (F ≈ 96,500 C/mol).\n• Divide by the **electrons per molecule** to get moles of substance.\n• **g = mol × molar mass**; 1 g/mol = 1 µg/µmol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the electrode collects two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted.","hl":"collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted","calc":["2 e⁻ per molecule"]},{"text":"That is Faraday's law: charge counts electrons. Sentence 6 gives the charge.","hl":"0.0965 C of charge was collected","calc":["mol e⁻ = 0.0965 ÷ 96,500 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]},{"text":"Divide by the electrons per molecule, then multiply by the molar mass.","calc":["mol alcohol = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol","m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ × 122 = 6.1 × 10⁻⁵ g = **61 µg**"]},{"text":"Skipping the ÷2 doubles the mass; dividing by four halves it; milligrams is a thousand times too large.","strike":["122 µg","31 µg","61 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 61 µg."}],"trap":"Treating moles of electrons as moles of alcohol: each molecule gives two electrons, so there are half as many molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two electrons for every alcohol molecule","note":"÷ 2"},{"t":"0.0965 C","note":"Q"},{"t":"F = 96,500 C/mol","note":"C → mol e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Charge to electrons.**","calc":["n(e⁻) = Q/F"]},{"text":"**Electrons to molecules to grams.**","calc":["n = n(e⁻)/z","m = n × M"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the passage gives moles of electrons directly, skip Q/F and start at the ÷ z step."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass from charge = (Q/F) ÷ electrons per molecule × molar mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-24a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cofactor structures (FAD, NAD, heme)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:24#1","pe3-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:24","sample-cp:48","sample-cp:51"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P5","passage":"An amperometric sensor tracks 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), a solvent residue, in factory wastewater. On its electrode, an engineered oxidase converts the alcohol into its ketone, acetophenone, by removing two hydrogen atoms as two protons and two electrons. A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion, held tightly in the active site, accepts both hydrogens and then passes them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂; no other substrate is consumed. The electrode then oxidizes each H₂O₂, collecting two electrons for every alcohol molecule converted (F = 96,500 C/mol). To calibrate the sensor, a 6.0 µL drop of a 3.0 mM standard of the alcohol is placed on the electrode, and every alcohol molecule in the drop reacts. In one field reading, 0.0965 C of charge was collected.","q":"Which cofactor accepts the two hydrogens, and what happens to it?","choices":["FMN, which is reduced to FMNH₂","FMNH₂, which is oxidized to FMN","NAD⁺, which is reduced to NADH","Heme, whose iron is reduced"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Riboflavin-derived, tightly bound, carries 2 H → a flavin. No AMP portion → FMN. The acceptor is the oxidized form, so it gains the 2 H."},"exp":"Sentence 3 describes a **flavin** made from riboflavin but **lacking FAD's AMP portion**: **FMN**. Like FAD in glucose oxidase, it **accepts 2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)** and is **reduced to FMNH₂**; it then hands them to O₂ and returns to FMN.","how":{"ask":"Identify the bound cofactor from the clues, then decide whether taking the hydrogens reduces or oxidizes it.","know":"• **FAD** (glucose oxidase, succinate dehydrogenase) and **FMN** are flavins made from riboflavin; they are tightly bound and carry **2 H**: FAD → FADH₂, FMN → FMNH₂.\n• **NAD⁺/NADP⁺** are loosely bound cosubstrates that take a hydride (H⁻).\n• Gaining H (electrons) = **reduction**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the cofactor is riboflavin-derived, lacks FAD's AMP portion and is held tightly.","hl":"A riboflavin-derived cofactor that lacks FAD's AMP portion","calc":["→ FMN, a flavin"]},{"text":"NAD⁺ is not riboflavin-based and is not tightly bound; heme is an iron porphyrin.","strike":["NAD⁺, which is reduced to NADH","Heme, whose iron is reduced"]},{"text":"The acceptor gains the hydrogens, so it starts as FMN, not FMNH₂.","calc":["FMN + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → FMNH₂ (reduced)"],"strike":["FMNH₂, which is oxidized to FMN"]},{"text":"Answer: FMN, which is reduced to FMNH₂."}],"trap":"Picking the reduced form (FMNH₂ or FADH₂) as the acceptor: the acceptor has to start oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"riboflavin-derived","note":"a flavin"},{"t":"held tightly in the active site","note":"not NAD⁺"},{"t":"accepts both hydrogens","note":"gets reduced"}]},{"text":"**Flavins carry two hydrogens.**","calc":["FAD + 2 H → FADH₂","FMN + 2 H → FMNH₂"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase uses bound FAD in the same way: the flavin takes two hydrogens from glucose, is reduced, and O₂ then reoxidizes it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a bound flavin (FAD or FMN) accepts 2 H and is reduced to its H₂ form; O₂ or another acceptor reoxidizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-25a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"steroid 6-6-6-5 skeleton","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:25#1","pe3-cp:25#2","pe5-cp:21#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:25","pe5-cp:21","sample-cp:42"],"q":"A hormone enters its target cell and binds an intracellular steroid receptor. Which molecule has the ring skeleton of a steroid?","choices":["Estradiol","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Prostaglandin E₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Tyrosine-derived hormones aren't steroids","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Steroid = four fused rings, 6-6-6-5, like cholesterol. Sex hormones (estr-, andr-, -diol, -one) are steroids; catecholamines, thyroid hormones and eicosanoids aren't."},"exp":"A steroid is defined by its **6-6-6-5 fused ring core** (rings A, B and C of six atoms, ring D of five). Estradiol has it. Epinephrine has one benzene ring, thyroxine has two separate rings joined by an ether O, and prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two chains.","how":{"ask":"Look for four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five, in each molecule.","know":"• **Steroid skeleton**: four **fused** rings, **6-6-6-5** (rings A, B, C, D), 17 ring carbons, derived from cholesterol.\n• Fused = neighboring rings share a bond.\n• Rings joined by a chain or an O are not fused, and a single ring is never a steroid.","steps":[{"text":"A steroid receptor binds molecules built on the steroid core.","hl":"steroid receptor"},{"text":"Draw the core to search for: three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring, all fused.","fig":["t3-steroid-abcd","cholesterol"],"calc":["rings: 6 + 6 + 6 + 5","fused: each pair shares one bond"]},{"text":"Estradiol has rings A–D (ring A aromatic): the 6-6-6-5 core."},{"text":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine with one benzene ring; thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not fused.","strike":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]},{"text":"Prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two side chains, a fatty-acid derivative.","strike":["Prostaglandin E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: estradiol."}],"trap":"Choosing any lipid-soluble or ring-containing hormone: only the fused 6-6-6-5 skeleton makes a steroid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"steroid receptor","note":"needs a steroid"},{"t":"ring skeleton","note":"count and fuse the rings"}]},{"text":"**Know the core.** Four rings fused edge to edge: three six-membered (A, B, C) and one five-membered (D).","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd","calc":["6 + 6 + 6 + 5 = four rings, 17 ring C"]},{"text":"**Spot it in a drawing.** Find the five-membered ring, then check that it is fused to three six-membered rings in a row; extra groups (OH, C=O, a side chain) do not matter.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule out look-alikes.** One ring, rings joined by a linker, or no rings at all: not a steroid."},{"text":"**Also:** every steroid hormone (estradiol, cortisol, progesterone) is built from cholesterol, which already has the 6-6-6-5 core; the body shortens its 8-carbon side chain (to 2 carbons in progesterone and cortisol, none in estradiol) and keeps rings A–D. Asked for a steroid's precursor, choose the lipid drawn with those four rings, not a fatty acid or another open-chain lipid.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steroid has four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five; look for that 6-6-6-5 skeleton and ignore the groups on it."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-21a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-26a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:26#2","pe3-cp:26#3","pe4-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:26","sample-cp:57"],"q":"A carbonate buffer (HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3) reads pH 10.8. To bring it to the pH where its buffer capacity is greatest, which compound should be added, and why?","choices":["NaHCO₃, since base:acid must fall from 3.2 to 1.0","Na₂CO₃, since base:acid must fall from 3.2 to 1.0","NaHCO₃, since base:acid must rise from 0.32 to 1.0","NaOH, since base:acid must rise from 0.32 to 1.0"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"pH above pKa: base form dominates","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Capacity peaks at pH = pKa. pH 10.8 sits above 10.3, so there is too much base form: add the acid form, HCO₃⁻ (NaHCO₃), to pull pH down."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.040 M NaHCO₃ with 0.080 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"pH","min":9,"max":12,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1.5,9.35],[3,9.7],[6,10.12],[7.5,10.3],[9,10.48],[11.4,10.8],[13.5,11.25]]}]},"exp":"**pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**. At pH 10.8 the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2. Capacity is greatest at **pH = pKa**, where the ratio is 1, so the pH must fall: add more of the **acid form**, NaHCO₃.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the pH must rise or fall to reach pKa, find the base:acid ratio now and at pKa, then add the form that moves it that way.","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]).\n• A buffer works best at **pH = pKa** ([base] = [acid]).\n• To **lower** pH, raise the share of the **acid** form; to raise pH, add the base form.","steps":[{"text":"Read the buffer pair and its pKa.","hl":"HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3","calc":["acid = HCO₃⁻, base = CO₃²⁻"]},{"text":"Find the current base:acid ratio.","hl":"pH 10.8","calc":["10.8 = 10.3 + log(ratio)","log(ratio) = 0.5","ratio = 10^0.5 ≈ **3.2**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":11.4,"text":"now: pH 10.8","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Best capacity is at pH = pKa = 10.3, ratio 1. The ratio must fall, so the pH must drop by 0.5: add the acid form.","calc":["pH 10.8 → 10.3: add HCO₃⁻ (as NaHCO₃)"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"pH = pKa 10.3","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Na₂CO₃ and NaOH raise the base share and push the pH higher; a ratio below one would mean the pH is already under pKa.","strike":["Na₂CO₃, since base:acid must fall from 3.2 to 1.0","NaOH, since base:acid must rise from 0.32 to 1.0","NaHCO₃, since base:acid must rise from 0.32 to 1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: NaHCO₃, since base:acid must fall from 3.2 to 1.0."}],"trap":"Adding the base form because the pH must \"reach\" pKa: here the buffer is above its pKa, so it needs more acid form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 10.3","note":"best buffering pH"},{"t":"pH 10.8","note":"current pH, above pKa"},{"t":"buffer capacity is greatest","note":"pH = pKa"}]},{"text":"**Write Henderson–Hasselbalch.** It links pH to the base:acid ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])","10.8 − 10.3 = 0.5 → ratio ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Aim for pH = pKa.** There the ratio is 1, and the buffer resists added acid and base equally well."},{"text":"**Lower pH = more acid form.** The pH is too high, so raise [HCO₃⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** for a phosphate buffer, the acid form is H₂PO₄⁻ (the monosodium salt, NaH₂PO₄); adding it lowers the pH, while Na₂HPO₄ raises it."},{"text":"**Also:** a buffer works only within pKa ± 1, where the base:acid ratio stays between one-tenth and ten; check each pH against that window.","calc":["pKa 10.3 → useful from pH 9.3 to 11.3"]},{"text":"**Also:** write Henderson–Hasselbalch with the two forms named before plugging in: the conjugate base (A⁻, the salt form) goes on top and the weak acid (HA) on the bottom. Then undo the log to get the ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","phosphate, pKa 7.2, target pH 6.9: log ratio = −0.3","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^−0.3 ≈ 0.5, i.e. 1 : 2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]); to lower the pH add the acid form, to raise it add the base form, and capacity peaks at pH = pKa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-27a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"peptide bond = amide + water","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2","pe3-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:27"],"q":"Glycine ethyl ester (103 Da) reacts with leucine (131 Da), forming one peptide bond and the dipeptide shown, 188 Da. What mass is released?","choices":["18 Da","29 Da","46 Da","234 Da"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"A dipeptide keeps most reactant mass","c":[3]},{"f":"The ester oxygen leaves too","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Lost mass = (103 + 131) − 188. Then check that it matches a small molecule you know."},"fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites","exp":"Here glycine's carbonyl starts as an **ethyl ester**, so leucine's amine attacks the ester C=O and the leaving group is **–OCH₂CH₃**; it takes one H from the amine and leaves as **ethanol**, not water. Check: 103 + 131 = 234; 234 − 188 = **46 Da**, the mass of one CH₃CH₂OH.","how":{"ask":"Find the mass lost when the ester and the amino acid join, and name what left.","know":"• A peptide bond links the **carbonyl C** of one residue to the **N** of the next.\n• From a free acid the bond releases **H₂O** (18 Da); from an ester, the amine displaces the **alcohol** instead.\n• Lost mass = sum of the reactant masses − product mass.","steps":[{"text":"Add the two reactant masses.","hl":"Glycine ethyl ester (103 Da) reacts with leucine (131 Da)","calc":["103 + 131 = 234 Da"]},{"text":"Subtract the dipeptide's mass.","hl":"188 Da","calc":["234 − 188 = **46 Da** = CH₃CH₂OH"]},{"text":"234 Da is the starting total; 18 Da assumes water leaves, but the carbonyl carries OCH₂CH₃, not OH; 29 Da leaves the ester O behind.","strike":["234 Da","18 Da","29 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 46 Da."}],"trap":"Assuming every peptide bond releases water: the molecule that leaves is the carbonyl's leaving group plus an amine H, here ethanol from the ethyl ester."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Glycine ethyl ester (103 Da)","note":"acyl donor, an ester"},{"t":"leucine (131 Da)","note":"amine partner"},{"t":"188 Da","note":"after joining"}]},{"text":"**Aminolysis.** The bond forms between glycine's carbonyl C and leucine's N; the ester's –OCH₂CH₃ and an amine H leave together.","fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** lost mass = reactants − product; a free acid releases water (18 Da), an ester releases its alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-28a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Michaelis–Menten assumptions","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:28#1","pe3-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:28#3","pe3-cp:28#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:28"],"q":"A student is planning Michaelis–Menten measurements on a purified enzyme. Which condition is NOT required for valid data?","choices":["Initial rates measured","Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate","Reaction run to equilibrium"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Michaelis–Menten uses initial rates","c":[0]},{"f":"Michaelis–Menten assumes [E] ≪ [S]","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Picture the end point: at equilibrium [S] has dropped and the net rate is zero, so no v₀ is left to measure. The other three are the standard set-up; equilibrium is the odd one."},"exp":"Michaelis–Menten analysis needs **initial rates (v₀)** under **steady state**, a **constant pH**, and **[E] ≪ [S]** so [S] stays at its set value. Letting the reaction reach **equilibrium** is not wanted: by then [S] has fallen, product has built up and the net rate is zero.","how":{"ask":"Check each condition against the assumptions behind v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).","know":"• **v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** assumes **initial rates** (little product, [S] ≈ starting value) and a **steady state** of ES.\n• **Constant pH** (a buffer) keeps the enzyme's charges and activity fixed.\n• **[E] ≪ [S]** so binding barely depletes free substrate.\n• At **equilibrium** the net rate is zero, so it gives no v₀.","steps":[{"text":"This is a NOT question: find the condition the analysis does not want.","hl":"NOT required"},{"text":"Initial rates are the data Michaelis–Menten uses: the slope at the start of the product-versus-time curve.","strike":["Initial rates measured"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed over time in one enzyme run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Product (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,22],[5,46],[10,68],[15,80],[20,86],[25,88],[30,89]]}]}},{"text":"A buffered pH holds the enzyme's ionizable groups steady, and [E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value.","strike":["Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the curve is flat: net rate zero, [S] no longer the starting value. That is not wanted."},{"text":"Answer: reaction run to equilibrium."}],"trap":"Thinking a reaction must be “complete” to be measured: Michaelis–Menten uses only the first, straight part of the curve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten","note":"uses v₀"},{"t":"NOT required","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Initial rates, steady state.** Measure v₀ while [ES] is steady and little product has formed; later the curve bends over.","calc":["v₀ = slope at t ≈ 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep the pH constant with a buffer; a changing pH changes the enzyme's activity during the run."},{"text":"**Also:** keep [E] much less than [S], so the enzyme does not tie up a significant share of the substrate.","calc":["[E] ≪ [S] → free [S] ≈ total [S]"]},{"text":"**Also:** letting the reaction reach equilibrium is not wanted: the net rate there is zero and [S] has changed, so rates must be initial."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** valid Michaelis–Menten data use initial rates at steady state, constant pH and [E] ≪ [S]; never wait for equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-29a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"net reaction from mechanism steps","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:29"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which equation is the balanced net reaction of enzyme Z's two-step cycle?","choices":["NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ + FMN → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O + FMNH₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Regenerated cofactors cancel in net reactions","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Net equations balance charge","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cross out what the cycle regenerates (E–FMN and E–FMNH₂); everything left over is the net reaction."},"exp":"Sentences 2 and 3 give the two steps. **Adding** them puts E–FMN and E–FMNH₂ on **both sides**, so they **cancel**, as a regenerated cofactor should. What remains: **NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O**, balanced in atoms and charge (+1 on each side).","how":{"ask":"Add the two steps, cancel what is regenerated, and check atoms and charge.","know":"• A **catalyst or bound cofactor** that is regenerated cancels from the net equation.\n• **Add** the steps; **cancel** species found on both sides.\n• Check **atoms** and **charge**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the first step, in which NADH reduces the bound flavin.","hl":"E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺"},{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the second step, which returns the enzyme to E–FMN; add the two.","hl":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","calc":["E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ + E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺ + E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Cancel the enzyme forms on both sides.","calc":["net: NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O","charge: +1 = +1 ✓"]},{"text":"The step alone is not the net reaction; leaving FMN and FMNH₂ in skips the cancelling; dropping H⁺ breaks the charge balance.","strike":["E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ + FMN → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O + FMNH₂","NADH + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Answer: NADH + H⁺ + O₂ + RNH₂ → NAD⁺ + RNHOH + H₂O."}],"trap":"Keeping the cofactor in the net equation: a species made in one step and used in the next is regenerated and cancels."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"tightly bound flavin","note":"regenerated each cycle"},{"t":"E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺","note":"step one"},{"t":"E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O","note":"step two"}]},{"text":"**Add, then cancel.** Anything on both sides, such as the enzyme's two flavin forms, drops out."},{"text":"**Check.** Count each element and the total charge on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalytic cycle's net equation is the sum of its steps with every regenerated species cancelled."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-30a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"identify functional group","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:30"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Which structure shows the hydroxamic acid formed by the second enzyme?","choices":["HC(=O)–N(OH)–R","HC(=O)–O–NH–R","HC(=O)–NH–R","HC(=O)–N(OCHO)–R"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Formyl bonds N, not O","c":[1]},{"f":"Hydroxamic acids keep the N–OH","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxamic acid = C(=O) on N, and that N keeps its OH. Find the choice with C(=O)–N and N–OH together."},"exp":"Sentence 3 makes the hydroxylamine RNHOH, and sentence 4 puts the formyl group on **that same nitrogen**. The product is an **amide whose nitrogen keeps its OH**: **HC(=O)–N(OH)–R**, a hydroxamic acid whose C=O and N–OH oxygens bind Fe³⁺.","how":{"ask":"Put the acyl group on the hydroxylamine's nitrogen and keep the N–OH.","know":"• **Hydroxamic acid**: R′–C(=O)–N(OH)–R, an amide with an OH on the nitrogen.\n• **Amide**: C(=O)–N; **ester**: C(=O)–O.\n• Both oxygens (C=O and N–OH) chelate metal ions such as Fe³⁺.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the formyl group is attached to the same nitrogen that sentence 3 hydroxylated.","hl":"attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen","calc":["RNHOH + formyl on N → HC(=O)–N(OH)–R"]},{"text":"That is the hydroxamic acid pattern: an amide nitrogen that also carries OH."},{"text":"Acyl on O makes an ester; acyl on N with no OH is an ordinary amide; formylating the OH too removes the N–OH.","strike":["HC(=O)–O–NH–R","HC(=O)–NH–R","HC(=O)–N(OCHO)–R"]},{"text":"Answer: HC(=O)–N(OH)–R."}],"trap":"Putting the acyl group on the oxygen: the passage says it goes on the nitrogen, which keeps its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"RNHOH","note":"hydroxylamine"},{"t":"to that same nitrogen","note":"N-acylation"},{"t":"grips Fe³⁺","note":"C=O and N–OH bind"}]},{"text":"**Name the pattern.**","calc":["amide: C(=O)–N","hydroxamic acid: C(=O)–N–OH"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydroxamic acid is an amide whose nitrogen also bears an OH, R′C(=O)N(OH)R."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-31a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"rate comparison from rate constants","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:31"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"Under the assay conditions, how many times faster is hydroxylation than the side reaction that releases H₂O₂?","choices":["0.17","5.5","6.0","7.3"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"6.6/1.1: 1.1 × 6 = 6.6 exactly."},"exp":"Sentence 7 says both reactions draw on the **same supply** of reduced enzyme, so each rate = k × [E–FMNH₂] and the concentration cancels. **Ratio = k(hydroxylation)/k(side)** = 6.6 s⁻¹ ÷ 1.1 s⁻¹ = **6.0**.","how":{"ask":"Divide the hydroxylation rate constant by the side-reaction rate constant.","know":"• **Rate = k × [reactant]**.\n• With the **same** reactant supply, the concentration cancels: **rate ratio = k ratio**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives both rate constants and says both reactions draw on the same reduced enzyme.","hl":"the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹","calc":["k₁ = 6.6 s⁻¹, k₂ = 1.1 s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Same supply means the concentration cancels in the ratio of rates.","calc":["rate₁/rate₂ = k₁[E]/k₂[E] = k₁/k₂"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["6.6 ÷ 1.1 = **6.0**"]},{"text":"The inverse ratio is upside down; a difference or a product is not a ratio of rates.","strike":["0.17","5.5","7.3"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0."}],"trap":"Dividing the smaller constant by the larger: \"how many times faster\" puts the faster reaction on top."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"6.6 s⁻¹","note":"hydroxylation"},{"t":"1.1 s⁻¹","note":"side reaction"},{"t":"both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme","note":"concentration cancels"}]},{"text":"**Ratios cancel shared terms.**","calc":["rate₁/rate₂ = k₁/k₂ when [shared reactant] is equal"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when two reactions use the same reactant at the same concentration, their rates compare as their rate constants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-33a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain classes","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:33#1","pe3-cp:33#2","pe3-cp:33#3","pe3-cp:33#4","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2","pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:33","pe4-cp:36","pe5-cp:42","sample-cp:50"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"In enzyme Z, the pair of residues that could hold the substrate's carboxylate is:","choices":["Arginine and histidine","Aspartate and glutamate","Leucine and valine","Serine and threonine"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Asp, Glu are negative, repel COO⁻","c":[1]},{"f":"Leu, Val have nonpolar side chains","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Basic side chains = the three that take H⁺: Lys (amine), Arg (guanidino), His (imidazole). Only one pair is drawn from those three."},"exp":"Sentence 5 says the two residues are **basic and hydrophilic**, as needed to pair with a COO⁻. **Arginine** (guanidino) and **histidine** (imidazole) have nitrogen side chains that take protons, as does lysine. Aspartate and glutamate are acidic; leucine and valine are nonpolar; serine and threonine are polar but neutral.","how":{"ask":"Sort each amino acid by its side chain: basic, acidic, polar neutral or nonpolar.","know":"• **Basic, hydrophilic**: Lys, Arg, His (N-containing side chains that take H⁺).\n• **Acidic**: Asp, Glu (side-chain COOH/COO⁻).\n• **Polar, neutral**: Ser, Thr (OH), Asn, Gln (amide).\n• **Nonpolar**: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe (hydrocarbon side chains).\n• Arg's guanidino side chain (pKa ≈ 12.5) stays protonated at ordinary pH, so free Arg is **+1** overall near pH 7–9.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the carboxylate is held by residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic.","hl":"two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic"},{"text":"Basic side chains contain nitrogen that takes H⁺: arginine's guanidino group and histidine's imidazole both qualify.","fig":"arginine"},{"text":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxylates, so they are acidic.","strike":["Aspartate and glutamate"]},{"text":"Leucine and valine are hydrocarbons (nonpolar); serine and threonine carry a neutral OH (polar, not basic).","strike":["Leucine and valine","Serine and threonine"]},{"text":"Also check arginine's charge in the buffer: compare the pH with each pKa.","calc":["α-COOH (≈2): pH above → COO⁻, −1","α-NH₂ (≈9): pH just below → mostly NH₃⁺, +1","guanidino (≈12.5): pH below → protonated, +1","net ≈ **+1**"]},{"text":"Answer: arginine and histidine."}],"trap":"Calling histidine nonpolar because of its ring: its imidazole nitrogens take H⁺, so it is grouped with the basic amino acids."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"basic and hydrophilic","note":"N-containing side chains"},{"t":"the substrate's carboxylate","note":"negative: needs a + partner"}]},{"text":"**Sort by side chain.**","calc":["basic: Lys, Arg, His","acidic: Asp, Glu","polar neutral: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln","nonpolar: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to pair with a negative group, look for the basic residues (Lys, Arg, His), whose nitrogen side chains carry + charge."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe4-cp:36","pe5-cp:42"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe4-36a","cpt-pe5-42a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"[H⁺]/[OH⁻] from pH (10^−pH, ΔpH factor)","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:34"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P6","passage":"Soil bacteria build iron-binding hydroxamic acids from 5-aminopentanoate, H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻, whose amino end is written here as RNH₂. Enzyme Z holds a tightly bound flavin, FMN, and first takes a hydride and a proton from NADH: E–FMN + NADH + H⁺ → E–FMNH₂ + NAD⁺. The reduced enzyme then uses O₂ to hydroxylate the substrate's nitrogen: E–FMNH₂ + O₂ + RNH₂ → E–FMN + RNHOH + H₂O. A second enzyme attaches a formyl group, HC(=O)–, to that same nitrogen, giving the hydroxamic acid that grips Fe³⁺. In enzyme Z's active site, the substrate's carboxylate is held by two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic. The enzyme is assayed in a borate buffer at pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14. Under these conditions, the rate constant for hydroxylation is 6.6 s⁻¹, while a wasteful side reaction that releases H₂O₂ instead has a rate constant of 1.1 s⁻¹; both draw on the same supply of reduced enzyme.","q":"What is [OH⁻] in the assay buffer?","choices":["2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M","5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M","5.3 × 10⁻⁶ M","8.7 × 10⁻⁶ M"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"pOH = 5.3 → 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶, and 10^0.7 ≈ 5 (log 5 ≈ 0.70)."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives **pH 8.7** and pH + pOH = 14, so **pOH = 5.3**. [OH⁻] = 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M**. [H⁺] = 10^−8.7 ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M is much smaller, as expected in a basic buffer.","how":{"ask":"Turn the pH into pOH, then into [OH⁻].","know":"• **pH + pOH = 14** at 25 °C.\n• **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH**; **[H⁺] = 10^−pH**.\n• log 2 ≈ 0.30, log 5 ≈ 0.70.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the buffer's pH and the relation to pOH.","hl":"pH 8.7 and 25 °C, where pH + pOH = 14","calc":["pOH = 14 − 8.7 = 5.3"]},{"text":"Convert pOH to concentration.","calc":["[OH⁻] = 10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M**"]},{"text":"10^−8.7 is [H⁺], not [OH⁻]; 5.3 and 8.7 are the pOH and pH themselves, not mantissas.","strike":["2.0 × 10⁻⁹ M","5.3 × 10⁻⁶ M","8.7 × 10⁻⁶ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ M."}],"trap":"Using 10^−pH for hydroxide: that gives [H⁺]."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 8.7","note":"basic buffer"},{"t":"pH + pOH = 14","note":"get pOH"}]},{"text":"**pH to pOH to [OH⁻].**","calc":["pOH = 14 − pH","[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH"]},{"text":"**Split fractional exponents.**","calc":["10^−5.3 = 10^0.7 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁶"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [OH⁻] = 10^−(14 − pH); write the exponent as a whole number plus a fraction to estimate the mantissa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-35a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"H-atom abstraction → radical","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:35#1","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:35"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"In the first experiment, what is the product when one catalytic step acts on 2-mercaptoethanol?","choices":["A thiyl radical, HOCH₂CH₂S•","A radical cation, HOCH₂CH₂SH•⁺","An alkoxyl radical, •OCH₂CH₂SH","A carbon radical, HOCH₂CH•SH"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Only the S–H bond breaks","c":[2,3]},{"f":"H-atom loss leaves a neutral radical","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H atom = H⁺ + e⁻, so the product is neutral with an unpaired electron on the atom that lost H. Only S–H is allowed here."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says each step takes **a whole hydrogen atom (H⁺ + e⁻)** off an **S–H**, and sentence 4 rules out O–H and C–H. So 2-mercaptoethanol loses the H on sulfur and keeps its electron count one short: a neutral **thiyl radical, HOCH₂CH₂S•**. Two of these then join into the S–S cross-link. Classic peroxidases do the same to phenols, making phenoxyl radicals.","how":{"ask":"Find which X–H the enzyme can break, then remove a whole hydrogen atom from it.","know":"• **H atom = H⁺ + e⁻**: removing it leaves a **neutral radical** (X•).\n• Removing **H⁺ only** leaves an **anion** (X⁻); removing **e⁻ only** leaves a radical cation.\n• Peroxidases usually take H• from **phenols** (Ar–OH → Ar–O•); among side chains that is **tyrosine**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says each step removes a hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group.","hl":"removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group","calc":["R–S–H − (H⁺ + e⁻) → R–S•"]},{"text":"That is H-atom abstraction, which leaves a neutral radical. Sentence 4 limits it to S–H.","hl":"cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds"},{"text":"Apply it to 2-mercaptoethanol: only the sulfur's H is taken.","calc":["HS–CH₂CH₂–OH → **HOCH₂CH₂S•** (thiyl radical)"]},{"text":"The radical cation lost only an electron; the oxygen and carbon radicals need O–H or C–H abstraction, which the enzyme cannot do.","strike":["A radical cation, HOCH₂CH₂SH•⁺","An alkoxyl radical, •OCH₂CH₂SH","A carbon radical, HOCH₂CH•SH"]},{"text":"Answer: a thiyl radical, HOCH₂CH₂S•."}],"trap":"Choosing an oxygen or carbon radical because the molecule has O–H and C–H bonds: this enzyme can break only the S–H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a proton plus an electron","note":"a whole H atom"},{"t":"from an S–H group","note":"where the radical forms"},{"t":"both an S–H and an O–H group","note":"only S–H reacts"}]},{"text":"**H atom vs proton.**","calc":["X–H − H⁺ → X⁻","X–H − H• → X•"]},{"text":"**Also:** a classic peroxidase takes H• from a phenol, Ar–OH → Ar–O•; among side chains only tyrosine is a phenol (lysine is an amine, leucine an alkyl, glutamine an amide).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** two radicals pair their unpaired electrons to form a new bond: two R–S• make R–S–S–R; two phenoxyl radicals make a C–C or C–O link.","fig":["lysine-sc","leucine-sc","glutamine-sc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme that removes H• (H⁺ + e⁻) leaves a neutral radical on the atom that held the H; check which X–H it is allowed to break."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-37a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"metal ion coordination","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:42#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:37"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","fig":"imidazole","q":"If a mutation replaced the loop's asparagine with histidine and the loop then bound Zn²⁺, which side-chain atom would best bind the metal?","choices":["The ring N without H","The ring N bearing H","The C between the ring N's","The β-carbon"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Metals bind lone pairs, not carbons","c":[2,3]},{"f":"The N–H pair is aromatic","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Zn²⁺ binds a free lone pair. In histidine's ring, only one N has a lone pair pointing out of the ring."},"exp":"The stem swaps the ligand: a histidine side chain, –CH₂–imidazole, now holds Zn²⁺. Zn²⁺ binds **nitrogen** and sulfur lone pairs, such as the imidazole N of His and the thiolate S of Cys. In the neutral ring, the N bearing H puts its lone pair into the aromatic sextet, so the **ring N without H** keeps an in-plane lone pair pointing out and is the donor.","how":{"ask":"Find the ring atom with a lone pair that is free to point at the metal.","know":"• **Zn²⁺ binds N and S donors**: the imidazole N of His and the thiolate S of Cys, plus carboxylate O.\n• In imidazole, the N–H nitrogen's lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet; the other ring N's lone pair lies in the ring plane, free to donate.\n• Carbon atoms have no lone pair to give a metal.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says a side chain in the loop is one of the metal's ligands, so it donates a lone pair.","hl":"is one of the ligands"},{"text":"The stem swaps that asparagine for histidine and the metal for Zn²⁺.","hl":"replaced the loop's asparagine with histidine","calc":["His side chain: –CH₂–imidazole (2 ring N, 3 ring C)"]},{"text":"A 2+ cation needs an atom with a free lone pair."},{"text":"Carbons have no lone pair to give.","strike":["The β-carbon","The C between the ring N's"]},{"text":"The N bearing H uses its lone pair in the aromatic sextet; the other ring N keeps its lone pair in the ring plane.","strike":["The ring N bearing H"]},{"text":"Answer: the ring N without H."}],"trap":"Picking the N–H nitrogen because both are nitrogens: its lone pair is part of the aromatic ring, so donating it would break aromaticity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the stem and the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"replaced the loop's asparagine with histidine","note":"new ligand"},{"t":"bound Zn²⁺","note":"coordination"},{"t":"is one of the ligands","note":"donates a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Two ring nitrogens.** One carries H and uses its lone pair in the ring; the other has a lone pair in the ring plane.","fig":"imidazole"},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the pH protonates that free ring N (pKa ≈ 6) and releases the metal, just as protonating aspartate or glutamate carboxylates releases Ca²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal cations bind lone pairs that are free to donate; in histidine that is the ring N without H, not the N–H nitrogen."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-38a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cofactor structures (FAD, NAD, heme)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:38#1","pe3-cp:38#2","pe3-cp:38#3","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3","pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:38"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"Which ring is the building block of the enzyme's porphyrin?","choices":["Pyrrole","Imidazole","Pyridine","Pyrimidine"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Porphyrin units are five-membered rings","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Porphyrin = 4 pyrroles. Ring key: pyrrole 5/1 N, imidazole 5/2 N, pyridine 6/1 N, pyrimidine 6/2 N. So five-membered, one N."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the porphyrin is four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each giving one N to the iron. Those rings are **pyrroles**, and a pyrrole is **five-membered with one N**. The five-membered ring with two N is imidazole (histidine's side chain, which binds the heme iron from below); six-membered with one N is pyridine, and with two N a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"Name the unit heme is made of, then describe that ring by its size and its count of nitrogens.","know":"• **Heme** = a porphyrin ring (four **pyrrole** units joined by =CH– bridges) with Fe²⁺ held by the four inward-facing N.\n• **Pyrrole**: 5 ring atoms, **1 N** (an N–H).\n• **Imidazole**: 5 ring atoms, **2 N**; the side chain of **His**, whose free N binds the heme Fe.\n• **Pyridine**: 6 ring atoms, 1 N. **Pyrimidine**: 6 ring atoms, 2 N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the porphyrin is built from four identical rings, each pointing one nitrogen at the iron.","hl":"four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges"},{"text":"That unit is pyrrole. Count its ring atoms and its nitrogens.","fig":"pyrrole","calc":["ring atoms: 4 C + 1 N = **5**","nitrogens: **1** (N–H)"]},{"text":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring. It is not part of the porphyrin; it is the residue whose free N lone pair binds the heme iron from below.","fig":["imidazole","t3-his-ring"],"strike":["Imidazole"]},{"text":"Six-membered rings are pyridine (one N) and pyrimidine (two N); neither builds a porphyrin.","fig":"pyridine","strike":["Pyridine","Pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: pyrrole (five-membered, one N)."}],"trap":"Picking imidazole because histidine is drawn next to heme: His only binds the iron; the ring itself is made of pyrroles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"held in a porphyrin","note":"the ring of heme"},{"t":"four identical aromatic rings","note":"the repeating unit"},{"t":"each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron","note":"one N per ring"}]},{"text":"**Heme = porphyrin + Fe.** The porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon bridges, with their four N pointing at the central iron."},{"text":"**Tell the rings apart by size and N count.**","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine"],"calc":["pyrrole: 5 atoms, 1 N","imidazole: 5 atoms, 2 N","pyridine: 6 atoms, 1 N"]},{"text":"**Also:** imidazole (two N) is the ring on histidine's R group; in heme proteins, His binds the heme iron through its N that carries a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**Also:** to name any ring, count its atoms first (5 or 6), then count the nitrogens (1 or 2)."},{"text":"**Also:** to write a ring's formula, give every ring atom its full set of bonds: pyrrole has four C–H and one N–H, so it is C₄H₅N; imidazole is C₃H₄N₂ and pyridine C₅H₅N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heme's porphyrin is four pyrroles (5 atoms, 1 N); imidazole (5 atoms, 2 N) is His, which binds the iron; pyridine has 6 atoms and 1 N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-39a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"resistivity = 1/conductivity","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:39"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P7","passage":"Tube-dwelling marine worms harden their protein tubes with a heme peroxidase that uses H₂O₂ to cross-link cysteine side chains. The enzyme's heme is an iron ion held in a porphyrin, which is built from four identical aromatic rings joined by one-carbon bridges, each ring pointing one nitrogen at the iron. In each catalytic step the enzyme removes one hydrogen atom, a proton plus an electron, from an S–H group; two of the resulting species then join to form an S–S cross-link. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that carries no hydrogen. It is active only when Ca²⁺ is bound in a loop of the protein, where an asparagine side chain, –CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂, is one of the ligands. In a first experiment, the purified enzyme was tested with 2-mercaptoethanol, HS–CH₂CH₂–OH, a small molecule with both an S–H and an O–H group. In a second experiment, researchers measured electrical conductivity at 20 °C: the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹, and the hardened tube wall at 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹.","q":"From the second experiment, what is the resistivity of the worm's body fluid?","choices":["2.5 Ω·cm","25 Ω·cm","4.0 × 10⁻² Ω·cm","2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]},{"f":"Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"(Ω·cm)⁻¹ → Ω·cm means take 1/x: 1/(4.0 × 10⁻²) = 100/4."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives the body fluid's **conductivity**, σ = 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹. **Resistivity is its reciprocal**: ρ = 1/σ = 1/(0.040 (Ω·cm)⁻¹) = **25 Ω·cm**. The tube wall, with far lower conductivity, has a far higher resistivity, 2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm.","how":{"ask":"Pick the body fluid's conductivity and take its reciprocal.","know":"• **ρ = 1/σ**: resistivity (Ω·cm) is the reciprocal of conductivity ((Ω·cm)⁻¹).\n• High conductivity ↔ low resistivity.\n• R = ρL/A for a sample of length L and area A.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the body fluid's conductivity, along with the tube wall's.","hl":"the worm's body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹","calc":["σ(fluid) = 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹"]},{"text":"Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity; the units flip too.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ = 1/0.040 = **25 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"Keeping the conductivity's number is not a reciprocal; 2.0 × 10⁵ belongs to the tube wall; 2.5 slips a factor of ten.","strike":["4.0 × 10⁻² Ω·cm","2.0 × 10⁵ Ω·cm","2.5 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 25 Ω·cm."}],"trap":"Taking the reciprocal of the wrong medium's value: read which material the question names."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"body fluid conducts at 4.0 × 10⁻² (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"σ of the fluid"},{"t":"the hardened tube wall","note":"the other medium"}]},{"text":"**Flip it.**","calc":["ρ = 1/σ; (Ω·cm)⁻¹ → Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Also:** ionic fluids conduct well (low ρ); dry, cross-linked protein conducts poorly (high ρ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** resistivity is one over conductivity; choose the value for the material asked about, then invert it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:40"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"In the phage pathway, what classes are enzyme X and enzyme Y?","choices":["Oxidoreductase; lyase","Transferase; lyase","Oxidoreductase; isomerase","Lyase; oxidoreductase"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Adding O to CH₃ is oxidation","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Isomerases keep every atom","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"O₂ consumed → redox class. A carbon leaves as a small molecule with no water and no redox → lyase."},"fig":["thymine","hydroxymethyluracil","uracil"],"exp":"Sentence 3 describes both steps. **Enzyme X** uses **O₂** to turn CH₃ into CH₂OH: an oxidation, so an **oxidoreductase**. **Enzyme Y** cuts off that carbon as formaldehyde **with no water added and no redox change**: breaking a C–C bond that way is the work of a **lyase**.","how":{"ask":"Classify each step by what changes: redox, group transfer, hydrolysis, or removal without water.","know":"• **Oxidoreductase**: oxidation–reduction (O₂ used, O added, H removed).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (methyl, phosphate) from a donor to the substrate.\n• **Hydrolase**: breaks a bond by adding water.\n• **Lyase**: breaks a bond without water or redox, releasing a group or forming a double bond.\n• **Isomerase**: same formula, atoms rearranged.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says enzyme X uses O₂ to turn thymine's methyl into CH₂OH.","hl":"enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH","calc":["–CH₃ + [O] → –CH₂OH: oxidation"]},{"text":"A reaction that consumes O₂ and adds an oxygen is redox, so enzyme X is an oxidoreductase."},{"text":"Sentence 3 also says enzyme Y releases the CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde with no water and no change in oxidation state.","hl":"with no water added and no change in oxidation state","calc":["5-hydroxymethyluracil → uracil + HCHO: lyase"]},{"text":"The order is fixed by the pathway; no donor group is moved; and losing a carbon changes the formula.","strike":["Lyase; oxidoreductase","Transferase; lyase","Oxidoreductase; isomerase"]},{"text":"Answer: oxidoreductase, then lyase."}],"trap":"Calling enzyme Y a hydrolase because a bond breaks: hydrolysis needs water, and the passage rules water out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"uses O₂ to oxidize","note":"oxidoreductase"},{"t":"releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde","note":"group removed"},{"t":"no water added and no change in oxidation state","note":"not hydrolase, not redox"}]},{"text":"**One question per step:** did electrons move, did a group move between molecules, did water split a bond, or did a group simply leave?"},{"text":"**Also:** adding a methyl group from a donor such as SAM, as when uracil becomes thymine, is a transferase step; oxidizing that methyl with O₂ is an oxidoreductase step.","fig":["uracil","thymine"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** O₂-dependent oxidation → oxidoreductase; group lost without water or redox → lyase; group moved from a donor → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-41a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ΔG° = −RT ln K","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:41"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"For the unfolding of Strand Q (F ⇌ U) at pH 6.4 and 37 °C, which expression gives ΔG°′, and what is its value?","choices":["−RT ln [U]/[F]; zero","−RT ln [U]/[F]; positive","+RT ln [U]/[F]; negative","−RT e^([U]/[F]); zero"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"ΔG° is −RT ln K","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Half unfolded means K = 1, and ln 1 = 0, so ΔG°′ = 0 whatever the temperature."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says that at pH 6.4 **exactly half** of Strand Q is unfolded, so **K = [U]/[F] = 1**. With **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K**, ln 1 = 0, so **ΔG°′ = 0**: at the transition midpoint neither form is favored.","how":{"ask":"Write K for unfolding from the passage's equilibrium data, then use ΔG°′ = −RT ln K.","know":"• For F ⇌ U: **K = [U]/[F]** (products over reactants).\n• **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** (natural log).\n• K > 1 → ΔG°′ < 0; K < 1 → ΔG°′ > 0; **K = 1 → ΔG°′ = 0**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium under these conditions.","hl":"exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium","calc":["[U] = [F]"]},{"text":"Write K for the reaction as written, then apply the free-energy relation.","calc":["K = [U]/[F] = 1","ΔG°′ = −RT ln 1 = **0**"]},{"text":"e^K is not the relation; a positive sign or a negative sign would need K below or above 1.","strike":["−RT e^([U]/[F]); zero","−RT ln [U]/[F]; positive","+RT ln [U]/[F]; negative"]},{"text":"Answer: −RT ln [U]/[F]; zero."}],"trap":"Assuming an unfolding ΔG°′ is always positive: its sign depends on K, and at the midpoint K = 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded","note":"K = 1"},{"t":"the pH at which half the molecules are folded","note":"the midpoint"}]},{"text":"**K from the data.**","calc":["K = [U]/[F] = fraction unfolded ÷ fraction folded"]},{"text":"**Sign from K.**","calc":["ln K > 0 → ΔG°′ < 0","ln K < 0 → ΔG°′ > 0","ln 1 = 0 → ΔG°′ = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** below the midpoint pH most molecules are folded, K < 1, so ΔG°′ for unfolding is positive; above it, negative."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG°′ = −RT ln K with K = [U]/[F] for unfolding; at the transition midpoint K = 1 and ΔG°′ = 0."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-42a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"unfolding curve cooperativity","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:42#1","pe3-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:42"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"Which strand unfolds more cooperatively, and what is its pK?","choices":["Q; pK 6.4","Q2; pK 6.9","Q; pK 6.9","Q2; pK 6.4"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Narrower, steeper transition means more cooperative","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare widths: 6.2–6.6 is 0.4 unit; 5.7–8.1 is 2.4 units. Narrower = more cooperative."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the transition widths: Strand Q unfolds **between pH 6.2 and 6.6** (0.4 unit), Strand Q2 **between 5.7 and 8.1** (2.4 units). A **narrow, steep** transition means the molecule unfolds all at once: **cooperative**. Strand Q's own midpoint is **pK 6.4**.","how":{"ask":"Compare the widths of the two transitions, then read that strand's midpoint.","know":"• **Cooperative** unfolding: all-or-none, so the curve drops over a **narrow** range.\n• Splitting a structure into separate units makes unfolding **less cooperative**: a broad curve.\n• **pK** (or Tm) = the midpoint, where half are folded.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says Strand Q unfolds sharply over a narrow pH range and Strand Q2 gradually over a wide one.","hl":"Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6","calc":["Q: 6.6 − 6.2 = 0.4 unit","Q2: 8.1 − 5.7 = 2.4 units"]},{"text":"A narrow transition means cooperative unfolding; the extra loops in Q2 let its tracts unfold separately.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction folded of Strands Q and Q2 versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Strand Q","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.2,0.95],[6.4,0.5],[6.6,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Strand Q2","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.7,0.95],[6.3,0.8],[6.9,0.5],[7.5,0.2],[8.1,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"Read Strand Q's midpoint from sentence 5.","hl":"is 6.4 for Strand Q","calc":["Q: pK 6.4"]},{"text":"Q2 is the broad, less cooperative strand, and 6.9 is its midpoint, not Q's.","strike":["Q2; pK 6.9","Q2; pK 6.4","Q; pK 6.9"]},{"text":"Answer: Q; pK 6.4."}],"trap":"Calling the strand with the higher midpoint more cooperative: cooperativity is about the steepness of the curve, not where it sits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"split into three short tracts by extra loops","note":"units unfold separately"},{"t":"unfolded sharply","note":"cooperative"},{"t":"unfolded gradually","note":"less cooperative"}]},{"text":"**Width = cooperativity.** Measure each curve from about 95% folded to about 5% folded."},{"text":"**Midpoint = pK.** Read it on the same curve you chose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the steeper (narrower) unfolding transition is the more cooperative one; its midpoint is its pK or Tm."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-43a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"ionization state at pH (pH vs pKa)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:43","sample-cp:47"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"Compared with the original Strand Q, at what pH does the i-motif built from the analog unfold?","choices":["A higher pH, because the analog is more readily protonated","A lower pH, because the analog is more readily protonated","A lower pH, because the analog is less readily protonated","The same pH, because only the hydrogen bonds hold it together"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[1,3]},{"f":"Higher pKa holds H⁺ more tightly","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with each pKa: at pH 5.5 the analog (pKa 5.1) is about 28% protonated, normal C (4.4) only about 7%. More C⁺, more pairs."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the i-motif needs **protonated N3**, and sentence 7 gives the analog the **higher pKa**. A higher pKa keeps N3 protonated at higher pH: at pH 5.5 about 28% of analog N3 is protonated versus about 7% for normal cytosine. More C·C⁺ pairs hold on as the pH rises, so the analog i-motif **unfolds at a higher pH**.","how":{"ask":"Use each pKa to judge how much protonated cytosine exists at pH 5.5, then link that to stability.","know":"• At pH < pKa a site is mostly protonated; at pH > pKa mostly not.\n• Fraction protonated = 1/(1 + 10^(pH − pKa)).\n• **Higher pKa → more protonated** at a given pH; lower pKa → less.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3 to hold the i-motif together.","hl":"must be protonated at N3","calc":["stability needs C⁺"]},{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the two pKa values; compare them.","hl":"whose N3 pKa is 5.1","calc":["analog 5.1 > normal 4.4"]},{"text":"At pH 5.5, find the protonated fraction of each.","calc":["analog: 1/(1 + 10^0.4) ≈ 0.28","normal: 1/(1 + 10^1.1) ≈ 0.07"]},{"text":"More protonated N3 → C·C⁺ pairs survive to a higher pH before the structure falls apart.","strike":["A lower pH, because the analog is more readily protonated","A lower pH, because the analog is less readily protonated"]},{"text":"Stability depends on that protonation, so the unfolding pH cannot be the same.","strike":["The same pH, because only the hydrogen bonds hold it together"]},{"text":"Answer: A higher pH, because the analog is more readily protonated."}],"trap":"Thinking a higher pKa means a stronger acid that gives up its H⁺: a higher pKa is a weaker acid, so it keeps its H⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"must be protonated at N3","note":"needs C⁺"},{"t":"N3 pKa of 4.4","note":"normal"},{"t":"N3 pKa is 5.1","note":"analog holds H⁺ more"},{"t":"at pH 5.5","note":"above both pKa values"}]},{"text":"**pKa vs pH.** The closer the pKa sits to (or above) the pH, the larger the protonated share."},{"text":"**Also:** a modification that lowers the pKa has the reverse effect: fewer C⁺, a less stable structure."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** if a structure needs a protonated base, raising that base's pKa stabilizes it and lowering the pKa destabilizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"base pairing rules (Chargaff, H-bonds)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:44#1","pe3-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:44"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P8","passage":"A C-rich DNA strand from a phage genome, Strand Q, folds into an i-motif, a four-stranded structure held together by C·C⁺ pairs in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A. In the infected cell, enzyme X uses O₂ to oxidize the methyl group of free thymine to CH₂OH, giving 5-hydroxymethyluracil; enzyme Y then releases that CH₂OH carbon as formaldehyde, leaving uracil, with no water added and no change in oxidation state. Researchers followed the unfolding of Strand Q and of Strand Q2, whose cytosines are split into three short tracts by extra loops, as the pH was raised. Strand Q unfolded sharply between pH 6.2 and 6.6, while Strand Q2 unfolded gradually between pH 5.7 and 8.1; the pK, the pH at which half the molecules are folded, is 6.4 for Strand Q and 6.9 for Strand Q2. At pH 6.4 and 37 °C, exactly half of the Strand Q molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. Unmodified cytosine has an N3 pKa of 4.4, and a chemist also rebuilt Strand Q from a cytosine analog whose N3 pKa is 5.1, then compared the two versions at pH 5.5.","q":"When Strand Q is copied into a complementary strand, which nucleoside is absent from the new strand, and what percentage of it is deoxyguanosine?","choices":["Deoxythymidine; 46%","Deoxyadenosine; 46%","Deoxythymidine; 21%","Deoxyguanosine; 33%"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Copy's G pairs with template's C","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Template T pairs with copy's A","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Swap partners: template A 0 → copy T 0; template C → copy G. Read both from sentence 2."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives Strand Q as **46% C, 33% T, 21% G and no A**. In the copy, each base pairs with its partner: template C → **dG (46%)**, T → dA (33%), G → dC (21%), and since the template has **no A**, the copy has **no dT**.","how":{"ask":"Pair each template base with its partner, then read off the missing nucleoside and the share of G.","know":"• Base pairing: **A ↔ T**, **G ↔ C**.\n• In the copy, each base's % = its partner's % in the template.\n• A base absent from the template → its **partner is absent** from the copy.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives Strand Q's base composition and says it has no A.","hl":"Strand Q contains 46% C, 33% T and 21% G, with no A","calc":["template: C 46, T 33, G 21, A 0"]},{"text":"Pair each base to build the complementary strand.","calc":["C 46 → **dG 46%**","T 33 → dA 33%","G 21 → dC 21%","A 0 → **dT 0%**"]},{"text":"The copy has dA (from the template's T) and dG (from its C); 21% is the template's G, which becomes dC in the copy.","strike":["Deoxyadenosine; 46%","Deoxyguanosine; 33%","Deoxythymidine; 21%"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxythymidine is absent, and the strand is 46% deoxyguanosine."}],"trap":"Reporting the base missing from the template itself: the copy is missing that base's partner."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"46% C, 33% T and 21% G","note":"template composition"},{"t":"with no A","note":"copy has no T"}]},{"text":"**Partner swap.**","calc":["template A → copy T","template T → copy A","template G → copy C","template C → copy G"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a complementary strand's base percentages are the template's partner percentages; a base missing from the template removes its partner from the copy."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-45a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"protein charge vs pI (ion exchange)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:45#1","pe3-cp:45#2","pe3-cp:45#3","pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2","pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:45"],"q":"Proteins with pI 4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 are loaded onto a cation-exchange column (negative beads). At which pH will exactly three bind?","choices":["pH 6.8","pH 7.8","pH 7.3","pH 9.8"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Proteins above their pI are negative","c":[3]},{"f":"Proteins at their pI won't bind","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the pIs above each candidate pH: pH 6.8 sits below 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, so three proteins are positive and bind."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge versus pH for the five proteins","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-10,"max":10,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6.4],[4,2.9],[4.6,0],[5,-2],[6,-5.9],[6.8,-7.6],[7,-7.8],[8,-8.6],[9,-8.9],[10,-9],[11,-9]]},{"name":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[3,5.5],[4,4.7],[5,2.8],[5.9,0],[6,-0.3],[6.8,-2.8],[7,-3.3],[8,-4.9],[9,-5.6],[10,-5.9],[11,-6]]},{"name":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,8],[4,7.9],[5,7.6],[6,6.2],[6.8,3],[7,1.9],[7.3,0],[8,-4.1],[9,-7],[10,-7.8],[11,-8]]},{"name":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6],[4,6],[5,5.9],[6,5.7],[6.8,4.8],[7,4.5],[8,1],[8.2,0],[9,-3.4],[10,-5.4],[11,-5.9]]},{"name":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[3,7],[4,7],[5,6.9],[6,6.7],[6.8,6.3],[7,6.1],[8,4.6],[9,1.5],[9.4,0],[10,-2.3],[11,-5]]}]},"exp":"Below its pI a protein is **positive** and binds the **negative beads** of a cation exchanger. At **pH 6.8**, only the pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 proteins are below their pI, so exactly **three** bind; the pI 4.6 and 5.9 proteins are negative and flow through.","how":{"ask":"For each candidate pH, count the proteins whose pI lies above it (the positive ones), and find the pH that gives three.","know":"• **pH < pI → net positive**; **pH > pI → net negative**; pH = pI → no net charge.\n• **Cation exchanger** = negative beads → binds **positive** proteins.\n• **Anion exchanger** = positive beads → binds **negative** proteins.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pIs.","hl":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4"},{"text":"Count positive proteins (pI above the buffer pH) at each candidate pH.","calc":["pH 6.8: pI 7.3, 8.2, 9.4 → **3**","pH 7.3: 8.2, 9.4 → 2 (the pI 7.3 protein is neutral)","pH 7.8: 8.2, 9.4 → 2","pH 9.8: none → 0"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","x":6.8}]}},{"text":"At pH 7.8 three proteins are negative, not positive; at pH 9.8 all five are negative; at pH 7.3 one protein is neutral, leaving two.","strike":["pH 7.8","pH 9.8","pH 7.3"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 6.8."}],"trap":"Counting the negative proteins: a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it holds the proteins that are positive, those with pI above the buffer pH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4","note":"the pIs"},{"t":"cation-exchange column","note":"negative beads"},{"t":"exactly three bind","note":"three positive proteins"}]},{"text":"**Charge from pI.** Below its pI a protein holds extra H⁺ and is positive; above its pI it is negative.","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Also:** a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it binds the positive proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, three of them."},{"text":"**Also:** an anion exchanger has positive beads and binds the negative proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 4.6 and 5.9, two of them."},{"text":"**Also:** two forms of one protein with almost the same mass but different net charge (say −3 and −5 at the buffer pH) cannot be split by size, so use ion exchange. Both are negative, so an anion exchanger holds both, and the −5 form clings tighter: it stays on the column longer and comes off only at a higher salt concentration.","calc":["same size → size exclusion fails","−3 form: released first, at lower salt","−5 form: released later, at higher salt"]},{"text":"**Also:** in electrophoresis the same sign rule sets the direction. When the buffer pH equals the pI, the charges cancel and the protein stays in its well; below its pI it is positive and drifts to the negative electrode (cathode); above its pI it is negative and drifts to the positive electrode (anode).","calc":["pH 6.8, pI 4.6: negative → toward the anode (+)","pH 6.8, pI 9.4: positive → toward the cathode (−)","a protein with pI 6.8 would not migrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** proteins are positive below their pI and negative above it; a cation exchanger binds the positive ones, an anion exchanger the negative ones."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-47a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"periodic trends (IE, EA)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:47","sample-cp:7"],"q":"Of four nonmetals in groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4, which has the highest first ionization energy, and why?","choices":["Sulfur, since it is the smallest atom","Chlorine, since its valence electrons are closest","Selenium, since its nucleus has more protons than sulfur","Bromine, since its nucleus has the most protons"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"IE rises going up a group","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Atoms shrink across a period","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ionization energy climbs toward the upper right of the table. Of S, Cl, Se, Br the upper-right one is Cl (group 17, period 3)."},"exp":"First ionization energy **rises across a period** (more protons, same shell) and **rises up a group** (fewer shells, so the outer electrons sit nearer the protons). The group 17, period 3 atom, chlorine, is the smallest of the four and holds its electrons most tightly.","how":{"ask":"Place the four atoms on the table and apply both ionization-energy trends.","know":"• First ionization energy **increases left → right** across a period and **increases bottom → top** in a group.\n• Smaller atom → outer electrons nearer the protons → harder to remove.\n• Among halogens, **F** (smallest) is the hardest to ionize.","steps":[{"text":"Place the atoms: period 3 holds S (16) and Cl (17); period 4 holds Se (16) and Br (17).","hl":"groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4"},{"text":"Across a period, group 17 beats group 16, and the group 16 atoms are the larger ones: S and Se lose.","strike":["Sulfur, since it is the smallest atom","Selenium, since its nucleus has more protons than sulfur"]},{"text":"Up a group, period 3 beats period 4: Br has the most protons but one more shell, so it loses.","strike":["Bromine, since its nucleus has the most protons"]},{"text":"**Also:** extend the trend up group 17: F > Cl > Br > I, so fluorine, the smallest halogen, is the hardest of them all to ionize."},{"text":"Answer: chlorine, since its valence electrons are closest to the nucleus."}],"trap":"Thinking a heavier atom with more protons holds its electrons more tightly: extra shells put them farther away and shield them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"highest first ionization energy","note":"first ionization energy"},{"t":"groups 16 and 17","note":"across a period"},{"t":"periods 3 and 4","note":"up and down a group"}]},{"text":"**Across a period:** more protons pull on the same shell, so ionization energy rises toward group 17 (and 18)."},{"text":"**Up a group:** fewer shells, a smaller atom, less shielding, so ionization energy rises toward the top."},{"text":"**Also:** of the halogens, fluorine is the hardest atom to ionize, because it is the smallest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** first ionization energy rises up a group and across a period; the smallest atom, top right, holds its electrons tightest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-48a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"kinematics x = ½at²","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:48#1","pe3-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:48","sample-cp:8"],"q":"A toy sled, released from rest, glides down a ramp with steady acceleration; the graph plots its position x against time. Which relationship fits the data, and what is the sled's acceleration?","choices":["x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 12 m/s²","x ∝ t; a = 0 m/s²"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pick a clean point: x = 12 m at t = 2 s. a = 2x/t² = 24/4."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Position of the sled","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position x (m)","min":0,"max":48,"step":12},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,3],[2,12],[3,27],[4,48]]}]},"exp":"From rest with constant acceleration, **x = ½at²**, so x grows with **t²** and the curve bends upward. At t = 2 s, x = 12 m, so a = 2x/t² = 2(12)/2² = **6 m/s²**. Check: at t = 4 s, x = ½(6)(16) = 48 m.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the curve is a line or a parabola, then read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","know":"• From rest, constant a: **x = ½at²** → x ∝ t² (parabola).\n• Constant velocity: x = vt → x ∝ t (straight line).\n• Solve for a: **a = 2x/t²**.","steps":[{"text":"The curve bends upward: the distance covered each second keeps growing.","calc":["0 → 3 → 12 → 27 → 48 m"],"strike":["x ∝ t; a = 0 m/s²"]},{"text":"Read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","calc":["x = 12 m at t = 2 s","a = 2x/t² = 2(12)/4 = **6 m/s²**"]},{"text":"3 m/s² drops the ½ (a = x/t²); 12 m/s² is the position reading itself.","strike":["x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 12 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²."}],"trap":"Forgetting the ½ in x = ½at², which halves the acceleration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"released from rest","note":"v₀ = 0"},{"t":"steady acceleration","note":"x = ½at²"}]},{"text":"**Shape first.** A parabola that bends upward means constant acceleration; a straight line means constant velocity."},{"text":"**Also:** for the same motion, the velocity–time graph is a straight line through the origin (v = at), while the position–time graph is the parabola."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest, x = ½at², so x ∝ t² and a = 2x/t²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-49a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"wave in new medium (f constant)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:49#1","pe3-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:49"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"Once a pulse passes from the muscle into the fat, which of the following describes its frequency and wavelength?","choices":["Frequency unchanged; wavelength shorter","Frequency unchanged; wavelength longer","Frequency decreased; wavelength unchanged","Frequency increased; wavelength unchanged"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"The source alone sets frequency","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"f is locked to the source. Slower medium → shorter λ, faster medium → longer λ."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says the **crystal alone sets** how many crests leave each second, so the **frequency stays 6.4 MHz** in every tissue. Sentence 2 says sound is **slower in fat** (1450 m/s) than in muscle (1600 m/s); since **λ = v/f**, the wavelength **shrinks** in the fat.","how":{"ask":"Decide which wave property the source fixes, then use λ = v/f for the one that changes.","know":"• The source fixes the **frequency**; it does not change between media.\n• **Speed** depends on the medium.\n• **λ = v/f**: with f fixed, λ follows v.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the crystal alone sets the number of crests per second: the frequency is fixed.","hl":"The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second","calc":["f = 6.4 MHz in muscle and in fat"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the speeds: the pulse slows when it enters fat.","hl":"where it travels at 1450 m/s","calc":["v: 1600 → 1450 m/s"]},{"text":"With f fixed, λ = v/f falls with v.","calc":["λ(muscle) = 1600/6.4 × 10⁶ = 0.25 mm","λ(fat) = 1450/6.4 × 10⁶ ≈ 0.23 mm → **shrinks**"]},{"text":"The frequency cannot rise or fall at the boundary, and a slower wave has a shorter λ, not a longer one.","strike":["Frequency decreased; wavelength unchanged","Frequency increased; wavelength unchanged","Frequency unchanged; wavelength longer"]},{"text":"Answer: frequency unchanged; wavelength shorter."}],"trap":"Letting the frequency change at the boundary: the boundary cannot add or remove crests, so f stays and λ adjusts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second","note":"f fixed"},{"t":"1600 m/s","note":"muscle"},{"t":"1450 m/s","note":"fat: slower"}]},{"text":"**What stays, what changes.** Frequency stays; speed and wavelength change together."},{"text":"**Direction.**","calc":["slower medium → shorter λ","faster medium → longer λ"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a wave crosses into a new medium, its frequency stays the same and its wavelength scales with its speed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-51a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"wave speed v = fλ","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:51#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:51"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"In the muscle, one wavelength of the ultrasound is about:","choices":["0.25 mm","25 mm","250 m","1.0 × 10¹⁰ m"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"m/s ÷ MHz = µm: 1600 ÷ 6.4 = 250 µm."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives **f = 6.4 MHz** and sentence 2 gives **v = 1600 m/s** in muscle. **λ = v/f** = 1600 m/s ÷ 6.4 × 10⁶ Hz = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.25 mm**.","how":{"ask":"Divide the speed in muscle by the probe's frequency, in hertz.","know":"• **v = fλ**, so **λ = v/f**.\n• 1 MHz = 10⁶ Hz; 1 mm = 10⁻³ m.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the speed of sound in muscle, and sentence 1 the frequency.","hl":"from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s","calc":["v = 1600 m/s, f = 6.4 × 10⁶ Hz"]},{"text":"Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength, so λ = v/f.","calc":["λ = 1600 ÷ (6.4 × 10⁶) = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.25 mm**"]},{"text":"Leaving out 10⁶ gives meters by the hundred; v × f is not a length; 25 mm slips two powers of ten.","strike":["250 m","1.0 × 10¹⁰ m","25 mm"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.25 mm."}],"trap":"Dropping the mega- prefix: 6.4 MHz is 6.4 million hertz."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"vibrates at 6.4 MHz","note":"f"},{"t":"where sound travels at 1600 m/s","note":"v in muscle"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange.**","calc":["v = fλ → λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Also:** use the speed for the tissue the question names; in fat (1450 m/s) the same probe gives a shorter wavelength."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** wavelength = speed in that medium ÷ frequency in hertz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-52a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"continuity Q = Av","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:52","pe3-cp:50"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P9","passage":"A sonographer studies blood flow in the kidney with a probe whose crystal vibrates at 6.4 MHz. Each pulse passes from muscle, where sound travels at 1600 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it travels at 1450 m/s, before reaching the kidney. The crystal alone sets how many wave crests leave the probe each second. In one image, an arteriole segment 150 µm long has a 6.0 × 10³ µm² cross section, and blood moves through it at an average speed of 3.5 mm/s. Downstream, the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section, so their combined area is larger than the arteriole's. Blood is treated as an incompressible fluid in steady flow.","q":"What is the average blood speed in each capillary?","choices":["0.0089 mm/s","2.5 mm/s","4.9 mm/s","700 mm/s"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Capillary blood flows slower than arteriole's","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Speed scales inversely with total area: 6.0 × 10³ ÷ 8.4 × 10³ ≈ 0.71, so the speed falls to about 0.71 × 3.5 mm/s."},"exp":"Sentence 6 makes blood **incompressible**, so the flow into the capillaries equals the flow in the arteriole: **A·v = N·a·u**. Sentence 5 gives N·a = 280 × 30 µm² = 8.4 × 10³ µm². u = (6.0 × 10³)(3.5 mm/s) ÷ (8.4 × 10³) = **2.5 mm/s**, slower than in the arteriole because the total area is larger.","how":{"ask":"Set the arteriole's flow equal to the combined capillary flow, then solve for the speed.","know":"• **Continuity** (incompressible flow): A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.\n• For N identical branches: A₂ = **N·a**.\n• Larger total area → lower speed.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the number of capillaries and each one's area, and says their combined area is larger.","hl":"the arteriole divides into 280 identical capillaries, each 30 µm² in cross section","calc":["N·a = 280 × 30 = 8.4 × 10³ µm²"]},{"text":"Incompressible flow means the volume per second is the same before and after the branch.","calc":["A·v = N·a·u"]},{"text":"Solve for u, using the arteriole's values from sentence 4.","calc":["u = (6.0 × 10³ × 3.5) ÷ 8.4 × 10³ = **2.5 mm/s**"]},{"text":"Using one capillary's area ignores the sharing; a speed above the arteriole's has the area ratio upside down; dividing by N again double-counts.","strike":["700 mm/s","4.9 mm/s","0.0089 mm/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.5 mm/s."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow capillaries make blood faster: each is narrow, but together they have more area, so the blood slows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"280 identical capillaries","note":"N"},{"t":"30 µm²","note":"a, each"},{"t":"their combined area is larger than the arteriole's","note":"speed drops"},{"t":"incompressible fluid in steady flow","note":"continuity"}]},{"text":"**Continuity with branches.**","calc":["A·v = N·a·u → u = Av/(Na)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at a branch point the flow is conserved, so the speed in each branch is the parent flow divided by the total branch area."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:50"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-50a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-53a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"K/Ksp expression (solids excluded)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:53#1","pe3-cp:53#2","pe3-cp:53#3","pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:53","sample-cp:3"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"Which of the following gives the solubility product expression and its value for the boiler scale?","choices":["Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]² = 3.7 × 10⁻¹¹","Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻] = 8.8 × 10⁻⁸","Ksp = [Mg²⁺]²[F⁻]² = 7.8 × 10⁻¹⁵","Ksp = [Mg²⁺] + [F⁻] = 6.3 × 10⁻⁴"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ksp raises ions to their coefficients","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Ksp is a product, not sum","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"MgF₂ → 1 Mg²⁺ + 2 F⁻, so Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]². Estimate: 2 × (4 × 10⁻⁴)² × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹¹."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives the equilibrium, **MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺ + 2F⁻**, and the saturated concentrations. Leave out the solid and raise every ion's concentration to its coefficient: **Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]²** = (2.1 × 10⁻⁴)(4.2 × 10⁻⁴)² = (2.1 × 10⁻⁴)(1.76 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹¹**.","how":{"ask":"Write Ksp from the dissolution equation, then plug in the saturated concentrations.","know":"• **Ksp** = product of ion concentrations, each to the power of its **coefficient**.\n• **Solids** (and pure liquids) are left out.\n• Use the concentrations of a **saturated** solution.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the dissolution equation and the concentrations in a saturated solution.","hl":"MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq)","calc":["coefficients: Mg²⁺ 1, F⁻ 2; MgF₂(s) left out"]},{"text":"Write the solubility product with each concentration raised to its coefficient.","calc":["Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]²"]},{"text":"Plug in the values from sentence 2.","hl":"[Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","calc":["(4.2 × 10⁻⁴)² = 1.76 × 10⁻⁷","Ksp = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ × 1.76 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹¹**"]},{"text":"Adding concentrations is never an equilibrium constant; leaving [F⁻] unsquared skips its coefficient; squaring [Mg²⁺] gives it a coefficient it doesn't have.","strike":["Ksp = [Mg²⁺] + [F⁻] = 6.3 × 10⁻⁴","Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻] = 8.8 × 10⁻⁸","Ksp = [Mg²⁺]²[F⁻]² = 7.8 × 10⁻¹⁵"]},{"text":"Answer: Ksp = [Mg²⁺][F⁻]² = 3.7 × 10⁻¹¹."}],"trap":"Squaring every concentration: only the ion with coefficient 2 is squared."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq)","note":"coefficients 1 and 2"},{"t":"in water saturated with it","note":"use these for Ksp"}]},{"text":"**Expression first.**","calc":["AₓBᵧ(s) ⇌ x A + y B → Ksp = [A]ˣ[B]ʸ"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a 1:1 salt such as CaCO₃, both exponents are 1: Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp multiplies the dissolved ions' concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, and leaves the solid out."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ΔG° = −RT ln K","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:55#1","pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:55"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"For the resin exchange at 28 °C, the equilibrium constant K is best described by:","choices":["K ≈ 0.37, so reactants are favored.","K ≈ 1.0, so neither side is favored.","K ≈ 2.7, so products are favored.","K ≈ 7.4, so products are favored."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Negative ΔG° means K above 1","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"−ΔG°/RT = 2.5/2.5 = 1, so K = e¹."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives **ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol** and **RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol**. Rearranging **ΔG° = −RT ln K** gives ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 1, so **K = e ≈ 2.7**. K > 1, so the **products** (resin holding M⁺, H⁺ released) are favored.","how":{"ask":"Turn ΔG° into ln K with −ΔG°/RT, then exponentiate and read the side.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K**, so **K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)**.\n• ΔG° < 0 → K > 1 → products favored.\n• Keep ΔG° and RT in the same units.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives ΔG° and RT in the same units.","hl":"where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol","calc":["−ΔG°/RT = 2.5/2.5 = 1"]},{"text":"Use ΔG° = −RT ln K.","calc":["ln K = 1 → **K = e ≈ 2.7**"]},{"text":"K > 1, so the products are favored.","calc":["M⁺ binds the resin; H⁺ is released"]},{"text":"A K below 1 flips the sign of ΔG°; K = 1 needs ΔG° = 0; 7.4 is e², twice the exponent.","strike":["K ≈ 0.37, so reactants are favored.","K ≈ 1.0, so neither side is favored.","K ≈ 7.4, so products are favored."]},{"text":"Answer: K ≈ 2.7, so products are favored."}],"trap":"Dropping the minus sign: a negative ΔG° means ln K is positive and K > 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol","note":"same units as ΔG°"},{"t":"ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol","note":"negative → K > 1"},{"t":"use e ≈ 2.7","note":"e¹"}]},{"text":"**From ΔG° to K.**","calc":["K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)"]},{"text":"**Also:** with R = 8.314 J/mol·K, convert kJ to J first; at 298 K, RT ≈ 2.48 kJ/mol."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K; a negative ΔG° means K > 1 and products are favored."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-56a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Le Châtelier shift","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:56"],"pid":"pe3-cp:P10","passage":"A laboratory treats its tap water before feeding it to a small boiler. The scale that builds up in the boiler is mainly magnesium fluoride, MgF₂(s) ⇌ Mg²⁺(aq) + 2F⁻(aq); in water saturated with it, [Mg²⁺] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴ M and [F⁻] = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. The most abundant dissolved metal ion in the feed water comes from a metal whose first three ionization energies are 420, 3050 and 4410 kJ/mol. A cation-exchange resin in its acid form removes that ion: M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq), where R is an anionic site fixed to the resin beads. At 28 °C, where RT ≈ 2.5 kJ/mol, this exchange has ΔG° = −2.5 kJ/mol (use e ≈ 2.7). When the resin is spent, it is restored by rinsing it with a concentrated solution that drives the exchange back to the left.","q":"Which solution should be used to regenerate the resin, and why?","choices":["HCl, since added H⁺ shifts the exchange left","NaOH, since OH⁻ removes H⁺, shifting the exchange right","Pure water, since dilution shifts the exchange right","Fresh resin, since added H–R shifts the exchange left"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium right","c":[1]},{"f":"Solids don't enter K","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To push left, add a product in high concentration. The dissolved product is H⁺, so use a strong acid."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the exchange, **M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq)**, and sentence 6 says regeneration drives it **left**. Adding a **product** does that, and the only dissolved product is **H⁺**: a concentrated strong acid such as **HCl** loads H⁺ back onto the resin and releases M⁺. Removing or diluting H⁺ shifts the other way, and the solid resin doesn't enter K.","how":{"ask":"Find which dissolved species, added in excess, pushes the exchange to the left.","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product shifts left; removing a product shifts right.\n• **Solids** (the resin) don't appear in K, so adding more doesn't shift it.\n• A strong acid supplies a high [H⁺].","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says regeneration must drive the exchange back to the left.","hl":"drives the exchange back to the left","calc":["need: shift ←"]},{"text":"Sentence 4 shows H⁺(aq) as the dissolved product; adding a product shifts the equilibrium left.","hl":"M⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ M–R(s) + H⁺(aq)","calc":["add H⁺ → Q > K → shift ←"]},{"text":"NaOH and pure water both lower [H⁺], which shifts it right; added solid resin doesn't shift it at all.","strike":["NaOH, since OH⁻ removes H⁺, shifting the exchange right","Pure water, since dilution shifts the exchange right","Fresh resin, since added H–R shifts the exchange left"]},{"text":"Answer: HCl, since added H⁺ shifts the exchange left."}],"trap":"Thinking more resin helps: solids don't enter the equilibrium expression."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"in its acid form","note":"H–R"},{"t":"drives the exchange back to the left","note":"add a product"}]},{"text":"**Which side is H⁺ on?** It is a product, so adding it pushes the reaction backward."},{"text":"**Also:** a water softener works the same way: a Na⁺-form resin takes up Ca²⁺, and a concentrated salt brine pushes Na⁺ back on."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to drive an ion exchange backward, flood it with the ion it released; solids don't shift an equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-57a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"VSEPR geometry","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:57#1","pe3-cp:57#2","pe3-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:57#4","pe3-cp:57#5"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:57"],"q":"In each species below, the central atom is bonded only to halogen atoms. Which one has an octahedral molecular geometry?","fig":"cpt-halide-centers","choices":["TeF₆","ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Octahedral needs six bonded atoms","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Octahedral = 6 domains, 0 lone pairs (AX₆): only TeF₆ has 6 bonds. ICl₄⁻ is square planar, ClF₃ T-shaped, SiCl₄ tetrahedral."},"exp":"Count electron domains on the central atom. **TeF₆**: 6 bonds, 0 lone pairs → **octahedral**. ICl₄⁻: 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs → square planar. ClF₃: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs → T-shaped. SiCl₄: 4 bonds → tetrahedral.","how":{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonds and lone pairs, then name the shape.","know":"• **Lone pairs** = (valence e⁻ − bonding e⁻) ÷ 2, after adding 1 e⁻ per negative charge and subtracting 1 per positive charge; each single bond uses 1 e⁻ of the central atom.\n• 6 bonds, 0 LP → **octahedral**.\n• 4 bonds + 2 LP → **square planar**.\n• 3 bonds + 2 LP → **T-shaped**.\n• 4 bonds, 0 LP → **tetrahedral**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the central atom's electron domains in each species.","hl":"octahedral molecular geometry","calc":["TeF₆: Te 6 e⁻, 6 bonds → 0 LP → 6 domains","ICl₄⁻: I 7 + 1 = 8 e⁻, 4 bonds → 2 LP → 6 domains","ClF₃: Cl 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 LP → 5 domains","SiCl₄: Si 4 e⁻, 4 bonds → 0 LP → 4 domains"]},{"text":"Only TeF₆ has all six domains as bonds, so atoms sit at all six octahedral corners.","calc":["TeF₆ → **octahedral**"]},{"text":"ICl₄⁻ is square planar (two lone pairs take opposite corners), ClF₃ is T-shaped, and SiCl₄ is tetrahedral.","strike":["ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"]},{"text":"Answer: TeF₆."}],"trap":"Picking ICl₄⁻ because it has six electron domains: its electron geometry is octahedral, but with two lone pairs the molecular shape is square planar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"central atom","note":"count its domains"},{"t":"octahedral molecular geometry","note":"6 bonds, no lone pairs"}]},{"text":"**Count electron domains.** Bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom set the electron geometry; only the bonded atoms set the molecular shape."},{"text":"**6 bonds, 0 lone pairs = octahedral.** TeF₆: Te's six valence electrons all go into six Te–F bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs is square planar: in ICl₄⁻ the lone pairs sit opposite each other."},{"text":"**Also:** 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs is T-shaped: ClF₃'s lone pairs take two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds and no lone pairs is tetrahedral, as in SiCl₄ (109.5° angles)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count domains on the central atom; 6 bonds/0 LP octahedral, 4 bonds/2 LP square planar, 3 bonds/2 LP T-shaped, 4 bonds/0 LP tetrahedral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-58a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hydrostatic pressure P = ρgh","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:58"],"q":"A tube sealed at the upper end stands in ethanol (ρ = 790 kg/m³), and the air inside is pumped out. Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and g = 9.8 m/s². How high can the ethanol rise?","choices":["13 m","0.76 m","1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]},{"f":"Air holds up 10 m water","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"h = P/(ρg) ≈ 10⁵ / (800 × 10) ≈ 12.5 m; the exact numbers land just above that."},"exp":"Nothing presses down on the liquid inside the tube, so it climbs until the pressure of the column at its base matches air pressure, **P = ρgh**, so the height is h = P/(ρg) = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8) ≈ **13 m**, higher than water's 10.3 m because ethanol is less dense.","how":{"ask":"Set the column's pressure ρgh equal to air pressure and solve for h.","know":"• A column under vacuum is held up by air pressure: **P_atm = ρgh**.\n• **h = P/(ρg)**: about 10 m for water and 0.76 m for mercury at 1 atm.\n• Lower density → taller column.","steps":[{"text":"Set air pressure equal to the column's pressure.","hl":"Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","calc":["P = ρgh"]},{"text":"Solve for h.","calc":["h = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8)","= 1.01 × 10⁵ / 7.7 × 10³ ≈ **13 m**"]},{"text":"1.3 m slips a power of ten; 7.8 × 10⁸ m multiplies instead of dividing; 0.76 m is mercury's height.","strike":["1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m","0.76 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 13 m."}],"trap":"Recalling the 0.76 m mercury height: that works only for mercury's high density."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pumped out","note":"vacuum above the liquid"},{"t":"ρ = 790 kg/m³","note":"ρ"},{"t":"1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Pressure balance.** Air pushes on the open surface; nothing pushes down inside the tube except the liquid's own weight.","calc":["P_atm = ρgh"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** h = P/(ρg); the less dense the liquid, the taller the column air pressure can hold up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"solid types (covalent network)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe3-cp:59#1","pe3-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:59#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:59"],"q":"Consider these period-2 elements and their melting points: Li 180 °C; B 2076 °C; F₂ −220 °C; Ne −249 °C. Which one is a covalent network solid in its standard state, and why?","choices":["Boron, since B–B bonds form a 3-D network","Lithium, since it is a solid at 25 °C","Fluorine, since covalent F–F bonds join its atoms","Neon, since its octet is complete"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Network solids melt extremely high","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The highest melting point among the nonmetals flags a covalent network."},"exp":"**Boron** forms a covalent network: each B atom is covalently bonded to several neighbors in a 3-D framework, so it melts only when covalent bonds break, hence **2076 °C**. F₂ and Ne are discrete molecules or atoms held by weak dispersion forces; lithium is a **metallic** solid.","how":{"ask":"Sort the elements by bonding type (network, molecular, atomic, metallic), using the melting points as a check.","know":"• **Covalent network solids** (diamond and graphite C, B, Si, SiO₂): every atom bonded covalently to several neighbors; very high melting points.\n• **Molecular** elements (N₂, O₂, F₂) and noble gases: weak forces between particles; low melting points.\n• **Metals** (Li, Be): metallic bonding, not covalent.","steps":[{"text":"Sort by melting point: thousands of degrees signals a network.","hl":"B 2076 °C"},{"text":"F₂ and Ne melt far below zero: separate molecules and lone atoms.","strike":["Fluorine, since covalent F–F bonds join its atoms","Neon, since its octet is complete"]},{"text":"Lithium is solid, but its bonding is metallic, not covalent.","strike":["Lithium, since it is a solid at 25 °C"]},{"text":"Answer: boron, since B–B bonds form a 3-D network."}],"trap":"Taking \"solid at room temperature\" as proof of a network: metals and ionic solids are solids too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"B 2076 °C","note":"very high: network"},{"t":"F₂ −220 °C","note":"molecular"},{"t":"covalent network solid","note":"atoms linked in a 3-D web"}]},{"text":"**Bonding type sets the melting point.** Breaking covalent bonds takes far more energy than separating molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** in period 2, carbon (diamond, graphite) is the classic network solid alongside boron; N₂ and O₂ are small molecules, and P or I can be ruled out at once because they are not in period 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent network solid has every atom covalently bonded to several neighbors, so it melts at very high temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-01a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS sign reasoning","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:1"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In the 320 K warehouse, Reaction I is best described as:","choices":["nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol.","spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −6.2 × 10⁴ kJ/mol.","spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −25 kJ/mol.","nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +149 kJ/mol."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"ΔS = 0.195 kJ/(mol·K), so TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ 62 kJ/mol, below ΔH = 87. ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol > 0, matching the unchanged mass."},"exp":"Sentence 4 reports no mass loss at 320 K, so ΔG must be positive there. Check it with sentence 3: TΔS = 320 K × 0.195 kJ/(mol·K) ≈ **62 kJ/mol**, less than ΔH = **87 kJ/mol**, so ΔG = ΔH − TΔS ≈ **+25 kJ/mol**: Reaction I is **nonspontaneous** at 320 K. It turns spontaneous only above T = ΔH/ΔS ≈ 446 K.","how":{"ask":"Decide the sign and size of ΔG for Reaction I at the warehouse temperature.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; ΔG > 0 means no net reaction.\n• ΔS is given in J: divide by 1000 before combining it with ΔH in kJ.\n• With ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0, the reaction turns spontaneous only above **T = ΔH/ΔS**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: at 320 K the bag lost no mass, so no ammonia escaped and ΔG must be positive.","hl":"lost no measurable mass over a whole season"},{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the two terms; put ΔS in kJ and multiply by the warehouse temperature.","hl":"ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K)","calc":["ΔS = 0.195 kJ/(mol·K)","TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ **62 kJ/mol** < ΔH = 87 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","calc":["ΔG ≈ 87 − 62 = **+25 kJ/mol** > 0"]},{"text":"TΔS only beats ΔH if J is never turned into kJ.","strike":["spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −6.2 × 10⁴ kJ/mol."]},{"text":"Subtracting the wrong way gives a negative ΔG that clashes with sentence 4; adding the terms treats TΔS as a cost.","strike":["spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −25 kJ/mol.","nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +149 kJ/mol."]},{"text":"Answer: nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +25 kJ/mol, as sentence 4 observed."}],"trap":"Calling a gas-releasing, entropy-raising decomposition spontaneous without multiplying ΔS by T: at 320 K the endothermic ΔH still wins."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol","note":"endothermic"},{"t":"ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K)","note":"J, not kJ"},{"t":"at 320 K","note":"the T to use"},{"t":"lost no measurable mass","note":"the observation: ΔG > 0"}]},{"text":"**Compare the two terms** in ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, in the same units.","calc":["TΔS = 320 × 0.195 ≈ 62 kJ/mol","ΔG ≈ 87 − 62 = +25 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Find the crossover.** Above T = ΔH/ΔS the entropy term wins, and the fertilizer would lose ammonia.","calc":["T = 87 ÷ 0.195 ≈ 446 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a passage observation of \"no change\" means ΔG > 0; for ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0 that holds while ΔH > TΔS, below T = ΔH/ΔS, with ΔS in kJ first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"K/Ksp expression (solids excluded)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:2"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In the sealed flask, which expression gives K for Reaction I, and how does P_NH₃ respond to the added powder?","choices":["K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ is unchanged","K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ decreases","K = [NH₄H₂PO₄]P_NH₃/[(NH₄)₂HPO₄]; P_NH₃ decreases","K = (P_NH₃)²; P_NH₃ is unchanged"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":5,"c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Strike every (s): only NH₃(g) is left, coefficient 1. A pure solid stays at activity 1, so more NH₄H₂PO₄ leaves Q = K and the ammonia pressure stays put."},"exp":"Sentence 1 shows the phases in Reaction I: two solids and one gas. Pure solids are left out, so **K = P_NH₃**, to the first power. Sentence 5 stirs in more NH₄H₂PO₄ at constant temperature, but a pure solid's activity stays 1, so Q still equals K and the ammonia pressure **does not change**.","how":{"ask":"Write K for Reaction I from the phases, then decide whether adding more solid product shifts it.","know":"• K contains gas pressures (or solute concentrations) raised to their coefficients.\n• **Pure solids and liquids are left out** (activity = 1).\n• Adding a pure solid, even a product, changes neither Q nor K.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the phase of each species in Reaction I: two solids and one gas.","hl":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g)","calc":["both phosphate salts (s): left out of K","NH₃(g): in K, coefficient 1"]},{"text":"Write K from the gas alone.","calc":["K = P_NH₃"]},{"text":"The coefficient of NH₃ is 1, and the solids stay out of K.","strike":["K = (P_NH₃)²; P_NH₃ is unchanged","K = [NH₄H₂PO₄]P_NH₃/[(NH₄)₂HPO₄]; P_NH₃ decreases"]},{"text":"Sentence 5 adds only more pure solid at constant temperature, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts.","hl":"extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature","strike":["K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ decreases"]},{"text":"Answer: K = P_NH₃; P_NH₃ is unchanged."}],"trap":"Applying Le Châtelier to a solid product: more NH₄H₂PO₄ does not raise its \"concentration\", so it pushes the equilibrium nowhere."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s)","note":"solid: out of K"},{"t":"NH₄H₂PO₄(s)","note":"solid: out of K"},{"t":"NH₃(g) (Reaction I)","note":"the only term in K"},{"t":"at constant temperature","note":"K is fixed"}]},{"text":"**Build K.** Gases and dissolved species only, each raised to its coefficient.","calc":["K = P_NH₃"]},{"text":"**Test the change.** A pure solid's activity is always 1, so adding it changes neither Q nor K. Only a change in a gas pressure, a concentration or the temperature could shift it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pure solids and liquids are left out of K, and adding or removing them never shifts the equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-03a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"redox from oxidation-state change","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:3#1","pe4-cp:3#2","pe3-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:3","pe3-cp:16"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In Reaction I, what happens to phosphorus's oxidation state, and what type of reaction is it?","choices":["It stays +5, so Reaction I is acid–base","It stays +5, so Reaction I is redox","It rises to +6, so Reaction I is redox","It rises to +7, so Reaction I is acid–base"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"P's highest oxidation state is +5","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Redox needs an oxidation-state change","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sentence 2 says only a proton moves (staying +1): acid–base. P is +5 both times (1 + P − 8 = −2)."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says a proton moves from NH₄⁺ to HPO₄²⁻. In HPO₄²⁻: (+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2, so P = +5. In H₂PO₄⁻: 2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1, so P = +5. Only an H⁺ moves, keeping its +1, so no element changes oxidation state: Reaction I is **acid–base, not redox**.","how":{"ask":"Assign phosphorus's oxidation state before and after Reaction I, then decide whether anything was oxidized or reduced.","know":"• Oxidation states: H = +1, O = −2; they add up to the species' charge.\n• **Redox** needs an element whose oxidation state changes. Moving H⁺ (still +1) changes nothing: that is **acid–base**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: Reaction I just moves a proton from ammonium to hydrogen phosphate.","hl":"a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion"},{"text":"Assign P before and after.","calc":["HPO₄²⁻: 1 + P − 8 = −2 → P = **+5**","H₂PO₄⁻: 2 + P − 8 = −1 → P = **+5**"]},{"text":"P stays +5 and cannot exceed it, so the rising values are wrong.","strike":["It rises to +6, so Reaction I is redox","It rises to +7, so Reaction I is acid–base"]},{"text":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is passed along, so the reaction is not redox.","strike":["It stays +5, so Reaction I is redox"]},{"text":"Answer: it stays +5, so Reaction I is acid–base."}],"trap":"Thinking that gaining a bond to H changes the central atom: the H arrives as H⁺, bringing no electrons, so P keeps its oxidation state."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a proton moves","note":"H⁺ transfer"},{"t":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s)","note":"HPO₄²⁻, charge −2"},{"t":"NH₄H₂PO₄(s)","note":"H₂PO₄⁻, charge −1"}]},{"text":"**Assign oxidation states.** H = +1, O = −2, and the sum equals the charge.","calc":["HPO₄²⁻: P = +5","H₂PO₄⁻: P = +5"]},{"text":"**Also:** nitrogen behaves the same way in the other ion. In NH₄⁺, N + 4(+1) = +1, so N = −3; in NH₃, N = −3 too. Losing H⁺ leaves N unchanged."},{"text":"**Classify.** No oxidation state changes and only H⁺ moves: acid–base, not redox."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reaction is redox only if some element's oxidation state changes; a pure H⁺ transfer is acid–base."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:16"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-16b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-04a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"molar volume 22.4 L at STP","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:4#1","pe4-cp:4#2","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:4"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P1","passage":"Diammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₂HPO₄, is a fertilizer that slowly gives off ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) ⇌ NH₄H₂PO₄(s) + NH₃(g) (Reaction I). In Reaction I, a proton moves from an ammonium ion to the hydrogen phosphate ion. For Reaction I, ΔH° = +87 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +195 J/(mol·K). A bag stored in a warehouse at 320 K under standard conditions lost no measurable mass over a whole season. In a sealed flask, the solid was allowed to reach equilibrium with its ammonia, and extra NH₄H₂PO₄ powder was then stirred in at constant temperature. On strong heating, both ammonium ions give up their ammonia: (NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g) (Reaction II). In one run, 59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol) was decomposed this way, and the ammonia released was measured at STP.","q":"In the strong-heating run, what volume of NH₃ was released?","choices":["5.0 L","10.1 L","20.2 L","22.1 L"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"59.4 ÷ 132 = 0.45 mol salt; × 2 = 0.90 mol NH₃; 0.9 × 22.4 ≈ 20 L."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives the mass and molar mass: 59.4 g ÷ 132 g/mol = **0.450 mol**. Reaction II releases 2 NH₃ per formula unit: **0.900 mol NH₃**. At STP one mole of ideal gas fills 22.4 L, so 0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 ≈ **20.2 L**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the salt's mass to moles, use Reaction II's ratio to get moles of NH₃, then use the STP molar volume.","know":"• moles = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• The coefficients give the **mole ratio**: 1 (NH₄)₂HPO₄ → 2 NH₃.\n• **V = n × 22.4 L/mol** at STP (0 °C, 1 atm) only; near 25 °C it is about 24.5 L/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the mass decomposed, its molar mass, and the STP condition.","hl":"59.4 g of (NH₄)₂HPO₄ (M = 132 g/mol)","calc":["n = 59.4 ÷ 132 = **0.450 mol**"]},{"text":"Sentence 6's Reaction II sets the ratio: each formula unit gives two NH₃.","hl":"(NH₄)₂HPO₄(s) → H₃PO₄(l) + 2NH₃(g)","calc":["n(NH₃) = 0.450 × 2 = **0.900 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to volume at STP.","hl":"measured at STP","calc":["V = 0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 L"]},{"text":"5.0 L halves instead of doubling, 10.1 L skips the ratio, and 22.1 L uses the 25 °C molar volume instead of STP's.","strike":["5.0 L","10.1 L","22.1 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 20.2 L."}],"trap":"Taking one mole of salt as one mole of gas: Reaction II, not Reaction I, describes the strong-heating run, and it releases two NH₃ per unit."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"59.4 g","note":"mass decomposed"},{"t":"M = 132 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"2NH₃(g)","note":"2 mol NH₃ per mol salt"},{"t":"measured at STP","note":"22.4 L/mol applies"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.**","calc":["59.4 ÷ 132 = 0.450 mol"]},{"text":"**Moles of reactant to moles of product** with the coefficients of the right reaction.","calc":["0.450 × 2 = 0.900 mol NH₃"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters at STP.** One mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 0 °C and 1 atm; at other conditions use PV = nRT.","calc":["0.900 × 22.4 = 20.16 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same coefficients give a gas used up. For a fuel, moles of O₂ = moles of fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient), then × 22.4 L/mol at STP."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V(gas, STP) = moles of reactant × (product coefficient ÷ reactant coefficient) × 22.4 L/mol, and 22.4 L/mol holds at STP only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-05a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Beer–Lambert A = εbc","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:5"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.12.","q":"Why is the spectrophotometer zeroed with reagent blank rather than with pure water?","choices":["To cancel the absorbance of the reagent itself","To remove the extra absorbance of the scratched wall","To raise the absorbance of the iron complex","To shift the complex's peak toward 562 nm"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"A blank holds no analyte","c":[2]},{"f":"Zeroing never moves absorption peaks","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The reference holds everything except the analyte. Zeroing on it subtracts the reagent's own absorbance, so later readings come from the iron complex alone."},"exp":"A reference (blank) sample contains everything in the sample **except the analyte**. The ferrozine reagent and its trace impurities can absorb a little at 562 nm, so zeroing on reagent blank **cancels the reagent's own absorbance** and leaves only the Fe²⁺ complex in later readings. It cannot remove the scratch: that flaw belongs to a different cuvette, which is why that cuvette still read 0.12 with blank alone.","how":{"ask":"Ask what the reference contains that the samples also contain, apart from the analyte.","know":"• The reference (blank) holds every component except the analyte; zeroing on it subtracts their absorbance.\n• Zeroing removes only what was in the zeroing cuvette.\n• Beer–Lambert: once the background is zeroed, A = εbc for the analyte alone.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2: the zero is set with reagent blank in a clean cuvette.","hl":"zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette"},{"text":"The blank holds the reagent but no iron, so zeroing subtracts whatever the reagent absorbs.","calc":["A(read) = A(sample) − A(reagent blank)","= A(Fe²⁺ complex)"]},{"text":"The scratched cuvette was not the one zeroed; it still reads 0.12 with blank alone.","hl":"filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.12","strike":["To remove the extra absorbance of the scratched wall"]},{"text":"Zeroing changes neither the absorbance of the complex nor where its peak sits.","strike":["To raise the absorbance of the iron complex","To shift the complex's peak toward 562 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: to cancel the absorbance of the reagent itself."}],"trap":"Thinking a blank cancels every flaw: it cancels only what was in its own cuvette."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"zeroed with reagent blank","note":"reference = all but iron"},{"t":"in a clean glass cuvette","note":"zero set on this cuvette"},{"t":"filled with reagent blank alone","note":"a flaw the zero missed"}]},{"text":"**The reference matches the sample minus the analyte.** Zeroing on it removes the reagent's share.","calc":["A(sample) = A(complex) + A(reagent)","zero on reagent → read A(complex)"]},{"text":"**Why matched cuvettes:** if the cuvettes used for sample and reference are matched, the cuvette's share cancels and only the analyte is left."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the reference holds everything but the analyte, so zeroing on it cancels the background; any extra absorber outside the reference (a scratch, a different cuvette) must be measured and subtracted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-06a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"photon energy E = hf = hc/λ","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3","pe3-cp:19#1","pe3-cp:19#2","pe3-cp:19#3","pe5-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:6","pe3-cp:19","pe5-cp:56"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.12.","q":"About how much energy does one photon at the complex's absorption peak carry?","choices":["0.45 eV","2.2 eV","22 eV","3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]},{"f":"Visible photons carry about 1.6–3.3 eV","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"E (eV) ≈ 1240 ÷ λ (nm) = 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ 2.2 eV, inside the visible range of about 1.8–3.1 eV."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives the peak, 562 nm, and hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm. E = hc/λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ **2.2 eV**. In joules that is about 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which must still be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV.","how":{"ask":"Convert the complex's absorption wavelength into photon energy in electron-volts.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter wavelength, more energy.\n• hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, so **E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm)**.\n• In joules: E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸)/λ in m; divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV.\n• Visible photons: about 1.8–3.1 eV.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds both numbers: the peak wavelength and hc in eV·nm.","hl":"absorbs most strongly at 562 nm"},{"text":"Use the eV·nm shortcut.","hl":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","calc":["E = 1240 ÷ 562 ≈ **2.2 eV**"]},{"text":"A joule value labeled eV and a tenfold slip both fall outside the visible range.","strike":["3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV","22 eV"]},{"text":"Dividing λ by hc inverts E = hc/λ.","strike":["0.45 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.2 eV."}],"trap":"Stopping at joules: hc/λ in SI units gives about 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which still has to be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"562 nm","note":"λ to use"},{"t":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","note":"shortcut"},{"t":"magenta complex","note":"absorbs green light"}]},{"text":"**Long route, in joules.**","calc":["E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (562 × 10⁻⁹ m) ≈ 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ 2.2 eV"]},{"text":"**Sanity check.** Red ≈ 700 nm ≈ 1.8 eV; violet ≈ 400 nm ≈ 3.1 eV. Green light, which a magenta solution absorbs, must fall in between."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm); to go from joules to eV, divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:19","pe5-cp:56"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-19a","cpt-pe5-56a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-07a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"standard curve + dilution factor","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:7#1","pe4-cp:7#2","pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:7"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.12.","q":"The first well's sample is diluted 5-fold with reagent blank and then reads A = 0.33. What was its original iron concentration?","choices":["2.4 μM","12 μM","34 μM","60 μM"],"ans":3,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A ∝ c: the standards give 0.22 per 8 μM. 0.33 is 1.5 × 0.22, so 1.5 × 8 = 12 μM diluted; × 5 = 60 μM."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the line: 0.22 per 8 μM, or **0.0275 per μM**. Sentence 4's reading, 0.93, lies above the top standard, so only the diluted reading is used: 0.33 ÷ 0.0275 = **12 μM** in the cuvette. Undo the 5-fold dilution: 12 × 5 = **60 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Read the in-range reading off the standard line, then multiply by the dilution factor.","know":"• **Beer's law**: A = εbc, so at a fixed path **A ∝ c**; the standards give the slope.\n• Only **interpolate**: a reading above the top standard must be diluted into range, never read off an extended line.\n• Original concentration = **diluted concentration × dilution factor**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the standards fix a straight line through the origin, so find its slope.","hl":"Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66","calc":["slope = 0.22 ÷ 8 = **0.0275 per μM**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Iron standards with ferrozine, read in the clean cuvette","x":{"label":"Fe²⁺ (μM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":8},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[8,0.22],[16,0.44],[24,0.66]]}]}},{"text":"Sentence 4 shows the first reading lies above the top standard, outside the proven range, so it is not used.","hl":"reads A = 0.93, above the top standard"},{"text":"Use the diluted reading from the stem, which falls inside the range.","hl":"reads A = 0.33","calc":["c(diluted) = 0.33 ÷ 0.0275 = **12 μM**"]},{"text":"Undo the dilution.","hl":"diluted 5-fold","calc":["c(original) = 12 μM × 5"]},{"text":"Dividing by 5 runs the dilution backwards, 12 μM forgets it, and 34 μM extrapolates the line to the out-of-range 0.93.","strike":["2.4 μM","12 μM","34 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 60 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the concentration in the cuvette: the sample was diluted, so multiply by 5."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8, 16 and 24 μM","note":"standards"},{"t":"0.22, 0.44 and 0.66","note":"their absorbances"},{"t":"above the top standard","note":"do not extrapolate"},{"t":"diluted 5-fold","note":"multiply back by 5"}]},{"text":"**Absorbance to concentration** with the standards' slope.","calc":["0.33 ÷ 0.0275 per μM = 12 μM"]},{"text":"**Undo the dilution.**","calc":["12 μM × 5 = 60 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** this is why the first reading was not used. A line is trusted only between its lowest and highest standards; a sample above the top standard is diluted into range, reread, and the result multiplied back."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** original concentration = (in-range absorbance ÷ slope of the standards) × dilution factor; never read a value off the line beyond the top standard."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-08a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"standard curve + dilution factor","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:8"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P2","passage":"Iron in well water can be measured with ferrozine, which binds Fe²⁺ to form a magenta complex that absorbs most strongly at 562 nm (hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm). The spectrophotometer is zeroed with reagent blank in a clean glass cuvette. Iron standards of 8, 16 and 24 μM, read in that same cuvette, give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66, a straight line through the origin. A sample from a well near a rusted pipe reads A = 0.93, above the top standard. A sample from a second well was read in a cuvette with a scratched wall and gave A = 0.71. When the scratched cuvette was rinsed and filled with reagent blank alone, it read A = 0.12.","q":"Which procedure gives the most reliable iron value for the sample from the well near the rusted pipe?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Iron standards with ferrozine, read in the clean cuvette","x":{"label":"Fe²⁺ (μM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":8},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[8,0.22],[16,0.44],[24,0.66]]}]},"choices":["Extrapolate the calibration line beyond A = 0.66","Report it as 24 μM, the top standard","Dilute it twofold, reread it and multiply by 2","Read it in a cuvette with a longer light path"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Don't extrapolate past the standards","c":[0]},{"f":"Longer path raises absorbance further","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Bring the reading inside the standards: 0.93 ÷ 2 ≈ 0.47, inside 0–0.66. Read that off the line, then multiply by 2."},"exp":"Sentence 3 shows the standards stop at A = 0.66, and sentence 4 says the sample reads 0.93, above them. The line is proven only between its standards, so **dilute** the sample by a known factor into that range, reread it, read the concentration off the line, and **multiply by the dilution factor**.","how":{"ask":"Choose the step that brings the sample's reading into the range the standards cover, then recovers the original concentration.","know":"• A calibration line is trusted only **between** its lowest and highest standards (interpolation).\n• Above the top standard: **dilute** (or shorten the path) to bring A into range.\n• Multiply the result by the dilution factor to get the original concentration.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the sample reads above the top standard.","hl":"reads A = 0.93, above the top standard"},{"text":"Sentence 3 shows the standards reach only A = 0.66.","hl":"give A = 0.22, 0.44 and 0.66"},{"text":"A longer path makes A larger still, and the line beyond 0.66 was never tested.","strike":["Read it in a cuvette with a longer light path","Extrapolate the calibration line beyond A = 0.66"]},{"text":"The sample holds more iron than the top standard, so it cannot be reported as that standard.","strike":["Report it as 24 μM, the top standard"]},{"text":"A twofold dilution puts it near A ≈ 0.47, inside the range; multiply the result by 2.","calc":["0.93 ÷ 2 ≈ 0.47 → ≈ 17 μM on the line","17 μM × 2 ≈ 34 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: Dilute it twofold, reread it and multiply by 2."}],"trap":"Extending the calibration line: Beer's law can bend at high absorbance, and no standard checks it there."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a straight line through the origin","note":"proven only up to the top standard"},{"t":"24 μM","note":"top standard"},{"t":"above the top standard","note":"out of range"}]},{"text":"**Interpolate, never extrapolate.** The standards prove the line only between 0 and 0.66; above that it may bend."},{"text":"**Bring it into range.** Dilute by a known factor (or shorten the path), reread, convert with the line, then multiply back."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reading above the top standard is diluted into range, reread, and multiplied by the dilution factor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-09a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"boiling point / volatility order","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3","pe1-cp:8#3","pe1-cp:8#1","pe1-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:9","pe1-cp:8"],"q":"The four isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O shown are separated by fractional distillation at atmospheric pressure. Which isomer leaves the column first?","choices":["Tetrahydropyran","Pentan-3-one","Pentanal","Cyclopentyl alcohol"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Alcohols H-bond, raising boiling point","c":[3]},{"f":"Ethers are less polar than carbonyls","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal masses: the fraction that comes over first is the one with the weakest attractions. No O–H and only a weak C–O–C dipole: the cyclic ether."},"exp":"Fractional distillation separates liquids by **boiling point**; the lowest-boiling one distills first. All four have the same formula, so dispersion forces are similar and polarity decides. Cyclopentanol **hydrogen-bonds** (bp ≈ 140 °C). The ketone and aldehyde have strong C=O dipoles (bp ≈ 102–103 °C). The cyclic ether tetrahydropyran has only a weak C–O–C dipole and no O–H (bp ≈ 88 °C), so it comes over **first**.","how":{"ask":"Find the isomer with the lowest boiling point, since it vaporizes and reaches the top of the column first.","know":"• Fractional distillation separates by **boiling point**.\n• At similar molar mass: **H-bonding** (alcohols, acids, amines) > **dipole–dipole** (ketones, aldehydes) > weak dipoles (ethers, alkyl halides).\n• An alcohol boils far higher than an ether or alkyl halide of similar mass.","steps":[{"text":"All four share a formula, so their masses are equal.","hl":"formula C₅H₁₀O"},{"text":"Classify each by its strongest attraction.","calc":["cyclopentanol: O–H → H-bonds","3-pentanone, pentanal: C=O → strong dipoles","tetrahydropyran: C–O–C → weak dipole only"]},{"text":"The H-bonding alcohol boils highest, so it comes over last.","strike":["Cyclopentyl alcohol"]},{"text":"The C=O compounds boil above the ether.","strike":["Pentan-3-one","Pentanal"]},{"text":"Answer: Tetrahydropyran."}],"trap":"Thinking a ring or a compact shape raises the boiling point here: with equal masses, polarity and H-bonding decide the order."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O","note":"equal molar mass"},{"t":"fractional distillation","note":"separates by boiling point"},{"t":"leaves the column first","note":"lowest boiling point"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.** H-bonding beats dipole–dipole, which beats a weak dipole.","fig":"cyclopentanol","calc":["bp: cyclopentanol ≈ 140 °C","pentanal ≈ 103 °C, 3-pentanone ≈ 102 °C","tetrahydropyran ≈ 88 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** an alcohol boils well above an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, because only the alcohol has an O–H to hydrogen-bond."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a distillation, the liquid with the weakest intermolecular forces (lowest boiling point) collects first."}],"choiceFigs":["tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","pentanal","cyclopentanol"],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:8"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-08d"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"alcohol oxidation (1°/2°/3°)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:10#1","pe4-cp:10#2","pe4-cp:10#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:10"],"q":"Four alcohols are each heated with chromic acid, H₂CrO₄, a strong oxidizing agent. Which alcohol is NOT oxidized?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Primary and secondary alcohols both oxidize","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Chromic acid needs an H on the C bearing the OH. A tertiary alcohol (that carbon has three C's, no H) won't oxidize: 2-methylbutan-2-ol."},"exp":"Oxidizing an alcohol removes **the H on the carbon bearing the OH** and turns C–OH into C=O. In **2-methylbutan-2-ol** that carbon carries three carbon groups and no H, so it is a tertiary alcohol and resists oxidation. Pentan-1-ol (primary) goes to pentanal and then pentanoic acid; the two secondary alcohols become ketones.","how":{"ask":"Classify each alcohol as 1°, 2° or 3° by counting the carbons on the carbinol carbon, then find the one with no H there.","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon holding the OH. Oxidation needs an **H on that carbon**.\n• **1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid** (a strong oxidant like H₂CrO₄ goes all the way to the acid).\n• **2° alcohol → ketone** (stops there).\n• **3° alcohol → no reaction** (no H on the carbinol carbon).","steps":[{"text":"The reagent is a strong oxidant, so any alcohol that can be oxidized will be.","hl":"chromic acid, H₂CrO₄"},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol: the OH carbon is CH₂, bonded to one carbon, so it is primary. It is oxidized to an aldehyde and then on to the acid.","calc":["CH₃(CH₂)₃CH₂OH → CH₃(CH₂)₃CHO → CH₃(CH₂)₃COOH"],"strike":["Pentan-1-ol"]},{"text":"Pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon is CH, bonded to two carbons, so both are secondary and become ketones.","calc":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃ → pentan-2-one","CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂ → 3-methylbutan-2-one"],"strike":["Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"2-Methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon holds two CH₃ groups and an ethyl, three carbons and no H. It is tertiary and cannot form a C=O without breaking a C–C bond.","calc":["(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃: carbinol C has 0 H → no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-Methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Picking a branched secondary alcohol because it looks crowded: branching elsewhere does not matter; only the H on the carbinol carbon does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"chromic acid","note":"strong oxidant"},{"t":"NOT oxidized","note":"look for the alcohol that cannot lose an H"}]},{"text":"**Find the carbinol carbon** in each name: the number before \"-ol\" tells you which carbon holds the OH. Count how many carbons are bonded to it: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°."},{"text":"**Primary alcohols** have two H's on the carbinol carbon: they go to an **aldehyde**, and a strong oxidant carries them on to the **carboxylic acid**.","calc":["RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH"]},{"text":"**Secondary alcohols** have one H there: they become a **ketone** and stop.","calc":["R₂CHOH → R₂C=O"]},{"text":"**Tertiary alcohols** have no H there: nothing to remove, so **no oxidation**.","calc":["R₃COH → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon carries an H: 1° gives aldehyde then acid, 2° gives ketone, 3° does not react."}],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-11a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"IUPAC alkene naming (E/Z)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:11#1","pe4-cp:11#2","pe4-cp:11#3","pe4-cp:11#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:11"],"q":"In CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃, shown, the bromine and the terminal CH₃ bonded to the CH carbon are cis to each other. What is the compound's name?","choices":["(Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Lowest locant goes to the C=C","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Br outranks any carbon group","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E/Z compares the higher-priority group on each alkene C, not the main chain. C2: CH₃ > H; C3: Br > ethyl. Br cis to CH₃ → Z; lowest C=C locant → pent-2-ene."},"exp":"The five-carbon chain holds the C=C, and numbering from the CH₃–CH= end puts it at **C2**, with Br on **C3**: 3-bromopent-2-ene. On C2, **CH₃ beats H**; on C3, **Br (atomic number 35) beats the ethyl carbon (6)**. Those two winners are on the same side, so the alkene is **Z**, even though the methyl and the ethyl are on opposite sides.","how":{"ask":"Number the chain to give the C=C the lowest locant, name the substituent, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon.","know":"• **Parent chain**: the longest chain that contains the C=C, numbered from the end **nearer the C=C**.\n• **Substituents** get the locant of the carbon they sit on.\n• **CIP priority**: on each alkene carbon, the atom of higher atomic number wins.\n• **Z** = the two winners on the **same side**; **E** = opposite sides.","steps":[{"text":"The longest chain through the C=C has five carbons. From the left, the C=C starts at C2; from the right, at C3. Number from the left.","hl":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","calc":["left: C1H₃–C2H=C3(Br)–C4H₂–C5H₃ → C=C at 2 ✓","right: C=C at 3 ✗"]},{"text":"Numbering from the ethyl end gives the C=C a locant of 3, which is higher than needed.","strike":["(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"]},{"text":"The only substituent is Br, on C3.","calc":["3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank the groups on each alkene carbon by the atomic number of the first atom.","calc":["C2: CH₃ (C, 6) > H (1) → CH₃ wins","C3: Br (35) > CH₂CH₃ (C, 6) → Br wins"]},{"text":"The winners, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side, so the alkene is Z. Calling it E comes from comparing the two alkyl groups instead of Br.","hl":"are cis to each other","strike":["(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: (Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene."}],"trap":"Reading E/Z as cis/trans of the carbon chain: the ethyl and the methyl are on opposite sides, but Br outranks the ethyl, so the winners are cis and the name is Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","note":"five carbons, one Br on an alkene carbon"},{"t":"the bromine and the terminal CH₃","note":"the groups whose positions are given"},{"t":"cis to each other","note":"same side; compare with the priorities"}]},{"text":"**Pick and number the chain.** Take the longest chain that includes both alkene carbons, and number from the end that reaches the C=C first.","calc":["C=C at 2 from the left vs 3 from the right → left"]},{"text":"**Name the substituents** with their locants and put them in front of the parent, in alphabetical order if there are several.","calc":["Br on C3 → 3-bromo + pent-2-ene"]},{"text":"**Rank by CIP.** On each alkene carbon, compare the two attached atoms; higher atomic number wins. If they tie, move out to the next atoms.","calc":["Br (35) > C (6) > H (1)"]},{"text":"**Assign Z or E.** Winners on the same side = Z (zusammen); on opposite sides = E (entgegen)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the longest chain containing the C=C from its nearer end, name the substituents, then put Z when the higher-priority groups on the two alkene carbons share a side and E when they do not."}],"fig":"cpt-z-3-bromopent-2-ene"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-12a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"vector sum magnitude range","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:12#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:12"],"q":"A hiker walks 11 km in a straight line, turns through any angle, then walks 7 km more. The hiker's distance from the start could NOT be:","choices":["4 km","10 km","18 km","19 km"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Resultant spans difference through sum","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Test the two extremes: turn straight back gives 11 − 7 = 4 km; keep going gives 11 + 7 = 18 km. Every result lies in 4–18, so 19 km can't happen."},"exp":"Two displacements add as vectors: the resultant lies between **|11 − 7| = 4 km** (turning back) and **11 + 7 = 18 km** (going straight on). **19 km** is outside that range, so it can't happen.","how":{"ask":"Find the smallest and largest possible magnitudes of the vector sum.","know":"• For vectors of magnitudes A and B: **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Minimum when they point opposite (180°), maximum when parallel (0°); every value between is reachable at some angle.","steps":[{"text":"The two legs are vectors of 11 km and 7 km at an adjustable angle.","hl":"turns through any angle"},{"text":"Compute the two extremes.","calc":["min = |11 − 7| = **4 km** (walks back)","max = 11 + 7 = **18 km** (keeps straight)","at 90°: √(11² + 7²) = √170 ≈ 13 km"]},{"text":"4 km and 18 km are the ends of the range, and 10 km lies inside it.","strike":["4 km","18 km","10 km"]},{"text":"Answer: 19 km."}],"trap":"Thinking the endpoints are impossible: turning fully back or not at all gives exactly 4 km or 18 km."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"11 km","note":"vector A"},{"t":"7 km","note":"vector B"},{"t":"any angle","note":"the sum can vary"},{"t":"could NOT","note":"look outside the range"}]},{"text":"**Smallest sum:** the vectors point opposite, so they subtract.","calc":["|A − B| = |11 − 7| = 4 km"]},{"text":"**Largest sum:** the vectors point the same way, so they add.","calc":["A + B = 11 + 7 = 18 km"]},{"text":"**Everything between is possible** at some angle; anything outside is not.","calc":["4 km ≤ R ≤ 18 km","19 km > 18 km → impossible"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** |A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B; a magnitude outside that range is impossible."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-13a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"curvature, focal length and lens power","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:13#1","pe4-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:13","sample-cp:43"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects into focus ahead of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 8.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"Flattening the central cornea with the laser has which effect on its radius of curvature and its power?","choices":["The radius increases, so the power decreases","The radius increases, so the power increases","The radius decreases, so the power decreases","The radius decreases, so the power increases"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Flatter surface has larger radius","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Larger radius means weaker lens","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flatter = part of a bigger sphere = larger R. Less curvature bends light less → longer f → lower power (P = 1/f), which moves the focus back."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the eyes focus **ahead of** the retina, so they converge light too strongly. Sentence 4 makes the cornea **flatter**: a flatter surface is part of a bigger sphere, so its **radius of curvature grows**. A larger radius bends light less, giving a longer focal length and **lower power** (P = 1/f), which moves the image back onto the retina.","how":{"ask":"Decide how flattening changes the cornea's radius of curvature and its focusing power.","know":"• **Flatter = larger radius of curvature** (a flat surface has an infinite radius).\n• For a refracting surface or lens, **f grows with R**; power P = 1/f falls.\n• **Myopia**: the eye is too converging, so the image forms in front of the retina; weakening it moves the focus back.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the laser makes the central surface flatter.","hl":"The central corneal surface is then made flatter"},{"text":"Flatter means less curved, part of a bigger sphere, so R grows.","calc":["flatter → larger R"]},{"text":"A smaller radius would be a steeper, more curved surface.","strike":["The radius decreases, so the power decreases","The radius decreases, so the power increases"]},{"text":"A larger R bends light less: f grows and power falls. Sentence 1 shows the eye was too strong, so this is the fix.","hl":"into focus ahead of the retina","strike":["The radius increases, so the power increases"]},{"text":"Answer: The radius increases, so the power decreases."}],"trap":"Pairing a larger radius with more power: a flatter surface is a weaker refractor, not a stronger one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"nearsighted","note":"too converging"},{"t":"ahead of the retina","note":"focus too short"},{"t":"made flatter","note":"less curved surface"}]},{"text":"**Flatter means a bigger circle.** A gently curved surface belongs to a sphere with a large radius.","calc":["more curved → small R","flatter → large R"]},{"text":"**Bigger radius, weaker focusing.** Focal length rises with R, so the cornea converges light less.","calc":["R up → f up → power down"]},{"text":"**Also:** a nearsighted eye focuses too strongly, in front of the retina; a longer focal length moves the image back onto the retina."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** flattening a refracting surface raises its radius of curvature, lengthens its focal length and lowers its power."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"uniform field between plates (E = V/d)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:14"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects into focus ahead of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 8.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"How strong is the electric field between the electrodes during the electroporation pulse?","choices":["0.58 V/m","9.0 V/m","9.0 × 10³ V/m","9.0 × 10⁶ V/m"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Convert first: 8.0 mm = 8.0 × 10⁻³ m. Then E = 72 ÷ (8.0 × 10⁻³) = 9.0 × 10³ V/m."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives parallel plates, so the field is uniform: **E = V/d**. With d = 8.0 mm = 8.0 × 10⁻³ m, E = 72 V ÷ 8.0 × 10⁻³ m = **9.0 × 10³ V/m**.","how":{"ask":"Find the uniform field between the two plates from the voltage and the gap.","know":"• Parallel plates: **E = V/d** (V/m = N/C).\n• **mm = 10⁻³ m**; kV = 10³ V.\n• Pulse timing does not enter E.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: parallel plates, the gap and the voltage.","hl":"two parallel plate electrodes 8.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse","calc":["d = 8.0 × 10⁻³ m","V = 72 V"]},{"text":"Divide voltage by gap.","calc":["E = 72 ÷ (8.0 × 10⁻³) = **9.0 × 10³ V/m**"]},{"text":"A gap left in mm, or read as μm, puts the power of ten off by 10³.","strike":["9.0 V/m","9.0 × 10⁶ V/m"]},{"text":"Multiplying V by d gives V·m, not a field.","strike":["0.58 V/m"]},{"text":"Answer: 9.0 × 10³ V/m."}],"trap":"Using the gap in millimetres: E = V/d needs d in metres, or the power of ten is off by 10³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"parallel plate electrodes","note":"uniform field, E = V/d"},{"t":"8.0 mm apart","note":"d, convert to m"},{"t":"72 V","note":"V"}]},{"text":"**Prefixes:** kilo = 10³, milli = 10⁻³, micro = 10⁻⁶."},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["E = 72 V ÷ (8.0 × 10⁻³ m)","= 9.0 × 10³ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for parallel plates, E = V/d in V/m; convert kV to V and mm to m before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"pulse period and frequency","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:15","pe3-cp:18"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects into focus ahead of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 8.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"What are the laser's pulse repetition frequency and average output power?","choices":["25 Hz; 4.5 W","0.025 Hz; 4.5 mW","25 Hz; 15 MW","83 MHz; 15 MW"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Pulse length sets peak power only","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"T = 40 ms = 0.040 s → f = 1/0.040 = 25 Hz. Average P = 0.18 J × 25 /s = 4.5 W."},"exp":"Sentence 5 says a new pulse **starts every 40 ms**, so T = 0.040 s and f = 1/T = **25 Hz**. Each pulse carries 0.18 J, so the average power is 0.18 J × 25 s⁻¹ = **4.5 W**. The 12 ns length gives the peak power instead: 0.18 J ÷ 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ s = 1.5 × 10⁷ W.","how":{"ask":"Find how often pulses start, then the energy delivered per second on average.","know":"• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds (ms = 10⁻³ s).\n• **Average power** = energy per pulse × f.\n• **Peak power** = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration (much larger).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the start-to-start time, the pulse length and the energy per pulse.","hl":"starts a new pulse every 40 ms","calc":["T = 0.040 s → f = 1/0.040 = **25 Hz**"]},{"text":"Average power is energy per pulse times pulses per second.","hl":"delivers 0.18 J","calc":["P(avg) = 0.18 J × 25 /s"]},{"text":"Leaving ms unconverted shrinks both by 10³.","strike":["0.025 Hz; 4.5 mW"]},{"text":"Dividing by the 12 ns pulse length gives the peak power, and 1/12 ns is not how often pulses start.","strike":["25 Hz; 15 MW","83 MHz; 15 MW"]},{"text":"Answer: 25 Hz; 4.5 W."}],"trap":"Using the pulse duration where the period belongs: the 12 ns is how long each flash lasts, not how often flashes repeat."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"every 40 ms","note":"period T"},{"t":"lasts 12 ns","note":"pulse duration, for peak power"},{"t":"0.18 J","note":"energy per pulse"}]},{"text":"**Two different powers.**","calc":["average: 0.18 J × 25 Hz = 4.5 W","peak: 0.18 J ÷ 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ s = 1.5 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = 1/(start-to-start time); average power = energy per pulse × f, and peak power = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:18"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-18a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-16a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"average vs peak power of pulses","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:16"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P3","passage":"In a study of myopia, the eyes of nearsighted rabbits bring light from distant objects into focus ahead of the retina. The team first delivers a gene into the outer corneal cells by electroporation. Each cornea sits between two parallel plate electrodes 8.0 mm apart, and a 72 V pulse is applied across them. The central corneal surface is then made flatter with a pulsed infrared laser. The laser starts a new pulse every 40 ms, and each pulse lasts 12 ns and delivers 0.18 J. Heat deposited in the treated spot spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds. In trial runs at the same peak power, continuous output scorched the tissue around the spot, while pulsed output left it near body temperature.","q":"Compared with a continuous beam, the pulsed output spares the tissue around the treated spot because:","choices":["heat dissipates from the spot during the off-time.","its mean output is larger than a continuous beam's.","pulsing shortens the light's wavelength.","the pulses reflect off the cornea."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Pulsing lowers average power","c":[1]},{"f":"Pulsing doesn't change wavelength","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Heat spreads out in a few ms, but pulses start only every 40 ms: each gap is far longer than the time heat needs to leave the spot."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says deposited heat spreads into neighboring tissue **within a few milliseconds**, and sentence 5 shows the beam is dark for nearly all of each 40 ms cycle. So between pulses the heat leaves the spot and the surroundings stay near body temperature (sentence 7). A continuous beam gives no off-time, so heat piles up and scorches the tissue.","how":{"ask":"Explain the burn difference from how fast heat spreads compared with the gap between pulses.","know":"• Heat flows from a warm spot into cooler surroundings over time.\n• Any off-time longer than that spreading time lets the tissue cool before the next burst.\n• At equal peak power, the pulsed beam's **average power** is far lower.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: heat leaves the spot within a few milliseconds.","hl":"spreads into the neighboring tissue within a few milliseconds"},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the off-time: each pulse lasts ns but pulses start every 40 ms, so the beam is off almost all the time.","hl":"starts a new pulse every 40 ms","calc":["off-time ≈ 40 ms ≫ a few ms"]},{"text":"With so much off-time the mean output is lower, not higher.","strike":["its mean output is larger than a continuous beam's."]},{"text":"Timing does not alter wavelength or reflection.","strike":["pulsing shortens the light's wavelength.","the pulses reflect off the cornea."]},{"text":"Answer: heat dissipates from the spot during the off-time.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Neighboring tissue temperature (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":120,"step":40},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":35,"max":55,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[40,41],[80,45],[120,49]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[1,38],[40,37.2],[41,38.2],[80,37.4],[81,38.4],[120,37.6]]}]}}],"trap":"Equal peak power does not mean equal energy per second: the pulsed beam is dark between pulses, so heat has time to leave."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"within a few milliseconds","note":"heat spreading time"},{"t":"every 40 ms","note":"one full cycle"},{"t":"at the same peak power","note":"same strength while on"},{"t":"near body temperature","note":"the outcome to explain"}]},{"text":"**Heat spreads** from a hot spot into cooler surroundings over time. Each off-time lets it leave before the next pulse arrives; a continuous beam never pauses."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulsed light at the same peak power heats nearby tissue less, because the gaps let heat dissipate and the average temperature stays lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-17a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"intermolecular force types","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:17#1","pe4-cp:17#2","pe4-cp:17#3","pe4-cp:17#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:17","sample-cp:28"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"In its pure liquid state, the compound from the vent air that is held together partly by hydrogen bonding is:","choices":["Dimethylamine","Acetaldehyde","Hydrogen sulfide","Propane"],"choiceFigs":["1-propanol","tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","ethyl-acetate"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Only N–H, O–H, F–H donate","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each formula for N–H, O–H or F–H. Only (CH₃)₂NH has one, along with the N lone pair, so only it both donates and accepts."},"exp":"Sentence 1 gives the formula **(CH₃)₂NH**: an N–H bond plus a lone pair on N, so its molecules both donate and accept hydrogen bonds (on top of dipole–dipole and London forces). Acetaldehyde's C=O only accepts, hydrogen sulfide's S–H is too weakly polar, and propane has London forces alone.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound whose molecules carry an H on N, O or F as well as a lone pair.","know":"• Every molecule has **London dispersion** forces.\n• Polar molecules add **dipole–dipole** forces.\n• **Hydrogen bonding** is a strong dipole–dipole force that needs H on N, O or F in the same liquid.\n• Aldehydes, ketones and ethers accept H-bonds but cannot donate them.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds the clue: the formula shows an H bonded directly to N.","hl":"(CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor","fig":"diethylamine-nh-hl"},{"text":"Sentence 5 names the other candidates; check each for H on N, O or F.","hl":"acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide","calc":["dimethylamine: N–H ✓","acetaldehyde: CH₃–CHO, H only on C","hydrogen sulfide: H–S, S not N/O/F","propane: C–H only"]},{"text":"The aldehyde can only accept, the S–H is too weakly polar, and propane is nonpolar.","strike":["Acetaldehyde","Hydrogen sulfide","Propane"]},{"text":"Answer: Dimethylamine."}],"trap":"Counting any H or any lone pair: the donor H must sit on N, O or F, so an aldehyde's C=O or an S–H bond does not give a hydrogen-bonded liquid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"(CH₃)₂NH","note":"N–H: a donor"},{"t":"acetaldehyde","note":"C=O: acceptor only"},{"t":"hydrogen sulfide","note":"S–H: no H-bond"},{"t":"propane","note":"London only"}]},{"text":"**Donor + acceptor.** Only an O–H, N–H or F–H can donate, like the N–H of a secondary amine.","fig":"cpt-methanol"},{"text":"**Also:** ammonia, NH₃, likewise has N–H bonds and a lone pair, so liquid NH₃ has hydrogen bonds plus London forces; a polar molecule without N–H, O–H or F–H has only dipole–dipole and London forces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list the forces from the bottom up: London (always), dipole–dipole (if polar), hydrogen bonding (only with H on N, O or F)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-18a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"weak base [OH⁻] ≈ √(KbC)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:18#1","pe4-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:18"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"Approximately what is [(CH₃)₂NH₂⁺] in the spent scrub water at equilibrium?","choices":["1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M","5.4 × 10⁻⁴ M","1.0 × 10⁻² M","5.2 × 10⁻² M"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"x = √(Kb·C) = √(5.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² M."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴ and C ≈ 0.20 M. Each base that reacts gives one (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ and one OH⁻, so Kb ≈ x²/C and x = √(Kb·C) = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ **1.0 × 10⁻² M**. That is about 5 % of C, small enough for the approximation.","how":{"ask":"Use Kb and the base concentration to find how much of the base is protonated.","know":"• B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻, so Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] = **x²/C** when x is small next to C.\n• **x = √(Kb·C)**; x is both [OH⁻] and [BH⁺].\n• To take a square root of 10ⁿ, make n even first, then halve it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the base equation, its Kb and the scrub water's concentration.","hl":"Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine"},{"text":"Write the small-x expression and solve for x.","calc":["Kb = x² / C","x = √(Kb · C)","Kb · C = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 = 1.08 × 10⁻⁴"]},{"text":"The exponent is already even, so halve it.","calc":["√1.08 ≈ 1.0; √10⁻⁴ = 10⁻²","x ≈ **1.0 × 10⁻² M**"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: x is small next to C.","calc":["0.010 ÷ 0.20 = 5 %"]},{"text":"Dividing by C instead of multiplying inverts the setup; Kb·C has no square root; Kb alone is not a concentration.","strike":["5.2 × 10⁻² M","1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M","5.4 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 × 10⁻² M."}],"trap":"Stopping at Kb·C: that product is x², so its square root is the concentration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"a weak base in water","note":"only a little reacts"},{"t":"Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴","note":"base constant"},{"t":"about 0.20 M","note":"starting concentration C"},{"t":"(CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻","note":"made 1 : 1, so both equal x"}]},{"text":"**Write Kb with x.** Each base that reacts makes one BH⁺ and one OH⁻, and the base left is still about C.","calc":["Kb = x·x / (C − x) ≈ x² / C"]},{"text":"**Solve for x** and take the root of the power of ten.","calc":["x = √(1.08 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² M"]},{"text":"**Also:** with an odd exponent, shift one factor of 10 first, e.g. √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹) = √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a weak base, [OH⁻] = [BH⁺] ≈ √(Kb·C); make the power of ten even, then halve it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-19a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"solubility: like dissolves like","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:19#1","pe4-cp:19#2","pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:19"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"If the bench test were repeated with dry sulfur dioxide, SO₂, in the flask, the expected result would be that:","choices":["the pressure falls, since polar SO₂ dissolves.","the pressure holds, since SO₂ is a nonpolar gas.","the pressure holds, since no gas dissolves in water.","the pressure falls, since SO₂ forms a base in water."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"SO₂ is bent and polar","c":[1]},{"f":"SO₂ in water forms acid","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask one thing: is the new gas polar like dimethylamine or nonpolar like propane? SO₂ is bent and polar, so it dissolves and the pressure falls."},"exp":"Sentences 2 and 3 show the pattern: polar dimethylamine dissolves, so its pressure falls, while nonpolar propane passes through untouched. **SO₂ is bent**, with a lone pair on S, so it is **polar** (and it reacts with water to give an acid). It dissolves like dimethylamine did, and **the pressure falls**.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether SO₂ behaves like the dimethylamine or like the propane in water.","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: polar or H-bonding molecules dissolve in water; nonpolar ones barely do.\n• A sealed container's gas pressure falls only if gas molecules leave the gas phase.\n• SO₂ is bent (polar); CO₂ is linear (nonpolar); hydrocarbons are nonpolar.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the pressure fell because the gas dissolved in the water.","hl":"the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the contrast: the nonpolar gas is not captured.","hl":"lets the propane pass through almost untouched"},{"text":"Classify the replacement gas.","calc":["SO₂: bent, lone pair on S → polar","SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (an acid)"]},{"text":"The passage already shows a gas dissolving, and SO₂ gives an acid, not a base.","strike":["the pressure holds, since no gas dissolves in water.","the pressure falls, since SO₂ forms a base in water."]},{"text":"Bent SO₂ is polar, so it dissolves.","strike":["the pressure holds, since SO₂ is a nonpolar gas."]},{"text":"Answer: the pressure falls, since polar SO₂ dissolves."}],"trap":"Assuming any swap of the gas stops the effect: the outcome depends only on whether the new gas is polar (or water-reactive) enough to dissolve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"captures the dimethylamine","note":"polar: dissolves"},{"t":"propane pass through almost untouched","note":"nonpolar: stays a gas"},{"t":"fell sharply","note":"gas left the gas phase"}]},{"text":"**Link solubility to pressure.** Dissolved gas no longer pushes on the walls; gas that stays above the water keeps the pressure up."},{"text":"**Contrast:** a nonpolar gas such as propane or methane barely dissolves in water, so its pressure stays near atmospheric."},{"text":"**Also:** the same rule ranks analogs of a drug. Replacing a ring C–H with N (benzene ring → pyridine ring), or adding an O or N that water can H-bond to, raises solubility; adding CH₃ groups or trading a polar group for C–H or C–Cl lowers it.","fig":"pyridine"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a polar or water-reactive gas dissolves and its pressure falls; a nonpolar gas barely dissolves and its pressure holds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-20a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ionization-energy jump → group/charge","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3","pe3-cp:54#1","pe3-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:20","pe3-cp:54"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P4","passage":"A fish-processing plant vents air that carries dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH, a gas with a strong fishy odor, along with propane from its heaters. The vent stream is bubbled through a water scrubber, which captures the dimethylamine but lets the propane pass through almost untouched. In a bench test, a few milliliters of water were squirted into a sealed flask of dry dimethylamine, and the pressure inside the flask fell sharply within seconds. Dimethylamine is a weak base in water, (CH₃)₂NH + H₂O ⇌ (CH₃)₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻, with Kb = 5.4 × 10⁻⁴, and the spent scrub water is about 0.20 M in dimethylamine. Traces of acetaldehyde and hydrogen sulfide were also found in the vent air. Before analysis, gas samples are dried over pellets of the oxide of a metal M, whose successive ionization energies were measured in a separate study. The plant's chemists want to know which ion M forms in that oxide.","q":"Based on the ionization energies in the chart, M belongs to:","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of metal M","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":10000,"step":2000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃","IE₄"],"series":[{"name":"M","values":[496,4560,6910,9540]}]},"choices":["group 1, forming M⁺.","group 2, forming M²⁺.","group 13, forming M³⁺.","group 14, forming M⁴⁺."],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Look for the cliff: IE₁ is low, then IE₂ is about nine times IE₁. One valence electron → group 1, 1+ ion."},"exp":"Sentence 6 points to M's successive ionization energies. In the chart, **IE₂/IE₁ ≈ 9.2**, while IE₃/IE₂ ≈ 1.5 and IE₄/IE₃ ≈ 1.4: the jump comes after one electron, so M has one valence electron. It is an **alkali metal (group 1)** and forms **M⁺**, so its oxide is M₂O.","how":{"ask":"Count how many electrons come off before the large jump in ionization energy.","know":"• Group 1 (alkali metals) jump after IE₁ → 1+; group 2 (alkaline earth metals) after IE₂ → 2+; group 13 after IE₃ → 3+.\n• Halogens (group 17) gain an electron; metalloids sit on the stair-step line; transition metals form several ions.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: M is identified by its successive ionization energies, so read the chart for the jump.","hl":"whose successive ionization energies were measured","calc":["IE₁ ≈ 496, IE₂ ≈ 4560","IE₃ ≈ 6910, IE₄ ≈ 9540 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Find the largest ratio between neighbors.","calc":["IE₂/IE₁ ≈ 9.2 ← the jump","IE₃/IE₂ ≈ 1.5","IE₄/IE₃ ≈ 1.4"]},{"text":"One electron comes off before the jump, so two, three or four valence electrons are ruled out.","strike":["group 2, forming M²⁺.","group 13, forming M³⁺.","group 14, forming M⁴⁺."]},{"text":"Answer: group 1, forming M⁺."}],"trap":"Picking the largest bar: IE₄ is the biggest value, but the group comes from where the jump happens, not from the tallest bar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"successive ionization energies","note":"look for the jump"},{"t":"which ion M forms","note":"charge = electrons before the jump"},{"t":"oxide of a metal M","note":"M₂O for a 1+ ion"}]},{"text":"**Why the jump:** after the valence electrons are gone, the next one comes from a filled inner shell, much closer to the nucleus."},{"text":"**Group names:** alkali metals (group 1, 1+), alkaline earth metals (group 2, 2+), transition metals (groups 3–12), halogens (group 17, 1−), noble gases (group 18). Sodium is an alkali metal and forms Na⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the number of electrons removed before the big ionization-energy jump equals the valence count, the group (for main-group metals) and the usual positive charge."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:54","pe4-cp:52"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-54a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-22a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"metric prefix conversion","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:22#1","pe4-cp:22#2","pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:22"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"Which value should be entered in the database for V's dissociation constant?","choices":["0.027 μM","0.27 μM","270 μM","2.7 × 10⁵ μM"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Bigger unit gives smaller number","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"1 μM = 1000 nM; a bigger unit means a smaller number. 270 ÷ 1000 moves the decimal three places left."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the value in nM and says the database stores μM. Since 1 μM = 1000 nM, divide by 1000: 270 nM = **0.27 μM**. A bigger unit always gives a smaller number.","how":{"ask":"Convert the nanomolar value to micromolar.","know":"• **Prefixes**: m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹, p = 10⁻¹².\n• 1 μM = 1000 nM, so **nM → μM: divide by 1000**.\n• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**; **M → μM: × 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the value is in nM, and the database wants μM.","hl":"a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM"},{"text":"Write the prefixes as powers of ten.","calc":["n = 10⁻⁹, μ = 10⁻⁶: a factor of 10³ apart"]},{"text":"Divide by 1000.","calc":["270 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.27 μM**"]},{"text":"Dividing by 10⁴, or keeping or growing the number, misreads the factor of 1000 between nano and micro.","strike":["0.027 μM","270 μM","2.7 × 10⁵ μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.27 μM."}],"trap":"Multiplying by 1000: a micromole is larger than a nanomole, so the number must get smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"270 nM","note":"value in nanomolar"},{"t":"every concentration in μM","note":"target unit"}]},{"text":"**Write the prefixes as powers of ten.** n = 10⁻⁹ and μ = 10⁻⁶: they differ by 10³."},{"text":"**Bigger unit, smaller number.** nM → μM: divide by 1000."},{"text":"**Also:** from pH to μM: [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM. At pH 6.3, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·³ M ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ M = 0.5 μM."},{"text":"**Also:** in a weighing problem, chain the prefixes. Going in, μM → M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL → L (× 10⁻³); coming out, grams → milligrams (× 1000)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert through powers of ten: nM → μM ÷ 1000, M → μM × 10⁶, and [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-23a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"data table/graph interpretation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2","pe4-cp:21#1","pe4-cp:21#2","pe4-cp:21#3","pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:23","pe4-cp:21"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"In which glioblastoma line does V enhance TMZ the most?","choices":["Line G1","Line G2","Line G3","Line G4"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Enhancement is a ratio, not difference","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Divide each pair: G1 150/15 = 10, G2 330/110 = 3, G3 42/1.4 = 30, G4 64/64 = 1. The biggest fold change wins."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives each line's pair. Enhancement is **dose alone ÷ dose with V**: G1 = 10, G2 = 3, **G3 = 30**, G4 = 1. G2 has the largest drop in μM (220), but only a threefold change; G3's small numbers hide the largest fold change.","how":{"ask":"Compute the fold change in TMZ dose for each line and pick the largest.","know":"• Potency changes are compared as **ratios**, not differences.\n• Fold enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the enhancer; 1 means no effect.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: each line's dose without and with V.","hl":"line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM","calc":["G1: 150 ÷ 15 = 10","G2: 330 ÷ 110 = 3","G3: 42 ÷ 1.4 = 30","G4: 64 ÷ 64 = 1"]},{"text":"An unchanged dose means no enhancement at all.","strike":["Line G4"]},{"text":"The biggest μM drop is not the biggest fold change.","strike":["Line G2"]},{"text":"Tenfold is smaller than thirtyfold.","strike":["Line G1"]},{"text":"Answer: Line G3."}],"trap":"Subtracting instead of dividing: a line that starts at a high dose shows a big μM drop even when the fold change is small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"without and with V","note":"a before/after pair per line"},{"t":"330 μM and 110 μM","note":"big drop, small ratio"},{"t":"42 μM and 1.4 μM","note":"small numbers, big ratio"}]},{"text":"**Ratios, not differences.**","calc":["G2: 330 − 110 = 220 μM, but 330/110 = 3","G3: 42 − 1.4 ≈ 41 μM, but 42/1.4 = 30"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare how much a treatment helps, divide the before value by the after value in each case and rank the ratios."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe4-cp:21","pe4-cp:24"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe4-21a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-24a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"experimental evidence logic","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:24"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P5","passage":"Glioblastoma cells often survive the drug temozolomide (TMZ) by tolerating the DNA lesions it creates. After a dose of TMZ, these lesions take about 9 hours to build up to their full level. Compound V is harmless to cells without such lesions but kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. The TMZ concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was measured without and with V: line G1, 150 μM alone and 15 μM with V; line G2, 330 μM and 110 μM; line G3, 42 μM and 1.4 μM; and line G4, 64 μM both ways. For healthy astrocytes, the values were 120 μM alone and 114 μM with V. In a binding assay, V bound its target protein with a dissociation constant of 270 nM, and the team's database stores every concentration in μM.","q":"Which schedule should kill the most glioblastoma cells?","choices":["Give V first, then TMZ 9 hours later","Give TMZ and V at the same time","Give TMZ first, then V 9 hours later","Give TMZ first, then V 2 hours later"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"V needs lesions already formed","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask which drug makes the target and which hits it. TMZ makes the lesions (full after about 9 h); V hits lesioned cells and is short-lived. So TMZ first, V once the lesions peak."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says V kills only cells that already carry TMZ lesions and is gone within about 2 hours. Sentence 2 says the lesions take about **9 hours** to build. So give **TMZ first**, then V about 9 h later, when its targets are at their peak. Giving V first, together, or after only 2 h wastes it before the lesions exist.","how":{"ask":"Decide which drug creates the state the other needs, and how long that state takes to build.","know":"• A sequence works when the first agent sets up the state the second one needs.\n• Match the delay to how long the first agent takes to act.\n• A short-lived second agent must arrive after that state exists.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: V kills only lesioned cells, and it is cleared within about 2 hours.","hl":"kills cells whose DNA carries them, and V is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the build-up time of those lesions.","hl":"take about 9 hours to build up to their full level"},{"text":"V before TMZ reverses the dependence.","strike":["Give V first, then TMZ 9 hours later"]},{"text":"Together, or with only a 2 h gap, V is cleared before the lesions have built up.","strike":["Give TMZ and V at the same time","Give TMZ first, then V 2 hours later"]},{"text":"Answer: Give TMZ first, then V 9 hours later."}],"trap":"Assuming the add-in always goes first: here V exploits damage TMZ creates, so TMZ must lead."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"about 9 hours","note":"time for the target state to build"},{"t":"kills cells whose DNA carries them","note":"V needs TMZ's lesions"},{"t":"within about 2 hours","note":"V is short-lived"}]},{"text":"**Mechanism → order.** First the agent that sets up the state; then, once the state exists, the agent that exploits it. If an add-in must block a pathway before a drug can work, the add-in goes first instead."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** give first the agent whose effect the other depends on, and time the second dose to arrive when that effect is fully in place."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-25a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"σ/π bond counting","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:25#1","pe4-cp:25#2","pe4-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:25"],"q":"N₂ has an N–N distance of 110 pm, versus 145 pm for the N–N single bond in hydrazine, H₂N–NH₂. Which bonding description best accounts for the shorter bond in N₂?","choices":["A σ bond plus one π bond","A σ bond plus two π bonds","Three σ bonds","Two σ bonds plus one π bond"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"A multiple bond has one σ","c":[2,3]},{"f":"N₂ is triple-bonded","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each N has 5 valence electrons: N≡N with one lone pair each. A triple bond is always one σ and two π, and it is the shortest."},"exp":"N₂ is **N≡N**: a triple bond made of **a σ bond plus two π bonds**. Only one σ bond can lie along the internuclear axis; each extra shared pair is a π bond from sideways p-orbital overlap. More bonding pairs pull the atoms closer, so the triple bond (110 pm) is much shorter than hydrazine's single bond (145 pm), and also shorter than an N=N double bond (σ + π, about 125 pm).","how":{"ask":"Match the very short bond to its bond order, then break that bond into σ and π parts.","know":"• Single bond = 1 σ; double = 1 σ + 1 π; triple = 1 σ + 2 π.\n• Only one σ bond can join two atoms.\n• Higher bond order → shorter, stronger bond (C–O 143 pm vs C=O ≈ 122 pm is the same pattern).","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two lengths.","hl":"110 pm, versus 145 pm","calc":["110 pm ≪ 145 pm → more than a single bond"]},{"text":"Two atoms can share only one σ bond.","strike":["Three σ bonds","Two σ bonds plus one π bond"]},{"text":"Lewis structure of N₂: :N≡N: (10 valence electrons), a triple bond.","strike":["A σ bond plus one π bond"]},{"text":"Answer: A σ bond plus two π bonds."}],"trap":"Counting every shared pair as σ: after the first σ bond, each additional bond between the same atoms is π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"N₂","note":"N≡N"},{"t":"N–N single bond","note":"σ only"},{"t":"shorter bond","note":"higher bond order"}]},{"text":"**σ vs π.** σ = head-on overlap along the axis (one per atom pair); π = side-by-side p overlap above and below the axis."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbonyl C=O is one σ plus one π, and it is shorter than a C–O single bond; a C≡N or C≡C is one σ plus two π."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** single = σ, double = σ + π, triple = σ + 2π; the more bonds, the shorter the bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-26a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"periodic trends (IE, EA)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:26#1","pe4-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:26"],"q":"Four neutral atoms have the ground-state configurations 1s²2s²2p⁶, [Ne]3s²3p⁵, [Ne]3s² and [Ar]4s¹. Which atom most readily gains an extra electron, and why?","choices":["Ne, since its 2p subshell is not yet full","Cl, since one added electron fills its 3p subshell","Mg, since its 3s subshell has a vacancy","K, since a second electron can pair in its 4s orbital"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Ne's 2p subshell is full","c":[0]},{"f":"Metals tend to lose electrons","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for p⁵: one electron completes the subshell. That configuration belongs to the halogen."},"exp":"The [Ne]3s²3p⁵ atom is **chlorine**, a halogen: **one more electron fills its 3p subshell**, giving the stable [Ar] configuration and releasing much energy (the most favorable electron affinity of the four). Neon (2p⁶) is already full, so an extra electron must go into a new shell. Magnesium's 3s² is full too, and potassium, a metal, tends to lose its 4s electron rather than gain one.","how":{"ask":"Find the configuration that one added electron would complete, and say why that is favorable.","know":"• **Electron affinity** is most favorable for halogens (p⁵ → p⁶).\n• Noble gases (full p) and s² atoms have no low-energy vacancy.\n• Metals (low ionization energy) tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","steps":[{"text":"Name each configuration.","calc":["1s²2s²2p⁶ = Ne","[Ne]3s²3p⁵ = Cl","[Ne]3s² = Mg","[Ar]4s¹ = K"]},{"text":"Magnesium's 3s is full, so it has no vacancy there.","hl":"[Ne]3s²","strike":["Mg, since its 3s subshell has a vacancy"]},{"text":"Neon's 2p is already full; potassium, a metal, holds an extra electron weakly.","hl":"1s²2s²2p⁶","strike":["Ne, since its 2p subshell is not yet full","K, since a second electron can pair in its 4s orbital"]},{"text":"One electron completes chlorine's 3p subshell.","hl":"[Ne]3s²3p⁵"},{"text":"Answer: Cl, since one added electron fills its 3p subshell."}],"trap":"Thinking any atom with room in an s orbital gains electrons easily: metals have low ionization energies and prefer to lose electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3p⁵","note":"one short of full"},{"t":"1s²2s²2p⁶","note":"already full"},{"t":"most readily gains an extra electron","note":"most favorable electron affinity"}]},{"text":"**Electron affinity trend:** most favorable toward the upper right (halogens); noble gases are the exception, with no room in their shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the atom that gains an electron most readily is the one an extra electron would bring to a filled p subshell: a halogen, not a noble gas or a metal."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-27a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"phase diagram transitions","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:27#1","pe4-cp:27#2","pe4-cp:27#3","pe4-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:54#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:27"],"q":"Compound Z's triple point is 0.40 atm and 15 °C. Its vapor, held at 0.10 atm, is cooled until frost forms at −10 °C. Which process makes the frost?","choices":["Deposition","Freezing","Condensation","Sublimation"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Below triple-point pressure, no liquid exists","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Sublimation goes solid to gas","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm) no liquid can exist, so gas goes straight to solid: deposition, the reverse of sublimation."},"exp":"At 0.10 atm, below the triple-point pressure, Z has no liquid phase, so cooling the vapor reaches only the **solid–gas line** (at −10 °C). There the gas turns straight into solid: **deposition**, the reverse of sublimation.","how":{"ask":"Which boundary does the cooling vapor cross below the triple-point pressure, and in which direction does it cross it?","know":"• A **phase diagram** plots pressure (up) against temperature (across). Solid sits at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid in between.\n• Each line is where two phases coexist, crossed one way or the other: **solid–gas = sublimation (solid → gas) / deposition (gas → solid)**, **solid–liquid = melting / freezing**, **liquid–gas = vaporization / condensation**.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet; the **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the path with the triple point.","hl":"held at 0.10 atm","calc":["0.10 atm < 0.40 atm (below the triple-point pressure)","−10 °C < 15 °C (colder than the triple point)"]},{"text":"Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so the only border on this path is the solid–gas line, which the vapor reaches at −10 °C.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"Freezing and condensation both involve a liquid, which has no region below 0.40 atm.","strike":["Freezing","Condensation"]},{"text":"The vapor is cooling, so it crosses the solid–gas line from the gas side to the solid side. Sublimation (solid → gas) is the reverse crossing, made on warming.","hl":"frost forms at −10 °C","strike":["Sublimation"]},{"text":"Answer: deposition (gas → solid)."}],"trap":"Naming the right line but the wrong direction: sublimation and deposition share the solid–gas line, and vapor cooling into frost is deposition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"triple point","note":"where all three lines meet"},{"t":"0.40 atm and 15 °C","note":"triple-point P and T"},{"t":"held at 0.10 atm","note":"the path's pressure"},{"t":"frost forms at −10 °C","note":"solid appears from the gas"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** up = more pressure, right = hotter. Cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; liquid is the wedge in between.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"**Place the path:** cooling at a fixed 0.10 atm moves left along a horizontal line from the gas region into the solid region, crossing only the solid–gas line.","calc":["0.10 < 0.40 atm → no liquid region on this path","gas → solid at −10 °C: deposition"]},{"text":"**Also:** warming the solid back along the same line reverses the crossing: solid → gas with no liquid stage, which is **sublimation**."},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the solid–liquid line (nearly vertical, above the triple point) is **melting** or freezing; a point on the liquid–gas line is **boiling** (vaporization) or condensation, and at 1 atm it gives the normal boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** two points are special. The **triple point** is the one T and P where solid, liquid and gas all coexist. The **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find which line the path crosses and in which direction: solid–gas = sublimation (warming) or deposition (cooling), solid–liquid = melting or freezing, liquid–gas = vaporization or condensation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-28a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"photon energy E = hf = hc/λ","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:28"],"q":"An atom has energy levels E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV. Which downward transition emits the photon with the longest wavelength?","choices":["E₄ → E₃","E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Longest λ means smallest energy gap","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"λ ∝ 1/ΔE, so longest λ = smallest gap. Gaps: E₄→E₃ 0.5, E₃→E₂ 1.5, E₂→E₁ 5, E₄→E₁ 7 eV. Smallest is 0.5 eV: E₄ → E₃."},"exp":"**A photon carries the energy gap, and λ = hc/ΔE**, so the smallest gap gives the longest wavelength. The gaps are 0.5 eV (E₄ → E₃), 1.5 eV (E₃ → E₂), 5.0 eV (E₂ → E₁) and 7.0 eV (E₄ → E₁); the 0.5 eV drop emits light near 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm, the longest.","how":{"ask":"Find the energy gap of each transition and pick the smallest one.","know":"• An emitted photon's energy equals the gap: **ΔE = hc/λ**, so **λ = hc/ΔE**.\n• Wavelength is inversely proportional to the gap: **smallest ΔE → longest λ**.\n• Shortcut: λ(nm) ≈ 1240/ΔE(eV).","steps":[{"text":"List the levels.","hl":"E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV"},{"text":"Compute each choice's gap.","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = **0.5 eV**","E₃ → E₂: −5.5 − (−7.0) = 1.5 eV","E₂ → E₁: −7.0 − (−12.0) = 5.0 eV","E₄ → E₁: −5.0 − (−12.0) = 7.0 eV"]},{"text":"λ = hc/ΔE, so convert the smallest and largest gaps to wavelengths.","calc":["0.5 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm","7.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 7.0 ≈ 177 nm"]},{"text":"The three larger gaps all give shorter wavelengths; E₄ → E₁, the largest gap, gives the shortest.","strike":["E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: E₄ → E₃."}],"trap":"Picking the biggest drop: a large gap means a high-energy photon, which has the shortest wavelength, not the longest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₁ = −12.0 eV","note":"lowest level"},{"t":"E₄ = −5.0 eV","note":"highest level"},{"t":"longest wavelength","note":"smallest gap"}]},{"text":"**The photon carries the gap.** A falling electron emits one photon with ΔE = E(upper) − E(lower).","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = 0.5 eV"]},{"text":"**Flip to wavelength:** λ = hc/ΔE, so wavelength and gap are inversely related.","calc":["λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ ΔE(eV)","1.5 eV → ≈ 830 nm; 0.5 eV → ≈ 2500 nm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = hc/ΔE, so the transition with the smallest energy gap emits the longest wavelength, and the largest gap the shortest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-29a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"identify functional group","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:29#1","pe4-cp:29#2","pe4-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:29"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"Which nitrogen of Compound 12 belongs to a tertiary amine?","choices":["Nα","Nβ","Nγ","Nδ"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"N bonded to C=O isn't amine","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each N for a C=O (amide, carbamate) or C=N (aromatic N) first; of what is left, pick the N with three carbons and no H."},"exp":"Sentence 2 lists each nitrogen's neighbors. **Nδ** is bonded to three carbons (two ring CH₂ groups and the benzyl CH₂) and no H, with no C=O: a **tertiary amine**. Nα sits on a carbonyl that also bears an O, a **carbamate**; Nβ sits on a C=O flanked by carbon, an **amide**; Nγ is the aromatic ring N of **pyridine**.","how":{"ask":"Classify each nitrogen by its neighbors, then find the amine with three carbons on N.","know":"• **Amine**: N–C and N–H single bonds only.\n• 1°, 2°, 3° = one, two or three carbons on N.\n• **Amide**: N–C(=O)–C; **carbamate**: N–C(=O)–O; **imine/aromatic N**: C=N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: Nδ has three carbons, no H and no carbonyl.","hl":"is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group","calc":["3 C on N, no C=O → tertiary amine"]},{"text":"Nα sits on a carbonyl that also bears an O: a carbamate.","hl":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","strike":["Nα"]},{"text":"Nβ sits on a C=O whose other partner is carbon: an amide.","hl":"the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon","strike":["Nβ"]},{"text":"Nγ is double-bonded within an aromatic ring.","strike":["Nγ"]},{"text":"Answer: Nδ."}],"trap":"Calling an amide or carbamate nitrogen an amine: once N is bonded to a C=O carbon, it is no longer an amine, however many carbons it carries."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group","note":"three C on N"},{"t":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","note":"carbamate"},{"t":"whose other partner is a carbon","note":"amide"},{"t":"pyridine ring atom","note":"aromatic C=N"}]},{"text":"**A tertiary amine** has three carbon groups on N and a lone pair, like triethylamine.","fig":"triethylamine"},{"text":"**Also:** an ester is C(=O)–O–C; next to an N, a C=O makes an amide, and N–C(=O)–O makes a carbamate.","fig":"cpt-fix-amino-ester-co-hl"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** rule out amides and carbamates (N–C=O) and aromatic or imine N (C=N) first, then count carbons on the amine N: three carbons, no H = tertiary."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-30a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"carbonyl reduction to alcohol","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:30#1","pe4-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:30"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"The organic product that forms from methyl 4-oxopentanoate in the side-chain steps is:","fig":"cpt-methyl-4-oxopentanoate","choices":["γ-Valerolactone","δ-Valerolactone","Pentane-1,4-diol","Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-gamma-valerolactone","cpt-delta-valerolactone","cpt-pentane-14-diol","cpt-methyl-4-hydroxypentanoate"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"NaBH₄ doesn't reduce esters","c":[2]},{"f":"C4 of an ester is γ","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters. New OH on C4 (γ) closes onto C1: ring C1–C4 + O = 5 atoms. 5-ring lactone = γ, 6-ring = δ."},"exp":"Sentence 4 says NaBH₄ reduces the **ketone** at C4 to a secondary alcohol but leaves the **ester** alone. The new OH sits on the γ-carbon of the ester, so on warming with acid it attacks the ester carbonyl in the same molecule, expels **methanol** and closes a ring of C1–C4 plus the O: five atoms, **γ-valerolactone**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which carbonyl NaBH₄ reduces, then count the ring formed when the new OH attacks the ester.","know":"• **NaBH₄** reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols; it does **not** reduce esters (that needs LiAlH₄).\n• A **γ-hydroxy ester** (OH on C4) cyclizes to a **five-membered lactone**; a δ-hydroxy ester (OH on C5) gives a six-membered one. The ester's alcohol part leaves.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the structure and a reagent that reduces ketones but not esters. Number from the ester carbon: C1 is the CO₂CH₃ carbon, C4 the ketone carbon.","hl":"CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃","calc":["C5H₃–C4(=O)–C3H₂–C2H₂–C1(=O)OCH₃"]},{"text":"NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone, so the ester survives; a diol would need LiAlH₄.","hl":"reduces ketones but leaves esters intact","calc":["C4=O → C4H–OH"],"strike":["Pentane-1,4-diol"]},{"text":"The C4 OH is γ to the ester carbonyl. With acid and warmth it attacks C1 and methanol leaves, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive.","hl":"warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed","strike":["Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"]},{"text":"Count the ring atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4 and the O.","calc":["ring = 4 C + 1 O = 5 atoms → γ-lactone"]},{"text":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5.","strike":["δ-Valerolactone"]},{"text":"Answer: γ-Valerolactone."}],"trap":"Letting NaBH₄ reduce the ester as well: it is too mild, so the ester stays to be attacked by the new OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"4-oxopentanoate","note":"ketone at C4 of an ester"},{"t":"NaBH₄","note":"reduces the ketone only"},{"t":"warmed with a trace of acid","note":"ring closure"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: what does the reducing agent touch?** NaBH₄ turns a C=O of an aldehyde or ketone into C–OH and leaves esters, acids and amides unchanged.","calc":["R₂C=O → R₂CH–OH; RCO₂R′ unchanged"]},{"text":"**Step 2: find the hydroxy ester.** Locate the new OH relative to the ester carbon: on the γ-carbon (C4) or the δ-carbon (C5)."},{"text":"**Step 3: cyclize.** The OH attacks the ester C=O inside the molecule, the OR′ group leaves as R′OH, and a lactone forms; count its ring atoms."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone; a γ-hydroxy ester closes to a five-membered lactone and a δ-hydroxy ester to a six-membered one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-31a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hydrolysis products","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:31"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"What happens to the intermediate in the KOH reflux step?","choices":["All three ester groups are hydrolyzed","Only the two ethyl esters are hydrolyzed","Only the lactone ring is opened","None of the ester groups is hydrolyzed"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Hot excess base hydrolyzes esters","c":[3]},{"f":"Lactones are cyclic esters","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the ester carbonyls, lactone included: with base in excess, every one becomes a carboxylate."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives two ethyl esters plus the lactone, heated with **about six equivalents** of KOH: base in excess. Hot hydroxide hydrolyzes esters (**saponification**), and a **lactone** is an ester closed into a ring, so it opens too. **All three** become carboxylates: two from the ethyl esters (plus ethanol) and one from the lactone, with an OH left on the chain.","how":{"ask":"Decide which ester groups excess hot base cleaves.","know":"• **Saponification**: RCOOR′ + OH⁻ → RCOO⁻ + R′OH, essentially irreversible.\n• A **lactone** is a cyclic ester and saponifies the same way (the ring opens).\n• Excess base → every ester reacts.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the ester groups present and the amount of base.","hl":"two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH","calc":["2 esters + 1 lactone = 3 ester carbonyls","≈ 6 equiv KOH: excess"]},{"text":"Hot hydroxide does cleave esters.","strike":["None of the ester groups is hydrolyzed"]},{"text":"Neither the lactone nor the open esters are spared when base is in excess.","strike":["Only the two ethyl esters are hydrolyzed","Only the lactone ring is opened"]},{"text":"Answer: All three ester groups are hydrolyzed."}],"trap":"Forgetting that a lactone is an ester: the ring opens under the same conditions that cleave the ethyl esters."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"two ethyl esters","note":"two esters"},{"t":"this lactone ring","note":"a cyclic ester"},{"t":"about six equivalents of aqueous KOH","note":"excess: saponifies all"}]},{"text":"**Saponification.** OH⁻ adds to the C=O, the alkoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate.","calc":["R–CO–OEt + OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + EtOH"]},{"text":"**Lactone:** the same reaction opens the ring, leaving –COO⁻ and –OH on one chain."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** excess hot hydroxide hydrolyzes every ester and lactone into a carboxylate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-32a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"free amine as nucleophile","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:32#1","pe4-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:32"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"In the SN2 step that formed Nδ, the species that attacks the benzyl bromide is:","choices":["Piperidinium ion","Free piperidine","Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Ammonium N has no lone pair","c":[0]},{"f":"Hydroxide can't deprotonate amine N–H","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxide (water pKa 15.7) easily deprotonates R₂NH₂⁺ (pKa 11.1), but not R₂NH (pKa ~35). So the lone-pair amine is what attacks."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the pKa values: water 15.7 > piperidinium 11.1, so hydroxide **removes H⁺ from the piperidinium ion** and frees **neutral piperidine**. Only the neutral amine has a **free lone pair**, so it attacks the benzylic carbon and displaces bromide, forming the tertiary amine Nδ. Hydroxide is far too weak to strip the N–H proton (pKa ≈ 35) to make the anion.","how":{"ask":"Use the pKa values to see what NaOH does to the salt, then find the species with a free lone pair.","know":"• An acid–base reaction favors the side with the weaker acid (higher pKa).\n• **Ammonium salts** are not nucleophilic: the N lone pair is tied up by H⁺.\n• **Free amines** (lone pair) are good SN2 nucleophiles.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the salt meets NaOH, and the two pKa values decide what happens.","hl":"piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7","calc":["11.1 < 15.7 → OH⁻ deprotonates piperidinium"]},{"text":"The salt's N has no free lone pair, so it cannot attack.","strike":["Piperidinium ion"]},{"text":"OH⁻ cannot remove the N–H of the neutral amine, and the electrophile is not deprotonated.","strike":["Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"]},{"text":"Answer: Free piperidine.","fig":"cpt-piperidine"}],"trap":"Thinking the base makes an even stronger nucleophile (the amide anion): hydroxide only reaches the free amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"piperidine hydrochloride","note":"R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻"},{"t":"aqueous NaOH","note":"frees the amine"},{"t":"SN2 reaction","note":"needs a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Why base first.** NaOH removes H⁺ from the ammonium salt, freeing the amine; only the free amine has a lone pair to attack carbon."},{"text":"**Too far:** the amide anion would need a base stronger than pKa ≈ 35.","fig":"cpt-piperidide"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amine salt must be deprotonated by a base stronger than the ammonium ion; the resulting neutral amine is the nucleophile."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-33a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"hydrolysis products","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:33#1","pe4-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:33"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P6","passage":"Compound 12, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbonyl carbon of a benzyl carbamate, R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph; Nβ carries one H and is bonded to a CH₂ carbon and to the carbon of a C=O group whose other partner is a carbon; Nγ is a pyridine ring atom, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons; and Nδ, in a piperidine ring, is bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a benzyl CH₂ group. Nδ was made by an SN2 reaction: piperidine hydrochloride was first stirred with aqueous NaOH, and benzyl bromide was then added (piperidinium pKa ≈ 11.1; water pKa ≈ 15.7). A side chain came from methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, which was treated with NaBH₄, a reagent that reduces ketones but leaves esters intact, and then warmed with a trace of acid as methanol was lost and a ring closed. Later, an intermediate carrying two ethyl esters and this lactone ring was heated under reflux with about six equivalents of aqueous KOH. For one analog, the carbamate on Nα was removed at the end by complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether.","q":"What does complete hydrolysis of the carbamate on Nα release?","choices":["R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and PhCH₂OH","R–NH–CO₂H plus toluene","R–NH₂ and benzyl formate","R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus PhCH₂OH"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Carbamates decarboxylate on full hydrolysis","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Split at the C=O: the N side becomes the free amine, the O side becomes the alcohol, and the carbonyl carbon leaves as CO₂ (carbonate in base)."},"exp":"Sentence 2 shows the carbamate, **R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph**, and sentence 6 hydrolyzes it completely in hot KOH. Hydroxide releases **benzyl alcohol** and a carbamate ion, R–NH–CO₂⁻, which loses **CO₂** to set free the **amine R–NH₂**; in KOH the CO₂ is trapped as **carbonate**. That is why a free amine could be extracted into ether.","how":{"ask":"Break the carbamate on both sides of its carbonyl and name the three pieces.","know":"• **Carbamate** = R–NH–C(=O)–OR′: amide and ester on one carbonyl.\n• Hydrolysis gives **amine + CO₂ + alcohol**; in base, CO₂ → CO₃²⁻.\n• Hydrolysis needs strong base (or acid) and heat.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the carbamate's structure, with N on one side of the C=O and the benzyl O on the other.","hl":"R–NH–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph","calc":["N side → R–NH₂","O side → PhCH₂OH","C=O carbon → CO₂ → CO₃²⁻ in base"]},{"text":"Sentence 6 says the hydrolysis is complete and a free amine is extracted, so the carbamate anion cannot be the end point.","hl":"complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH, and the free amine was extracted into ether","strike":["R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus PhCH₂OH"]},{"text":"The benzyl group leaves as benzyl alcohol, not toluene or an ester.","strike":["R–NH–CO₂H plus toluene","R–NH₂ and benzyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and PhCH₂OH."}],"trap":"Stopping at the carbamic acid or its anion: these lose CO₂ on their own, so complete hydrolysis frees the amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"benzyl carbamate","note":"N–C(=O)–OCH₂Ph"},{"t":"complete hydrolysis in hot aqueous KOH","note":"hydrolysis conditions"},{"t":"the free amine was extracted","note":"the amine was released"}]},{"text":"**Two cleavages.** Ester side → alcohol; amide side → amine; the carbonyl carbon becomes CO₂."},{"text":"**Also:** only a step with strong base or acid and heat cleaves a carbamate; NaBH₄ in the cold or an SN2 step leaves it intact."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** complete hydrolysis of a carbamate gives the amine, the alcohol and CO₂ (as carbonate in base)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-34a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Lineweaver–Burk inhibitor type","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:34#1","pe4-cp:34#2","pe4-cp:34#3","pe4-cp:34#4","pe4-cp:34#5","pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:34"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"Which type of inhibition does the probe show, and what is its effect on KM?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Lineweaver–Burk plot with and without the probe","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.25,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No probe","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0],[0,0.2],[0.5,0.6],[1,1]]},{"name":"+ Probe","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0],[0,0.5],[0.5,1.5],[1,2.5]]}]},"choices":["Noncompetitive; KM is unchanged","Competitive; KM is increased","Uncompetitive; KM is decreased","Mixed; KM is increased or decreased"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Unchanged x-intercept means unchanged KM","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"LB plot: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. y from 0.20 to 0.50 → Vmax 5 → 2 μM/s; x fixed → KM stays 4 μM. Lines cross on the x-axis."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives the intercepts. The **y-intercept (1/Vmax)** rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM, so **Vmax fell from 5 to 2 μM/s**. Meanwhile the x-intercept, which equals −1/KM, held at −0.25 μM⁻¹, so **KM stays 4 μM**. Vmax down with KM unchanged is **noncompetitive** inhibition, as expected when a probe bonds to the active-site cysteine and takes enzyme out of action.","how":{"ask":"Translate each intercept into Vmax or KM, then match the pattern to an inhibitor type.","know":"• Lineweaver–Burk: 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax.\n• **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**; **x-intercept = −1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: Vmax same, KM up (lines cross on the y-axis).\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same (lines cross on the x-axis).\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed; uncompetitive: both fall (parallel lines).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: which intercept moved and which stayed.","hl":"the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹","calc":["Vmax = 1/0.20 = 5 μM/s → 1/0.50 = **2 μM/s**","KM = 1/0.25 = **4 μM**, both lines"]},{"text":"Vmax fell while KM held: the lines cross on the x-axis.","calc":["Vmax ↓, KM =  → noncompetitive"]},{"text":"Competitive inhibition would keep Vmax and raise KM, the reverse of this plot.","strike":["Competitive; KM is increased"]},{"text":"Any KM change, up or down, would move the x-intercept.","strike":["Uncompetitive; KM is decreased","Mixed; KM is increased or decreased"]},{"text":"Answer: Noncompetitive; KM is unchanged."}],"trap":"Mixing up the axes: the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so a rising y-intercept means a lower Vmax, not a higher KM."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which intercept it belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM","note":"1/Vmax up → Vmax down"},{"t":"x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹","note":"−1/KM fixed → KM fixed"}]},{"text":"**Read the intercepts.**","calc":["Vmax: 5 → 2 μM/s","KM: 4 μM → 4 μM"]},{"text":"**Match the pattern.** Vmax down, KM unchanged: noncompetitive. A probe that bonds covalently to the enzyme acts the same way, by removing working enzyme."},{"text":"**Also:** competitive keeps Vmax and raises KM (lines meet on the y-axis); mixed lowers Vmax and changes KM (lines meet off both axes); uncompetitive lowers both (parallel lines)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a double-reciprocal plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = −1/KM; see which one moved, then name the inhibitor type."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-35a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ping-pong vs sequential binding","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:35#1","pe4-cp:35#2","pe4-cp:35#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:35"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"Which mechanism best fits the kinetic findings for enzyme Q?","choices":["Random sequential","Ordered, acetyl-CoA first","Ordered, antibiotic first","Ping-pong"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Sequential needs both substrates bound first","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask first: does a product leave before the second substrate binds? Yes (CoA leaves, acetyl-enzyme forms) → ping-pong."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme and **CoA was released before the antibiotic bound**, with an **acetyl-cysteine enzyme intermediate** in between. A product leaving before the second substrate binds, through a modified enzyme, is the **ping-pong** mechanism.","how":{"ask":"Decide sequential vs ping-pong from when the first product leaves.","know":"• **Sequential**: a ternary complex (E·A·B) forms before any product leaves.\n• **Ordered**: one substrate must bind first; **random**: either can.\n• **Ping-pong**: the first product leaves (enzyme modified) before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: CoA left before the antibiotic bound, and a covalent acetyl-enzyme formed in between.","hl":"CoA was released before any antibiotic bound"},{"text":"No ternary complex forms, so no sequential mechanism fits.","strike":["Random sequential","Ordered, acetyl-CoA first"]},{"text":"The antibiotic binds only the acetylated enzyme, after CoA leaves.","strike":["Ordered, antibiotic first"]},{"text":"Answer: Ping-pong."}],"trap":"Reading \"acetyl-CoA reacts first\" as ordered sequential: in an ordered mechanism both substrates are bound together before any product leaves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"reacted with free enzyme Q","note":"first substrate alone"},{"t":"CoA was released before any antibiotic bound","note":"product out first"},{"t":"an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine","note":"modified enzyme"}]},{"text":"**Ping-pong:** E + A → F + P, then F + B → E + Q; a product leaves first, often with a covalent enzyme intermediate."},{"text":"**Ordered sequential:** substrate B binds only the E·A complex, never free enzyme. **Random:** E·A and E·B both form; either way, both are bound before release."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** product out before the second substrate = ping-pong; otherwise sequential, ordered if only one substrate binds free enzyme and random if either can."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-37a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"σ/π bond counting","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:37#1","pe4-cp:37#2","pe4-cp:37#3","pe4-cp:37#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:37"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P7","passage":"Enzyme Q, a bacterial acetyltransferase, makes aminoglycoside antibiotics useless by moving an acetyl group onto them from acetyl-CoA. In kinetic studies, acetyl-CoA reacted with free enzyme Q and CoA was released before any antibiotic bound, and an acetyl group attached to an active-site cysteine was detected between the two steps. To block enzyme Q, chemists made a probe that contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups; the nitrile groups make the C=C electron-poor, so the cysteine thiol adds to it. In a crystal structure of the complex, the probe's benzene ring sits deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. Initial rates measured with and without the probe gave a double-reciprocal plot in which the y-intercept rose from 0.20 to 0.50 s/μM while the x-intercept stayed at −0.25 μM⁻¹.","q":"How many π bonds does the probe contain?","fig":"cpt-benzoyl-methylene-malononitrile","choices":["5","7","9","11"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"π bonds = bonds beyond the first: double 1, triple 2, benzene ring 3. 3 + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 2 × 2 (two C≡N) = 9."},"exp":"Sentence 3 lists the probe's multiple bonds. Any bonded pair shares one **σ bond**; every extra bond is a **π bond**. So the C=O gives 1 π, the C=C 1 π, each C≡N 2 π, and the benzene ring (three C=C) 3 π: **3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**.","how":{"ask":"Give each multiple bond its π count, then add group by group.","know":"• **Single bond** = 1 σ, 0 π.\n• **Double bond** (C=O, C=C) = 1 σ + **1 π**.\n• **Triple bond** (C≡N, C≡C) = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• **Benzene ring** = three C=C = **3 π**.\n• More shared pairs pull atoms closer: a **double bond is shorter than a single bond**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: it lists every group with multiple bonds.","hl":"one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring and two C≡N groups"},{"text":"The ketone C=O is a double bond: one σ bond holds the atoms, and the second is π.","calc":["C=O = 1 σ + 1 π → 1 π"]},{"text":"Each C≡N is a triple bond: one σ and two π.","calc":["2 × C≡N = 2 × 2 π = 4 π"]},{"text":"The ring has three C=C (3 π), and the extra C=C has 1 π. Add everything.","calc":["3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 4 (C≡N) = 9 π"]},{"text":"Counting each C≡N as only 1 π gives 7; also counting the ring as a single π gives 5; counting all three lines of each triple bond as π gives 11.","calc":["3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7","1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5","3 + 1 + 1 + 6 = 11"],"strike":["7","5","11"]},{"text":"**Also:** the π bond adds a second shared pair, so C=O (about 120 pm) is shorter than a C–O single bond (about 140 pm)."},{"text":"Answer: 9."}],"trap":"Counting a triple bond as one π bond: only its first bond is σ, so the other two are both π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"one benzene ring","note":"three C=C"},{"t":"one ketone C=O","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"one C=C outside the ring","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"two C≡N groups","note":"two triple bonds"}]},{"text":"**σ first, then π.** Between two atoms the first bond is always σ (head-on overlap); every extra bond is π (side-by-side p overlap).","calc":["double = 1 σ + 1 π","triple = 1 σ + 2 π"]},{"text":"**Count by group.** Benzene ring 3 π; each C=O or C=C 1 π; each C≡N or C≡C 2 π. Then add.","calc":["ring 3 + C=O 1 + C=C 1 + C≡N 2 + C≡N 2 = 9"]},{"text":"**Also:** more bonds between the same two atoms means a shorter, stronger link: triple < double < single in length."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count one π per double bond and two per triple bond; a benzene ring has three; add them group by group."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-38a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"Beer–Lambert A = εbc","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:38#1","pe4-cp:38#2","pe4-cp:39#1","pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:38"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"What is the p-nitrophenolate concentration in the well that reads A = 0.81?","choices":["11 μM","45 μM","180 μM","900 μM"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"εb = 1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25 = 4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹. c = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M; × 10⁶ for μM."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives ε at the wavelength read, 405 nm: 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹, and the path, 0.25 cm. Sentence 3 gives A = 0.81. Beer's law: c = A/(εb) = 0.81 ÷ (1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25) = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M = **180 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange Beer's law for c, using the ε that matches the wavelength read and the real path length.","know":"• **A = εbc** (ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹, b in cm, c in M).\n• Use ε at the wavelength actually measured.\n• 1 M = 10⁶ μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the reading and its wavelength.","hl":"In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81"},{"text":"Sentence 2 gives ε at that wavelength and the path length.","hl":"ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm","calc":["εb = 1.8 × 10⁴ × 0.25 = 4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹"]},{"text":"Solve for c.","calc":["c = 0.81 ÷ 4500 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M","= **180 μM**"]},{"text":"Multiplying by b, or leaving b out, mishandles the path; the 350 nm ε belongs to a different wavelength.","strike":["11 μM","45 μM","900 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 180 μM."}],"trap":"Grabbing the first ε in the passage: a compound has a different ε at each wavelength, so match ε to the wavelength read."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"at 405 nm, A = 0.81","note":"the reading"},{"t":"1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm","note":"the ε to use"},{"t":"3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm","note":"wrong wavelength"},{"t":"0.25 cm deep","note":"path length b"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange Beer's law.**","calc":["c = A/(εb) = 0.81/(4.5 × 10³ M⁻¹) = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"**Also:** an absorbance counts only what differs between sample and reference; a smudged or mismatched well adds its own A, which must be measured and subtracted."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A/(εb), with ε for the measured wavelength and b the true path length; convert M to μM at the end."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-39a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"Beer–Lambert A = εbc","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:39"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"Which change would bring the most active wells into the reader's linear range?","choices":["Halve the liquid depth in those wells","Double the liquid depth in those wells","Concentrate those samples twofold","Read them with a brighter lamp"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Longer path raises absorbance","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A ∝ b: the depth is the path. Halving 0.25 cm to 0.125 cm halves A: 3.3 → about 1.65, under 1.8."},"exp":"Sentence 4 says the reader is linear only to A = 1.8, but the strongest wells read about 3.3. Sentence 2 says the liquid depth sets the light path, and A = εbc, so **halving the depth halves A** (3.3 → ≈ 1.65), within range. Diluting twofold would work for the same reason; a deeper or more concentrated sample raises A, and a brighter lamp changes nothing.","how":{"ask":"Find a change that lowers A by about half, using A = εbc.","know":"• **A = εbc**: A scales with path length b and concentration c.\n• In a microplate, the liquid depth is the path length.\n• A is a log ratio of intensities, so the lamp's brightness cancels.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: the readings are about twice the reader's limit.","hl":"a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3","calc":["3.3 ÷ 1.8 ≈ 1.8: need A roughly halved"]},{"text":"Sentence 2 ties the path to the liquid depth.","hl":"holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path","calc":["b: 0.25 → 0.125 cm → A ≈ 1.65"]},{"text":"More depth or more concentration raises A.","strike":["Double the liquid depth in those wells","Concentrate those samples twofold"]},{"text":"A brighter lamp does not change the ratio that defines A.","strike":["Read them with a brighter lamp"]},{"text":"Answer: Halve the liquid depth in those wells."}],"trap":"Thinking a stronger signal (brighter lamp) helps: the problem is too much absorbance, which only a smaller b or c can fix."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"only up to A = 1.8","note":"the limit"},{"t":"read about 3.3","note":"too high"},{"t":"which sets the light path","note":"depth = b"}]},{"text":"**Use A = εbc.** Lower b or c and A falls in proportion.","calc":["A ∝ b × c"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reading above the detector's linear range is brought down by diluting the sample or shortening the light path."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-40a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:40#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:40"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"At which pH would a HEPES buffer NOT be useful?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of HEPES (acid form) with NaOH","x":{"label":"NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":13,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"HEPES + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,4.3],[2,6.55],[5,7.02],[10,7.5],[15,7.98],[18,8.45],[19,8.78],[19.8,9.5],[20,10.2],[20.5,11.3],[22,11.9],[25,12.3]]}]},"choices":["pH 6.7","pH 7.2","pH 8.4","pH 8.9"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Buffers work near pKa, ±1 unit","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Useful window = pKa ± 1 = 6.5 to 8.5. Only one choice falls outside it."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives pKa 7.5 (the half-equivalence point on the curve) and the tenfold rule, which is **pKa ± 1**: from pH 6.5 to 8.5. At pH 8.9, base : acid = 10^1.4 ≈ 25 : 1, more than tenfold, so **pH 8.9** is outside the useful range; the other three lie inside.","how":{"ask":"Turn the tenfold rule into a pH window around the pKa, then find the choice outside it.","know":"• Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]).\n• A ratio between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1 means **pH = pKa ± 1**.\n• On a titration curve, pH = pKa at half-equivalence.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the pKa and the tenfold limit.","hl":"HEPES (pKa 7.5)","calc":["window = 7.5 ± 1 = 6.5 to 8.5"]},{"text":"Check each choice against the window.","calc":["6.7, 7.2, 8.4: inside","8.9: outside (ratio ≈ 25 : 1)"]},{"text":"These lie within one unit of the pKa.","strike":["pH 6.7","pH 7.2","pH 8.4"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 8.9."}],"trap":"Picking the pH farthest from the assay's working values rather than the one outside pKa ± 1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 7.5","note":"center of the window"},{"t":"by more than tenfold","note":"± 1 pH unit"}]},{"text":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch.** Each pH unit away from the pKa is a tenfold change in base : acid.","calc":["pH − pKa = log(base/acid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a buffer is effective within pKa ± 1; outside that, one form outnumbers the other more than tenfold."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"catalytic residue roles (nucleophile/acid/base)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:41#1","pe4-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:41"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"The atom of p-nitrophenyl acetate that the serine oxygen attacks is the:","choices":["Carbonyl carbon","Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen","Acetyl CH₃ carbon"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Nucleophiles attack δ+ atoms, not oxygens","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Nucleophiles go for the δ+ atom. In an ester that is the C=O carbon, tugged by two oxygens at once; O atoms donate electrons and a CH₃ is not electrophilic."},"exp":"Sentence 1 says the serine oxygen, made more nucleophilic by the histidine, forms an **acyl-enzyme** and releases p-nitrophenolate. So it bonds to the most **electrophilic** atom, the **carbonyl carbon**, made δ+ by two oxygens. A tetrahedral intermediate forms, the bond to the aryl oxygen breaks, p-nitrophenolate leaves, and the acetyl group sits on the serine.","how":{"ask":"The attacking oxygen has lone pairs; which atom of the ester is electron-poor enough to accept them, leaving the acyl group on the enzyme?","know":"• A nucleophile (Ser O, Cys S, His N, Asp O) attacks an **electrophilic** atom.\n• In an ester R–C(=O)–O–R′, the **carbonyl carbon** is bonded to two O atoms and is strongly δ+.\n• Oxygens are electron-rich: a nucleophile does not attack them.\n• In a phosphate ester the same role falls to **P**, bonded to four O atoms.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 holds the clue: an activated serine oxygen attacks and an acyl-enzyme forms while p-nitrophenolate leaves.","hl":"attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"Find the electron-poor atom: the carbonyl carbon, pulled on by two oxygens.","calc":["C=O carbon bonded to 2 O → δ+ → the electrophile","Ser–OH + CH₃C(=O)OAr → Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃ + ArO⁻"]},{"text":"Oxygen atoms are electron-rich, so the nucleophile attacks neither the carbonyl nor the bridging oxygen; the bridging O leaves with the ring.","strike":["Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen"]},{"text":"The CH₃ carbon has no δ+ pull and no leaving group.","strike":["Acetyl CH₃ carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: Carbonyl carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the bridging oxygen because the bond that breaks touches it: the nucleophile forms its new bond to the carbonyl C, and the bridging O leaves with the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"acyl-enzyme","note":"acyl group bonded to the enzyme"},{"t":"active-site serine","note":"hydroxyl O nucleophile"},{"t":"removed its O–H proton","note":"activated nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Electrophile = δ+.** In an ester, the carbonyl C is bonded to two electronegative O atoms."},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase does the same at phosphorus: an Asp O or His N attacks the δ+ P of a phosphate ester, the alcohol leaves, and a phospho-enzyme forms.","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme nucleophile attacks the most δ+ atom (the carbonyl C of an ester, the P of a phosphate), and the group on the other O leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"[H⁺]/[OH⁻] from pH (10^−pH, ΔpH factor)","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:42"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"What is [H₃O⁺] in the stopped reactions, and how does it compare with the value in neutral water?","choices":["1000 μM; higher than in neutral water","3 μM; higher than in neutral water","1000 μM; lower than in neutral water","0.001 μM; higher than in neutral water"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[1,3]},{"r":2,"c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"pH 3 → 10⁻³ M = 10⁻³ × 10⁶ μM. Neutral water is 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM, ten thousand times less."},"exp":"Sentence 7 gives pH 3 and asks for μM. [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M; × 10⁶ μM/M = **1000 μM**. At 25 °C, neutral water holds only a tenth of a micromolar, so the stopped mixture is **far above neutral** (more acidic).","how":{"ask":"Convert the pH into molar [H₃O⁺], then into μM, and compare with 10⁻⁷ M.","know":"• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**.\n• **M → μM: × 10⁶**.\n• Neutral water at 25 °C: 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 holds the clue: the pH and the unit wanted.","hl":"acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Convert to μM.","calc":["10⁻³ M × 10⁶ μM/M = 10³ μM"]},{"text":"Dividing instead of multiplying gives a tiny number, and the pH itself is not a concentration.","strike":["0.001 μM; higher than in neutral water","3 μM; higher than in neutral water"]},{"text":"Lower pH means more H₃O⁺ than neutral water.","strike":["1000 μM; lower than in neutral water"]},{"text":"Answer: 1000 μM; higher than in neutral water."}],"trap":"Treating a lower pH as less H₃O⁺: pH is a negative log, so pH 3 has ten thousand times more H₃O⁺ than pH 7."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 3","note":"[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻³ M"},{"t":"in micromolar units","note":"× 10⁶"}]},{"text":"**Powers of ten.**","calc":["10⁻³ M = 10⁻³ × 10⁶ μM = 1000 μM","neutral: 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM; anything above 0.1 μM is more acidic than neutral water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-43a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"kcat/KM catalytic efficiency","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:43#1","pe4-cp:43#2","pe4-cp:43#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:43"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P8","passage":"A lab characterizes a bacterial esterase whose active-site serine, once a nearby histidine has removed its O–H proton, attacks the substrate p-nitrophenyl acetate to form an acyl-enzyme and release p-nitrophenolate. The p-nitrophenolate product absorbs with ε = 1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 405 nm and 3.6 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 350 nm, and each microplate well holds liquid 0.25 cm deep, which sets the light path. In one well read at 405 nm, A = 0.81. The plate reader gives a straight-line response only up to A = 1.8, yet the most active wells read about 3.3. Assays are buffered with HEPES (pKa 7.5), and a buffer counts as useful only while neither of its forms outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM, fitting gave kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4. Reactions were stopped by acidifying to pH 3, and the lab software records [H₃O⁺] in micromolar units.","q":"How do the catalytic efficiency (kcat/KM) and Vmax change when the pH rises from 7.2 to 8.4?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Initial rate vs [S] at 0.80 nM enzyme, from each pH's kcat and KM","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":560,"step":70},"y":{"label":"v₀ (nM/s)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"pH 7.2","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[35,56],[70,74.7],[140,89.6],[280,99.6],[560,105.4]]},{"name":"pH 8.4","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[35,112],[70,168],[140,224],[280,268.8],[560,298.7]]}]},"choices":["kcat/KM rises 1.5-fold; Vmax triples","kcat/KM rises 6-fold; Vmax triples","kcat/KM rises 1.5-fold; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM halves; Vmax triples"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"At fixed [E], Vmax tracks kcat","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"kcat ×3, KM ×2 → kcat/KM ×3/2. [E] fixed → Vmax ×3, like kcat."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the constants. **kcat/KM**: 140/35 = 4 → 420/70 = 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹, up **1.5×**. **Vmax = kcat[E]**: with [E] fixed at 0.80 nM, Vmax rises from 112 to 336 nM/s, up **3×**. The chart shows the higher plateau at pH 8.4.","how":{"ask":"Compute kcat/KM at each pH, then use Vmax = kcat[E] at the fixed enzyme concentration.","know":"• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**.\n• **Vmax = kcat × [E]total**.\n• A change in KM alone leaves Vmax unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: kcat and KM at both pH values, at one enzyme concentration.","hl":"kcat = 140 s⁻¹ and KM = 35 μM at pH 7.2, but kcat = 420 s⁻¹ and KM = 70 μM at pH 8.4","calc":["kcat/KM: 4 → 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ (×1.5)"]},{"text":"Vmax follows kcat at fixed [E].","hl":"At an enzyme concentration of 0.80 nM","calc":["Vmax: 140 × 0.80 = 112 → 420 × 0.80 = 336 nM/s (×3)"]},{"text":"Multiplying by the KM change, or dividing by it alone, misuses kcat/KM.","strike":["kcat/KM rises 6-fold; Vmax triples","kcat/KM halves; Vmax triples"]},{"text":"Vmax tracks kcat, so it cannot stay the same.","strike":["kcat/KM rises 1.5-fold; Vmax is unchanged"]},{"text":"Answer: kcat/KM rises 1.5-fold; Vmax triples."}],"trap":"Treating a higher KM as higher efficiency: KM sits in the denominator of kcat/KM."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"kcat = 140 s⁻¹","note":"turnover at pH 7.2"},{"t":"KM = 70 μM","note":"larger KM at pH 8.4"},{"t":"0.80 nM","note":"[E] fixed"}]},{"text":"**Efficiency.**","calc":["pH 7.2: 140/35 = 4","pH 8.4: 420/70 = 6"]},{"text":"**Vmax.**","calc":["112 → 336 nM/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** kcat/KM changes by (kcat factor ÷ KM factor), and Vmax changes with kcat when [E] is fixed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-44a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"buoyancy / floating","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:44#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:44"],"q":"A 6.0 g cork floats on a pond, partly submerged, pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water (1 g/cm³). Which property of water mainly holds the cork up?","choices":["Buoyancy","Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Viscosity resists flow, not weight","c":[2]},{"f":"Capillary action needs narrow tubes","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Displaced mass = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g, equal to the cork's mass: the buoyant force alone balances its weight."},"exp":"The displaced water has mass 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = **6.0 g**, the same as the cork. By Archimedes' principle the upward **buoyant force equals the weight of the displaced water**, so buoyancy alone balances the cork's weight. Surface tension matters only for very light objects, such as a water strider, whose legs dimple the surface while displacing almost no water.","how":{"ask":"Decide which property of water supplies the upward force, using the displaced volume as the clue.","know":"• **Buoyancy**: F_b = ρ_water × V_displaced × g (Archimedes).\n• **Surface tension**: cohesion among surface water molecules; supports only tiny weights on a dimpled surface.\n• Viscosity resists flow; capillary action lifts water in narrow tubes.","steps":[{"text":"Weigh the displaced water.","hl":"pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water","calc":["m = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g"]},{"text":"Compare with the cork.","hl":"A 6.0 g cork","calc":["6.0 g displaced = 6.0 g cork → buoyant force = weight"]},{"text":"Surface tension supports only tiny weights that displace almost nothing; viscosity and capillarity give no upward support.","strike":["Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"]},{"text":"Answer: Buoyancy."}],"trap":"Seeing \"floats on water\" and thinking surface tension: check how much water the object displaces first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"partly submerged","note":"it sank in and displaced water"},{"t":"6.0 cm³","note":"displaced volume"},{"t":"1 g/cm³","note":"turns volume into mass"}]},{"text":"**Contrast:** a 10 mg insect whose legs displace a fraction of a cubic millimetre is held up by **surface tension**, the cohesion between water molecules at the surface; buoyancy there is far too small."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** displaced weight equal to the object's weight → buoyancy; displaced weight negligible and the surface dimpled → surface tension."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-46a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"diffraction vs refraction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:46"],"q":"Viewed from the side in white light, a 7 mm pencil standing in a glass of water (n = 1.33) looks broken at the water line, with no colored fringes. This appearance is caused by:","choices":["Refraction","Diffraction","Dispersion","Polarization"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Dispersion produces colored fringes","c":[2]},{"f":"Diffraction needs wavelength-sized openings","c":[1]},{"f":"Polarization doesn't bend light","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Image bent where light crosses a boundary, no color: refraction (light slows in water). Color fringes → dispersion; slit-sized openings → diffraction; filters/glare → polarization."},"exp":"Light from the underwater part of the pencil **changes speed as it crosses the water–air surface** (v = c/n, slower in water) and bends away from the normal as it exits, so that part appears shifted. Bending at a boundary between two media is **refraction**, described by Snell's law, n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.","how":{"ask":"Is the light crossing into a new medium, or bending around an edge close to its wavelength in size?","know":"• **Refraction**: light changes speed at a boundary between media (v = c/n) and bends: n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.\n• **Diffraction**: bending around an edge or through a gap, strong only when its size is near the wavelength (visible λ ≈ 0.4–0.7 μm).\n• **Dispersion**: n varies with λ, so white light spreads into colors.\n• **Interference**: overlapping waves add (bright) or cancel (dark), making fringes.","steps":[{"text":"The light leaving the underwater pencil crosses from water into air, a change of medium, so its speed and direction change.","hl":"looks broken at the water line","calc":["v in water = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.33 ≈ 2.3 × 10⁸ m/s","faster in air → bends away from the normal at the surface"]},{"text":"Compare the pencil with the wavelength: diffraction is noticeable only for objects near λ in size.","hl":"7 mm pencil","calc":["visible λ ≈ 0.5 μm","7 mm ÷ 0.5 μm = 14,000 wavelengths → diffraction negligible"],"strike":["Diffraction"]},{"text":"Dispersion would fringe the image with colors, which the stem rules out; polarization changes the field's orientation, not the light's direction.","hl":"no colored fringes","strike":["Dispersion","Polarization"]},{"text":"Answer: refraction."}],"trap":"Choosing diffraction because the light bends: bending around an edge needs an object near the wavelength, while bending at a boundary between media is refraction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glass of water (n = 1.33)","note":"a second medium"},{"t":"7 mm pencil","note":"far larger than λ"},{"t":"broken at the water line","note":"image shifted at the boundary"},{"t":"no colored fringes","note":"not dispersion"}]},{"text":"**New medium → speed change → bending.** That is refraction, with Snell's law n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂."},{"text":"**Size versus wavelength.** Diffraction matters only when an object or gap is near λ: bright and dark rings around 1 μm specks in green laser light are diffraction, not refraction.","calc":["7 mm ≫ 0.5 μm → refraction dominates","1 μm ≈ 2 × 0.532 μm → diffraction rings"]},{"text":"**Also:** bright and dark fringes form by interference: waves in step add (bright), and waves half a wavelength out of step cancel (dark)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bending where light enters a new medium is refraction (Snell's law); bending around an obstacle near λ in size is diffraction; colors split because n varies with λ is dispersion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-47a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"solubility: like dissolves like","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:47"],"q":"Saturated fatty acids of molar mass 144, 172, 312 and 340 g/mol are compared. Which dissolves best in water at room temperature?","choices":["C₇H₁₅COOH","C₉H₁₉COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH","C₂₁H₄₃COOH"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Longer nonpolar tails lower water solubility","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same head group, so solubility tracks tail length: pick the smallest molar mass, which has the fewest CH₂ groups."},"exp":"Each acid has the same polar **–COOH head**; they differ only in the length of the **nonpolar tail**. The longer the tail, the more hydrophobic the molecule and the less it dissolves. The 144 g/mol acid, **C₇H₁₅COOH** (octanoic acid, eight carbons), has the shortest tail and so the greatest water solubility.","how":{"ask":"Rank the acids by hydrocarbon chain length and pick the shortest.","know":"• Saturated fatty acid: CH₃(CH₂)ₙCOOH, one polar head.\n• Each extra CH₂ adds nonpolar surface → lower water solubility.\n• Molar mass rises with chain length.","steps":[{"text":"Match each formula to its chain length.","calc":["C₇H₁₅COOH: 8 C, 144 g/mol","C₉H₁₉COOH: 10 C, 172 g/mol","C₁₉H₃₉COOH: 20 C, 312 g/mol","C₂₁H₄₃COOH: 22 C, 340 g/mol"]},{"text":"The long chains are the most hydrophobic.","strike":["C₂₁H₄₃COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH"]},{"text":"Ten carbons is longer than eight.","strike":["C₉H₁₉COOH"]},{"text":"Answer: C₇H₁₅COOH."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger molecule has more polar contacts: the head group is the same in all four, so extra size is all nonpolar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Saturated fatty acids","note":"same head, different tails"},{"t":"dissolves best in water","note":"least hydrophobic"}]},{"text":"**Head vs tail.** The –COOH head H-bonds with water; the tail does not.","fig":"palmitic-acid"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among molecules with the same polar group, the one with the shortest hydrocarbon chain is the most water-soluble."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-48a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleobase tautomers","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:48#1","pe4-cp:48#2","pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2","pe4-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:48"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which form of guanine predominates in DNA under physiological conditions and makes the normal pair with cytosine?","fig":"guanine","choices":["Keto–amino form","Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Enol tautomers of guanine are rare","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Default forms: C=O stays C=O (keto), –NH₂ stays –NH₂ (amino). Any choice with \"enol\" or \"imino\" is a rare, mutagenic form."},"exp":"Sentence 3 says G mispairs only when it **briefly** takes a **rare enol** form, so the normal form is the other one. At physiological pH guanine is almost entirely the **keto–amino** tautomer: C6=O, N1–H and a 2-NH₂ group. That face (acceptor, donor, donor) pairs with cytosine through **three hydrogen bonds**. Enol and imino forms swap a donor for an acceptor and mispair.","how":{"ask":"Pick the tautomer that dominates at physiological pH and gives standard pairing.","know":"• G, T and U are mainly **keto** (lactam: C=O with ring N–H); A and C are mainly **amino**.\n• **Enol** (C–OH) and **imino** (C=NH) forms are rare and change the donor/acceptor pattern → mispairing.\n• G·C: 3 H-bonds; A·T: 2.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the enol is the rare, brief form that mispairs.","hl":"only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer"},{"text":"The normal form keeps C6=O and the 2-NH₂ group.","calc":["keto at O6, amino at C2 → G·C with 3 H-bonds"]},{"text":"Any enol or imino shift is a rare, mutagenic form.","strike":["Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"]},{"text":"Answer: Keto–amino form."}],"trap":"Thinking the enol is favored because it looks aromatic: in nucleobases the keto (lactam) form dominates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"rare enol tautomer","note":"minor form, mutagenic"},{"t":"misinserts T opposite G","note":"mispairing"},{"t":"hydrogen-bonding faces","note":"donor/acceptor pattern"}]},{"text":"**Keto wins.** G, T and U sit mainly as keto (C=O, ring N–H); A and C as amino.","fig":"guanine"},{"text":"**Count the H-bonds pair by pair.** An H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one hydrogen bond: keto G with C gives 3; A with T gives 2.","fig":"cytosine"},{"text":"**Also:** a rare enol tautomer mispairs. Enol guanine has O6–H (donor) and N1 (acceptor) in place of C6=O and N1–H, so its face changes and the H-bond count in a drawn pair changes with it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** G, T and U are mainly keto at physiological pH and A, C mainly amino; rare enol or imino tautomers mispair."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-49a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"radioactive decay A/Z changes","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:42#2","pe1-cp:42#1","pe6-cp:32#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:49","pe1-cp:42","pe6-cp:32"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"When a ³H atom in a labeled strand decays, which element replaces it, and how does the new nucleus compare with the ³H nucleus?","choices":["Helium; one more proton and one fewer neutron","Helium; one more proton and a mass number one higher","Hydrogen; the same proton count and one fewer neutron","Lithium; two more protons and two fewer neutrons"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"β⁻ decay keeps mass number","c":[1]},{"f":"β⁻ decay raises Z by one","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. Z goes up by one (H → He), N down by one, A stays 3."},"exp":"Sentence 6 says tritium decays by **β⁻ emission**: a neutron turns into a proton and an electron, which leaves with an antineutrino. **Z rises by 1** (hydrogen, Z = 1, becomes helium, Z = 2), **N falls by 1** (2 → 1), and the mass number stays 3: ³H → ³He.","how":{"ask":"Apply the β⁻ change to Z, N and A, then name the new element.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: n → p⁺ + e⁻ + antineutrino.\n• Z + 1, N − 1, A unchanged.\n• **β⁺ decay / electron capture**: Z − 1, N + 1.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the label is tritium, and it decays by β⁻ emission.","hl":"tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission"},{"text":"β⁻ turns one neutron into one proton.","calc":["³H: Z = 1, N = 2","→ Z = 2, N = 1, A = 3: ³He"]},{"text":"A stays 3, and the element must change.","strike":["Helium; one more proton and a mass number one higher","Hydrogen; the same proton count and one fewer neutron"]},{"text":"One decay changes one neutron, not two.","strike":["Lithium; two more protons and two fewer neutrons"]},{"text":"Answer: Helium; one more proton and one fewer neutron."}],"trap":"Treating β⁻ like α decay or losing mass: the electron carries almost no mass, so only Z and N trade by one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"tritium (³H)","note":"Z = 1, A = 3"},{"t":"β⁻ emission","note":"n → p + e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Balance the decay.**","calc":["³₁H → ³₂He + ⁰₋₁e + antineutrino"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ decay raises Z by 1 and lowers N by 1 at constant A, so the atom becomes the next element in the periodic table."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:42","pe6-cp:32"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-42b","cpt-pe6-32a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain classes","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:50#1","pe4-cp:50#2","pe5-cp:18#1","pe5-cp:18#2","pe6-cp:48#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:50","pe5-cp:18","pe6-cp:48"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which change to one of the two active-site aspartates would most likely keep the polymerase working?","choices":["Asp → Glu","Asp → Asn","Asp → Lys","Asp → Phe"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Asn, Lys, Phe lack carboxylate groups","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keep the carboxylate to keep the Mg²⁺. Asp and Glu are the two acidic (–COO⁻) residues; Asn drops the charge, Lys reverses it, Phe removes all polarity."},"exp":"Sentence 2 says the aspartates hold Mg²⁺ through their **side-chain carboxylates**, and losing that group (alanine) abolishes activity. Glutamate keeps a –COO⁻, one CH₂ longer, so it is a **conservative substitution**. Asparagine is uncharged, phenylalanine nonpolar, and lysine positive, so none can hold the metal.","how":{"ask":"What feature of the aspartate side chain does the job, and which replacement keeps that feature?","know":"• **Conservative substitution**: replace a residue with one of similar chemistry (charge, polarity, size), usually keeping function.\n• At pH 7: Asp and Glu are −1 (carboxylates); Lys and Arg are +1; Asn is neutral; Phe is nonpolar.\n• Asp and Glu differ only by one CH₂ in the side chain.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the carboxylates hold the Mg²⁺, and removing them abolishes activity.","hl":"the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Glutamate also ends in a carboxylate, so the metal stays bound.","fig":"glutamate"},{"text":"Asparagine and phenylalanine have no charge, so the metal is lost.","strike":["Asp → Asn","Asp → Phe"]},{"text":"Lysine is positive at pH 7 and would repel the Mg²⁺.","strike":["Asp → Lys"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp → Glu."}],"trap":"Choosing Asn because it looks almost the same: an amide is uncharged, and the carboxylate charge is what holds the metal."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"side-chain carboxylates","note":"negative groups"},{"t":"Mg²⁺ ions","note":"positive partner"},{"t":"replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity","note":"the charge is essential"}]},{"text":"**Keep the chemistry.** Glu is the only standard residue besides Asp with a side-chain carboxylate.","calc":["Asp: –CH₂–COO⁻","Glu: –CH₂–CH₂–COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep an Asp's function, swap it for Glu, since both carry a carboxylate; Asn, Lys or Phe lose the negative charge."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe5-cp:18","pe6-cp:48"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-18a","cpt-pe6-48a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-51a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"base pairing rules (Chargaff, H-bonds)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:51"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Counting every base pair, how many hydrogen bonds does the template duplex contain?","choices":["366","384","450","768"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"150 pairs = 300 bases. A = 22% → 66 A·T pairs; G = 28% → 84 G·C pairs. 66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252."},"exp":"Sentence 5 gives a 150-bp duplex (300 bases) with A = 22%. Chargaff: T = 22%, so G = C = 28%. That is **66 A·T pairs** and **84 G·C pairs**. Each A·T pair has 2 hydrogen bonds and each G·C pair 3: 66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252 = **384**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the base composition into numbers of A·T and G·C pairs, then add their hydrogen bonds.","know":"• **Chargaff**: A = T and G = C in a duplex.\n• **A·T: 2 H-bonds; G·C: 3 H-bonds.**\n• A duplex of n base pairs has 2n bases.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 holds the clue: the length in base pairs and the share of adenine.","hl":"150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases","calc":["bases = 2 × 150 = 300","A = T = 22% → 66 each","G = C = 28% → 84 each"]},{"text":"Each A pairs with a T, each G with a C.","calc":["66 A·T pairs, 84 G·C pairs"]},{"text":"Add the hydrogen bonds.","calc":["66 × 2 + 84 × 3 = 132 + 252"]},{"text":"Swapping 2 and 3, doubling the count, or giving every pair 3 bonds all miss.","strike":["366","768","450"]},{"text":"Answer: 384."}],"trap":"Applying the percentage to base pairs instead of bases: 22% of all bases is 66 adenines, so 66 A·T pairs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"150 base pairs","note":"300 bases"},{"t":"22% of all bases","note":"A, so T too"},{"t":"every base in a Watson–Crick pair","note":"Chargaff holds"}]},{"text":"**Chargaff.**","calc":["A + T = 44% → G + C = 56% → G = 28%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** H-bonds = 2 × (A·T pairs) + 3 × (G·C pairs), with pairs counted from the bases by Chargaff's rule."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-52a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ionization-energy jump → group/charge","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:52"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P9","passage":"A team studied the accuracy of a DNA polymerase from a hot-spring bacterium. In its active site, the side-chain carboxylates of two aspartate residues hold the Mg²⁺ ions that position the incoming nucleotide, and replacing either aspartate with alanine abolishes activity. The enzyme checks each new base pair by its hydrogen-bonding faces, and it misinserts T opposite G only when G briefly takes up a rare enol tautomer whose donor and acceptor pattern changes. To find substitutes for Mg²⁺, four other metals were tried, with valence configurations Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹. The template was a synthetic duplex 150 base pairs long in which adenine made up 22% of all bases, with every base in a Watson–Crick pair. Newly made strands were traced with thymidine labeled with tritium (³H), which decays by β⁻ emission.","q":"Which metal could NOT readily replace Mg²⁺ in the polymerase, and why?","choices":["Sr, since its ion is too large","Ni, since its d⁸ configuration is unstable","Cd, since its 4d subshell is filled","Rb, since it has one 5s electron"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Ni, Sr, Cd form 2+ ions","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look at the outer s subshell: s² → 2+ is easy; s¹ (an alkali metal) gives only 1+."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the configurations. Ni, Sr and Cd each have **two outer s electrons** that come off easily, giving common **2+ ions** like Mg²⁺. Rb has a single 5s electron: removing it leaves a noble-gas core, and a second electron would have to come from that core at a huge energy cost. So **Rb forms only Rb⁺** and cannot stand in for Mg²⁺.","how":{"ask":"For each metal, decide whether it readily forms a 2+ ion, from its valence configuration.","know":"• Group 1 (s¹): forms only **1+**.\n• Group 2 (s²): forms **2+**.\n• Transition metals lose their outer s electrons first, so 2+ is common (Ni²⁺, Cd²⁺, Zn²⁺, Fe²⁺).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 holds the clue: each metal's outer subshell.","hl":"Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹","calc":["Ni: 4s² → Ni²⁺","Sr: 5s² → Sr²⁺","Cd: 5s² → Cd²⁺","Rb: 5s¹ → Rb⁺ only"]},{"text":"These three each lose two s electrons easily; size, d⁸ and a full d subshell do not block a 2+ ion.","strike":["Sr, since its ion is too large","Ni, since its d⁸ configuration is unstable","Cd, since its 4d subshell is filled"]},{"text":"Answer: Rb, since it has one 5s electron."}],"trap":"Rejecting a transition metal because of its d electrons: the d count does not stop a metal from losing its two outer s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"Mg²⁺ ions","note":"need a 2+ ion"},{"t":"Rb, [Kr]5s¹","note":"one valence electron"}]},{"text":"**Why not Rb²⁺:** the second ionization energy of an alkali metal is enormous, because it would break into a filled shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal can stand in for a divalent ion only if it readily forms 2+; alkali metals (s¹, like Na, K, Rb) form only 1+."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-53a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"½CV² capacitor energy","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:53#1","pe4-cp:53#2","pe4-cp:53#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:53"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"How high must the toolbox be lifted to store as much energy as the charged capacitor bank?","choices":["2.0 m","4.1 m","2.0 × 10³ m","2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"U = ½ × 80 × 10⁻⁶ × (3.0 × 10³)² = 40 × 10⁻⁶ × 9 × 10⁶ = 360 J. h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) ≈ 360 ÷ 176 ≈ 2 m."},"exp":"Sentence 2 gives C = 80 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ F and V = 3.0 kV = 3.0 × 10³ V, so **U = ½CV²** = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = **360 J**. Sentence 4 sets this equal to **mgh** for 18 kg: h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) ≈ **2.0 m**.","how":{"ask":"Compute the capacitor's stored energy, then the height that gives the toolbox the same potential energy.","know":"• **Capacitor energy: U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts).\n• **Gravitational potential energy: U = mgh**, so h = U/(mg).\n• Prefixes: **μF = 10⁻⁶ F**, **kV = 10³ V**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the bank's capacitance and voltage.","hl":"charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV","calc":["C = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ F","V = 3.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Compute U = ½CV², squaring the exponent too.","calc":["V² = 9.0 × 10⁶ V²","U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = **360 J**"]},{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the mass and g; set U = mgh.","hl":"lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²)","calc":["h = 360 ÷ (18 × 9.8) = 360 ÷ 176.4 ≈ 2.04 m"]},{"text":"Prefix slips put the height off by 10³ or 10⁶ the wrong way.","strike":["2.0 × 10³ m","2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Leaving out the ½ doubles the energy and the height.","strike":["4.1 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 m."}],"trap":"Forgetting to square the power of ten in V²: (3.0 × 10³)² is 9.0 × 10⁶, not 9.0 × 10³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"80 μF","note":"C, × 10⁻⁶ F"},{"t":"3.0 kV","note":"V, × 10³ V"},{"t":"18 kg","note":"mass to lift"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefixes.** Micro is 10⁻⁶ and kilo is 10³."},{"text":"**Capacitor energy:** U = ½CV². Square the voltage, exponent included.","calc":["U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 9.0 × 10⁶ = 360 J"]},{"text":"**Equal energies:** set ½CV² = mgh and solve for h.","calc":["h = 360 J ÷ 176.4 N ≈ 2.0 m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to match a capacitor's energy with a lifted mass, convert to F and V, compute U = ½CV², then h = U/(mg)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-54a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"charge Q = It (amp-hours)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-cp:54#1","pe4-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:54#3","pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:54","pe6-cp:40"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"Approximately how much charge can the fully charged pack deliver on a full discharge?","choices":["3.5 C","210 C","1.3 × 10⁴ C","1.8 × 10⁵ C"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"An amp-hour is 3600 coulombs","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"1 A·h = 3600 C. 3.5 × 3600 = 12,600 C ≈ 1.3 × 10⁴ C."},"exp":"Sentence 2 rates the pack at **3.5 A·h**, a current × time, which is a charge. Since 1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C, Q = 3.5 × 3600 = 12,600 C ≈ **1.3 × 10⁴ C**. The 14.4 V would turn this into an energy (QV), not a charge.","how":{"ask":"Convert the amp-hour rating into coulombs.","know":"• **Q = I·t** (C = A × s).\n• **1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C**.\n• Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 holds the clue: the pack's amp-hour rating.","hl":"rated 3.5 A·h"},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds.","calc":["Q = 3.5 A × 3600 s = 12,600 C"]},{"text":"Leaving A·h unconverted, or using 60 s per hour, undercounts the seconds.","strike":["3.5 C","210 C"]},{"text":"Multiplying by 14.4 V gives joules, not coulombs.","hl":"14.4 V","strike":["1.8 × 10⁵ C"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.3 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"Bringing in the voltage: the charge rating does not depend on it; volts only matter for energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"3.5 A·h","note":"a charge rating"},{"t":"14.4 V","note":"not needed for charge"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.** An amp-hour already is a charge.","calc":["1 A·h = 3600 C"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage matters only for energy (E = QV): 12,600 C × 14.4 V ≈ 1.8 × 10⁵ J."},{"text":"**Also:** when an electrode material is rated per gram, multiply by its mass in grams to get the capacity, then convert: 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = I·t, and 1 A·h = 3600 C; ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-40a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-55a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"impulse F = mΔv/Δt","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:55#1","pe4-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:55"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"While the electrode arm speeds up, what is the average net force on it?","choices":["8.4 N","9.8 N","840 N","8.4 × 10³ N"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Δv = 140 − 20 = 120 cm/s = 1.2 m/s. F = 0.35 kg × 1.2 m/s ÷ 0.050 s = 0.42 ÷ 0.05."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives m = 350 g = **0.350 kg** and speeds from 20 to 140 cm/s, so Δv = 120 cm/s = **1.2 m/s**, over Δt = 0.050 s. F = mΔv/Δt = 0.350 × 1.2 ÷ 0.050 = **8.4 N**.","how":{"ask":"Convert to SI, find the change in velocity, then divide momentum change by time.","know":"• **F = ma = mΔv/Δt** (N = kg·m/s²).\n• **1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm/s = 10⁻² m/s**: convert before multiplying.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 holds the clue: the arm's mass, its two speeds and the time.","hl":"a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s","calc":["m = 0.350 kg","Δv = 1.40 − 0.20 = 1.2 m/s"]},{"text":"Divide the momentum change by the time.","calc":["F = 0.350 × 1.2 ÷ 0.050 = **8.4 N**"]},{"text":"Grams or cm/s left unconverted inflate F by 10³ or 10².","strike":["8.4 × 10³ N","840 N"]},{"text":"Using 1.4 m/s, the final speed, skips the subtraction.","strike":["9.8 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.4 N."}],"trap":"Using the final speed as Δv: the arm was already moving at 20 cm/s, so subtract it first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"350 g","note":"m, → kg"},{"t":"from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s","note":"Δv, → m/s"},{"t":"in 0.050 s","note":"Δt"},{"t":"nearly constant net force","note":"average = constant"}]},{"text":"**Average force = mass × velocity change ÷ time.**","calc":["F = mΔv/Δt"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert to SI first: g → kg (÷ 1000) and cm/s → m/s (÷ 100)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** F = mΔv/Δt with mass in kg and speeds in m/s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"E = 0 inside a conductor","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:56"],"pid":"pe4-cp:P10","passage":"A portable spot welder joins thin steel sheets with a short, intense pulse of current. Its 14.4 V lithium-ion pack, rated 3.5 A·h, charges an 80 μF capacitor bank to 3.0 kV through a boost circuit. When the operator presses the trigger, a spring-loaded copper electrode arm with a mass of 350 g first snaps onto the sheets, its speed rising from 20 cm/s to 140 cm/s in 0.050 s under a nearly constant net force, and the bank then discharges through the joint. To give trainees a sense of scale, the manual compares the energy stored in the charged bank with the energy needed to lift an 18 kg toolbox (g = 9.8 m/s²). The control board sits inside a closed steel enclosure, which, near the heavy welding cables, is exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C. Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal or the cavity.","q":"At equilibrium, why is the field inside the steel enclosure zero?","choices":["Free electrons redistribute.","The steel absorbs it.","Its electrons are all bound.","The box has no net charge."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Metals have mobile, unbound electrons","c":[2]},{"f":"Metals don't absorb static fields","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Any field left inside would push free electrons, so equilibrium (charges at rest) means their surface charge has cancelled it."},"exp":"Sentence 6 gives the clue: the free electrons **move, then stop**. In the external field (sentence 5) they drift to the enclosure's surfaces, leaving one side negative and the other positive, and that surface charge makes a field that **cancels** the applied one inside. Once the charges are at rest (electrostatic equilibrium), E inside must be zero; otherwise they would still be moving.","how":{"ask":"Explain why charges at rest in a conductor imply no field inside it.","know":"• A conductor has **free electrons** that move whenever a field acts on them.\n• They pile up on the **surface**, and the field they make opposes the applied one.\n• **Electrostatic equilibrium**: charges stopped, so E inside = 0 and any excess charge sits on the surface.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 holds the clue: the free electrons move and then stop.","hl":"Once the enclosure's free electrons stop moving"},{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the applied field that set them moving.","hl":"exposed to an external field of about 2.2 × 10³ N/C","calc":["electrons drift → surface charges → opposing field"]},{"text":"Static fields are cancelled, not absorbed, and a metal has free electrons.","strike":["The steel absorbs it.","Its electrons are all bound."]},{"text":"Zero net charge alone does not cancel an outside field; the charges must separate.","strike":["The box has no net charge."]},{"text":"Answer: Free electrons redistribute."}],"trap":"Thinking the metal blocks or absorbs the field: the field inside is cancelled by the conductor's own rearranged charges."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues in the passage**.","marks":[{"t":"free electrons stop moving","note":"electrostatic equilibrium"},{"t":"external field","note":"what the charges respond to"},{"t":"no electric field anywhere inside","note":"the result to explain"}]},{"text":"**Free electrons move in a field.** They drift until they reach the surface and pile up there."},{"text":"**Their field opposes the outside field.** If any field were left inside, electrons would keep moving."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, free electrons move to the surface until their field cancels any field inside, so E = 0 there."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-57a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"use a given formula","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:57#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:57"],"q":"Blood pressure in a clinic patient reads 144/96 mmHg, with a heart rate of 64 beats/min. Using MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d), with P_s systolic and P_d diastolic pressure, what is the mean arterial pressure?","choices":["96 mmHg","112 mmHg","120 mmHg","128 mmHg"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"MAP is above diastolic","c":[0]},{"f":"MAP sits nearer diastolic than systolic","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pulse pressure 144 − 96 = 48; a third of it is 16; MAP = 96 + 16. The heart rate is not needed."},"exp":"Systolic P_s = 144 mmHg and diastolic P_d = 96 mmHg (the lower number). Pulse pressure = 144 − 96 = 48 mmHg; a third of it is 16 mmHg. MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**. The heart rate does not enter the formula.","how":{"ask":"Identify systolic and diastolic, then substitute into the given formula.","know":"• A blood pressure is written systolic/diastolic: the first (higher) number is systolic.\n• MAP sits closer to diastolic because the heart spends longer in diastole.\n• Use only the quantities the formula asks for.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two pressures.","hl":"144/96 mmHg","calc":["P_s = 144, P_d = 96"]},{"text":"Apply the formula.","hl":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","calc":["⅓(144 − 96) = 16","MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**"]},{"text":"Starting from systolic reverses the formula, and the plain average or the diastolic value alone skip it.","strike":["128 mmHg","120 mmHg","96 mmHg"]},{"text":"Answer: 112 mmHg."}],"trap":"Using the heart rate or a simple average: the question supplies the formula, so plug in exactly what it asks for."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"144/96 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"64 beats/min","note":"not in the formula"},{"t":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","note":"the formula given"}]},{"text":"**Same formula, other form:** P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d) = (P_s + 2P_d)/3.","calc":["(144 + 2 × 96)/3 = 336/3 = 112"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a formula given, identify each symbol (diastolic = the lower number), substitute, and ignore data the formula does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-58a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"action potential ion phases","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:58"],"q":"A neuron's membrane potential at its axon hillock is graphed over a single action potential. Which event is mainly responsible for the change in potential at 1.8 ms?","choices":["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, and Na⁺ flows in","Voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, and K⁺ flows out","The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps Na⁺ out","Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, and Ca²⁺ flows in"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump is slow","c":[2]},{"f":"Ca²⁺ entry occurs at terminals","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Find 1.8 ms on the trace: between threshold (about 1.6 ms) and the peak (about 2.1 ms), the steep upstroke, which is Na⁺ rushing in."},"exp":"At **1.8 ms** the trace is in its steep **rising phase**, between threshold (≈ −55 mV at 1.6 ms) and the peak (≈ +35 mV at 2.1 ms). Depolarization opens **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels**, and Na⁺ flows in down its gradient, driving the potential up. K⁺ efflux causes the later fall, the pump only restores gradients slowly, and Ca²⁺ entry happens at the terminal.","how":{"ask":"Find where 1.8 ms falls on the trace (rising, falling or undershoot) and match that phase to its ion movement.","know":"• **Threshold** (≈ −55 mV) at the hillock → voltage-gated **Na⁺ channels open: Na⁺ in** (depolarization).\n• Then voltage-gated **K⁺ channels: K⁺ out** (repolarization) and an undershoot (hyperpolarization).\n• Ca²⁺ influx and transmitter release come later, at the terminal.","steps":[{"text":"Place 1.8 ms on the trace.","hl":"at 1.8 ms","calc":["1.6 ms ≈ −55 mV (threshold) → 2.1 ms ≈ +35 mV (peak)","1.8 ms: steep rise"]},{"text":"A rising potential means positive charge entering: Na⁺."},{"text":"K⁺ out is the falling phase, the pump is too slow, and Ca²⁺ enters at the terminal.","strike":["Voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, and K⁺ flows out","The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps Na⁺ out","Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, and Ca²⁺ flows in"]},{"text":"Answer: voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, and Na⁺ flows in."}],"trap":"Reading the time as after the peak: check whether the trace is still climbing at that time before choosing K⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"axon hillock","note":"where the spike starts"},{"t":"single action potential","note":"one action potential"},{"t":"at 1.8 ms","note":"read the phase here"}]},{"text":"**Order of events:** graded potentials reach threshold at the hillock → Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ in → K⁺ channels open, K⁺ out → hyperpolarization → rest."},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ influx and neurotransmitter release come last, at the axon terminal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an action-potential trace, upstroke = Na⁺ in, downstroke = K⁺ out, undershoot = K⁺ channels slow to close."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-59a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"heat q = mcΔT vs q = mL","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-cp:59#1","pe4-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:59"],"q":"A 300 W heater warms 0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C. Assuming all of the heater's energy goes into the ethanol, approximately how long does the heating take?","choices":["0.20 s","72 s","200 s","272 s"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"t = mcΔT ÷ P. ΔT = 50; 0.50 × 50 = 25; 25 × 2400 J = 60,000 J; 60,000 ÷ 300 = 200 s."},"exp":"**q = mcΔT** = 0.50 kg × 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C) × 50 °C = 60 kJ = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. **t = q/P** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J ÷ 300 J/s = **200 s**.","how":{"ask":"Find the heat the ethanol absorbs, convert it to joules, then divide by the heater's power.","know":"**q = mcΔT**, with m in the unit c uses (kJ/(kg·°C) takes kg; J/(g·°C) takes g) and **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. **P = q/t**, so **t = q/P**; 1 W = 1 J/s and 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Note the values; c is per kilogram, so the mass stays in kg.","hl":"0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C"},{"text":"Use the temperature change, not either reading: ΔT = 18 gives 72 s and ΔT = 68 gives 272 s.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["72 s","272 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","q = **60 kJ** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"Divide by the power, in joules per second.","hl":"300 W","calc":["t = q ÷ P","t = 60 000 J ÷ 300 J/s","t = **200 s**"]},{"text":"Dividing 60 kJ by 300 W without changing kJ to J gives a time 1000 times too short.","strike":["0.20 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 s."}],"trap":"Mixing kJ with W (J/s): 60 ÷ 300 = 0.20 s. Convert kJ to J first. Using 18 or 68 as ΔT gives 72 s or 272 s."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"300 W","note":"power: 300 J each second"},{"t":"0.50 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)","note":"per kg, so keep kg"},{"t":"from 18 °C to 68 °C","note":"start → end"}]},{"text":"**Match the units:** c is in kJ per kg per °C, so the mass goes in as 0.50 kg; no gram conversion.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","0.50 × 2.4 = 1.2","1.2 × 50 = **60 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Time from power: t = q/P.** Power is in W = J/s, so change kJ to J first.","calc":["60 kJ = 60 000 J","t = 60 000 ÷ 300 = **200 s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT with the mass in the unit c uses, then t = q ÷ P with q in joules (1 W = 1 J/s)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-01a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:1"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"Approximately what mass of F should be weighed out for one stock?","choices":["1.2 mg","12 mg","0.12 mg","1.2 g"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Moles first: 40 μM × 0.25 L = 10 μmol. Then 10 μmol × 122 g/mol ≈ 1200 μg, a little over 1 mg."},"exp":"Sentence 3 gives the stock: moles = M × V = (40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L)(0.2500 L) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol. Sentence 1 gives the molar mass: 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 122 g/mol ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻³ g, so **about 1.2 mg**. The buffer density is not needed.","how":{"ask":"How many moles of F does the 250.0 mL stock hold, and what mass is that?","know":"• **mol = M × V**, with V in litres.\n• **μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, **mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**; 1 g = 1000 mg.\n• A solvent density matters only when a mass of solution must be turned into a volume.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the stock's volume and concentration; sentence 1 gives the molar mass of F.","hl":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock","calc":["M = 40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L","V = 0.2500 L","molar mass = 122 g/mol"]},{"text":"Concept: moles = M × V (litres), then mass = moles × molar mass, then g → mg.","calc":["n = (40 × 10⁻⁶)(0.2500) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","m = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 122 ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻³ g"]},{"text":"Reading μM as mM gives a mass a thousand times too big.","strike":["1.2 g"]},{"text":"A slip in the mL → L step moves the answer by ten either way.","strike":["12 mg","0.12 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.2 mg."}],"trap":"Using the 1.02 g/mL density from sentence 3: it describes the buffer, not F, and plays no part in mol = M × V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"122 g/mol","note":"grams per mole of F"},{"t":"density 1.02 g/mL","note":"not needed"},{"t":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock","note":"V and M"}]},{"text":"**Units first.** Put every quantity in mol, L and g before multiplying.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","250.0 mL = 0.2500 L"]},{"text":"**Chain it.** mol = M × V; mass = mol × molar mass; then g → mg (× 1000)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity (M) × volume (L) × molar mass; convert μM and mL first, and ignore a solvent density that the math does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"Le Châtelier shift","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:2#1","pe5-cp:2#2","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:2"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"In Trial 2, after the cyclodextrin is added, the system is best described by the statement that:","choices":["it shifts toward free E, and Kd is unchanged.","it shifts toward EF, and Kd is unchanged.","it shifts toward free E, and Kd becomes larger.","it does not shift, and Kd is unchanged."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[1]},{"f":"K changes only with temperature","c":[2]},{"f":"Removing a reactant shifts equilibrium","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Le Châtelier: pulling out a reactant (free F) pushes E + F ⇌ EF backward, so EF falls apart; the temperature never changed, so Kd did not either."},"exp":"The cyclodextrin removes free F, a reactant of E + F ⇌ EF. **Removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium toward reactants** (Le Châtelier's principle), so EF dissociates and the bound share drops. The temperature is unchanged, so **Kd is unchanged**: only Q moved.","how":{"ask":"In Trial 2, which way did E + F ⇌ EF shift, and did Kd change?","know":"• **Le Châtelier's principle**: a system at equilibrium shifts to offset a stress.\n• **Removing a reactant** (or adding a product) → shift toward reactants.\n• **Adding a reactant** (or removing a product) → shift toward products.\n• A trapping agent acts by lowering the free concentration of what it grabs.\n• **K (or Kd) changes only with temperature.**","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the cyclodextrin traps free F: a reactant of E + F ⇌ EF is being pulled out.","hl":"traps free F","calc":["E + F ⇌ EF","cyclodextrin + F → trapped F: free [F] drops"]},{"text":"Concept, Le Châtelier: removing a reactant makes Q = [EF]/([E][F]) rise above K, so the reverse reaction runs and EF falls apart.","calc":["[F] ↓ → Q > K → shift left"],"strike":["it shifts toward EF, and Kd is unchanged."]},{"text":"Sentence 6 says Kd was the same at 30 °C, and the bound share did change, so the mixture shifted.","hl":"was the same","strike":["it shifts toward free E, and Kd becomes larger.","it does not shift, and Kd is unchanged."]},{"text":"Answer: it shifts toward free E, and Kd is unchanged."}],"trap":"Thinking Kd must change because less complex is present: a stress moves the concentrations, not the constant."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"at equilibrium","note":"Q = K before the stress"},{"t":"traps free F","note":"a reactant is removed"},{"t":"was the same","note":"Kd did not change"}]},{"text":"**Stress and response.** Removing a reactant makes Q > K, so the reverse reaction runs until Q = K again; the constant itself never moves at fixed temperature.","calc":["free F removed → Q > K → shift left","EF ↓ → bound share drops"]},{"text":"**Name the principle.** This is Le Châtelier's principle. Other named principles describe electrons, not equilibria: the Pauli exclusion principle (no two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers) and the uncertainty principle (position and momentum cannot both be known exactly)."},{"text":"**Also:** removing a product shifts right. In an esterification, RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O, pulling water out makes Q < K and **raises the ester yield**; adding a reactant does the same.","calc":["remove H₂O → Q falls below K","→ shift right → more ester"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding or removing a reactant or product shifts an equilibrium to offset the change (Le Châtelier's principle), while K stays constant unless the temperature changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-03a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"solubility: like dissolves like","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:3"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"Which analog is most likely to be more soluble in water than F, shown?","fig":"1-phenylethanol","choices":["Analog P","Analog Q","Analog R","Analog S"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Adding CH₂ or Br lowers solubility","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Losing an –OH lowers solubility","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each change for a new H-bonding group. Only one change adds an O–H that water can bond to; every other change adds grease or removes the only polar group."},"exp":"Water dissolves molecules it can **hydrogen bond** to. Analog R adds a phenol **–OH**, a new H-bond donor and acceptor, so it should be more soluble. Analog P adds a CH₂, Analog S a Br, and Analog Q loses the only –OH: all are **less** soluble.","how":{"ask":"Which single change gives water a new place to hydrogen bond, without adding nonpolar bulk?","know":"• **Raise solubility**: add –OH, –NH₂, a ring N (CH → N), or a group that ionizes at the pH.\n• **Lower solubility**: add CH₂/CH₃ or halogens, or swap a polar group for C–H or an ether.\n• Compare each analog with the lead, one change at a time.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says F's –OH is its only polar group; the rest is a benzene ring and C–H, so F barely dissolves.","hl":"F's –OH is its only polar group"},{"text":"Concept: water dissolves what it can hydrogen bond to. Sort each change in sentence 8: polar group added, or grease added and polar group lost.","calc":["P: + CH₂ → less polar","Q: –OH → –H → loses H-bonding","R: + ring –OH → more H-bonding","S: + ring Br → less polar"]},{"text":"Adding a CH₂ or a Br adds grease with no new H-bonding.","strike":["Analog P","Analog S"]},{"text":"Losing the –OH removes the one group water binds.","strike":["Analog Q"]},{"text":"Answer: Analog R, the one that adds an –OH."}],"trap":"Picking the bromo analog because a C–Br bond is polar: a halogen adds a dipole but cannot hydrogen bond, so it does not help water dissolve the molecule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"only slightly soluble in water","note":"goal: raise solubility"},{"t":"a single change","note":"compare each analog with F"},{"t":"adds an –OH to the ring","note":"new H-bond donor and acceptor"}]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water dissolves what it can hydrogen bond to; nonpolar C–H surface pushes a molecule out of water."},{"text":"**Also:** the same logic runs other changes: replacing a ring CH with N (benzene → pyridine) adds an H-bond acceptor and raises solubility, while turning –OH into –OCH₃ or adding a methyl lowers it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an analog is more water-soluble when its change adds a group that hydrogen bonds to water (–OH, N, NH, an ion); added carbons, methyls or halogens lower solubility."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-04a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"kcat/KM catalytic efficiency","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:4#1","pe5-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:4"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P1","passage":"Compound F, 1-phenylethanol (122 g/mol), blocks a yeast hydrolase by binding only the free enzyme in its active site: E + F ⇌ EF. At 30 °C and pH 7.0, F has Ki = 5.0 μM, and the enzyme's intrinsic kcat/KM is 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹. For each run, the team dissolves F fully in assay buffer (density 1.02 g/mL) to make 250.0 mL of a 40 μM stock. In Trial 1, the assay [F] was raised from 15 μM to 45 μM with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged, and the initial rate fell further. In Trial 2, a cyclodextrin that traps free F as an inert complex was added to an enzyme–F mixture at equilibrium. The share of enzyme bound as EF then dropped, while the Kd measured before and after the addition at 30 °C was the same. F's –OH is its only polar group, so F is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Four analogs each differ from F by a single change: P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, Q replaces the –OH with –H, R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and S adds a –Br at that same ring position.","q":"In Trial 1, what are Ki and the intrinsic kcat/KM at the higher [F]?","choices":["5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ki, kcat/KM ignore inhibitor concentration","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants of the molecules at set T and pH; they'd be the same at [I] = 0 or 1 M. Changing [I] changes only the observed rate: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶."},"exp":"**Ki** (the EI dissociation constant) and **kcat/KM** (the enzyme's catalytic efficiency) are **constants** of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at a fixed temperature and pH. Tripling [I] inhibits more enzyme and slows the observed rate, but both values stay **5.0 μM and 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹**, read straight from the data.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities change when [F] changes, and which are fixed by the enzyme, the inhibitor, T and pH?","know":"• **Ki** = [E][I]/[EI], an equilibrium constant: set by the enzyme, inhibitor, T and pH, not by how much inhibitor is added.\n• **KM, kcat and kcat/KM** are properties of the enzyme with its substrate under fixed conditions.\n• Raising [I] changes the **observed** rate (and the apparent KM or Vmax), not these constants.\n• The apparent kcat/KM measured with a competitive inhibitor present falls as [I] rises; the intrinsic constant does not.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says only [F] changed in Trial 1: substrate, temperature and pH were held.","hl":"with the substrate concentration, temperature and pH unchanged","calc":["[F]: 15 μM → 45 μM (× 3)"]},{"text":"Concept: Ki is a dissociation constant, so it does not scale with [F]; tripling it or dividing it by 3 confuses the constant with the concentration.","strike":["15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme itself; dividing it by 3 treats the slower observed rate as a new constant.","strike":["5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Read both constants straight from sentence 2.","hl":"F has Ki = 5.0 μM"},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹."}],"trap":"Scaling a constant with [F] (Ki × 3 = 15 μM): more inhibitor means more inhibition, not a new Ki."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"At 30 °C and pH 7.0","note":"conditions that set the constants"},{"t":"Ki = 5.0 μM","note":"a constant"},{"t":"intrinsic kcat/KM","note":"the enzyme's own constant"},{"t":"raised from 15 μM to 45 μM","note":"3× more inhibitor"}]},{"text":"**Constants versus conditions.** A constant is fixed by the identity of the molecules and by T and pH; a concentration is something the experimenter sets.","calc":["Ki = [E][F]/[EF]: more F → more EF, same ratio"]},{"text":"**Read, don't recompute.** When a question asks for a constant under a new [F], copy the value from the passage unchanged."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ki, KM, kcat and kcat/KM are constants of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at fixed T and pH, so raising [I] changes the observed rate but none of these values."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-05a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"carbonyl reduction to alcohol","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:5#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:5"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which product does the dehydrogenase form from butanal?","fig":"butanal","choices":["2-Butanol","1-Butanol","Butane","Butanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-2-butanol","1-butanol","cpt-pe5-butane","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"C=O reduction keeps O in place","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Carboxylic acids come from oxidation","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H⁻ + H⁺ added across C=O gives an OH on that same carbon. The aldehyde carbon is the chain end, so the OH stays on C1."},"exp":"The hydride and the proton add **H₂ across the C=O**, so the CHO carbon (C1) becomes CH₂OH: **1-butanol, a primary alcohol**. 2-Butanol would put the O on C2, butane would need 4 e⁻, and butanoic acid is an oxidation.","how":{"ask":"A hydride plus a proton add H₂ across the C=O. Which product keeps the oxygen, now as OH, on the carbon that held it?","know":"• **Reduction** of a carbonyl adds 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ (here H⁻ from NADH and H⁺ from water) across C=O; no C–C bond is made or broken.\n• An **aldehyde** carbon has one C neighbor, so it becomes a **primary** alcohol; a **ketone** carbon has two, so it becomes a **secondary** alcohol.\n• Removing the O altogether (to an alkane) takes 2 more electrons; a carboxylic acid is an oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says a hydride and a proton add to butanal's C=O, and no C–C bond is made or broken.","hl":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water"},{"text":"Concept: H⁻ + H⁺ add H₂ across the C=O. Butanal's C=O sits on carbon 1 with an H (an aldehyde), so C1 ends up holding OH, two H and the propyl chain.","calc":["CH₃CH₂CH₂–CHO + H⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃CH₂CH₂–CH₂OH"]},{"text":"2-Butanol has its OH on carbon 2, which never held the oxygen.","strike":["2-Butanol"]},{"text":"Butane has lost the O (4 e⁻ gained, not 2). Butanoic acid has more bonds to O: an oxidation, not a reduction.","strike":["Butane","Butanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-butanol, a primary alcohol."}],"trap":"Thinking any carbonyl reduction gives a secondary alcohol: that holds for ketones; an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"reduce butanal","note":"aldehyde, C=O on C1"},{"t":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","note":"2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺"},{"t":"making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond","note":"same skeleton"}]},{"text":"**Count what is added.** A hydride is H with 2 electrons; a proton is H⁺ with none. Together they make H₂: 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺."},{"text":"**Open one bond of the C=O.** The H⁻ bonds to the carbon and the H⁺ to the oxygen, leaving C–OH on the same carbon."},{"text":"**Also:** a ketone gives a **secondary** alcohol the same way: 2-butanone (C=O on C2, between CH₃ and CH₂CH₃) is reduced to 2-butanol, R–CH(OH)–R.","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on a C=O gives H–C–OH on the same carbon: an aldehyde becomes a primary alcohol and a ketone a secondary alcohol, with no change in the carbon skeleton."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cofactor structures (FAD, NAD, heme)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:6#1","pe5-cp:6#2","pe5-cp:6#3","pe5-cp:6#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:6"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which labeled atom of FAD lies in the ring system all three flavins share?","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-parts","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Riboflavin has no phosphate or AMP","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The common core of riboflavin, FMN and FAD is the flavin: the three fused rings holding four N. Pick the atom in that tricyclic part, W; phosphate, ribose and adenine are add-ons."},"exp":"All three flavin cofactors are built on the **isoalloxazine ring**: three fused six-membered rings with four N, where W sits. FMN adds a phosphate (Z) to riboflavin, and only FAD adds the AMP part, with its ribose (Y) and **adenine (X)**.","how":{"ask":"Which part of FAD is the flavin, the piece common to all three cofactors, and which labeled atom sits in it?","know":"• **Riboflavin** (vitamin B₂) = isoalloxazine ring + ribityl chain. **FMN** = riboflavin + one phosphate. **FAD** = FMN + AMP (a second phosphate, ribose and adenine).\n• **Isoalloxazine**: three fused six-membered rings in a row holding four N and two C=O; it carries the electrons.\n• Adenine is a purine (a six- fused to a five-membered ring); among the three cofactors only FAD has it.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says only the part that all three flavins contain takes the electrons, so look for that shared part in FAD.","hl":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-flavin"},{"text":"Concept: the shared part is the isoalloxazine ring, three fused rings with four ring N and two C=O. W, highlighted, is one of its nitrogens."},{"text":"Build the three cofactors by adding pieces, as sentence 2 does.","calc":["riboflavin = isoalloxazine + ribityl chain","FMN = riboflavin + phosphate","FAD = FMN + AMP (phosphate + ribose + adenine)"]},{"text":"X is in adenine and Y is the ring O of ribose: both belong to the AMP half, which only FAD has.","strike":["X","Y"]},{"text":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all.","strike":["Z"]},{"text":"Answer: W, on the isoalloxazine ring."}],"trap":"Choosing the adenine because it is also a fused, nitrogen-rich ring system; it has only two rings and comes with FAD's AMP part alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"FAD","note":"flavin adenine dinucleotide"},{"t":"atoms W–Z mark four regions","note":"one label per part"},{"t":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","note":"the shared core"}]},{"text":"**Recognize the flavin.** Three six-membered rings fused in a row, four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine ring, the redox part of every flavin cofactor."},{"text":"**Also:** read the name, flavin **adenine** dinucleotide. Adenine (X) and its ribose (Y) arrive only with the AMP that FAD adds to FMN; riboflavin and FMN have no adenine."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** riboflavin, FMN and FAD share the isoalloxazine ring (three fused rings, four N); FMN adds a phosphate, and only FAD adds AMP with its adenine."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-06b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-07a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:7#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:7"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"What is the approximate molar ratio of Cl⁻ ions to complex in the crystallization mix?","choices":["9000:1","4500:1","9:1","4.5:1"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻: 2 × 18 = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. Per complex: 36,000 ÷ 4 = 9000. HEPES is irrelevant."},"exp":"Only MgCl₂ supplies Cl⁻, two per formula unit: [Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. **Divide in the same units:** 36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000** Cl⁻ per complex.","how":{"ask":"Find the chloride concentration, put both in the same unit, then divide.","know":"• In one solution, a **particle ratio = concentration ratio**, because the volume cancels.\n• Count only the salt that supplies the ion, times the ions per formula unit (MgCl₂ → 2 Cl⁻).\n• Put both in one unit first: 1 mM = 1000 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the salt and the protein: only MgCl₂ holds chloride, and the HEPES buffer is a distractor.","hl":"18 mM MgCl₂","calc":["[Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM"]},{"text":"Concept: each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻; a particle ratio is a concentration ratio in one unit, so convert to the protein's unit.","calc":["36 mM × 1000 μM/mM = 36,000 μM"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000**"]},{"text":"4500 counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂, 9 divides mM by μM without converting, and 4.5 makes both mistakes.","strike":["4500:1","9:1","4.5:1"]},{"text":"Answer: about 9000 Cl⁻ ions per complex."}],"trap":"Dividing 36 by 4.0 directly: the two numbers are in different units (mM and μM)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"18 mM MgCl₂","note":"two Cl⁻ per formula unit"},{"t":"20 mM HEPES","note":"no chloride"},{"t":"4.0 μM, a monomer","note":"one protein per complex"}]},{"text":"**Count the ion.** Each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻.","calc":["2 × 18 mM = 36 mM Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**Match the units, then divide.**","calc":["36 mM = 36,000 μM","36,000 ÷ 4.0 = 9000 per complex"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a ratio of particles in one solution is the ratio of their concentrations in the same unit, counting ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-08a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"chemiosmotic coupling","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:8"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P2","passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane complex passes electrons from NADH to a quinone through three bound flavin cofactors: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure given with a question, atoms W–Z mark four regions, and only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons. The transfer is strongly exergonic, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes, while detergent-dissolved complex with no sealed membrane warms the solution far more. The cells live in seawater rich in Na⁺. For crystallization, the purified complex (4.0 μM, a monomer) was mixed with 18 mM MgCl₂, which dissolves fully into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻, and 20 mM HEPES buffer. The same cells reduce butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO, with a soluble dehydrogenase that moves a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O, making or breaking no carbon–carbon bond. That dehydrogenase also works with NADPH or reduced NMN: NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group, NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each of these carriers, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"The transfer releases little heat in intact membranes. The explanation most consistent with this observation is that:","choices":["the free energy is used to pump Na⁺ outward.","the free energy is used to re-form NADH.","the free energy is used to speed the electrons.","the free energy is released to warm the cytoplasm."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Coupling stores most energy, not heat","c":[3]},{"f":"Transfer energy doesn't speed electrons","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Membrane redox energy not lost as heat is banked as an ion gradient; marine bacteria often pump Na⁺. Pumping Na⁺ outward."},"exp":"ΔG for the transfer is fixed by its start and end states, so the energy cannot simply vanish. The enzyme **couples** the electron transfer to **pumping Na⁺ out against its gradient**, storing the free energy as an ion gradient instead of releasing it as heat.","how":{"ask":"Decide what work an exergonic membrane redox step can do, so that its energy does not come out as heat.","know":"• An exergonic step (ΔG < 0) can drive an uphill one if one enzyme **couples** them.\n• Membrane redox complexes couple electron flow to **moving ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) up their gradient**, storing energy as an electrochemical gradient.\n• Energy that does work is not released as heat.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the transfer is exergonic but gives off little heat while the membrane is intact.","hl":"releases little heat in intact membranes"},{"text":"Concept: little heat means the free energy did work instead; the work a membrane enzyme can do is moving ions across the sealed membrane."},{"text":"Sentence 5 names the ion at hand: pumping Na⁺ out builds a gradient that later drives ATP synthesis or the flagellum."},{"text":"Re-forming NADH would run the step backward and cost energy; warming the cytoplasm is exactly the heat that is missing.","strike":["the free energy is used to re-form NADH.","the free energy is released to warm the cytoplasm."]},{"text":"The electrons end up bound to the quinone; their speed is not a store of energy.","strike":["the free energy is used to speed the electrons."]},{"text":"Answer: the free energy is used to pump Na⁺ outward."}],"trap":"Looking for an enzyme trick that makes the reaction release less energy; catalysts never change ΔG, so the energy must go into work."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"membrane complex","note":"can move ions across"},{"t":"strongly exergonic","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"releases little heat","note":"energy did work"},{"t":"no sealed membrane","note":"no gradient → heat"}]},{"text":"**Energy coupling:** an exergonic reaction can drive an uphill process when one enzyme links them; the energy is then stored, not lost as heat."},{"text":"**In membranes, the uphill process is ion pumping.** Mitochondria pump H⁺; many marine bacteria pump Na⁺. Either way the gradient stores the energy for later work."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a membrane redox step releases far less heat than its ΔG, the energy was coupled to pumping ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) against their gradient."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hydrostatic pressure P = ρgh","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:9#1","pe5-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:9"],"q":"Open tank A, 0.40 m wide, holds water (1.0 g/cm³); open tank B, 4.0 m wide, holds oil (0.80 g/cm³). Both liquids are 1.5 m deep. How does the absolute pressure at the bottom of tank A compare with that in tank B?","choices":["Greater in A, since water is denser than oil","The same, since the depths are equal","Greater in B, since tank B holds more liquid","Greater in B, since B's base area is larger"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Fluid pressure ignores container width","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Pressure depends on density too","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same depth and same atmosphere, so only ρ differs. Denser liquid gives more pressure at that depth."},"exp":"P = P_atm + ρgh. Both bottoms are 1.5 m down and both tanks are open, so the pressures differ only through ρ: water (1000 kg/m³) beats oil (800 kg/m³), so **P_A > P_B**. Tank width and the amount of liquid do not matter.","how":{"ask":"In P = P_atm + ρgh, which factor differs between the two bottoms?","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh** for an open container.\n• Pressure at a depth depends only on ρ, g and h, not on the container's width, shape or volume.\n• Same liquid, same depth → same pressure; same depth, denser liquid → more pressure.","steps":[{"text":"Write P for each bottom.","calc":["P_A = P_atm + (1000)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 14.7 kPa","P_B = P_atm + (800)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 11.8 kPa"]},{"text":"The depths match, but the densities don't, so the pressures can't be equal.","hl":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","strike":["The same, since the depths are equal"]},{"text":"Width and the amount of liquid never enter ρgh.","strike":["Greater in B, since tank B holds more liquid","Greater in B, since B's base area is larger"]},{"text":"Answer: greater in A, because water is denser."}],"trap":"Thinking the big tank, with far more liquid, must press harder on its bottom: pressure at a depth ignores the container size."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"water (1.0 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_A"},{"t":"oil (0.80 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_B"},{"t":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","note":"same h"}]},{"text":"**Only three things count.** At a point below an open surface, the pressure is P_atm plus ρgh: density, gravity and depth."},{"text":"**Also:** with the same liquid in both tanks, the pressures at equal depth would be exactly equal, however different the widths or volumes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare pressures at points in open containers, compare ρ × depth; ignore the container's width, shape and volume."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-09b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-11a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"molecular polarity from shape","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:11#1","pe5-cp:11#2","pe5-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:11"],"q":"Each answer choice shows a molecule's bonds, without the lone pairs. Which molecule is polar?","choices":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride","Nitrogen trichloride"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-becl2","cpt-pe5-so3","cpt-pe5-cf4","cpt-pe5-ncl3"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Identical bonds, no lone pair: nonpolar","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Symmetric shapes cancel bond dipoles: BeCl₂ linear, SO₃ trigonal planar, CF₄ tetrahedral are nonpolar. NCl₃'s lone pair makes it pyramidal, so its dipoles don't cancel: polar."},"exp":"In **NCl₃**, nitrogen holds three N–Cl bonds plus **a lone pair** (4 domains), which makes a pyramid whose bond dipoles cannot cancel. BeCl₂ is linear, SO₃ trigonal planar and CF₄ tetrahedral, each with identical bonds and no lone pair on the middle atom, so their dipoles cancel.","how":{"ask":"Find each shape by VSEPR, then ask whether the bond dipoles cancel.","know":"• **VSEPR**: count bonding domains and lone pairs around the middle atom.\n• Identical bonds in a **symmetric shape** (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) cancel: nonpolar.\n• A **central-atom lone pair** (trigonal pyramidal, bent) leaves a net dipole: polar.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each shape.","hl":"Which molecule is polar?","calc":["BeCl₂: 2 domains, 0 lone pairs → linear","SO₃: 3 domains, 0 lone pairs → trigonal planar","CF₄: 4 domains, 0 lone pairs → tetrahedral","NCl₃: 3 bonds + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal"]},{"text":"The three symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles.","strike":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride"]},{"text":"Answer: nitrogen trichloride."}],"trap":"Judging from the drawing alone: the drawings omit lone pairs, so count N's five valence electrons to find the hidden pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without the lone pairs","note":"count them yourself"},{"t":"polar","note":"net dipole ≠ 0"}]},{"text":"**VSEPR first:** count the bonding domains plus lone pairs around the middle atom; that sets the shape."},{"text":"**Count electrons on the central atom.** N has 5 valence electrons: 3 go to bonds, 2 stay as a lone pair.","calc":["N: 5 − 3 = 2 → one lone pair"]},{"text":"**Shape decides cancellation.** A lone pair pushes the three N–Cl bonds into a pyramid, so their dipoles add up instead of canceling."},{"text":"**Also:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds, such as linear BeCl₂, trigonal planar SO₃ and tetrahedral CF₄, are nonpolar even though each bond is polar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles; a central-atom lone pair leaves a net dipole."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-12a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ideal gas law PV = nRT","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:12#1","pe5-cp:12#2","pe5-cp:12#3","pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:12"],"q":"A sample of gas under a free piston is at 50 °C and 2.4 atm and contains 0.80 mol. Which change, with all other variables held constant, would NOT double its volume?","choices":["Raising the temperature from 50 °C to 100 °C","Adding 0.80 mol of the same gas","Lowering the pressure from 2.4 atm to 1.2 atm","Raising the temperature from 50 °C to 373 °C"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Volume tracks moles, inversely tracks pressure","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gas laws run in kelvin: 50 °C → 100 °C is 323 → 373 K, only about 15% more, not double. 373 °C is 646 K, 2 × 323, so that one does double V. Heating to 100 °C."},"exp":"From PV = nRT, **V ∝ nT/P** with T in **kelvin**. 50 °C is 323 K and 100 °C is 373 K, so V grows only by 373/323 ≈ 1.15. Doubling n (0.80 → 1.60 mol), halving P (2.4 → 1.2 atm) or doubling the kelvin temperature (323 → 646 K, which is 373 °C) each doubles V.","how":{"ask":"Turn each change into a factor on n, T (in kelvin) or P, and find the one that does not give ×2.","know":"• **Ideal gas: V = nRT/P**, so **V ∝ nT/P**.\n• T must be in **kelvin**: T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n• ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V; ×2 P halves V; ×½ P doubles V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the starting temperature to kelvin.","hl":"50 °C","calc":["T = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"Raising the temperature to 100 °C: find the kelvin factor.","hl":"Raising the temperature from 50 °C to 100 °C","calc":["100 °C = 373 K","factor = 373 ÷ 323 ≈ **1.15**","V rises about 15%, not ×2"]},{"text":"Raising it to 373 °C means 646 K, which is 2 × 323 K: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles.","calc":["646 ÷ 323 = 2"],"strike":["Raising the temperature from 50 °C to 373 °C"]},{"text":"Doubling the amount takes n from 0.80 to 1.60 mol, and V ∝ n.","strike":["Adding 0.80 mol of the same gas"]},{"text":"Lowering the pressure from 2.4 atm to 1.2 atm halves P; V ∝ 1/P, so V doubles. (Raising P to 4.8 atm would halve V instead.)","strike":["Lowering the pressure from 2.4 atm to 1.2 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: raising the temperature from 50 °C to 100 °C."}],"trap":"Doubling the Celsius number: 50 → 100 °C is only 323 → 373 K, a 15% change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"free piston","note":"V is free to change"},{"t":"50 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"NOT double","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Write V in terms of the others:** V = nRT/P, so V ∝ nT/P.","calc":["V₂/V₁ = (n₂/n₁)(T₂/T₁)(P₁/P₂)"]},{"text":"**Also: pressure sits in the denominator.** Doubling P halves V; halving P doubles V.","calc":["P ×2 → V ×½","P ×½ → V ×2"]},{"text":"**Also: always use kelvin.** Doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the kelvin temperature.","calc":["50 °C → 323 K","100 °C → 373 K","373/323 ≈ 1.15"]},{"text":"**Also:** with P and n both fixed, V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles's law). Cooling shrinks a gas sample even when none is lost: the same moles simply fill less space.","calc":["60 °C → 0 °C: 333 K → 273 K","V₂ = V₁ × 273/333 ≈ 0.82 V₁, about 18% smaller","the °C ratio (0/60) would wrongly give zero"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V ∝ nT/P with T in kelvin: ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V, ×2 P halves it, and doubling °C does not double V."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-13a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleon counting (A = Z + N)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:20#1","pe5-cp:10#1","pe5-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:13","pe5-cp:10"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"A contaminant nucleus found in one dressing has 112 nucleons, 64 of them neutrons. Which element is it?","choices":["Silver","Cadmium","Indium","Gadolinium"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Z counts protons, not neutrons","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Z = A − N = 112 − 64 = 48: cadmium, one element past silver."},"exp":"**Z = A − N** = 112 − 64 = **48**, and element 48 is **cadmium**. Silver (Z = 47) at A = 112 would carry 65 neutrons; indium is 49; gadolinium (64) takes the neutron count as Z. The passage being about silver says nothing about this nucleus: only Z names the element.","how":{"ask":"Find Z from the nucleon and neutron counts, then match it on the periodic table.","know":"• **Z = A − N**: protons = nucleons − neutrons.\n• Z alone names the element; isotopes of one element share Z but differ in N.\n• The **average atomic mass** is a weighted mean of a mixture, never a mass number.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says silver has two stable isotopes, of mass numbers 107 and 109; a nucleus with A = 112 matches neither, so work out its Z.","hl":"two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag"},{"text":"The stem gives both counts.","hl":"112 nucleons, 64 of them neutrons","calc":["A = 112, N = 64"]},{"text":"Subtract to get the protons.","calc":["Z = 112 − 64 = **48**"]},{"text":"Element 48 is cadmium. Silver (47) and indium (49) are one off; gadolinium (64) uses the neutron count as Z.","strike":["Silver","Indium","Gadolinium"]},{"text":"Answer: cadmium."}],"trap":"Assuming the nucleus is silver because the passage is about silver: only the proton count names the element."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"112 nucleons","note":"A"},{"t":"64 of them neutrons","note":"N"},{"t":"107.87 u","note":"average, not A"}]},{"text":"**Get Z.** Protons = nucleons − neutrons.","calc":["Z = 112 − 64 = 48 → Cd"]},{"text":"**Check with silver.** Every Ag nucleus has 47 protons, so the tracked ¹⁰⁹Ag has 109 − 47 = 62 neutrons.","calc":["¹⁰⁹Ag: N = 109 − 47 = 62","¹⁰⁷Ag: N = 107 − 47 = 60"]},{"text":"**Also:** two isotopes of one element share Z, so a mass number one lower means exactly one neutron fewer, and a lower neutron-to-proton ratio. Carbon-11, a PET tracer, against stable carbon-12:","calc":["Z(C) = 6 for both","¹¹C: N = 11 − 6 = 5; ¹²C: N = 12 − 6 = 6","N/Z: 5/6 ≈ 0.83 versus 6/6 = 1.00"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Z = mass number − neutrons, and Z alone names the element; neutrons = mass number − Z."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe5-cp:10"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-10a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-14a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"molecular formula from structure","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:14","sample-cp:39"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"What is the molecular formula of the sensor's model compound, shown?","fig":"cpt-pe5-4-methylimidazole","choices":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₆N₂","C₄H₇N₂","C₄H₁₀N₂"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Two N atoms force even H","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two N → even H count, so H₅ and H₇ are out. H₁₀ would be a ring with no double bonds; aromatic imidazole has two, 4 H fewer: C₄H₆N₂."},"exp":"The ring has five atoms, **3 C and 2 N**, plus a CH₃ carbon: **4 C**. Two ring carbons each hide **one H**, the ring carbon holding the methyl has none, the CH₃ has **3 H**, and the N drawn with H adds **one more**: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6, so **C₄H₆N₂**. Missing the N–H gives C₄H₅N₂, an H on both N gives C₄H₇N₂, and saturating the ring gives C₄H₁₀N₂.","how":{"ask":"How many carbons and nitrogens are there, and how many hidden H does each atom need to reach its full set of bonds?","know":"• Unlabeled corners and line ends are C; each C makes **4 bonds** and each neutral N makes **3**; missing bonds are hidden H.\n• An aromatic ring keeps its double bonds, so count them when filling each atom.\n• An H drawn on a heteroatom (N–H) counts too; an N with a single and a double ring bond needs no H.\n• With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the five-membered ring keeps both nitrogens but only one bears H; the line off the ring is a CH₃.","hl":"one bearing H","calc":["C: 3 ring + 1 methyl = **4 C**","N: **2**"]},{"text":"Concept: give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H.","calc":["2 ring C (2 ring bonds + 1 double-bond extra = 3) → **1 H each** = 2 H","ring C with CH₃: 4 bonds already → **0 H**","CH₃ → **3 H**","N–H → **1 H**; other N (single + double) → **0 H**","H total: 2 + 3 + 1 = **6** → **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"C₄H₅N₂ forgets the N–H, and C₄H₇N₂ adds an H to the N that already has three bonds.","strike":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₇N₂"]},{"text":"C₄H₁₀N₂ fills the ring as if both double bonds were gone (the saturated ring).","strike":["C₄H₁₀N₂"]},{"text":"Answer: C₄H₆N₂."}],"trap":"Giving both nitrogens an H: the N with a double bond already has three bonds, so only the drawn N–H counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"4-methylimidazole","note":"imidazole ring + CH₃"},{"t":"aromatic side chain","note":"ring keeps its double bonds"},{"t":"one bearing H","note":"only one N–H"}]},{"text":"**Worked example: pyridine.** Its six-membered ring has 5 C corners and one N. Each C shows one single and one double ring bond (3 bonds), so it hides 1 H; the N has a single and a double ring bond (3 bonds), so it has none.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["5 C × 1 H = 5 H","N: 0 H","→ **C₅H₅N**"]},{"text":"**Same count here.** Two ring C hide 1 H each, the ring C bearing the methyl hides none, the CH₃ has 3, one N carries its drawn H, and the other N, like pyridine's, has three bonds and no H.","calc":["2 + 0 + 3 + 1 = 6 H","→ **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"**Also:** the parent rings to know by formula: imidazole (five-membered, two N, one N–H) is C₃H₄N₂; pyrrole (five-membered, one N–H) is C₄H₅N; pyridine (six-membered, one N, no N–H) is C₅H₅N. Methyl on a ring carbon adds CH₂ to the formula."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count every C and N, then give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H, and only an N drawn with H carries one. A substituted ring carbon loses the H it would otherwise hide."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-15a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"molar volume 22.4 L at STP","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:15"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"Approximately how many moles of CO₂ does one ampoule contain?","choices":["1.5 × 10⁻³ mol","1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol","6.7 × 10² mol"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"STP gas mole fills 22.4 L","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"22.4 L is 22,400 cm³ per mole. The ampoule is about 1/670 of that, so n is a bit over one-thousandth of a mole."},"exp":"V = 33.6 cm³ = 0.0336 L. At 273 K and 1 atm, n = V/22.4 L·mol⁻¹ = 0.0336/22.4 = **1.5 × 10⁻³ mol**. Using n = PV/RT at 273 K gives the same result.","how":{"ask":"What is the ampoule volume in litres, and what fraction of a mole is that at STP?","know":"• **STP**: 273 K, 1 atm; 1 mol of ideal gas = 22.4 L.\n• **1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• Or n = PV/RT with V in L and T in K.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the ampoule's volume in cm³; convert it to litres.","hl":"internal volume 33.6 cm³","calc":["33.6 cm³ = 33.6 mL = 0.0336 L"]},{"text":"Concept: sentence 7 says a mole fills 22.4 L at these conditions, so divide the volume by 22.4 L/mol.","calc":["n = 0.0336 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"Skipping the cm³ → L step, or doing it twice, misses by a thousand.","strike":["1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]},{"text":"22.4 ÷ 0.0336 is litres per litre, not moles: the ratio is upside down.","strike":["6.7 × 10² mol"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol."}],"trap":"Dividing 33.6 by 22.4 without converting cm³ to L, which gives a full mole of gas in a small ampoule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"33.6 cm³","note":"convert to L"},{"t":"273 K and 1.00 atm","note":"STP"},{"t":"one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L","note":"molar volume"}]},{"text":"**Sense check.** A mole of gas fills 22.4 L, about a large bucket. A small ampoule holds only thousandths of a mole."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles of gas at STP = volume in litres ÷ 22.4 L/mol (or PV/RT); convert cm³ or mL to litres first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"fluorescence energy (Stokes shift)","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:16#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:16"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P3","passage":"Silver dressings fight wound infection by slowly releasing Ag⁺, and a lab tracks that release with a fluorescent sensor. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, in nearly equal amounts; the dressings are made with silver enriched in the heavier isotope, so mass spectrometry can follow the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal. The sensor grips Ag⁺ through 4-methylimidazole, a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain whose five-membered ring keeps both ring nitrogens, one bearing H. Bound sensor fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm. To save money, a student tried a 590 nm laser as the light source; even at its highest power, no 520 nm emission was detected, and the buffer absorbs no visible light. Because wound fluid is rich in CO₂, each test chamber is dosed by breaking a sealed glass ampoule of internal volume 33.6 cm³ filled with pure CO₂ at 273 K and 1.00 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.","q":"The student's result is best explained by the fact that:","choices":["photons at 590 nm are too low in energy.","photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat.","the laser at 590 nm is too dim.","the buffer absorbs light at 590 nm."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Brightness doesn't change photon energy","c":[2]},{"f":"Longer wavelength photons carry less energy","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare wavelengths: excitation must be shorter than emission. 590 nm is longer than 520 nm, so each photon falls short."},"exp":"E = hc/λ, so a 590 nm photon (≈ 2.1 eV) has **less** energy than a 520 nm photon (≈ 2.4 eV). Fluorescence emits less energy than was absorbed, never more, so **no photon can be emitted at 520 nm**. More laser power only adds photons.","how":{"ask":"Does one 590 nm photon have enough energy to produce one 520 nm photon?","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter λ means more energy per photon (E in eV ≈ 1240 ÷ λ in nm).\n• **Fluorescence** emits less energy than it absorbs; the difference is lost as heat.\n• So the exciting λ must be **shorter** than the emitted λ. Intensity changes how many photons arrive, not the energy of each.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the emission and the working excitation; compare the energy per photon of the 590 nm laser with that of the 520 nm emission.","hl":"fluoresces green at 520 nm when excited at 470 nm","calc":["E(590) ≈ 1240/590 ≈ 2.1 eV","E(520) ≈ 1240/520 ≈ 2.4 eV"]},{"text":"Concept: fluorescence emits less energy than it absorbs. The 590 nm photon has less energy than the emission needs, so it has no excess to shed.","strike":["photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat."]},{"text":"Sentence 5 rules out power and the buffer.","hl":"even at its highest power","strike":["the laser at 590 nm is too dim.","the buffer absorbs light at 590 nm."]},{"text":"Answer: 590 nm photons are too low in energy."}],"trap":"Thinking a brighter beam can make up the gap: each photon still carries too little energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"fluoresces green at 520 nm","note":"emitted photon"},{"t":"excited at 470 nm","note":"shorter λ, more energy"},{"t":"highest power","note":"more photons, same energy each"}]},{"text":"**Energy runs downhill in fluorescence.** The molecule absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a photon of lower energy (longer λ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an excitation wavelength longer than the emission wavelength can never produce that fluorescence, because each absorbed photon must carry more energy than the photon emitted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-17a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"chirality centers","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:17#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:17"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"Threonine's second stereocenter is the labeled atom:","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Stereocenters need four different groups","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter = sp³ with four different groups. The β-carbon holds H, OH, CH₃ and Cα, so X. The carboxyl C is planar, CH₃ has three H, NH₂ has two H."},"exp":"Besides the α-carbon, only the **β-carbon (X)** holds four different groups: H, OH, CH₃ and the α-carbon. The carboxyl carbon (W) is planar with three groups, the methyl carbon (Y) has three identical H, and the amine N (Z) has two identical H. Threonine's two stereocenters both make it chiral, which is what a CD signal needs.","how":{"ask":"Apart from the α-carbon, which labeled atom carries four different groups?","know":"• A **stereocenter** (chiral carbon) bears four different groups, so the molecule is not superimposable on its mirror image.\n• **CD** works because a chiral molecule absorbs the two circular polarizations of light by different amounts; in proteins the chiral α-carbons start it.\n• Every standard amino acid except **glycine** has a chiral α-carbon; **threonine** and **isoleucine** also have a chiral β-carbon.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 defines the target: a carbon with four different groups. List the groups on each labeled atom; X is highlighted.","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-beta","calc":["W, carboxyl C: =O, OH, Cα → **3 groups, planar**","X, Cβ: H, OH, CH₃, Cα → **4 different**","Y, CH₃: three identical H","Z, N: H, H, Cα → **not a stereocenter**"],"hl":"A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups"},{"text":"The methyl carbon carries three identical H, and the amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely.","strike":["Y","Z"]},{"text":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups.","strike":["W"]},{"text":"Answer: X, the β-carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the carboxyl carbon because its neighbors look varied; a planar carbon with only three groups cannot be a stereocenter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers","note":"chirality gives the signal"},{"t":"L-threonine","note":"the residue to test"},{"t":"one stereocenter, the α-carbon","note":"not labeled"},{"t":"has a second one at a labeled atom","note":"find it"}]},{"text":"**Test each atom.** Count its groups, hidden H included; all four must differ."},{"text":"**Also:** the α-carbon (H, NH₂, COOH, side chain) is the stereocenter in every amino acid but glycine, whose α-carbon carries two H. Isoleucine, like threonine, adds a second one at Cβ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a stereocenter is an sp³ carbon with four different groups; besides the α-carbon of every amino acid but glycine, threonine's β-carbon (H, OH, CH₃, Cα) is one too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"gel electrophoresis reading","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:19#1","pe5-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:19"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"As sampling time increases in the ligase reaction, which pattern is expected in the lanes?","choices":["The 40-kDa band fades as heavier bands appear","The 40-kDa band fades as lighter bands appear","All bands stay the same in every lane","Every band runs farther, and none fades"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"r":2,"c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Is mass being added or removed? Ubiquitin is added, so new bands sit above the starting one, in steps of about one ubiquitin each."},"exp":"Each ubiquitin adds about 8.5 kDa, so the substrate becomes **heavier species** (≈ 48.5, 57, 65.5 kDa …). On SDS-PAGE, heavier chains run **less far**, so over time the 40-kDa band **fades** and a **ladder of higher bands** grows. Boiling in SDS stops each sample at its time point.","how":{"ask":"Does the reaction add or remove mass, and where do heavier chains run on the gel?","know":"• **SDS-PAGE** coats chains with SDS, giving the same charge per mass, so they separate by **size** alone.\n• **Smaller** chains run **farther** from the well; larger chains stay nearer.\n• Boiling in SDS buffer denatures the enzymes and stops the reaction in each sample.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 says ubiquitin is only added and nothing is cut: the enzyme gains mass.","hl":"the ligase cuts no protein"},{"text":"Concept: on SDS-PAGE heavier species run a shorter distance, so new bands appear above the 40-kDa band.","strike":["The 40-kDa band fades as lighter bands appear","Every band runs farther, and none fades"]},{"text":"Each lane captures a later moment of the reaction, so the lanes differ.","strike":["All bands stay the same in every lane"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":90,"step":15},"y":{"label":"Share of substrate (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"40-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[10,78],[30,45],[90,12]]},{"name":"Ubiquitinated bands","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[2,5],[10,22],[30,55],[90,88]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: the 40-kDa band fades as heavier bands appear."}],"trap":"Reading every time course as cleavage: here mass is added, so new bands appear above the start, not below."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"8.5-kDa ubiquitin","note":"mass added each time"},{"t":"the ligase cuts no protein","note":"no smaller fragments"},{"t":"heated in SDS loading buffer","note":"each sample frozen in time"}]},{"text":"**Gel geography.** On SDS-PAGE, the well is at the top; light chains travel farthest, heavy chains stay near the top."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a time course on SDS-PAGE, decide whether the reaction adds or removes mass; the starting band fades and new bands grow above it (mass added) or below it (mass removed)."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-19b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-20a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"molar mass bookkeeping","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:20#1","pe5-cp:20#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:20"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P4","passage":"A lab studies a 40-kDa bacterial glucokinase that traps glucose by moving a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate; no water or other molecule takes part. The enzyme is followed by circular dichroism (CD), which needs chiral centers, since only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most residues have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but L-threonine (atoms W–Z labeled in the structure given with a question) has a second one at a labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups. To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes the enzyme, a glutamate was mutated to glutamine, which carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. In cells, an E3 ligase attaches 8.5-kDa ubiquitin to the enzyme one at a time, so chains of several build up, and the ligase cuts no protein; samples taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min were heated in SDS loading buffer and run side by side on SDS-PAGE. Left open to air, the enzyme's one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers; samples of that stock taken at 3, 20 and 120 min were mixed with SDS buffer containing no reducing agent, so S–S bonds stay intact, and run on a second gel.","q":"The molar mass of the glucose 6-phosphate formed is:","choices":["80 g/mol","260 g/mol","327 g/mol","687 g/mol"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Adding phosphate makes glucose heavier","c":[0]},{"f":"687 is each side's total","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"The group that leaves ATP is ATP − ADP = 80 g/mol, and it all lands on glucose: 180 + 80."},"exp":"The group moved is ATP − ADP = 507 − 427 = 80 g/mol, so glucose 6-phosphate = 180 + 80 = **260 g/mol**. Check: 507 + 180 = 427 + 260 = 687 g/mol on each side.","how":{"ask":"What mass does the cofactor lose, and where does it go?","know":"• In a **group transfer**, one molecule's loss is the other's gain.\n• **Mass is conserved**: (substrate + cofactor) = (product + spent cofactor).\n• Group mass = cofactor − spent cofactor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the ATP, glucose and ADP masses and says no other molecule takes part; the group that moves is ATP − ADP.","hl":"ATP (507 g/mol)","calc":["507 − 427 = 80 g/mol"]},{"text":"Concept: what leaves the donor lands on the acceptor. Add the group to glucose.","calc":["180 + 80 = 260 g/mol"]},{"text":"Check the total: both sides weigh the same, so 687 g/mol is a sum of two molecules, not one product.","calc":["507 + 180 = 687 = 427 + 260"],"strike":["687 g/mol"]},{"text":"80 g/mol is the group alone, and 327 g/mol subtracts glucose from ATP.","strike":["80 g/mol","327 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 260 g/mol."}],"trap":"Stopping at the 80 g/mol group: that is only what moves, not the product."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"moving a phosphoryl group","note":"group transfer"},{"t":"ADP (427 g/mol)","note":"spent donor"},{"t":"no water or other molecule takes part","note":"mass is conserved"}]},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.** What leaves the donor is exactly what lands on the acceptor.","calc":["product = substrate + (cofactor − spent cofactor)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in any group-transfer reaction, product mass = substrate + (donor − spent donor)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-22a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"chromatography type by binding interaction","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:22#1","pe5-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:22","pe3-cp:46"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"How is the DEAE step best categorized?","choices":["Anion exchange","Cation exchange","Affinity","Size exclusion"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Positive resin + negative protein + salt elution = an exchanger for negative ions. A tag and a competitor would make it affinity."},"exp":"Below pH 8.5 sits its pI (4.9), so aromatase is **negative** when loaded and binds the **positive** DEAE groups. Rising NaCl displaces it by charge screening. A positive resin that exchanges negative ions is **anion exchange**.","how":{"ask":"What charge do the protein and the resin carry, and what releases the protein?","know":"• Above its **pI**, a protein is negative; below it, positive.\n• **Anion exchange**: + resin (e.g., DEAE) binds − proteins. **Cation exchange**: − resin (e.g., CM) binds + proteins. Salt elutes both.\n• **Affinity**: a specific tag or ligand binds; a competitor elutes (His-tag on Ni²⁺, eluted by imidazole).\n• **Size exclusion**: no binding; large proteins elute first.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the enzyme's pI; it is loaded at pH 8.5, above the pI, so aromatase is negative.","hl":"pI 4.9","calc":["pH 8.5 > pI 4.9 → net charge −"]},{"text":"Concept: an exchanger is named for the ion it binds. Sentence 4 says the resin is positive, so it holds negative ions: not a cation exchanger.","hl":"carry a positive charge","strike":["Cation exchange"]},{"text":"Salt, not a competing ligand, releases it, and a binding step rules out size exclusion.","strike":["Affinity","Size exclusion"]},{"text":"Answer: Anion exchange."}],"trap":"Naming the column after the resin's own charge: a positive resin binds anions, so it is an anion exchanger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"pI 4.9","note":"below pH 8.5 → protein −"},{"t":"carry a positive charge","note":"resin binds anions"},{"t":"NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M","note":"salt elutes → ion exchange"}]},{"text":"**Also:** a step where a His₆-tagged protein binds immobilized Ni²⁺ and is eluted by imidazole is **affinity**: the tag binds a specific metal site, and imidazole, the His side-chain ring, competes for the Ni²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to categorize a column step, ask what binds the protein and what releases it: charge and salt → ion exchange (named for the ion the resin binds), a tag and a competitor → affinity, no binding → size exclusion."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:46"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-46a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-23a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"amphipathic lipid structures","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:23#1","pe6-cp:44#1","pe6-cp:44#2","pe6-cp:44#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:23","pe6-cp:44"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"Which of the additives shown would be most effective for the denaturing gel?","choices":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Hexadecane","Glycerol","Sodium dodecyl sulfate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-dmso","cpt-pe5-hexadecane","p1-glycerol","sodium-dodecyl-sulfate"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Detergents need polar head, nonpolar tail","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for one molecule with both a long C–H chain and a polar head. DMSO and glycerol lack a tail; hexadecane lacks a head."},"exp":"A detergent must be **amphipathic**. SDS has a **12-carbon tail** that coats the proteins' oily surfaces and a charged **sulfate head** that faces water; it also unfolds them, which is fine for a denaturing gel. DMSO and glycerol have no tail; hexadecane has no head.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has, on one molecule, a long C–H chain and a polar or charged head?","know":"• A **detergent** is amphipathic: a long hydrophobic tail plus a polar head. Tails coat the protein's lipid-facing surface; heads face water, forming micelles.\n• Ionic detergents (SDS) also denature; nonionic sugar-headed ones (octyl glucoside, dodecyl maltoside) keep the fold.\n• A molecule that is only polar or only nonpolar cannot bridge the two environments.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 sets two jobs, pull the proteins out of the bilayer and keep them in water, and it allows unfolding.","hl":"kept dissolved in water"},{"text":"Concept: both jobs take an amphipathic detergent, a long C–H tail and a polar head on one molecule. Sort each drawing into tail and head.","calc":["SDS: tail **C₁₂**, head **–OSO₃⁻ Na⁺**","DMSO: tail **none**, small polar S=O","hexadecane: tail **C₁₆**, head **none**","glycerol: tail **none**, **3 O–H**"]},{"text":"DMSO and glycerol are all head.","strike":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Glycerol"]},{"text":"Hexadecane is all tail.","strike":["Hexadecane"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium dodecyl sulfate."}],"trap":"Choosing glycerol because it is very water-soluble; with no tail it cannot cover a membrane protein's hydrophobic surface."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"pulled out of the lipid bilayer","note":"needs a tail"},{"t":"kept dissolved in water","note":"needs a head"},{"t":"unfolding is acceptable","note":"a harsh, ionic detergent is fine"}]},{"text":"**Read each drawing.** A chain of only C and H is oily (the tail); O–H groups or charges are polar (the head). A detergent has both on one molecule."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to move a membrane protein into water, use an amphipathic detergent (long hydrophobic tail + polar head); choose a nonionic one if the fold must survive."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe6-cp:44"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-44a","cpt-pe6-44b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-25a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"experimental evidence logic","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:25#1","pe5-cp:25#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:25"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P5","passage":"Aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step from a 19-carbon steroid substrate that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group of estradiol. It removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. To purify this membrane enzyme (pI 4.9) from bacteria, a lab broke the cells in 400 mL of lysis buffer holding 50 mM sodium phosphate (pH 7.5) and 150 mM NaCl (58.5 g/mol), and the extract was desalted. It was then loaded at pH 8.5 onto DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge; aromatase bound while most other proteins washed through, and a NaCl gradient rising to 0.5 M released it. After three column steps in all, mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene, the GST tag was retained, and the enzyme eluted from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta. Separately, for a denaturing gel of every membrane protein, where unfolding is acceptable, the proteins had to be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water by one of four additives drawn with a question.","q":"The purified aromatase is most strongly shown to be natively folded by the observation that:","choices":["its kcat matches that of placental aromatase.","its mass matches the one predicted from the gene.","its GST tag stayed attached through purification.","it passed through three column steps."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Unfolding doesn't change a protein's mass","c":[1]},{"f":"Column steps don't test folding","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only activity proves the fold: a denatured chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purification history. Matching the placenta enzyme's kcat."},"exp":"Turnover needs a properly built **active site**, so a kcat equal to the placenta aromatase's shows a **native-like fold**. Mass, the retained tag and the column steps would look the same for an unfolded chain.","how":{"ask":"Which of these results would come out differently if the aromatase were unfolded?","know":"• The **native fold** (tertiary structure) builds the active site; an unfolded chain keeps the same sequence, mass and tags.\n• **kcat** (turnover number) counts catalytic events per active site per second, so it needs a correctly formed site.\n• Mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps report identity and purity, not structure.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 lists mass, tag and peak shape, and sentence 6 adds activity. Look for the one result that depends on 3-D shape.","hl":"mass predicted from the gene"},{"text":"Concept: ask whether an unfolded chain would give the same result. Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded aromatase has exactly the same mass.","hl":"mass spectrometry gave the mass predicted from the gene","strike":["its mass matches the one predicted from the gene."]},{"text":"A tag stays attached, and a protein passes through columns, whether or not it is folded.","strike":["its GST tag stayed attached through purification.","it passed through three column steps."]},{"text":"Catalysis needs a correctly built active site, so matching the natural enzyme's kcat shows a native-like fold. Answer: its kcat matches that of placental aromatase."}],"trap":"Trusting the exact mass: it proves the right chain was made, but a denatured chain weighs the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"mass predicted from the gene","note":"same if unfolded"},{"t":"the GST tag was retained","note":"same if unfolded"},{"t":"its kcat equaled that of aromatase isolated from placenta","note":"needs a working active site"},{"t":"natively folded","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Ask of each result: would an unfolded protein give the same answer?** If yes, it says nothing about the fold."},{"text":"**Also:** mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps are identical for folded and unfolded protein, so none of them can show the fold."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a native-like function (the same kcat, the same ligand Kd) shows a purified protein is folded; mass, tags and purity do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"fusion Coulomb barrier","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:26#1","pe5-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:26"],"q":"In massive stars, fusing two ¹²C nuclei needs a far hotter core than fusing two ¹H nuclei. This is best explained by the fact that:","choices":["¹²C nuclei have a much greater Coulomb repulsion.","¹²C nuclei feel a weaker strong force.","heavier nuclei move faster at a given temperature.","¹²C atoms must first be stripped of electrons."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"At equal T, heavier particles slower","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Coulomb barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂: 1 × 1 for hydrogen versus 6 × 6 for carbon, a 36-fold larger barrier to climb."},"exp":"Nuclei must get within about a femtometre before the short-range strong force can bind them, and their **Coulomb repulsion** grows as Z₁Z₂. Two carbon nuclei (Z = 6) repel 36 times as strongly as two protons, so only much faster nuclei get close enough: a hotter core.","how":{"ask":"What keeps two nuclei apart, and how does it scale with their charge?","know":"• Nuclei are positive and repel (Coulomb), with a barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂.\n• The **strong force** binds nucleons but only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• Temperature sets the average kinetic energy; at the same T, heavier particles move more slowly.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the charge products.","hl":"two ¹²C nuclei","calc":["H + H: Z₁Z₂ = 1","C + C: Z₁Z₂ = 36"]},{"text":"At equal temperature, heavier nuclei are slower, not faster.","strike":["heavier nuclei move faster at a given temperature."]},{"text":"The strong force only matters once they are close; and stellar cores are fully ionized anyway.","strike":["¹²C nuclei feel a weaker strong force.","¹²C atoms must first be stripped of electrons."]},{"text":"Answer: ¹²C nuclei have a much greater Coulomb repulsion."}],"trap":"Blaming the strong force: it is the electric repulsion, before contact, that sets the needed temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ¹²C nuclei","note":"Z = 6 each"},{"t":"two ¹H nuclei","note":"Z = 1 each"},{"t":"far hotter core","note":"more kinetic energy needed"}]},{"text":"**Two forces.** Electric repulsion acts at long range and pushes nuclei apart; the strong force pulls only once they nearly touch. High temperature gives nuclei the speed to get through the repulsion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fusion needs very high temperatures because only very fast nuclei can push through their electric repulsion, and that barrier grows with the product of the charges."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-26b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-27a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enantiomer properties","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:27#1","pe5-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:27","sample-cp:41"],"q":"(R)-Carvone, shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone, smells of spearmint, while the S form smells of caraway. Which property differs between them?","fig":["cpt-pe5-r-carvone","cpt-pe5-s-carvone"],"choices":["Sign of rotation","Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Enantiomers share achiral physical properties","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror-image pair = identical in every achiral test (bp, IR, solubility in ethanol). Only plane-polarized light (or a chiral receptor, like smell) tells them apart. Sign of rotation."},"exp":"The two forms are **enantiomers** (mirror images at one stereocenter). In an achiral setting they share boiling point, density, solubility and IR spectrum; they differ only in the **sign of optical rotation** and in how they bind chiral partners, such as the protein odor receptors that tell them apart.","how":{"ask":"Decide which properties can tell two mirror-image molecules apart.","know":"• **Enantiomers** have identical physical properties in an achiral environment: bp, mp, density, solubility in achiral solvents, IR and NMR.\n• They rotate plane-polarized light by **equal amounts in opposite directions**.\n• They interact differently with **chiral** things: enzymes, receptors, chiral columns.","steps":[{"text":"R and S at the same single stereocenter, with the same connectivity, make the two forms enantiomers.","hl":"shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone"},{"text":"Boiling point, IR and solubility in an achiral solvent such as ethanol are the same for both.","strike":["Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"]},{"text":"Answer: the sign of optical rotation."}],"trap":"Concluding from the different smells that the molecules differ in ordinary physical properties: the odor difference comes from chiral receptors."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mirror image","note":"enantiomers"},{"t":"smells of spearmint","note":"chiral receptor response"}]},{"text":"**Classify the pair.** Same connectivity, opposite configuration at the one stereocenter: enantiomers."},{"text":"**Achiral tests can't tell them apart.** Heat, solvents like ethanol and IR light treat both the same."},{"text":"**Also:** enantiomers smell or act differently because odor receptors and enzymes are made of L-amino acids and are therefore chiral, binding each form differently."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** enantiomers share bp, density, solubility and IR; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in interactions with chiral partners."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"continuity Q = Av","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2","pe1-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:28","pe1-cp:26"],"q":"Water flows at 0.40 m/s through a hose of 2.4 cm inner diameter. Assuming steady flow of an incompressible fluid, what is the speed of the water where the hose narrows to 0.80 cm?","choices":["3.6 m/s","1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s","0.40 m/s"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Flow speeds up where pipes narrow","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Diameter ratio 2.4/0.80 = 3, so the area falls 9-fold and the speed rises 9-fold from 0.40 m/s."},"exp":"For an incompressible fluid, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. Since A = πd²/4, v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)² = 0.40 × (2.4/0.80)² = 0.40 × 9 = **3.6 m/s**.","how":{"ask":"By what factor does the cross-sectional area shrink, and how does that change the speed?","know":"• **Continuity**: volume flow rate Q = Av is the same everywhere in a pipe.\n• **A = πd²/4**, so area scales with diameter squared.\n• Halving the diameter quarters the area and makes the fluid 4 times faster.","steps":[{"text":"Find the diameter ratio.","hl":"2.4 cm inner diameter","calc":["d₁/d₂ = 2.4/0.80 = 3"]},{"text":"Square it for the area ratio, and scale the speed up by that factor.","calc":["A₁/A₂ = 3² = 9","v₂ = 0.40 × 9 = 3.6 m/s"]},{"text":"Using the diameter ratio unsquared, or dividing by the area ratio, gets the size or the direction wrong.","strike":["1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s"]},{"text":"The same flow through less area can't keep the same speed.","strike":["0.40 m/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.6 m/s."}],"trap":"Scaling by the diameter ratio instead of its square: flow depends on area."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 m/s","note":"v₁"},{"t":"2.4 cm inner diameter","note":"d₁"},{"t":"narrows to 0.80 cm","note":"d₂"}]},{"text":"**Continuity.** What enters each second must leave each second: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.","calc":["v₂ = v₁ × (d₁/d₂)²"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a narrowing pipe, speed rises by the square of the diameter ratio (halve d → 4× v)."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:26","pe3-cp:50","pe3-cp:52"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-26a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"electron configuration","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:29"],"q":"Which of the following ions has the same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas?","choices":["Se²⁻","Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Half or full d isn't noble-gas","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Electrons = Z − charge; noble counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86. Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = 36 (Kr) ✓. Fe³⁺ 23, Cu⁺ 28, Sn²⁺ 48: none."},"exp":"**Electrons = Z − charge.** Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36**, the count of krypton. Fe³⁺ has 23, Cu⁺ 28 and Sn²⁺ 48, none a noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54); a half-filled or full d subshell is not a noble-gas configuration.","how":{"ask":"Count each ion's electrons and compare with the noble-gas counts.","know":"• **Electrons = Z − charge** (a 2− ion has Z + 2).\n• Noble-gas electron counts: **2 (He), 10 (Ne), 18 (Ar), 36 (Kr), 54 (Xe)**.\n• Transition-metal ions lose s electrons first but keep their d electrons, so they rarely match a noble gas.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons in each ion.","hl":"same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas","calc":["Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36** = Kr ✓","Fe³⁺: 26 − 3 = 23","Cu⁺: 29 − 1 = 28","Sn²⁺: 50 − 2 = 48"]},{"text":"23, 28 and 48 are not noble-gas counts: Fe³⁺ is [Ar]3d⁵, Cu⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰, and Sn²⁺ is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","strike":["Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Se²⁻."}],"trap":"Choosing an ion with a half-filled or full d subshell: those are stable, but not noble-gas configurations."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"noble gas","note":"2, 10, 18, 36, 54 electrons"},{"t":"ground-state","note":"count electrons"}]},{"text":"**Count electrons.** Subtract a positive charge from Z; add a negative charge to it.","calc":["Se: Z = 34, charge 2− → 36"]},{"text":"**Match a noble gas.** 36 electrons is krypton's count, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** electrons = Z − charge, and an ion is noble-gas-like only if that count is 2, 10, 18, 36 or 54."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-30a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"data table/graph interpretation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:30"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which tyrosine can be tagged selectively, and in which buffer?","choices":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer B","Y118, in buffer B"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Selective = exactly one nonzero in a buffer's column. Buffer A tags Y21 and Y64 together; buffer B tags only Y64. Y64, in buffer B."},"exp":"**Selective** means one residue reacts while every other residue reads zero. In buffer B only **Y64** is tagged (50%); in buffer A, Y21 and Y64 are tagged together, and Y118 is never tagged.","how":{"ask":"Under which buffer does exactly one tyrosine react while the others stay at zero?","know":"• **Selective modification**: under one condition, the target is labeled and **every other residue reads 0%**.\n• Read the data **one condition at a time**; a high percentage alone is not enough if another residue is also labeled.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives buffer A as one row: two tyrosines are tagged.","hl":"Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer A: Y21 and Y64 both > 0 → **not selective**"]},{"text":"Concept: selective means only that residue reacts. Nothing tagged in buffer A is tagged alone: Y21 brings Y64 with it, and Y64 brings Y21.","strike":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A"]},{"text":"The same sentence gives buffer B as one row: only one tyrosine reacts.","hl":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer B: Y64 > 0, Y21 = 0, Y118 = 0 → **Y64 alone**"]},{"text":"Y118 reads none in both buffers, so it is never tagged at all, selectively or otherwise.","strike":["Y118, in buffer B"]},{"text":"Answer: Y64, in buffer B."}],"trap":"Picking the largest percentage (Y21 at 70%): it is tagged alongside Y64, so it is not selective."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each number means** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"buffer A","note":"condition 1"},{"t":"Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none","note":"two residues react"},{"t":"buffer B","note":"condition 2"},{"t":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","note":"one residue reacts"}]},{"text":"**Define the target.** A selective condition labels the residue of interest while all the others read zero. Size of the percentage doesn't matter; exclusivity does."},{"text":"**Scan each condition across.** Count the nonzero entries in each row: one nonzero entry is a selective condition for that residue.","calc":["buffer A: 2 nonzero → none selective","buffer B: 1 nonzero (Y64) → Y64 selective"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a selective condition, read the table one condition at a time and pick the condition where only the target residue reacts and every other residue reads 0%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structures","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:32#3","pe5-cp:32#1","pe5-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:32"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"The catalytic acid is most likely which residue?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the enzyme versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Relative activity (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,99],[6,98],[6.5,97],[7,95],[7.5,86],[8.3,50],[8.8,22],[9.3,8],[9.8,3],[10.3,1],[11,0]]}]},"choices":["Cysteine","Tyrosine","Aspartate","Arginine"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Asp pKa near 4, Arg 12.5","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Activity drops where the needed H⁺ leaves, at pH ≈ pKa. The drop here sits near pH 8, matching Cys –SH (~8.3); Asp ~4, Tyr ~10, Arg ~12.5 don't fit."},"exp":"Activity tracks how much of the residue is still protonated, so the **pH at 50% activity equals its pKa**. On this plot that is **pH ≈ 8.3**, which matches cysteine's thiol (–SH, pKa ≈ 8.3). Asp (≈ 3.9), Tyr (≈ 10.1) and Arg (≈ 12.5) would put the midpoint elsewhere.","how":{"ask":"Read the pH at half activity; that pH is the pKa of the residue. Which side chain has that pKa?","know":"• When activity depends on one ionizable group, the **pH at 50% activity = that group's pKa** (Henderson–Hasselbalch: half protonated at pH = pKa).\n• Side-chain pKa values: **Asp ≈ 3.9, Glu ≈ 4.1, His ≈ 6.0, Cys ≈ 8.3, Tyr ≈ 10.1, Lys ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5**.\n• A falling curve: the group must be **protonated** (an acid); a rising curve: it must be deprotonated (a base).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the residue must be protonated to act, so activity falls as it loses its proton with rising pH.","hl":"must be protonated to act"},{"text":"Concept: the pH at half activity is the residue's pKa. Read the midpoint: drop from 50% activity to the x-axis.","calc":["50% activity at **pH ≈ 8.3**","pKa ≈ 8.3"]},{"text":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) would lose its proton at much lower pH; its midpoint would sit near 4.","strike":["Aspartate"]},{"text":"Tyrosine (≈ 10.1) and arginine (≈ 12.5) stay protonated past pH 9, so their activity would not drop until pH 10 or higher.","strike":["Tyrosine","Arginine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Thinking a residue that must be protonated has to be basic, like arginine: the midpoint, not the direction, identifies the residue."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"loses activity as the pH rises","note":"the group loses H⁺"},{"t":"must be protonated to act","note":"active form is HA"},{"t":"catalytic acid","note":"match the pKa"}]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pKa.** At pH = pKa a group is half protonated, so an enzyme that needs it protonated runs at half speed there.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","at pH = pKa: [A⁻] = [HA] → 50%"]},{"text":"**Match the pKa.** A midpoint near 8.3 fits cysteine's –SH; Asp/Glu sit near 4, His near 6, Lys near 10.5."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the pH at half activity is the catalytic group's pKa; match it to a side chain: Asp/Glu ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Cys ≈ 8.3, Lys ≈ 10.5."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-32b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-33a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"denaturation exposes buried residues","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:33","pe6-cp:31"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which explanation is most consistent with the buried tryptophan reacting only after heating?","choices":["Heating broke noncovalent contacts","Heating hydrolyzed the peptide backbone","Heating reduced a disulfide bond","Heating made the reagent more reactive"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Heat denaturation leaves covalent bonds intact","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same mass + no reductant + no protease = no covalent change. The only thing left that heat can break is the network of weak interactions holding the fold."},"exp":"Heating to 70 °C **denatures** the enzyme: it breaks the **H-bonds, hydrophobic contacts and salt bridges** that hold the fold, so the buried tryptophan becomes exposed. No covalent bond broke, which is why the mass is unchanged; the control rules out a change in the reagent.","how":{"ask":"Which explanation fits all three facts: reaction after heating, unchanged mass, and an unchanged reagent?","know":"• **Denaturation** (heat, urea, pH extremes) breaks noncovalent interactions and unfolds a protein; the covalent chain stays intact.\n• **Hydrolysis** of peptide bonds or **reduction** of disulfides changes the chain chemically (and the mass).\n• Buried residues are shielded by the fold; unfolding exposes them.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the heated chain keeps exactly its original mass, and no protease was present: the backbone is intact.","hl":"exactly its original mass","strike":["Heating hydrolyzed the peptide backbone"]},{"text":"No reducing agent, and a reduction would change the mass too.","hl":"no reducing agent","strike":["Heating reduced a disulfide bond"]},{"text":"The control removes the reagent as the cause.","hl":"equally fast at both temperatures","strike":["Heating made the reagent more reactive"]},{"text":"Concept: a fold is held by many noncovalent contacts; heat breaks them, the chain unfolds, and the buried residue is exposed with no covalent change."},{"text":"Answer: noncovalent contacts broke, unfolding the enzyme and exposing the tryptophan."}],"trap":"Assuming heat must break covalent bonds to expose a residue: unfolding alone does it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"buried in the core","note":"hidden by the fold"},{"t":"exactly its original mass","note":"no covalent change"},{"t":"free tryptophan reacts equally fast","note":"reagent unchanged by heat"}]},{"text":"**Weak bonds hold the fold.** Tertiary structure rests on many weak interactions; heat breaks them and the chain unfolds, while peptide and disulfide bonds survive."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when heating exposes a buried residue with no change in mass, the protein has unfolded (denatured) through broken noncovalent interactions, not through any covalent cleavage."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-31a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"salt naming from neutralization","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:34#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:34"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which salt is in the mass-spectrometry buffer?","choices":["Methylammonium formate","Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Esters aren't salts","c":[3]},{"f":"Formic acid gives formate, not acetate","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Amine + acid = H⁺ hand-off: CH₃NH₂ → methylammonium, HCOOH → formate (formic ↔ formate, acetic ↔ acetate). Methylammonium formate; methyl formate would be an ester."},"exp":"The base takes H⁺ from the acid: **CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻**. The salt is named cation first: **methylammonium formate**.","how":{"ask":"Move one H⁺ from the acid to the base, then name the cation and the anion.","know":"• **Acid + base → salt** of the base's conjugate acid (cation) and the acid's conjugate base (anion).\n• An amine RNH₂ becomes RNH₃⁺ (ammonium-type cation); HCOOH becomes HCOO⁻ (formate).\n• Salts are named **cation, then anion**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 8 says methylamine is neutralized by formic acid: move one H⁺ from the acid to the amine N.","hl":"neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"Concept: acid + base gives a salt, named cation then anion: CH₃NH₃⁺ is methylammonium; HCOO⁻ is formate."},{"text":"Acetate needs acetic acid, dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH, and methyl formate is an ester, not a salt.","strike":["Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: methylammonium formate."}],"trap":"Picking the ester: neutralization moves a proton and makes ions; an ester needs an alcohol and a condensation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"methylamine","note":"the base"},{"t":"formic acid, HCOOH","note":"the acid"},{"t":"volatile buffer","note":"a salt of two volatile partners"}]},{"text":"**Move one proton.** The acid gives H⁺; the base accepts it.","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃NH₃⁺","HCOOH − H⁺ → HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"**Name cation, then anion.** methylammonium + formate."},{"text":"**Also:** a weak base plus a weak acid gives the salt of their conjugates: NH₃ + HCN → NH₄⁺ + CN⁻; name the cation first, then the anion: ammonium cyanide."},{"text":"**Also:** an ionic compound is named cation, then anion, with no Greek prefixes (those belong to molecular compounds such as N₂O₄, dinitrogen tetroxide). NH₄⁺ is ammonium, never \"ammonia\" (ammonia is the neutral base NH₃), and a one-atom anion ends in -ide.","calc":["NH₃ + HBr → NH₄⁺ + Br⁻: ammonium bromide","NH₄⁺ with NO₃⁻: ammonium nitrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid + base → a salt of the base's conjugate acid and the acid's conjugate base, named cation then anion."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-34b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-35a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"protein charge vs pI (ion exchange)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:35"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P6","passage":"A bacterial hydrolase loses activity as the pH rises, falling to half near pH 8.3 (plotted); its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and no other active-site side chain has a pKa between 7 and 9. Its substrate pocket holds a tryptophan and a proline, and mutating either blocks binding: the substrate stacks on the larger ring, the smaller ring kinks the backbone, and neither ring is a nucleotide base. A reagent tags the enzyme's three tyrosines; percent tagged in buffer A was Y21 70%, Y64 30%, Y118 none, and in buffer B was Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. A tryptophan buried in the core stays unmodified by a tryptophan-specific reagent at 20 °C, but it reacts after the enzyme is held at 70 °C with no reducing agent or protease present; the heated, unreacted chain keeps exactly its original mass, and free tryptophan reacts equally fast at both temperatures. To add a charge near the pocket, a phenylalanine was replaced with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring –COOH (pKa about 4.2) sits opposite the CH₂, and binding was measured at pH 7.0. The native enzyme (N) and a succinylated form (S), with one lysine capped as –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻, carry net charges of +9 and +7 at pH 5.5 and differ in mass by under 0.5%. Their mixture is resolved on one column with a rising gradient of the salt made by neutralizing formic acid with NaOH (Na = 23 g/mol). Samples for mass spectrometry go into a volatile buffer made by neutralizing methylamine, CH₃NH₂, with formic acid, HCOOH.","q":"Which method separates N from S, and which form elutes first?","choices":["Cation exchange; S elutes first","Cation exchange; N elutes first","Anion exchange; S elutes first","Size exclusion; N elutes first"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Anion exchangers hold only anions","c":[2]},{"f":"Near-equal masses defeat size exclusion","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Both forms are positive, so use a cation exchanger. The form with fewer positive charges holds on less tightly and is displaced first by the rising salt."},"exp":"Both forms are **positive** at pH 5.5, so they bind a **cation exchanger** (negative resin). Salt displaces the weaker binder first: the **succinylated form (+7)** elutes before the native form (+9). Their masses are nearly equal, so size exclusion cannot separate them.","how":{"ask":"What sign of charge do both forms carry, which resin binds that sign, and which form binds more weakly?","know":"• **Cation exchanger**: negative resin, binds positive proteins. **Anion exchanger**: positive resin, binds negative proteins.\n• A rising salt gradient displaces the **least** charged binder first.\n• **Size exclusion** separates by size only.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives both forms' charges at pH 5.5 and their near-equal masses.","hl":"+9 and +7 at pH 5.5","calc":["native +9, succinylated +7 → both positive","mass difference < 0.5% → not a size separation"]},{"text":"Concept: a protein sticks to a resin of opposite charge. Positive proteins bind a negative resin: a cation exchanger.","strike":["Anion exchange; S elutes first"]},{"text":"Near-identical masses rule out size exclusion.","strike":["Size exclusion; N elutes first"]},{"text":"The +7 form binds less tightly, so it leaves at lower salt; the +9 form comes off later.","strike":["Cation exchange; N elutes first"]},{"text":"Answer: cation exchange, with S eluting first."}],"trap":"Reasoning that the added carboxylate makes the enzyme \"negative\" and choosing an anion exchanger: the net charge is still positive."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"+9 and +7 at pH 5.5","note":"both positive; N more so"},{"t":"differ in mass by under 0.5%","note":"not a size separation"},{"t":"rising gradient","note":"weaker binder elutes first"}]},{"text":"**Match the resin to the sign.** A protein sticks to a resin of opposite charge; the more charge it carries, the more salt it takes to remove it."},{"text":"**Also:** at a pH where both forms are negative (say pH 8, net −3 and −5), the same pair would go on an anion exchanger, and the more negative modified form would bind more tightly and elute later."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to separate a protein from a form that differs only in charge, use ion exchange of the sign both carry; the form with less net charge of that sign elutes first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-36a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"identify functional group","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:36#1","pe5-cp:36#2","pe5-cp:36#3","pe5-cp:36#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:36"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"Based on the passage's data, which compound is the starting liquid?","choices":["Compound W","Compound X","Compound Y","Compound Z"],"choiceFigs":["butanal","cpt-pe5-2-butanone","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid","cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Acids show a very broad O–H","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Two O in the formula leaves the acid and the ester. No broad O–H band rules out the acid."},"exp":"C₄H₈O₂ has two O, so only the acid (Y) and the ester (Z) fit. A carboxylic acid would show a **very broad O–H band** (about 2500–3300 cm⁻¹); none is present. A C=O near 1740 cm⁻¹ plus a strong C–O band near 1200 cm⁻¹ marks an **ester**: Z, methyl propanoate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has two O, a C=O, and no O–H?","know":"• **Ketone**: C=O with C on both sides (≈ 1715 cm⁻¹).\n• **Aldehyde**: C=O with an H (≈ 1725; weak C–H pair just below 2900).\n• **Carboxylic acid**: C=O with OH (≈ 1710; very broad O–H 2500–3300).\n• **Ester**: C=O with OR (≈ 1740; strong C–O 1150–1250).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the formula first: C₄H₈O₂ has two O, so a one-O aldehyde or ketone is out.","hl":"C₄H₈O₂","strike":["Compound W","Compound X"]},{"text":"The same sentence reports no broad O–H band, so no carboxylic acid.","hl":"no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹","strike":["Compound Y"]},{"text":"Concept: name the carbonyl group from its neighbors and confirm with IR. A high C=O (1742 cm⁻¹) plus a strong C–O band fits an ester.","hl":"strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"Answer: compound Z, the ester methyl propanoate."}],"trap":"Picking the acid because it matches the formula: a carboxylic acid always shows its broad O–H band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈O₂","note":"two O"},{"t":"1742 cm⁻¹","note":"ester C=O"},{"t":"no broad band","note":"no O–H"}]},{"text":"**Look past the O.** The carbonyl carbon has two other bonds; name the group from those two neighbors. Here, a carbon and an –OCH₃: an ester.","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"**Also:** carbon on both sides of the carbonyl carbon makes a **ketone**, as in 2-butanone (CH₃COCH₂CH₃).","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Also:** an H on the carbonyl carbon makes an **aldehyde** (butanal), and an –OH makes a **carboxylic acid** (butanoic acid).","fig":"butanal"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify a carbonyl compound from what is bonded to the C=O carbon (H, C, OH or OR), and confirm with the formula and IR: broad O–H for an acid, about 1740 plus C–O for an ester."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-37a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ideal gas law PV = nRT","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:37#1","pe5-cp:37#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:37"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"What pressure remains in the flask once it is back at 298 K?","choices":["2.4 atm","6.0 atm","5.4 atm","12 atm"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Fewer gas moles, lower pressure","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count gas only: 4 + 5 = 9 mol → 4 NO (water is liquid). Pre-divide: 5.4 ÷ 9 = 0.6, × 4 = 2.4 atm."},"exp":"In a rigid flask at fixed T, **P ∝ moles of gas**. Gas moles go from 4 + 5 = 9 to 4 (the water is liquid), so P = 5.4 atm × 4/9 = **2.4 atm**.","how":{"ask":"Count gas moles before and after from the coefficients, then scale the pressure by that ratio.","know":"• At constant V and T, PV = nRT gives **P ∝ n(gas)**: P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before.\n• Take the moles from the **coefficients** when the reactants are in the stoichiometric ratio and react completely.\n• Count **only gases**; (l) and (s) species add no pressure (water's small vapor pressure is ignored).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the flask is rigid at 298 K, and sentence 3 returns it to 298 K: only the gas moles change.","hl":"a rigid flask at 298 K"},{"text":"Concept: at fixed V and T, P is proportional to gas moles. Gas moles before: both reactants are gases.","hl":"in a 4:5 mole ratio","calc":["n_before = 4 + 5 = **9**"]},{"text":"Gas moles after: the mix is exactly stoichiometric, so nothing is left over; NO is a gas, and sentence 3 keeps the water liquid.","calc":["n_after = 4 (NO) + 0 (H₂O, liquid) = **4**"],"hl":"the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure"},{"text":"Scale the pressure.","calc":["P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before","P_final = 5.4 atm × 4/9","P_final = **2.4 atm**"]},{"text":"6.0 atm counts the liquid water as gas (5.4 × 10/9); 12 atm flips the ratio (5.4 × 9/4); 5.4 atm assumes the pressure does not change.","strike":["6.0 atm","12 atm","5.4 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 atm."}],"trap":"Counting every product coefficient: a liquid or solid product takes up almost no volume and adds no pressure."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"rigid flask","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"returned to 298 K","note":"T fixed"},{"t":"4:5 mole ratio","note":"stoichiometric"},{"t":"6 H₂O(l)","note":"liquid: no pressure"}]},{"text":"**P tracks gas moles.** With V and T fixed, PV = nRT makes P directly proportional to n.","calc":["P₂/P₁ = n₂/n₁"]},{"text":"**Also: read the gas moles from the coefficients.** Add the coefficients of the (g) species on each side.","calc":["left: 4 + 5 = 9 mol gas","right: 4 mol gas (the 6 mol of water is liquid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant V and T, P_final = P_initial × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before), counting only (g) species from the balanced equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-38a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"solubility: like dissolves like","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:38#1","pe5-cp:38#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:38"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"The layers that hold naphthalene and the benzoate ion, respectively, are:","choices":["Organic; aqueous","Aqueous; organic","Organic; organic","Aqueous; aqueous"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Ions stay in the water layer","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neutral and nonpolar → organic layer (naphthalene). Any ion → water (benzoate⁻ from the sodium salt). Organic; aqueous."},"exp":"**Like dissolves like.** Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon, so it moves into **dichloromethane**; benzoate is an **anion**, held in **water** by ion–dipole attraction. Layer density decides only which phase sits on top.","how":{"ask":"Which compound is neutral and nonpolar, which is an ion, and which solvent does each resemble?","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: neutral, nonpolar molecules go to the organic layer; ions are surrounded by water dipoles and stay in the aqueous layer.\n• Dichloromethane is an organic solvent that does not mix with water.\n• Density only decides which layer is on top (dichloromethane sinks), not where a compound goes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 labels the two solids: naphthalene is a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate is a salt.","hl":"naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon"},{"text":"Concept: like dissolves like. Naphthalene goes to the organic solvent; in water the salt is Na⁺ plus the benzoate anion, held by water's dipoles.","hl":"sodium benzoate"},{"text":"Aqueous; organic swaps both; aqueous; aqueous puts a hydrocarbon in water; organic; organic puts an ion in a nonpolar solvent.","strike":["Aqueous; organic","Aqueous; aqueous","Organic; organic"]},{"text":"Answer: organic; aqueous."}],"trap":"Placing compounds by layer position: dichloromethane is denser and lies under the water, but which layer is on top says nothing about where a compound dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon","note":"organic layer"},{"t":"sodium benzoate, a salt","note":"ions stay in water"},{"t":"water and dichloromethane","note":"two immiscible layers"}]},{"text":"**Sort by charge first.** An ion, cation or anion, stays in water: it cannot leave its shell of water dipoles for a nonpolar solvent."},{"text":"**Then by polarity.** A neutral molecule with no polar groups, such as a hydrocarbon, moves into the organic layer."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an extraction, like dissolves like: a neutral nonpolar compound goes to the organic layer, and an ionized species stays in water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-39a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"IC50/Ki from inhibition curve","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:39#1","pe5-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:39"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"Approximately what is the inhibition constant of K?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enzyme activity versus inhibitor concentration","x":{"label":"log₁₀([I] / M)","min":-8,"max":-3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (% of uninhibited)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-8,99],[-7.5,97],[-7,91],[-6.5,76],[-6,50],[-5.5,24],[-5,9],[-4.5,3],[-4,1],[-3,0]]}]},"choices":["0.1 μM","1 μM","6 μM","50 μM"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At 50% activity read x: log[I] = −6. Undo the log: 10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM."},"exp":"Read across from **50% activity** to the curve and down: **log[I] = −6**. The x-axis is the log of the molar concentration, so [I] = 10⁻⁶ M, and 10⁻⁶ M × (10⁶ μM per M) = **1 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Find 50% activity, read the x-value below it, then undo the log and convert M to μM.","know":"• On a log-scale plot, the x-value is **log₁₀[I]**: [I] = 10^x in the axis unit.\n• **Check the unit** on the axis label (here M) before converting: 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M.\n• The half-maximal point (IC₅₀) is read straight down from 50% activity.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the inhibition constant is the concentration at half-maximal activity: find 50% on the y-axis and drop to the x-axis.","hl":"half-maximal activity","calc":["50% activity → log₁₀([I]/M) = **−6**"]},{"text":"Concept: the x-axis is log₁₀ of molar [K]; undo the log, then convert M to μM.","calc":["[I] = 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **1 μM**"]},{"text":"0.1 μM is 10⁻⁷ M, one decade off: it is the point where activity is still about 90%.","strike":["0.1 μM"]},{"text":"6 μM reads the log value as if it were a concentration, and 50 μM reads the percentage instead of the x-axis.","strike":["6 μM","50 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the axis number itself (6) as a concentration: the x-axis is a log, so the concentration is 10 raised to it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K","note":"x is a log, in M"},{"t":"half-maximal activity","note":"read at 50%"}]},{"text":"**Read across, then down.** Go to 50% on the y-axis, across to the curve, then straight down to the x-axis."},{"text":"**Undo the log and mind the units.** The axis gives log₁₀([I]/M); raise 10 to it to get molar, then convert.","calc":["x = −6 → 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM","x = −5 would be 10⁻⁵ M = 10 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an activity-versus-log[I] curve, find 50% activity, drop to the x-axis, raise 10 to that value in the axis unit, then convert to the asked unit (10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"Hill coefficient cooperativity","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:40#1","pe5-cp:40#2","pe5-cp:40#3","pe5-cp:40#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:40"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P7","passage":"To block a bacterial kinase, chemists built inhibitor K from a fruity-smelling liquid of formula C₄H₈O₂, whose IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹, another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹, and no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. One step needed nitric oxide, made in a rigid flask at 298 K that held NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture was sparked, 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) went to completion, and the flask was returned to 298 K, where the water is liquid with negligible vapor pressure. In the workup, a crude solid holding naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, and sodium benzoate, a salt, was shaken with water and dichloromethane, which form two layers. Kinase activity was then plotted against log₁₀ of the molar concentration of K (shown with a question), and the inhibition constant is read as the concentration of K giving half-maximal activity. The kinase is a dimer with two identical substrate sites; a fit of its binding data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6, and the curve is not S-shaped but rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly. For comparison, binding curves of two related kinases, monomeric X and tetrameric Y, are plotted with a question; both reach full saturation at high substrate.","q":"What does the kinase's Hill coefficient indicate about substrate binding?","choices":["Positive cooperativity","Negative cooperativity","No cooperativity","Exactly two binding sites"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hill n: > 1 positive, = 1 none, < 1 negative cooperativity; n is never a site count. 0.6 → negative cooperativity."},"exp":"A Hill coefficient **n < 1** means **negative cooperativity**: binding at one site lowers the affinity of the others, so the curve rises quickly and then flattens out early. n > 1 would be positive cooperativity (sigmoidal), and n = 1 independent sites (hyperbolic). n is not a count of sites; it can only reach the number of sites in the limit of perfect positive cooperativity.","how":{"ask":"Compare the Hill coefficient with 1.","know":"• **n < 1**: negative cooperativity (one binding makes the next harder).\n• **n > 1**: positive cooperativity (one binding makes the next easier; sigmoidal curve).\n• **n = 1**: no cooperativity (independent sites; hyperbolic curve).\n• n measures interaction between sites; it is at most the number of sites, never a direct count.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the kinase's Hill coefficient, 0.6, with a curve that is not S-shaped.","hl":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","calc":["n = 0.6 < 1 → **negative cooperativity**"]},{"text":"Concept: compare n with 1. Positive cooperativity needs n > 1, and no cooperativity needs n = 1 exactly.","strike":["Positive cooperativity","No cooperativity"]},{"text":"n is not the number of sites: a dimer has two sites, but n = 0.6 only says each binding hinders the next, and a tetramer could show n = 0.6 too.","strike":["Exactly two binding sites"]},{"text":"Answer: negative cooperativity."}],"trap":"Reading n as the number of binding sites, or calling any n other than 1 positive: n below 1 means the sites hinder each other."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"a dimer with two identical substrate sites","note":"two sites, not n"},{"t":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","note":"below 1"}]},{"text":"**n < 1: negative cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next harder, so the curve rises fast and then flattens out early (the n = 0.6 curve).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation for three Hill coefficients (same half-saturation, 4 μM)","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"n = 2.5","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.03],[2,0.15],[3,0.33],[4,0.5],[6,0.73],[8,0.85],[12,0.94],[16,0.97],[20,0.98]]},{"name":"n = 1","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.2],[2,0.33],[3,0.43],[4,0.5],[6,0.6],[8,0.67],[12,0.75],[16,0.8],[20,0.83]]},{"name":"n = 0.6","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.3],[2,0.4],[3,0.46],[4,0.5],[6,0.56],[8,0.6],[12,0.66],[16,0.7],[20,0.72]]}]}},{"text":"**n > 1: positive cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next easier, so the curve is sigmoidal (the n = 2.5 curve)."},{"text":"**n = 1: no cooperativity.** The sites bind independently, and the curve is a plain hyperbola, like a monomer's."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the Hill coefficient against 1: n > 1 is positive cooperativity, n < 1 negative cooperativity, n = 1 none (independent sites)."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-40b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pathway inhibition target","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:41"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"To accumulate pigment X while eliminating both toxins, the researchers should knock out:","choices":["enzyme K.","enzyme L.","enzyme M.","enzymes K and M."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Knockouts stop their own products forming","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A dead enzyme piles up its own substrate. X is the substrate of the step X → Y, so knock out that step's enzyme: X builds, and neither Y nor Z can form."},"exp":"To keep an intermediate but block everything it becomes, **inhibit the enzyme that consumes it**. Enzyme L converts X → Y, so knocking it out piles up X and stops both Y and Z. Knocking out K stops X itself; knocking out M still lets toxic Y form.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme uses up X?","know":"• In a linear pathway, an intermediate **accumulates** when the enzyme that **consumes** it is blocked.\n• Blocking any step **before** the intermediate stops its own production.\n• Blocking a step **further downstream** still lets the products in between form.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the pathway order: map each enzyme to its step.","hl":"W → X → Y → Z","calc":["K: W → X","L: X → Y","M: Y → Z"]},{"text":"Concept: an intermediate piles up when the step that uses it is blocked. Sentence 2 makes Y and Z the toxins; X is made by K and used by L, so block L and X accumulates while Y and Z cannot form.","hl":"both Y and Z are toxic"},{"text":"Knocking out K shuts the pathway off before X, alone or with M.","strike":["enzyme K.","enzymes K and M."]},{"text":"Knocking out M stops only Z; toxic Y still forms.","strike":["enzyme M."]},{"text":"Answer: enzyme L."}],"trap":"Blocking the last enzyme out of habit: that stops only the final product and leaves the toxic intermediate Y."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"W → X → Y → Z","note":"linear pathway"},{"t":"K, L and M","note":"one enzyme per step"},{"t":"both Y and Z are toxic","note":"stop everything after X"}]},{"text":"**Block the exit, not the entrance.** An intermediate piles up when its consuming step is blocked and production continues."},{"text":"**Upstream blocks lose everything downstream.** If K is missing, X is never made, and neither are Y and Z."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to accumulate an intermediate of a linear pathway and stop all later products, inhibit the enzyme that consumes it (the step right after it)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"balance an equation (atoms & charge)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:43#3","pe5-cp:43#4","pe5-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:43","sample-cp:59"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P8","passage":"Gut bacteria turn a colorless precursor into a dye in three steps, W → X → Y → Z, catalyzed in order by enzymes K, L and M. X is the bright pigment that is wanted, but both Y and Z are toxic to the host, and any one or two of the enzymes can be knocked out. A crystal structure of enzyme K with a nucleotide analog shows the analog's doubly charged phosphate within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge, and its flat purine ring lying parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact; no metal ion is in the site. The same bacteria release a neutral, rotten-egg-smelling gas through cysteine desulfhydrase, a pyridoxal phosphate enzyme: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___, where the blank is the gas. They also free nitrogen with urease, which turns urea, (NH₂)₂CO, plus one H⁺ into two NH₄⁺ ions and one HCO₃⁻ ion; the only other reactant is the solvent, and both sides carry the same atoms and charge. To label proteins with X, the lab uses an activated ester of X; the free-energy profile for its hydrolysis is plotted with a question, reactants at the start of the curve and products at its end, and steps with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature.","q":"In the desulfhydrase equation, what is the missing product?","choices":["H₂S","HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Charge must balance across equation","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charge: left 0; pyruvate⁻ + NH₄⁺ = 0, so the missing piece is neutral. Leftover atoms are S + 2 H → H₂S."},"exp":"Count atoms and charge. Left: C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0. Right so far: pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ plus NH₄⁺ = C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0. The leftover is **S + 2 H with no charge = H₂S**.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the known products' atoms and charge from the reactants'; whatever is left is the missing product.","know":"• Atoms and charge are both conserved.\n• **Missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms**, with the leftover charge.\n• Then name the leftover (S + 2 H, neutral = H₂S; N + 4 H, +1 = NH₄⁺) and recheck the total charge.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the full equation and says the gas is neutral. Total the reactants.","hl":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O","calc":["cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S (charge 0) + H₂O","= **C₃H₉NO₃S**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Concept: missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms, with the charge checked. Total the known products.","calc":["pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ + NH₄⁺","= **C₃H₇NO₃**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S − C₃H₇NO₃ = **H₂S**, charge 0 − 0 = 0"]},{"text":"HS⁻ has one H too few and a − charge the sides do not need; adding H⁺ leaves a + charge unbalanced; SO₂ adds O atoms that are not available.","strike":["HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"]},{"text":"Answer: H₂S."}],"trap":"Balancing only the atoms: a choice can carry the right sulfur yet break the charge balance."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻","note":"zwitterion, net 0"},{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"known product, +1"},{"t":"missing product","note":"atom bookkeeping"}]},{"text":"**Count the left side.** Cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S plus water.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Count the right side.** Pyruvate (−1) and ammonium (+1).","calc":["C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Name the leftover.** S and 2 H, no charge: H₂S."},{"text":"**Also:** the same subtraction names an ammonium ion: a leftover of one N, four H and a +1 charge is NH₄⁺, as when an amino acid loses its amino group."},{"text":"**Also:** finish by checking charge: here 0 on the left and −1 + 1 + 0 = 0 on the right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms; name what is left, then confirm the total charge matches on both sides."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe5-43b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-45a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid titration order","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:45#1","pe5-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:45"],"q":"Fully protonated glutamic acid, shown with its acidic groups labeled A–C, is titrated with NaOH. Which labeled group loses its proton first?","fig":"cpt-pe5-glu-sites","choices":["B","A","C","All three at once"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Groups titrate one at a time","c":[3]},{"f":"Ammonium groups deprotonate last","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Glu loses H⁺ by pKa: α-COOH ~2, side-chain COOH ~4, α-NH₃⁺ ~10. First off = the α-COOH, group C."},"exp":"Groups lose H⁺ in order of **increasing pKa**: the **α-COOH (C, ≈ 2.2)** first, then the side-chain COOH (B, ≈ 4.3), then the α-NH₃⁺ (A, ≈ 9.7). The pKa values are far apart, so the groups titrate one after another.","how":{"ask":"Rank the labeled groups by pKa; the most acidic group (smallest pKa) gives up H⁺ first.","know":"• As base is added, groups deprotonate in order of **increasing pKa**.\n• Typical pKa: α-COOH ≈ 2, side-chain COOH ≈ 4, α-NH₃⁺ ≈ 9–10, basic side chains (Lys ε-NH₃⁺ ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5) last.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each label.","hl":"labeled A–C","calc":["A = α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.7)","B = side-chain COOH (≈ 4.3)","C = α-COOH (≈ 2.2)"]},{"text":"Order by pKa.","calc":["C (2.2) < B (4.3) < A (9.7)","**first C**, second B, third A"]},{"text":"B goes second, A goes last, and the well-separated pKa values rule out a single step.","strike":["B","A","All three at once"]},{"text":"Answer: C, the α-carboxyl."}],"trap":"Picking the side-chain group first because it is farther out: the α-COOH is the most acidic, pulled by the nearby NH₃⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Fully protonated","note":"every group holds its H⁺"},{"t":"titrated with NaOH","note":"rising pH"},{"t":"first","note":"lowest pKa"}]},{"text":"**Lowest pKa goes first.** The α-COOH (≈ 2) is made extra acidic by the neighboring α-NH₃⁺."},{"text":"**Then the side-chain acid.** Glutamate's γ-COOH has pKa ≈ 4.3.","calc":["order: α-COOH → side-chain COOH → α-NH₃⁺"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a basic amino acid the order ends with the side chain: lysine goes α-COOH (≈ 2.2), α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.0), then ε-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10.5)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a fully protonated amino acid loses H⁺ in order of increasing pKa: α-COOH, acidic side chain, α-NH₃⁺, basic side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-46a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"energy diagram Ea and ΔH","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:46#1","pe5-cp:46#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:46"],"q":"In the enthalpy profile shown, E₁ is the reactant level, E₂ the catalyzed peak, E₃ the uncatalyzed peak and E₄ the product level. For the reverse reaction with the enzyme, what are the activation energy and ΔH?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enthalpy along the reaction coordinate, uncatalyzed and catalyzed","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Enthalpy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":160,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,40],[3,72],[4,118],[5,140],[6,128],[7,100],[8,75],[9,62],[10,60]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,36],[3,55],[4,80],[5,90],[6,85],[7,74],[8,65],[9,61],[10,60]]}]},"choices":["E₂ − E₁ and E₄ − E₁","E₂ − E₄ and E₁ − E₄","E₃ − E₄ and E₁ − E₄","E₂ − E₄ and E₄ − E₁"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Reverse reaction starts at products","c":[0]},{"f":"The enzyme's path peaks lower","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Reverse starts at the products, E₄. Climb to the enzyme's peak: E₂ − E₄. ΔH is end minus start: E₁ − E₄."},"exp":"Run backward, the reaction **starts at the products (E₄)** and ends at the reactants (E₁). With the enzyme the barrier is the climb to the lower peak: **Ea(rev) = E₂ − E₄** = 90 − 60 = 30 kJ/mol. **ΔH(rev) = E₁ − E₄** = 30 − 60 = −30 kJ/mol, the forward ΔH with its sign flipped; the enzyme moves neither end.","how":{"ask":"Start from the products, measure up to the catalyzed peak for Ea, and take end minus start for ΔH.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − starting energy**, using the peak of the path asked about.\n• **ΔH = final − initial**; reversing a reaction flips the sign of ΔH.\n• A catalyst lowers the peak but leaves both ends where they were.","steps":[{"text":"Read the levels.","calc":["E₁ (reactants) = **30**","E₂ (catalyzed peak) = **90**","E₃ (uncatalyzed peak) = **140**","E₄ (products) = **60** kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Reverse means starting from the products.","hl":"For the reverse reaction","calc":["start = E₄, end = E₁"]},{"text":"Activation energy: from the start up to the enzyme's peak. Measuring from E₁ is the forward barrier, and E₃ is the peak without the enzyme.","calc":["Ea(rev) = **E₂ − E₄** = 90 − 60 = 30 kJ/mol"],"strike":["E₂ − E₁ and E₄ − E₁","E₃ − E₄ and E₁ − E₄"]},{"text":"ΔH = end − start; E₄ − E₁ is the forward ΔH.","calc":["ΔH(rev) = **E₁ − E₄** = 30 − 60 = −30 kJ/mol"],"strike":["E₂ − E₄ and E₄ − E₁"]},{"text":"Answer: E₂ − E₄ and E₁ − E₄."}],"trap":"Measuring from the reactant level out of habit: for the reverse reaction, every difference starts at the products."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₂ the catalyzed peak","note":"enzyme path"},{"t":"E₄ the product level","note":"reverse starts here"},{"t":"reverse reaction","note":"products → reactants"}]},{"text":"**Ea: start level to the peak of the path asked.**","calc":["forward, enzyme: E₂ − E₁ = 60 kJ/mol","reverse, enzyme: E₂ − E₄ = 30 kJ/mol","reverse, no enzyme: E₃ − E₄ = 80 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** ΔH = final − initial, and both curves start and end at the same levels, so ΔH is the same with or without a catalyst; reversing the reaction only flips its sign.","calc":["forward: E₄ − E₁ = +30 kJ/mol","reverse: E₁ − E₄ = −30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − starting plateau (the catalyzed peak for the catalyzed path) and ΔH = final − initial; for a reverse reaction, start from the products."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-47a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Atwood machine acceleration","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:47#1","pe5-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:47"],"q":"Crates of 6.0 kg and 10 kg hang on a rope over a frictionless pulley. What is the magnitude of their acceleration, as a fraction of g?","choices":["g/4","2g/5","3g/5","2g/3"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Divide by the total mass","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Use the mass difference, not ratio","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂) = (4.0/16)g. The masses cancel to a pure number times g."},"exp":"Net force = (m₂ − m₁)g = (10 − 6.0 kg)g = (4.0 kg)g, and it accelerates both crates, 16 kg in all: a = (4.0/16)g = **g/4**.","how":{"ask":"What is the net force on the system, and what total mass does it move? Keep g as a symbol.","know":"• Treat both masses and the rope as one system.\n• **Net force** = weight difference = (m₂ − m₁)g.\n• **a = F_net / (m₁ + m₂)**; always less than g.","steps":[{"text":"Find the net force.","hl":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","calc":["F_net = (10 − 6.0) kg × g = (4.0 kg)g"]},{"text":"Divide by the total mass.","calc":["a = (4.0 kg)g ÷ 16 kg = **g/4**"]},{"text":"Dividing by only one crate's mass gives 2g/3 or 2g/5.","strike":["2g/3","2g/5"]},{"text":"3g/5 is just the mass ratio 6.0/10, not a force over a mass.","strike":["3g/5"]},{"text":"Answer: g/4."}],"trap":"Dividing by one crate's mass: the net force must accelerate everything that moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","note":"m₁ and m₂"},{"t":"frictionless pulley","note":"no friction, no mass"},{"t":"as a fraction of g","note":"keep g as a symbol"}]},{"text":"**One system.** The rope makes the two crates move together, so the weight difference drives the combined mass.","calc":["a = (m₂ − m₁)g / (m₁ + m₂)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for two masses over a pulley, a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂), a fraction of g that is always less than 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"heat q = mcΔT vs q = mL","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:48#1","pe5-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:48","aamc-phys-qpack:Q23"],"q":"The heating curve shown follows 2.0 g of ice warmed from −40 °C to liquid water at 90 °C. Which step requires the most heat?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 2.0 g of water","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-40],[0.17,0],[0.84,0],[1.59,90]]}]},"choices":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice","Warming the water","Boiling the water"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Water boils at 100 °C","c":[3]},{"f":"Ice's specific heat is below water's","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read widths on the heat axis: ice slope ≈ 0.17 kJ, plateau ≈ 0.67 kJ, liquid slope ≈ 0.75 kJ. The widest segment wins."},"exp":"Per gram: warming ice 2.1 × 40 ≈ 84 J; melting 334 J; warming water 4.18 × 90 ≈ 376 J. For 2.0 g, that is about 0.17, 0.67 and **0.75 kJ**: **warming the liquid** takes the most heat here. No boiling occurs.","how":{"ask":"How wide is each segment of the curve along the heat axis?","know":"• **Plateaus** (phase changes): q = mL; L_fus(water) = 334 J/g, L_vap = 2260 J/g.\n• **Slopes** (one phase warming): q = mcΔT; c(ice) ≈ 2.1, c(water) = 4.18 J/(g·°C).\n• Heat for a step = its horizontal width on a heat-added axis.","steps":[{"text":"Check which steps occur.","hl":"liquid water at 90 °C","strike":["Boiling the water"]},{"text":"Compute each step for 2.0 g.","calc":["ice: 2.0 × 2.1 × 40 ≈ 0.17 kJ","melt: 2.0 × 334 ≈ 0.67 kJ","water: 2.0 × 4.18 × 90 ≈ 0.75 kJ"]},{"text":"The ice slope is narrowest, and the melting plateau is narrower than the liquid slope.","strike":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice"]},{"text":"Answer: warming the water."}],"trap":"Assuming the phase change always takes the most heat: here 90 degrees of liquid warming outweighs melting."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"−40 °C","note":"start: ice"},{"t":"liquid water at 90 °C","note":"end: no boiling"},{"t":"most heat","note":"widest segment"}]},{"text":"**Read widths, not heights.** On a heat-added axis, each segment's horizontal width is the heat it took."},{"text":"**Also:** if the curve had gone on to steam, vaporizing would dwarf every other step: L_vap = 2260 J/g, almost seven times L_fus and more than five times the heat to warm water from 0 to 100 °C."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find the step of a heating curve that takes the most heat, compare mL for each plateau and mcΔT for each slope (or read widths); vaporization wins only if boiling is part of the curve."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-49a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pressure P = F/A","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:49#1","pe5-cp:49#2","pe5-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:49"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"If each pad were enlarged to 40 cm on a side, with the same downward force, what pressure would one pad exert on the floor?","choices":["4.5 Pa","45 Pa","450 Pa","4.5 kPa"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Divide in cm² first: 72 N ÷ 1600 cm² = 0.045 N/cm². Each m² is 10⁴ cm², so × 10⁴ → 450 Pa."},"exp":"Pressure is force over area in square metres, **P = F/A**. Sentence 2 gives the force, 72 N, and the stem enlarges each pad to 0.40 m × 0.40 m = 0.16 m², so P = 72 N ÷ 0.16 m² = **450 Pa**. One cm² is 10⁻⁴ m², not 10⁻² m².","how":{"ask":"Take the force from the passage, find the enlarged pad's area in square metres, then divide.","know":"• **Pressure: P = F/A**, in Pa = N/m².\n• **1 cm = 10⁻² m, so 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m²**: multiply the side lengths in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.\n• 1 kPa = 10³ Pa.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the force: the push straight down on the floor.","hl":"presses straight down on the floor with 72 N"},{"text":"Concept: P = F/A with A in m². The stem replaces the passage's 6.0 cm side; find the new area in cm², then in m².","hl":"40 cm on a side","calc":["A = 40 × 40 = 1600 cm²","1600 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 0.16 m²"]},{"text":"Divide the force by the area.","calc":["P = 72 N ÷ 0.16 m² = 4.5 × 10² Pa = 450 Pa"]},{"text":"4.5 Pa converts the area with 10⁻² instead of 10⁻⁴; 45 Pa slips one extra power of ten into the area; 4.5 kPa drops one.","strike":["4.5 Pa","45 Pa","4.5 kPa"]},{"text":"Answer: 450 Pa."}],"trap":"Squaring the length but not the conversion: 1 cm² is (10⁻² m)² = 10⁻⁴ m²."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens** in the passage and the stem.","marks":[{"t":"square foot pad","note":"area = side²"},{"t":"40 cm on a side","note":"new side; convert to m"},{"t":"72 N","note":"the force"},{"t":"what pressure would one pad exert","note":"P = F/A"}]},{"text":"**P = F/A.** Pressure is the force spread over each square metre of contact."},{"text":"**Also: convert cm² to m².** Multiply the sides in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.","calc":["40 × 40 = 1600 cm²","1600 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 0.16 m²","72 ÷ 0.16 = 450 Pa"]},{"text":"**Also: take F from the passage.** A passage states the force in a sentence; pick the number in newtons that describes the push on the surface, here the 72 N pressing straight down."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P = F/A with A in m²; for sides in cm, multiply them and then × 10⁻⁴ before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-50a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"capacitor charge Q = CV","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:50#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:50"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"What average current flows through the coil during a pulse?","choices":["4.8 A","4.8 mA","1.9 kA","24 mA"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Amps = coulombs per second, so ignore the joules. Q = 30 μ × 800 = 24,000 μC = 0.024 C; ÷ 0.005 s: 24 ÷ 5 ≈ 5, powers cancel → about 5 A."},"exp":"Sentence 4 gives the pulse capacitor. **Q = CV** = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V = 0.024 C. **I = Q/t** = 0.024 C ÷ (5.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **4.8 A**. The 9.6 J of stored energy is not needed.","how":{"ask":"Find the charge that leaves the plates, then divide it by the discharge time in seconds.","know":"• **Q = CV** (C in farads, V in volts, Q in coulombs).\n• **Average current: I = Q/t** (A = C/s).\n• **μ = 10⁻⁶, m = 10⁻³**: convert μF and ms first.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the pulse capacitor, its voltage and the discharge time; the 9.6 J is a distractor.","hl":"a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V","calc":["C = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = 800 V","t = 5.0 ms = 5.0 × 10⁻³ s"]},{"text":"Concept: current is charge per time. Find the charge on the plates with Q = CV.","calc":["Q = (30 × 10⁻⁶)(800) = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"All of that charge flows out in the stated time; divide.","hl":"in 5.0 ms","calc":["I = 0.024 C ÷ 0.0050 s = 4.8 A"]},{"text":"4.8 mA leaves the time in milliseconds; 24 mA is the charge, 0.024 C, read as a current without dividing by t.","strike":["4.8 mA","24 mA"]},{"text":"1.9 kA divides the energy by the time, 9.6 J ÷ 0.0050 s, which is a power in watts, not a current.","strike":["1.9 kA"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.8 A."}],"trap":"Reaching for the stored energy: current is charge per time, so use Q = CV and skip the joules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"C"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V"},{"t":"9.6 J","note":"not needed"},{"t":"5.0 ms","note":"t"},{"t":"average current","note":"I = Q/t"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV.** The stored charge is capacitance × voltage, in farads × volts.","calc":["Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ × 800 = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"**Also: average current = charge ÷ time**, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s.","calc":["5.0 ms = 0.0050 s","I = 0.024 ÷ 0.0050 = 4.8 A"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a capacitor discharge, I_avg = Q/t = CV/t, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s; the stored energy is a distractor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-51a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"parallel-plate capacitance factors","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:51","sample-cp:6"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P9","passage":"A rescue robot is tested on soft flooring that dents when the pressure under a foot exceeds a set limit. Each square foot pad, 6.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when the robot's other foot is lifted. Under each pad, a touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor with air between its plates, which stays wired to a 12 V battery while the robot is on; designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. To keep its joints warm, the robot fires brief pulses through a heating coil of constant resistance: a 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J and empties fully through the coil in 5.0 ms. A planned upgrade replaces that capacitor with a 120 μF one that must deliver the same charge per pulse.","q":"Which change would increase the charge stored on the touch sensor's plates?","choices":["Inserting a glass sheet","Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller","Swapping to a 6 V battery"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Less area, wider gap: less C","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Q ∝ V for fixed capacitor","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Battery attached means V is locked, so Q rises only if C rises. C = κε₀A/d: only a dielectric (bigger κ) helps; apart, smaller plates or lower V all cut Q."},"exp":"The battery holds V at 12 V, so the charge **Q = CV** can grow only through a larger C. Since **C = κε₀A/d**, a glass sheet (κ ≈ 5) raises C about fivefold, and the battery moves about five times the charge onto the plates.","how":{"ask":"Hold V fixed and decide which change raises C = κε₀A/d.","know":"• **Q = CV**; with the battery connected, **V is fixed**.\n• **C = κε₀A/d**: C rises with larger area A, smaller separation d, or a dielectric (κ > 1).\n• At fixed C, a higher V also raises Q.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says the sensor stays wired to the battery: V is fixed, so Q follows C.","hl":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","calc":["Q = C × 12 V"]},{"text":"Concept: C = κε₀A/d. A glass sheet is a dielectric (κ ≈ 5) replacing the air: C rises about fivefold, and so does Q.","hl":"Inserting a glass sheet","calc":["C' = κC ≈ 5C","Q' = 5C × 12 V = 5Q"]},{"text":"Moving the plates apart raises d, and cutting the plates shrinks A; both lower C and so lower Q.","strike":["Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller"]},{"text":"A 6 V battery halves V, which halves Q at the same C.","strike":["Swapping to a 6 V battery"]},{"text":"Answer: inserting a glass sheet."}],"trap":"Thinking a dielectric lowers the charge because it weakens the field: that happens only in an isolated capacitor; with the battery connected, V and E stay fixed and the plates take on κ times the charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"parallel-plate capacitor","note":"C = κε₀A/d"},{"t":"air between its plates","note":"κ ≈ 1 now"},{"t":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"more stored charge","note":"raise Q = CV"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d.** At fixed V, anything that raises C raises Q."},{"text":"**Also: the four ways up.** More plate area, a smaller gap, a dielectric, or a higher voltage each increase Q.","calc":["A ×2 → Q ×2","d ×½ → Q ×2","κ = 5 → Q ×5","V ×2 → Q ×2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d, charge rises with larger area, smaller separation, an inserted dielectric, or a higher voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-53a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"wave speed v = fλ","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:53"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"After the laser is retuned, its frequency is:","choices":["one-third as high.","three times as high.","unchanged.","one-ninth as high."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The wavelength triples (500 → 1500 nm). Since fλ is constant, the frequency falls by the same factor."},"exp":"In air, c = fλ is constant. The wavelength goes from 500 nm to 1500 nm, three times longer, so the frequency falls to **one-third** of its old value. Power does not affect frequency.","how":{"ask":"By what factor did λ change, and how must f change to keep fλ constant?","know":"• **c = fλ**; in one medium c is fixed.\n• So **f ∝ 1/λ**: doubling λ halves f, tripling λ cuts f to one-third.\n• Photon energy E = hf also falls by that factor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the change in wavelength: it triples.","hl":"from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm","calc":["1500 / 500 = 3"]},{"text":"Concept: sentence 1 keeps c = fλ fixed, so f scales as 1/λ.","calc":["f₂ = f₁ × (500/1500) = f₁/3"]},{"text":"Longer λ cannot mean higher f.","strike":["three times as high."]},{"text":"f must change, and by the first power of the ratio, not its square.","strike":["unchanged.","one-ninth as high."]},{"text":"Answer: one-third as high."}],"trap":"Squaring the ratio as if f ∝ 1/λ²: the relation is first power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"500 nm to 1500 nm","note":"λ × 3"},{"t":"does not depend on wavelength","note":"c fixed"}]},{"text":"**Inverse scaling.** With c = fλ fixed, any factor on λ appears as its reciprocal on f."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in one medium, f = c/λ, so multiplying the wavelength by n divides the frequency by n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-54a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"photon energy E = hf = hc/λ","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-cp:54#1","pe5-cp:54#2","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:54"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"Compared with the infrared photon, what frequency and wavelength does the ultraviolet photon have?","choices":["Three times the frequency and one-third the wavelength","Three times the frequency and three times the wavelength","One-third the frequency and three times the wavelength","Nine times the frequency and one-third the wavelength"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[1,3]},{"f":"Photon energy rises with frequency","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"E ∝ f and E ∝ 1/λ. Triple the energy: three times the frequency and one-third the wavelength."},"exp":"**E = hf**: energy is proportional to frequency, so 3× the energy means **three times the frequency**. **E = hc/λ**: energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, so 3× the energy means **one-third the wavelength**.","how":{"ask":"Scale frequency and wavelength by the energy factor.","know":"• **E = hf** (h is a constant): f ∝ E.\n• **E = hc/λ** (c fixed): λ ∝ 1/E.\n• A photon with k times the energy has k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the energy factor between the two photons: k = 3.","hl":"three times the energy"},{"text":"Concept: E = hf, so frequency scales directly with energy.","calc":["f = E/h → f(UV) = 3 × f(IR)"]},{"text":"Wavelength scales inversely with energy.","calc":["λ = hc/E → λ(UV) = λ(IR) ÷ 3"]},{"text":"Wavelength cannot rise with energy, frequency cannot fall, and nothing squares here.","strike":["Three times the frequency and three times the wavelength","One-third the frequency and three times the wavelength","Nine times the frequency and one-third the wavelength"]},{"text":"Answer: Three times the frequency and one-third the wavelength."}],"trap":"Scaling wavelength the same way as frequency: since c = fλ is fixed, they always move in opposite directions."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"three times the energy","note":"k = 3"},{"t":"ultraviolet photon","note":"higher-energy one"}]},{"text":"**Frequency follows energy.** E = hf, so f is proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → f × 3"]},{"text":"**Wavelength opposes energy.** E = hc/λ, so λ is inversely proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → λ ÷ 3"]},{"text":"**Also:** a photon with k times the energy has 1/k the wavelength, because f and λ multiply to the fixed speed c."},{"text":"**Also:** amplitude and intensity describe a beam, not one photon. A brighter beam has more photons per second, but each photon's energy, frequency and wavelength stay the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf = hc/λ, so k times the energy means k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-55a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"wave speed v = fλ","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:55#1","pe5-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:55"],"pid":"pe5-cp:P10","passage":"A wearable health monitor shines light into skin from several LEDs and a tunable laser, all in air, where the speed of light does not depend on wavelength. Its blue-green LED emits at 480 nm, and that light's frequency sets the energy each photon delivers to a dye in the sensor. A near-infrared LED, chosen because its light passes through skin far better than visible light, emits photons that each carry 1.55 eV; the product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. For deeper imaging, the tunable laser is retuned from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm at the same power. A bench test compares two other sources: each ultraviolet photon from one carries three times the energy of an infrared photon from the other, and the detector counts single photons.","q":"Approximately what is the frequency of the blue-green LED's light?","choices":["6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz","6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Light frequencies are huge, not tiny","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"f = c/λ: 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷. Digits 3 ÷ 4.8 ≈ 0.63; powers 10⁸⁺⁷ = 10¹⁵ → 0.63 × 10¹⁵ = 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Visible light always lands near 10¹⁴."},"exp":"**f = c/λ** with λ in metres: 480 nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m, so f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m) ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**, inside the visible band of about 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","how":{"ask":"Convert the wavelength to metres, then divide the speed of light by it.","know":"• **c = fλ**, so **f = c/λ**, with c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s and λ in **metres**.\n• 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• Visible light (about 400–750 nm) has f ≈ 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so any visible answer must land near 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the wavelength in nanometres; convert it to metres.","hl":"480 nm","calc":["480 nm × 10⁻⁹ m/nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Concept: f = c/λ, with λ in metres. Divide c by λ.","calc":["f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m)","= (3 ÷ 4.8) × 10¹⁵ Hz","= 0.625 × 10¹⁵ ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Check the band: blue-green light must sit between 4 and 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. 10⁵ Hz skips the nm conversion (radio range), 10¹⁷ Hz uses picometres (X-ray range), and 10⁻¹⁵ divides λ by c.","strike":["6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}],"trap":"Dividing by 480 instead of 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m: keep λ in metres, and use the visible band as a sanity check."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"480 nm","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"blue-green LED","note":"visible: expect ~10¹⁴ Hz"}]},{"text":"**Fix the unit.** Speed of light is in m/s, so λ must be in m.","calc":["480 × 10⁻⁹ m = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Divide.**","calc":["f = c/λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** visible light spans about 400–750 nm, which is about 7.5 × 10¹⁴ down to 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz; a visible-light frequency far outside 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz signals a unit error."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = c/λ with λ in metres (nm × 10⁻⁹), then check that visible light lands near 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Ka·Kb = Kw (pKa + pKb = 14)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:57#1","pe5-cp:57#2","pe5-cp:57#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:57"],"q":"Hypochlorous acid, HOCl, has a pKa of 7.5 in water. What is Kb of its conjugate base, the hypochlorite ion?","choices":["3.2 × 10⁻⁷","3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb 6.5. 10^−6.5 = 10^0.5 × 10⁻⁷, and 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2, so Kb ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."},"exp":"**pKa + pKb = 14**, so pKb = 14 − 7.5 = 6.5, and **Kb = 10^−pKb** = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**. Check: Ka·Kb = (3.2 × 10⁻⁸)(3.2 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ = Kw.","how":{"ask":"Get pKb from the pKa, then turn pKb into Kb.","know":"• For a conjugate pair: **pKa + pKb = 14** and **Ka·Kb = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴** (at 25 °C).\n• **Kb = 10^−pKb**.\n• A weaker acid (higher pKa) has a stronger conjugate base (larger Kb).","steps":[{"text":"Start from the acid's pKa.","hl":"pKa of 7.5","calc":["pKb = 14 − 7.5 = **6.5**"]},{"text":"Convert pKb to Kb.","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ = 10⁰·⁵ × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"Check with Ka·Kb = Kw.","calc":["Ka = 10⁻⁷·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸","Kb = Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷ ✓"]},{"text":"3.2 × 10⁻⁸ is Ka itself, 3.2 × 10⁻²² multiplies Ka by Kw instead of dividing, and 6.5 is pKb, not Kb.","strike":["3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."}],"trap":"Stopping at pKb = 6.5: the question asks for Kb, so finish with Kb = 10^−pKb."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa of 7.5","note":"the acid's strength"},{"t":"Kb of its conjugate base","note":"base strength"}]},{"text":"**Use the pair rule.**","calc":["pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb = 6.5"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.**","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷"]},{"text":"**Also:** Kb = 10^−pKb; a half-unit pKb gives 10⁰·⁵ ≈ 3.16 in front of the power of ten."},{"text":"**Also:** Ka·Kb = Kw, so Kb = Kw/Ka; the weaker the acid (smaller Ka), the stronger its conjugate base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKb = 14 − pKa and Kb = 10^−pKb (equivalently Kb = Kw/Ka)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-58a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"catalyst lowers Ea, not ΔG/K","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe5-cp:58#1","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:58"],"q":"Acetic acid and ethanol form ethyl acetate and water over an acidic resin catalyst at 70 °C. Which change would NOT increase the equilibrium amount of ester?","choices":["Doubling the amount of ethanol","Adding a drying agent that removes water","Doubling the amount of acetic acid","Grinding the resin catalyst finer"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Adding reactant, removing product shift forward","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Sort the choices: reactant added, product removed, reactant added... and one that only changes how fast equilibrium is reached. Rate is not yield."},"exp":"Adding ethanol or acetic acid (reactants) or removing water (product) shifts the equilibrium toward ethyl acetate (Le Châtelier). A catalyst, in any amount or particle size, speeds both directions equally: it reaches equilibrium sooner but **does not change K or the yield**.","how":{"ask":"Which change only alters the rate, leaving Q and K untouched?","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a reactant or removing a product shifts the reaction toward products.\n• A **catalyst** lowers Ea for the forward and reverse steps equally: faster equilibrium, same position.\n• Only temperature changes K.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium.","calc":["CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O"]},{"text":"Adding either reactant lowers Q below K, so more ester forms.","strike":["Doubling the amount of ethanol","Doubling the amount of acetic acid"]},{"text":"Removing water, a product, also lowers Q.","strike":["Adding a drying agent that removes water"]},{"text":"Finer resin only exposes more catalyst surface: faster, not further.","hl":"acidic resin catalyst"},{"text":"Answer: grinding the resin catalyst finer."}],"trap":"Thinking more catalyst means more product: a catalyst never moves the equilibrium position."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acidic resin catalyst","note":"rate only"},{"t":"NOT","note":"find the one that fails"},{"t":"equilibrium amount","note":"yield, not speed"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of change.** Concentration changes move Q away from K and shift the reaction; catalyst changes leave Q and K alone and only speed things up."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise an equilibrium yield, add a reactant or remove a product; changing the catalyst (amount, surface area or type) changes only the rate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-59a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"work = area under F–x","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe5-cp:59#1","pe5-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:59"],"q":"A robot drags a sled 12 m on a level track. The graph shows its parallel pull and the friction on the sled. How much work does the pull do?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Forces on the sled versus position","x":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Pull","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2,20],[4,40],[6,40],[8,40],[10,40],[11,20],[12,0]]},{"name":"Friction","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,5],[12,5]]}]},"choices":["360 J","480 J","300 J","240 J"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Area under the pull line = trapezoid: average of the two bases × height. Top 6 m, bottom 12 m → 9 × 40 = 360 J. Friction line is irrelevant."},"exp":"Work done by a changing force is the **area under its F–x line**. Split the pull into a triangle, a rectangle and a triangle: ½(4)(40) + (6)(40) + ½(2)(40) = 80 + 240 + 40 = **360 J**. Friction is not part of the pull's work.","how":{"ask":"Find the area under the pull line only, from 0 to 12 m.","know":"• **W = area under the F–x graph** (N × m = J) for a force along the motion.\n• Split the area into **rectangles (base × height)** and **triangles (½ × base × height)**.\n• Use only the force the question names.","steps":[{"text":"Read the corners of the pull line off the graph.","hl":"How much work does the pull do","calc":["0 → 4 m: rises 0 → 40 N","4 → 10 m: flat at 40 N","10 → 12 m: falls 40 → 0 N"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"]}},{"text":"Find the area of each piece.","calc":["ramp up: ½ × 4 m × 40 N = 80 J","plateau: 6 m × 40 N = 240 J","ramp down: ½ × 2 m × 40 N = 40 J"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work by the pull"}]}},{"text":"Add the pieces.","calc":["W = 80 J + 240 J + 40 J = **360 J**"]},{"text":"480 J is 40 N × 12 m, treating the pull as 40 N the whole way; 240 J keeps only the flat part.","strike":["480 J","240 J"]},{"text":"300 J subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m = 60 J, which gives the net work, not the pull's work.","strike":["300 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 360 J."}],"trap":"Multiplying the peak force by the whole distance; the ramps are triangles, worth only half of their rectangles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12 m","note":"total distance"},{"t":"parallel pull","note":"all of the pull does work"},{"t":"work does the pull do","note":"pull only, not friction"}]},{"text":"**Work is force × distance**, but this pull changes with position, so no single force works. Instead, the **work equals the area under the pull line**.","graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work"}]}},{"text":"**Also: split the shape into rectangles and triangles.** Here: a rising ramp, a flat top and a falling ramp.","calc":["triangle: ½ × base × height","rectangle: base × height"]},{"text":"**Ignore the friction line.** It shares the axes, but the question asks only about the pull.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Friction","x":6,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the work by a varying force is the area under the line of that force on an F–x plot; cut it into rectangles and triangles, add the pieces, and use only the force asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"IR O–H / C=O peaks","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:1#1","pe6-cp:1#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:1","sample-cp:40"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"If the Route A reduction had left unreacted cyclohexanone in the liquid, in which region would the spectrum shown have an extra strong band?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectrum of the unknown liquid","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Unknown","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,80],[800,72],[1000,45],[1070,30],[1150,70],[1250,78],[1350,62],[1450,55],[1550,88],[1650,92],[1700,92],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2860,45],[2930,30],[3000,65],[3100,50],[3200,30],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,45],[3650,82],[3800,92],[4000,94]]}]},"choices":["1050–1150 cm⁻¹","1680–1750 cm⁻¹","2850–3000 cm⁻¹","3200–3600 cm⁻¹"],"choiceFigs":null,"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"C–O and C–H bands already present","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Ketones have no O–H","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"IR pair to memorize: broad ~3300 = O–H, sharp ~1700 = C=O. A leftover ketone adds its C=O near 1700, where this trace now sits near the top."},"exp":"Cyclohexanone's signature is its **C=O stretch, a sharp, strong band near 1715 cm⁻¹**. The trace shown stays near 92% transmittance across **1680–1750 cm⁻¹**, so the reduction used up the ketone; any left over would add a dip there. The broad O–H near 3350, the C–H dips just under 3000 and the C–O dip near 1070 belong to cyclohexanol and are already present.","how":{"ask":"Which bond does cyclohexanone have that cyclohexanol lacks, and where does it absorb?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **N–H** 3300–3500, narrower; **C–H** 2850–3100; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weaker, ~1650.\n• Carboxylic acid = C=O plus a very broad O–H that spreads down toward 2500.\n• Alcohol = O–H with no C=O; ketone = C=O with no O–H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2: the ketone was reduced with NaBH₄; any unreacted ketone keeps its C=O.","hl":"reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol","fig":"cyclohexanone"},{"text":"A ketone C=O gives a sharp, strong dip near 1700 cm⁻¹. Here the trace stays near the top, so a leftover ketone would show up as a new dip.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":1700}],"shade":[{"x1":1680,"x2":1750,"text":"C=O window"}]},"calc":["1700 cm⁻¹: about **92 %** → no C=O now"]},{"text":"The broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ is the O–H of cyclohexanol, and a ketone has no O–H.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"strike":["3200–3600 cm⁻¹"]},{"text":"The C–H dips (2850–2930) and the C–O dip near 1070 are already there; the ketone adds no new band in either region.","strike":["2850–3000 cm⁻¹","1050–1150 cm⁻¹"]},{"text":"Answer: 1680–1750 cm⁻¹.","fig":"cyclohexanol"}],"trap":"Picking the O–H region because it holds the deepest dip: that band comes from the alcohol already formed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"reduced with NaBH₄","note":"C=O → CH–OH expected"},{"t":"unreacted cyclohexanone","note":"C=O would remain"},{"t":"IR spectrum","note":"which bonds are present"}]},{"text":"**Read dips, not peaks.** Low transmittance means a bond absorbed that light."},{"text":"**Know five windows.**","calc":["O–H: 3200–3600, broad","N–H: 3300–3500","C–H: 2850–3100","C=O: ~1700, sharp","C=C: ~1650, weak"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to spot a leftover starting material, find the bond it has and the product lacks, and look in that bond's window: ~1700 cm⁻¹ for C=O, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ for a broad O–H."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1","mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-01b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-02a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choosing an analytical technique","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:2#1","pe6-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:2"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"Which technique would best determine the R : S ratio of the Route B alcohol?","choices":["Polarimetry","¹H NMR spectroscopy","IR spectroscopy","Mass spectrometry"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Enantiomers share NMR, IR and mass","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror images differ only in how they rotate plane-polarized light. Observed rotation ÷ pure rotation gives the excess, and from it the ratio."},"exp":"The two products are **enantiomers**: same groups, same connectivity, same mass. NMR, IR and MS all read them the same. Only a chiral measurement tells them apart: **polarimetry**. The observed rotation divided by the pure R rotation gives the enantiomeric excess, and from that the R : S ratio. (For constitutional isomers, ¹H NMR integration would be the tool, since their peaks differ.)","how":{"ask":"What kind of isomers are these, and which measurement gives a different signal for each of them?","know":"• **Constitutional isomers** sharing functional groups: IR looks alike, but ¹H NMR peaks differ, and each integral ∝ number of H, so integrals give the ratio.\n• **Enantiomers**: identical NMR, IR and MS in an achiral setting; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation.\n• % ee = observed rotation ÷ rotation of the pure enantiomer × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says every molecule has the same groups and the same connectivity, and the two forms are mirror images: the two products are enantiomers.","hl":"mirror images"},{"text":"IR only identifies functional groups, which both share.","strike":["IR spectroscopy"]},{"text":"NMR integration works for constitutional isomers, whose peaks differ, but mirror images give the same peaks; their masses also match.","strike":["¹H NMR spectroscopy","Mass spectrometry"]},{"text":"The passage gives the rotation of the pure R form; the measured rotation of the mixture gives the excess of one form.","hl":"rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle","calc":["ee = observed ÷ pure","e.g. ee 60% → 80 : 20"]},{"text":"Answer: polarimetry."}],"trap":"Reaching for NMR integration because it measures ratios of constitutional isomers: enantiomers give one identical spectrum."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"same functional groups","note":"IR can’t separate them"},{"t":"same connectivity","note":"not constitutional isomers"},{"t":"mirror images","note":"enantiomers"}]},{"text":"**Match the method to the difference.**","calc":["different groups → IR","different H environments → ¹H NMR areas","mirror images → optical rotation"]},{"text":"**Integration counts H.** For two constitutional isomers, divide each unique peak’s area by its H count to get the mole ratio; enantiomers never give two separate peaks to compare."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to measure how much of each isomer is present, choose the technique whose signal differs between them: ¹H NMR integrals for constitutional isomers, polarimetry for enantiomers; IR only names functional groups."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"carbocation rearrangement","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:3#1","pe6-cp:3#2","pe6-cp:3#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:3"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"What compound does the Route B Grignard step produce, after the acid is added?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane","choices":["2-Methylpentan-3-ol","3-Methylpentan-3-ol","2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Grignards turn aldehydes, ketones into alcohols","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tertiary cation on C2, H shifts from C1 → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO. EtMgBr adds Et to the CHO carbon → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–Et, a secondary alcohol."},"exp":"BF₃ binds the epoxide O and the ring opens toward the **tertiary cation on C2**. A **1,2-hydride shift** from C1 moves the + to C1, where O forms C=O: **2-methylpropanal, (CH₃)₂CH–CHO**. In the Grignard step the ethyl Grignard adds to that carbonyl carbon, and aqueous acid protonates the alkoxide: **(CH₃)₂CHCH(OH)CH₂CH₃**, a secondary alcohol.","how":{"ask":"Which carbonyl compound does the BF₃ step make, and what does an ethyl Grignard plus acid do to it?","know":"• A Lewis acid (BF₃) takes a lone pair of the epoxide O; the ring opens to the **more stable carbocation** (3° > 2° > 1°).\n• A **1,2-hydride or alkyl shift** from the C–O carbon then builds C=O there: an H left on it → aldehyde; only carbons → ketone.\n• RMgBr + aldehyde → (acid workup) secondary alcohol; RMgBr + ketone → tertiary alcohol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the start and the conditions: 2,2-dimethyloxirane with BF₃, a Lewis acid, opening toward the more stable cation. That cation is the tertiary one on C2 (two methyls), not the primary CH₂.","hl":"opened toward the more stable carbocation","fig":"cpt-pe6-oxirane-cation","calc":["C2⁺: 3 carbon neighbors → 3°","C1⁺: 1 carbon neighbor → 1°"]},{"text":"An H shifts from C1 to C2, and the O on C1 forms C=O. C1 keeps one H, so it is an aldehyde, as the passage says. A methyl shift would instead give butan-2-one.","hl":"1,2-hydride shift","calc":["(CH₃)₂C⁺–CH₂–O–BF₃⁻ → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO"],"strike":["3-Methylpentan-3-ol"]},{"text":"Grignard step: the ethyl group attacks the C=O carbon; acid then protonates the O⁻. The product is an alcohol, not a ketone, and nothing oxidizes it.","hl":"ethylmagnesium bromide","calc":["(CH₃)₂CH–CHO + CH₃CH₂MgBr → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(O⁻)–CH₂CH₃","H₃O⁺ → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃"],"strike":["2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methylpentan-3-ol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃, a secondary alcohol."}],"trap":"Opening the epoxide at the less hindered CH₂ as in a basic opening, or shifting a methyl instead of the hydride the passage names: either leads to the wrong carbonyl and the wrong alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"BF₃","note":"Lewis acid"},{"t":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","note":"C2 has two CH₃"},{"t":"aldehyde C–H stretch","note":"the first step gives an aldehyde"},{"t":"aqueous acid was added last","note":"O⁻ → OH"}]},{"text":"**Lewis acid on O, open toward the stable cation.** BF₃ accepts a lone pair from the epoxide O, and the C–O bond that breaks leaves the tertiary cation.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane"},{"text":"**Pick the intermediate from the product.** The shift comes from the C–O carbon: if it keeps an H the carbonyl is an aldehyde, if only carbons remain it is a ketone.","calc":["C2⁺ then H shift from C1 → aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Grignard step.** R⁻ adds to the carbonyl carbon; the acid workup gives OH.","calc":["aldehyde → 2° alcohol","ketone → 3° alcohol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a Lewis acid, an epoxide opens to its most stable carbocation and a 1,2-shift builds the C=O; then a Grignard adds its R group to that carbonyl carbon, and acid workup gives the alcohol."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-04a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"chirality centers","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:4#1","pe6-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:4","sample-cp:2"],"passage":"A fragrance lab made two alcohols and checked every product before scaling up. In Route A, cyclohexanone was reduced with NaBH₄ in methanol and quenched with dilute acid, and the IR spectrum of the distilled six-carbon liquid was recorded. Part of that liquid was then warmed with H₃PO₄, and the volatile material that distilled out was compared with it by IR. In Route B, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the new carbonyl compound showed an aldehyde C–H stretch. That aldehyde was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether, and aqueous acid was added last. Every molecule of the Route B alcohol has the same functional groups and the same connectivity, but the sample may be a mix of R and S mirror images; pure R rotates plane-polarized light by a known angle and pure S by the same angle the other way. Finally, the products were compared with a natural floral alcohol, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, drawn with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z, which occurs as one enantiomer only.","q":"In the natural floral alcohol, which marked carbon is a chirality center?","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"CH₂, CH₃ carbons are never chiral","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter needs four different groups, so skip every CH₂ (two H) and CH₃ (three H). Only the CH branch carbon, X (H, CH₃, propyl, CH₂CH₂OH), is left."},"exp":"To be a **chirality center**, a tetrahedral (sp³) carbon must hold **four different groups**. **X (C3)** carries H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH and CH₂CH₂CH₃. W and Z are CH₂ carbons (two H each) and Y is a CH₃. With exactly one such center and no mirror plane, the molecule is **chiral**.","how":{"ask":"Check each marked carbon for four different attached groups.","know":"• **Chirality center**: sp³ carbon with **four different groups**.\n• Instant rejects: **CH₃, CH₂** (repeated H), and any **C=C, C=O or aromatic** carbon.\n• One chirality center and no internal mirror plane → the **molecule is chiral**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 names the compound, 3-methylhexan-1-ol, and says it exists as one enantiomer, so it must hold a chirality center. List the groups on each marked carbon of the drawn structure.","hl":"occurs as one enantiomer only","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"Reject carbons with repeated hydrogens.","calc":["W: CH₂OH → H, H ✗","Y: CH₃ → H, H, H ✗","Z: CH₂ → H, H ✗"]},{"text":"W, Y and Z each carry at least two hydrogens.","strike":["W","Y","Z"]},{"text":"Check the remaining carbon.","calc":["X: H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH, CH₂CH₂CH₃ → all different ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: X."}],"trap":"Picking the carbon that holds the OH: being next to a heteroatom doesn't help if it still has two H atoms."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","note":"six-carbon chain, CH₃ on C3"},{"t":"one enantiomer only","note":"the molecule is chiral"},{"t":"chirality center","note":"four different groups"}]},{"text":"**The four-groups test.** An sp³ carbon is a chirality center only if all four substituents differ; two hydrogens on one carbon fail at once.","calc":["CH₃, CH₂: never","CH with three different other groups: yes"]},{"text":"**Compare the chains, not just the first atom.** On C3, both CH₂ neighbors start alike, but one leads to OH and the other to a CH₃ end, so they differ.","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with exactly one chirality center has no internal mirror plane, so it is chiral: its mirror image cannot be superimposed and the two are enantiomers."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a tetrahedral carbon holding four different groups can be a chirality center (skip every CH₂, CH₃ and double-bonded carbon); one such center and no mirror plane makes the molecule chiral."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P1"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-05a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"motion graph slopes","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:5#1","pe6-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:8#1","pe3-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:5","pe3-cp:8"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"If the sled's motion were replotted as velocity against time, its displacement from 4 to 8 s would be found as:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Sled position over time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":21,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,0],[1.5,0.13],[2,0.5],[2.5,1.13],[3,2],[3.5,3.13],[4,4.5],[5,7.5],[6,10.5],[7,13.5],[8,16.5],[8.5,17.81],[9,18.75],[9.5,19.31],[10,19.5]]}]},"choices":["the slope of the velocity curve from 4 to 8 s.","the area under the velocity curve from 4 to 8 s.","the velocity at 8 s minus the velocity at 4 s.","the change in velocity from 4 to 8 s divided by 4 s."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Slope of v–t is acceleration","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Velocity differences carry m/s units","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"On a v–t graph, slope = acceleration and area = displacement. Displacement asks for the area."},"exp":"On a velocity–time graph the **slope is the acceleration** and the **area under the curve is the displacement**, since each thin strip is v × Δt. From 4 to 8 s the sled cruises at 3 m/s, so the area is a rectangle: 3 m/s × 4 s = **12 m**, matching the position graph's rise from 4.5 m to 16.5 m.","how":{"ask":"Which feature of a v–t graph adds up v × Δt?","know":"• Slope of an x–t graph = **velocity**; slope of a v–t graph = **acceleration**.\n• Newton’s 2nd law: F_net = ma, so F_net = 0 exactly when v is constant.\n• Straight x–t → constant v; steepening curve → speeding up; flattening curve → slowing.\n• Area under a v–t graph = **displacement**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the sled holds a steady speed in the middle stage; from 4 to 8 s the position graph is straight.","hl":"holds it at a steady cruising speed","calc":["slope = (16.5 − 4.5) m ÷ (8 − 4) s = 3 m/s"]},{"text":"Plot the slopes as a v–t graph.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The same sled: velocity versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[1,0],[4,3],[8,3],[10,0]]}]},"calc":["4–8 s: flat at 3 m/s"]},{"text":"Displacement adds v × Δt for every instant: the area under the curve.","calc":["area = 3 m/s × 4 s = **12 m**","x–t check: 16.5 − 4.5 = 12 m ✓"]},{"text":"The slope of v–t, or a change in v over a time, gives acceleration, not displacement.","strike":["the slope of the velocity curve from 4 to 8 s.","the change in velocity from 4 to 8 s divided by 4 s."]},{"text":"A velocity difference has units of m/s, not m; here it is even zero.","strike":["the velocity at 8 s minus the velocity at 4 s."]},{"text":"Answer: the area under the velocity curve from 4 to 8 s."}],"trap":"Taking the slope by habit: slope of x–t gives velocity, but on a v–t graph the slope gives acceleration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"replotted as velocity against time","note":"a v–t graph"},{"t":"displacement from 4 to 8 s","note":"area, not slope"},{"t":"steady cruising speed","note":"flat on v–t"}]},{"text":"**Slope of x–t = velocity.** Steep = fast, flat = stopped, a straight line = steady speed."},{"text":"**Slope of v–t = acceleration; area under v–t = displacement.** By F_net = ma, a straight x–t stretch (flat v–t) has zero net force.","calc":["speeding up: F along v","slowing: F against v","steady v: F_net = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on x–t, slope = velocity; on v–t, slope = acceleration and area under the curve = displacement."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2","mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:8"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-08a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"gel electrophoresis reading","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:6#1","pe6-cp:6#2","pe6-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:6"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"What is the approximate length of fragment P?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus length","x":{"label":"Fragment length (bp)","min":0,"max":9000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[250,6.4],[500,5.4],[1000,4.4],[2000,3.4],[4000,2.4],[8000,1.4]]}]},"choices":["About 290 bp","About 1200 bp","About 2800 bp","About 6000 bp"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Bracket it: 2.9 cm lies between the 2000 bp (3.4 cm) and 4000 bp (2.4 cm) bands, so the size is between them; halfway on a log scale is about 2800."},"exp":"DNA is negative (one phosphate per nucleotide), so it runs from the − wells toward the + electrode, and **shorter fragments move farther** through the gel mesh. 2.9 cm lies **between the 2000 bp band (3.4 cm) and the 4000 bp band (2.4 cm)**, halfway in distance. Distance tracks log(size), so halfway between 2000 and 4000 is 2000 × √2 ≈ **2800 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 2.9 cm, and where between them does fragment P fall?","know":"• DNA’s phosphate backbone is negative, so it moves toward the + electrode (the anode).\n• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; a ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Distance falls about linearly with log(size): here each doubling costs 1.0 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the clue: fragment P traveled 2.9 cm. Find 2.9 cm on the distance axis of the ladder graph.","hl":"fragment P traveled 2.9 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["2000 bp → 3.4 cm (ran farther)","4000 bp → 2.4 cm (ran less far)","2.9 cm lies between → **2000–4000 bp**"],"strike":["About 1200 bp","About 6000 bp"]},{"text":"Place it: 2.9 is halfway between 3.4 and 2.4, so it is half a doubling above 2000.","calc":["2000 × 2^0.5 ≈ 2000 × 1.41 ≈ **2800 bp**"]},{"text":"290 bp reuses the distance digits as a size; a fragment that short would run past the 250 bp band.","strike":["About 290 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 2800 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negative electrode","note":"wells at −; DNA heads to +"},{"t":"phosphate backbone carries negative charge","note":"why DNA moves in a field"},{"t":"fragment P traveled 2.9 cm","note":"read across the ladder curve"}]},{"text":"**Why DNA moves.** One negative phosphate per nucleotide gives DNA a net − charge, so the field pulls it toward the + electrode, the anode."},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, long ones are held back. Bracket the unknown between two ladder bands, then place it on the curve."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** DNA is negative and runs toward +; shorter DNA migrates farther, so size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2","mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-06b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-08a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:8#2","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:8"],"passage":"In a forensic DNA lab, a restriction digest and a size ladder were loaded into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel, and the field pulled the DNA through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. Digest fragment P traveled 2.9 cm and fragment Q traveled 4.9 cm, and the ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their lengths. The running buffer is normally slightly basic, but one bottle that had stood open for weeks absorbed CO₂ and read pH 6.4 at room temperature, where pure water is neutral. After each run, a camera sled on a straight, level track photographs the gel. A motor starts the sled from rest, holds it at a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a halt over the gel, while a sensor logs its position. The gel is then fixed in an acidic bath buffered at pH 3.4, made by mixing a weak acid HA with its sodium salt NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, 30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration.","q":"What ratio of A⁻ to HA does the fixing bath need?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30 mL of weak acid HA with NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"HA + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.26],[3,2.75],[6,3.1],[9,3.33],[12,3.52],[15,3.7],[18,3.88],[21,4.07],[24,4.3],[27,4.65],[29,5.16],[30,8.29],[31,11.39],[33,11.85],[36,12.13],[40,12.33]]}]},"choices":["2:1","3:10","1:1","1:2"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Below pKa, acid form dominates","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Half-equivalence (15 mL) pH = pKa ≈ 3.7. Target is 0.3 lower, and 0.3 log units = factor 2, so base/acid = ½ = 0.50."},"exp":"Equivalence is at **30 mL** (the steep jump), so half-equivalence is **15 mL**, where pH = **pKa ≈ 3.7**. Henderson–Hasselbalch: 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3 is the log of the base:acid ratio, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50**, a ratio of 1:2.","how":{"ask":"Read the pKa at half-equivalence, then raise 10 to (pH − pKa).","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])**, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa)**.\n• At the **half-equivalence volume**, [A⁻] = [HA] and pH = pKa.\n• Equivalence = the midpoint of the steep jump.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the titration: 30 mL of HA with NaOH of equal concentration, so equivalence comes at 30 mL. Find the steep jump, then halve that volume.","hl":"30 mL of HA was titrated with NaOH of equal concentration","calc":["equivalence ≈ 30 mL → half-equivalence = 15 mL"]},{"text":"Read the pH there: that is the pKa.","calc":["pH at 15 mL ≈ 3.7 = pKa"]},{"text":"Apply the ratio form of Henderson–Hasselbalch.","hl":"pH 3.4","calc":["pH − pKa = 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3","ratio = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50 = **1:2**"]},{"text":"2:1 inverts the ratio, 1:1 is the buffer at its pKa, and 3:10 uses the exponent as the ratio.","strike":["2:1","1:1","3:10"]},{"text":"Answer: 1:2."}],"trap":"Reading the pKa at the equivalence point (pH ≈ 8.3) instead of at half the equivalence volume."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"30 mL","note":"acid volume; equal molarity → equivalence at 30 mL"},{"t":"pH 3.4","note":"target buffer pH"},{"t":"ratio of A⁻ to HA","note":"base over acid"}]},{"text":"**pKa from the curve.** Halfway to equivalence, half the acid has become A⁻, so the log term is zero and pH = pKa.","calc":["V(½ eq) = 15 mL → pKa ≈ 3.7"]},{"text":"**Ratio form.** Solve Henderson–Hasselbalch for the ratio.","calc":["[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa) = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50"]},{"text":"**Also:** a pH exactly 1 unit below the pKa gives a base:acid ratio of 0.1, and a pH equal to the pKa gives 1; each unit is a factor of 10."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** base:acid = 10^(pH − pKa), with the pKa read at the half-equivalence volume."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P2"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS sign reasoning","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:9#1","pe6-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:47#3","pe1-cp:47#1","pe1-cp:47#2","pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:9","pe1-cp:47"],"q":"Supercooled water at −10 °C freezes spontaneously once seeded, and its surroundings warm. Which statement about this freezing is correct?","choices":["ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG < 0","ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0, ΔG < 0","ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG < 0","ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG > 0"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[2,3]},{"f":"Freezing lowers entropy","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read signs from the stem: spontaneous → ΔG < 0; surroundings warm → heat released, ΔH < 0; liquid → solid is more ordered, ΔS < 0. All three negative."},"exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, the only requirement. The surroundings warm, so heat leaves the water: **ΔH < 0**. Freezing orders the molecules: **ΔS < 0**. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays negative because |ΔH| > T|ΔS| at this low temperature: an enthalpy-driven process.","how":{"ask":"Read each sign from an observation: spontaneity, heat flow and order.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0, the only sign that is required.\n• Surroundings warm → the system releases heat → ΔH < 0; surroundings cool → ΔH > 0.\n• More order (gas → liquid → solid, fewer particles) → ΔS < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Spontaneous: ΔG is negative.","hl":"freezes spontaneously","strike":["ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG > 0"]},{"text":"The surroundings warm, so heat flows out of the water: exothermic.","hl":"its surroundings warm","strike":["ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"Liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative.","strike":["ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0, ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"Check: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is negative only if the enthalpy term wins.","calc":["ΔH < 0, and −TΔS > 0 (it opposes)","ΔG < 0 → |ΔH| > T|ΔS| (enthalpy-driven)"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0, ΔG < 0."}],"trap":"Believing entropy must rise for anything spontaneous; only the total entropy (system + surroundings) must rise, and the released heat raises the surroundings' entropy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"freezes spontaneously","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"its surroundings warm","note":"heat released: ΔH < 0"},{"t":"Supercooled water","note":"liquid → solid: ΔS < 0"}]},{"text":"**Only ΔG is required.** Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; nothing forces ΔH or ΔS to a particular sign."},{"text":"**Also: ΔH and ΔS can each have either sign**, as long as ΔG = ΔH − TΔS comes out negative:","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS + → spontaneous at every T","ΔH −, ΔS − → spontaneous at low T (enthalpy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS + → spontaneous at high T (entropy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS − → never spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** spontaneity needs only ΔG < 0; read ΔH from heat flow and ΔS from order, then check that ΔH − TΔS is negative."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:47","pe4-cp:1"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-47c"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-10a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylatable residues (Ser/Thr/Tyr)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:24#3","pe1-cp:24#1","pe1-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:10","pe1-cp:24"],"q":"A protein kinase is given ATP whose terminal phosphate carries ³²P, plus four peptides that differ only in the side chain shown. Which peptide becomes radioactive?","choices":["Tyrosine","Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"choiceFigs":["tyrosine-sc","phenylalanine-sc","leucine-sc","cysteine-sc"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Kinases phosphorylate –OH, not –SH","c":[3]},{"f":"Phe and Leu have no –OH","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphorylation = a phosphate ester on a side-chain OH. Scan the four drawings for –OH; only the phenol ring carries one."},"exp":"A kinase moves the terminal (γ) phosphate of ATP onto a **side-chain –OH**, forming a phosphate ester; here the ³²P label rides along. Of the four side chains, only **tyrosine** has an –OH (on its phenol ring). Phenylalanine has the same ring without the OH, leucine is all carbon and hydrogen, and cysteine has an –SH, which these kinases do not phosphorylate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawn side chain has an –OH for the kinase to put the labeled phosphate on?","know":"• Protein kinases transfer ATP’s γ-phosphate to an –OH of **Ser, Thr or Tyr**.\n• Phe = Tyr without the OH; Val, Leu, Ile, Ala, Met have no OH.\n• The radioactive label goes wherever the γ-phosphate goes.","steps":[{"text":"The label sits on the phosphate the kinase transfers, so the peptide that gets phosphorylated becomes radioactive.","hl":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P"},{"text":"Check each side chain for an –OH.","calc":["phenol ring with OH → yes","ring with no OH → no","isobutyl, all C–H → no","CH₂–SH → no"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine and leucine have no oxygen at all, and cysteine’s sulfur is not a kinase target.","strike":["Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"fig":"phenylalanine-sc"},{"text":"Answer: tyrosine.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"}],"trap":"Picking phenylalanine because it looks like tyrosine: without the –OH there is no oxygen for the phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"protein kinase","note":"adds phosphate to –OH"},{"t":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P","note":"label follows the phosphate"},{"t":"differ only in the side chain","note":"compare side chains only"}]},{"text":"**What a kinase needs.** An –OH oxygen attacks the γ-phosphorus of ATP; the product is a phosphate ester (phospho-Ser, -Thr or -Tyr)."},{"text":"**Spot the OH.**","calc":["Ser: CH₂–OH","Thr: CH(OH)–CH₃","Tyr: phenol OH","Phe, Leu, Val, Ala, Met: none"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only side chains with an –OH (Ser, Thr, Tyr) can be phosphorylated by a protein kinase; Phe is Tyr without the OH and cannot."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:24"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-24c"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-11a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"proportionality test from data","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:11#1","pe6-cp:11#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:11"],"q":"The resistance R of a copper wire was measured for several lengths L, as plotted. Which relationship between R and L do the data support?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Resistance of a copper wire versus its length","x":{"label":"Length L (m)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Resistance R (Ω)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Wire","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.6,1.5],[1.2,3],[1.8,4.5],[2.4,6]]}]},"choices":["R ∝ L","R ∝ L²","R ∝ √L","R ∝ 1/L"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Square and root laws plot curved","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Inverse laws decrease with L","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Double L, R doubles (1.5 → 3.0); ×4 L gives ×4 R. Same factor in and out means direct proportion: R ∝ L."},"exp":"Doubling L from 0.6 to 1.2 m doubles R from 1.5 to 3.0 Ω, and quadrupling L (to 2.4 m) quadruples R (to 6.0 Ω). The ratio R/L stays **2.5 Ω/m**, and the points fall on a straight line through the origin: **R ∝ L**.","how":{"ask":"Divide each R by its L: is that ratio constant, or is R/L² constant instead?","know":"• y ∝ x: y/x is constant and the graph is a straight line through the origin; doubling x doubles y.\n• y ∝ x²: y/x² is constant; doubling x multiplies y by 4.\n• y ∝ √x: doubling x multiplies y by about 1.4. y ∝ 1/x: y × x is constant, and y falls as x grows.","steps":[{"text":"Test the ratios instead of eyeballing the line.","hl":"Which relationship between R and L","calc":["R/L: 1.5 ÷ 0.6 = **2.5**; 3.0 ÷ 1.2 = **2.5**","4.5 ÷ 1.8 = **2.5**; 6.0 ÷ 2.4 = **2.5** Ω/m"]},{"text":"R/L² changes from point to point, so the square law fails.","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.6² = 1.5 ÷ 0.36 ≈ 4.2","6.0 ÷ 2.4² = 6.0 ÷ 5.76 ≈ 1.0"],"strike":["R ∝ L²"]},{"text":"Doubling test: L × 2 (0.6 → 1.2 m) gives R × 2 (1.5 → 3.0 Ω), which is linear. A square-root law would give only × 1.4, and an inverse law would halve R.","calc":["√L: 1.5 × 1.41 ≈ 2.1 Ω (measured 3.0)","1/L: 1.5 ÷ 2 = 0.75 Ω (measured 3.0)"],"strike":["R ∝ √L","R ∝ 1/L"]},{"text":"Answer: R ∝ L."}],"trap":"Calling any rising graph \"proportional\": a square law also rises, so test a ratio. Here only R/L stays fixed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"resistance R","note":"y axis: ohms"},{"t":"several lengths L","note":"x axis: meters"},{"t":"relationship between R and L","note":"which power law?"}]},{"text":"**Two ratio tests.** Divide y by x and by x²; whichever ratio stays fixed names the law.","calc":["y/x fixed → y ∝ x","y/x² fixed → y ∝ x²"]},{"text":"**Doubling shortcut.** Double x and watch y: × 2 → linear, × 4 → square, × 1.4 → square root, × ½ → inverse."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** test y/x and y/x² across the data (or double x): a constant y/x, or y doubling with x, means y ∝ x; a constant y/x², or y × 4, means y ∝ x²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-12a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cyclohexane chair equatorial preference","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:12#1","pe6-cp:12#2","pe6-cp:12#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:12"],"q":"A chemist draws trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane, shown, in its lower-energy chair. Which substituent occupies an axial position in that chair?","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx","choices":["Only the methyl","Only the tert-butyl","Both substituents","Neither substituent"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"tert-Butyl prefers equatorial","c":[1]},{"f":"Trans-1,3 means one axial, one equatorial","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Trans-1,3 on a chair = one up-axial, one equatorial. tert-Butyl always takes equatorial (it is huge), so the methyl is the axial one."},"exp":"In a trans-1,3 ring the groups sit on opposite faces, and a chair then gives C1 and C3 **one axial and one equatorial** group in either conformation. The ring flips so the bulky **tert-butyl is equatorial**, which leaves the **methyl axial**.","how":{"ask":"Can this substitution pattern put both groups equatorial? If not, which group takes the axial spot?","know":"• Around a chair the axial bonds alternate up, down, up, so C1 and C3 point their axial bonds to the same face.\n• **Diequatorial is possible only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4**; cis-1,2, cis-1,4 and trans-1,3 always keep one group axial.\n• When one group must be axial, the favored chair gives that spot to the **smaller** group (tert-butyl ≫ isopropyl > ethyl ≈ methyl).","steps":[{"text":"Read the pattern: trans means opposite faces, and the groups sit on C1 and C3.","hl":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"At C1 and C3 same-face bonds are the same type, so opposite faces force one axial and one equatorial group in both chairs. Both axial and both equatorial are impossible.","calc":["trans-1,3 → one axial + one equatorial","both axial ✗; both equatorial ✗"],"strike":["Both substituents","Neither substituent"]},{"text":"Compare the two chairs by the cost of the axial group: tert-butyl axial is far worse than methyl axial.","calc":["CH₃ axial: ≈ 7 kJ/mol of strain","tert-butyl axial: > 20 kJ/mol","favored chair: tert-butyl equatorial"],"strike":["Only the tert-butyl"]},{"text":"Answer: only the methyl is axial."}],"trap":"Assuming the favored chair always has every group equatorial; that is true only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4 patterns."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","note":"opposite faces, C1 and C3"},{"t":"lower-energy chair","note":"best placement of groups"},{"t":"axial position","note":"straight up or down"}]},{"text":"**Big groups go equatorial.** An axial group bumps the two axial H atoms on its own face; the bigger the group, the bigger the penalty, so tert-butyl is effectively locked equatorial.","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"**Check the pattern.** Axial bonds alternate up and down around the ring, so C1 and C3 share a direction.","calc":["same face at C1, C3 → same type (cis-1,3)","opposite faces at C1, C3 → one of each (trans-1,3)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the patterns that can be diequatorial are cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4; for those, the favored chair puts both groups equatorial."},{"text":"**Also:** in every other pattern (cis-1,2, cis-1,4, trans-1,3) one group must be axial, and the favored chair hands that spot to the smaller group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** place the biggest group equatorial first, then ask whether the pattern lets the second group be equatorial too; if not, the smaller group is axial."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-13a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"hybridization from electron domains","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:28#1","pe1-cp:33#1","pe1-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:13","pe1-cp:28","pe1-cp:33"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Which labeled atom of pNPP has trigonal planar geometry?","fig":"pnpp-sites","choices":["I","II","III","IV"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Four-bonded P is tetrahedral","c":[1]},{"f":"Two bonds, two lone pairs: bent","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ring or double-bond carbon ⇒ sp² ⇒ planar, ~120°. P with four O and an O with two lone pairs are both four-domain atoms."},"exp":"Trigonal planar means **three electron domains: sp², about 120°**. Atom IV is a **benzene-ring carbon**, bonded to three atoms with no lone pair: sp² and planar. Atom II, phosphorus, has four bonded O (tetrahedral, sp³); atom III, the bridging O, has two bonds and two lone pairs (bent, sp³); atom I is a terminal O⁻ with only one bond. The same three-domain count makes the **C=N carbon of a Schiff base** sp², about 120°.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has exactly three electron domains and no lone pair?","know":"• Domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double bond counts once).\n• 4 domains → sp³, ~109.5° (bent or pyramidal with lone pairs); 3 → sp², 120°; 2 → sp, 180°.\n• Aromatic ring carbons and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon are sp² and planar; a **Schiff base** is an imine, C=N.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the beads hold the enzyme through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links. The C=N carbon has three electron domains: sp², trigonal planar, about 120°. Look for a labeled atom with the same three-domain count.","hl":"imine (C=N)"},{"text":"Count domains on each labeled atom.","fig":"pnpp-sites","calc":["I: terminal O⁻, one bond → no angle","II: P with four O → 4 → tetrahedral","III: O with two bonds + two lone pairs → 4 → bent","IV: ring carbon, three bonds → 3 → **sp²**"]},{"text":"Atoms I, II and III are not three-domain centers.","strike":["I","II","III"]},{"text":"Answer: atom IV, the aromatic ring carbon."}],"trap":"Calling the bridging O planar because it has only two atoms attached: its two lone pairs make four domains, so it is bent."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Schiff bases","note":"imines, C=N"},{"t":"trigonal planar","note":"3 domains, sp², 120°"},{"t":"four atoms labeled I to IV","note":"count each one"}]},{"text":"**Domains decide the shape.** Count each bonded atom once (double bonds too) and add lone pairs.","calc":["3 domains → sp², 120°","4 domains → sp³, 109.5°"]},{"text":"**Double and aromatic bonds mark sp² carbons.** Every ring carbon of benzene and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon is planar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbon in a double or aromatic bond (C=O, C=N, C=C, benzene) has three electron domains, so it is sp² and trigonal planar (~120°); four domains, counting lone pairs, means sp³."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3","mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:28","pe1-cp:33"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-28a","cpt-pe1-33a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-14a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"Vmax and KM from rate curves","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:14#1","pe6-cp:14#2","pe6-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:14"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Compared with the no-drug curve, how do the plateau and KM of the curve with drug D compare?","choices":["Same plateau; higher KM","Same plateau; lower KM","Lower plateau; same KM","Lower plateau; higher KM"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"r":2,"c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Plateau = Vmax, half-plateau [S] = KM. Vmax same → curves level off together; KM tripled → the drug curve rises more slowly at low [S]."},"exp":"On a Michaelis–Menten plot the **plateau height is Vmax** and the **[S] at half the plateau is KM**. With drug D, Vmax stays 45 μmol/min, so both curves **level off at the same height**; KM triples from 2.0 to 6.0 mM, so the drug curve reaches half-height (22.5 μmol/min) only at **a higher [S]**. That pattern, same Vmax with larger KM, is what a competitive inhibitor gives.","how":{"ask":"Which table entry changed, and which feature of the curve does that entry control?","know":"• **Vmax** sets the height of the plateau.\n• **KM** is the [S] at which v = Vmax/2; a larger KM shifts the half-plateau point right.\n• Same KM → the half-max points coincide even if the plateaus differ; same Vmax → same plateau even if KM differs.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the table: with D, Vmax is still 45 μmol/min but KM rose from 2.0 to 6.0 mM. Vmax is the same in both rows, so both curves level off at the same height.","hl":"with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","strike":["Lower plateau; same KM","Lower plateau; higher KM"]},{"text":"KM rises with the drug, so half of that plateau is reached at a larger [S].","calc":["without D: half-height at 2.0 mM","with D: half-height at 6.0 mM"],"strike":["Same plateau; lower KM"]},{"text":"Also: if instead Vmax changed and KM stayed fixed, the plateaus would differ while the half-plateau points lined up at one [S]."},{"text":"Answer: same plateau, higher KM."}],"trap":"Thinking any inhibitor lowers the plateau: only a change in Vmax moves the plateau; a KM change moves the half-plateau point."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM","note":"no drug"},{"t":"with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","note":"only KM changed"},{"t":"initial rates over many pNPP concentrations","note":"rate versus [S] curve"}]},{"text":"**Two features, two constants.**","calc":["plateau height = Vmax","[S] at half-plateau = KM"]},{"text":"**Read the table row by row.** Same Vmax → same plateau. Bigger KM → the curve climbs more slowly and reaches half-height further right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Vmax sets how high a rate-versus-[S] curve levels off, and KM is the [S] where it reaches half that height; a table change in one constant moves only its own feature."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-15a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"Lineweaver–Burk inhibitor type","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:15","sample-cp:1"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"The type of inhibition that compound Q shows is:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Double-reciprocal plot with and without compound Q","x":{"label":"1/[S] (mM⁻¹)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"1/v (min/μmol)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"No Q","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,4],[2,6],[3,8],[4,10]]},{"name":"With Q","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,8],[2,14],[3,20],[4,26]]}]},"choices":["Competitive","Uncompetitive","Noncompetitive","Mixed"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Uncompetitive, noncompetitive, mixed lower Vmax","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"y-intercept = 1/Vmax. Both lines start at 2 on the y-axis, so Vmax is unchanged and only the slope (KM/Vmax) grew: KM up, Vmax same."},"exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. Both lines meet the y-axis at 2 min/μmol, so **Vmax is unchanged** (0.5 μmol/min). The line with Q is three times steeper, so its x-intercept moves toward zero: **KM is tripled**. KM up with Vmax unchanged is **competitive inhibition**: Q competes with substrate, and enough substrate overcomes it.","how":{"ask":"Do the lines share a y-intercept, an x-intercept, a slope, or none of these?","know":"• y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: KM up, Vmax same → lines meet on the y-axis.\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same → meet on the x-axis.\n• Uncompetitive: both down by the same factor → parallel lines.\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed → lines cross off both axes.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says Q was tested once without Q and once with a fixed amount, and the data are a double-reciprocal plot. On that plot the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so compare the two lines’ intercepts.","hl":"double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form"},{"text":"Read the y-intercepts: both lines start at 2.","calc":["1/Vmax = 2 min/μmol in both → Vmax same"],"strike":["Noncompetitive","Mixed"]},{"text":"Compare slopes: the Q line is steeper, so the lines are not parallel.","calc":["no Q: slope 2","with Q: slope 6 → KM ×3"],"strike":["Uncompetitive"]},{"text":"Same Vmax and higher KM means the inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site."},{"text":"Answer: competitive inhibition."}],"trap":"Reading the steeper line alone as \"less activity, so Vmax fell\": check the y-intercept, which holds steady."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it","note":"compare two lines"},{"t":"double-reciprocal","note":"1/v vs 1/[S]"}]},{"text":"**What each part of the line means.**","calc":["y-intercept = 1/Vmax","x-intercept = −1/KM","slope = KM/Vmax"]},{"text":"**Four patterns.**","calc":["competitive: KM ↑, Vmax same","noncompetitive: Vmax ↓, KM same","uncompetitive: both ↓, parallel","mixed: Vmax ↓, KM changed"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, lines meeting on the y-axis mean competitive, on the x-axis noncompetitive, parallel lines uncompetitive, and crossing elsewhere mixed; the same patterns read straight from a table of KM and Vmax."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-16a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"allosteric regulation conditions","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:16"],"passage":"A clinical lab assays a bacterial phosphatase with p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV; the enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases yellow p-nitrophenolate. For a reusable sensor, the enzyme is fixed to an aldehyde-coated bead through Schiff bases, imine (C=N) links formed with its lysines. Drug D was tested by measuring initial rates over many pNPP concentrations, with these fitted constants: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. Compound Q was tested the same way, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of it, and those results are plotted in double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) form. The phosphatase is also a regulatory enzyme: ATP binds separate regulatory subunits and shifts it toward its low-activity T state. In a last set of runs, the pNPP and ATP levels were varied while all other conditions stayed the same.","q":"Under which conditions would the phosphatase release p-nitrophenolate fastest?","choices":["High [pNPP] and high [ATP]","High [pNPP] and low [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and high [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and low [ATP]"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":2,"c":[0,2]},{"f":"Rate rises with substrate concentration","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"T state = low activity, so whatever favors T is an inhibitor: keep ATP low. pNPP is the substrate: keep it high."},"exp":"Rate rises with **substrate** until saturation, so pNPP should be **high**. **ATP binds the regulatory subunits and favors the low-activity T state**: it is an **allosteric inhibitor**, so it should be **low**. High [pNPP] with low [ATP] gives the fastest release of p-nitrophenolate. (With an allosteric activator instead, you would want the activator high.)","how":{"ask":"Is each varied molecule a substrate, an activator or an inhibitor, and which level of each speeds the reaction?","know":"• More substrate → faster, up to Vmax.\n• Allosteric activator (favors the R state) → want it high; allosteric inhibitor (favors T) → want it low.\n• An allosteric regulator binds a site other than the active site, often on separate regulatory subunits.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says ATP binds the regulatory subunits and shifts the enzyme toward its low-activity T state: ATP is an allosteric inhibitor, so it must be low.","hl":"shifts it toward its low-activity T state","strike":["High [pNPP] and high [ATP]","Low [pNPP] and high [ATP]"]},{"text":"pNPP is the substrate the enzyme cuts, so more of it fills more active sites and the rate rises toward Vmax.","hl":"p-nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP)","strike":["Low [pNPP] and low [ATP]"]},{"text":"Also: if the regulator were an activator that favors the R state, the best choice would be high activator plus high substrate."},{"text":"Answer: high [pNPP] and low [ATP]."}],"trap":"Choosing \"high\" for everything: a high level of an allosteric inhibitor slows the enzyme, whatever the substrate level."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"low-activity T state","note":"inhibitor, keep low"},{"t":"separate regulatory subunits","note":"allosteric site, not the active site"},{"t":"pNPP and ATP levels were varied","note":"substrate and regulator"},{"t":"fastest","note":"maximize the rate"}]},{"text":"**Sort each molecule by role.**","calc":["substrate → high","activator → high","inhibitor → low"]},{"text":"**T versus R.** Allosteric enzymes flip between a low-activity T state and a high-activity R state; a molecule that stabilizes T slows the enzyme at any substrate level."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the fastest conditions combine high substrate, high allosteric activator and low allosteric inhibitor; identify each molecule’s role from the passage first."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P3"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-17a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"electrolyte vs metallic conduction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:17"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"Which statement correctly describes the charge carriers in the KCl–agar gel?","choices":["Ions migrate through the gel, which is electrolytic conduction","Free electrons drift through the gel, which is metallic conduction","Free electrons drift through the gel, which is electrolytic conduction","Ions migrate through the gel, which is metallic conduction"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Metallic conduction uses electrons, not ions","c":[3]},{"f":"Salt gels carry no free electrons","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Metal = electron sea; salt solution = K⁺ and Cl⁻ that move. The gel is a salt solution, so the carriers are charged particles of the salt."},"exp":"The gel is water saturated with **KCl**, so it holds **K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions** free to move: K⁺ drifts one way and Cl⁻ the other. Charge carried by moving ions is **electrolytic conduction**. In the **copper leads**, by contrast, current is carried by **free electrons** drifting through the metal lattice (metallic conduction).","how":{"ask":"Is the gel a metal or a salt solution, and what moves in each kind of conductor?","know":"• **Metallic conduction**: free (delocalized) electrons drift through a metal lattice; wires, leads.\n• **Electrolytic conduction**: dissolved ions migrate; solutions, gels, salt bridges, cell fluids.\n• A circuit can use both: electrons in the wire, ions in the solution.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the reference electrode connects through an agar gel saturated with KCl, so the gel holds dissolved K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions free to move.","hl":"agar gel saturated with KCl"},{"text":"A watery gel is not a metal, so the metallic labels are out.","strike":["Free electrons drift through the gel, which is metallic conduction","Ions migrate through the gel, which is metallic conduction"]},{"text":"Free electrons do not travel through an electrolyte; only ions move there.","strike":["Free electrons drift through the gel, which is electrolytic conduction"]},{"text":"Also: in the copper leads the carriers are free electrons, which is metallic conduction.","hl":"copper leads"},{"text":"Answer: ions, electrolytic conduction."}],"trap":"Assuming electrons carry current everywhere in a circuit: they do in the wires, but in a salt bridge or gel the ions move."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"copper leads","note":"metal: electrons"},{"t":"saturated with KCl","note":"dissolved ions"},{"t":"charge carriers","note":"name the carrier"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of conductor.**","calc":["metal → free electrons → metallic","electrolyte → ions → electrolytic"]},{"text":"**One circuit, two carriers.** Electrons leave an electrode through the wire; inside the solution or bridge, ions carry the charge the rest of the way around."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a metal the charge carriers are free electrons (metallic conduction); in a solution, gel or salt bridge they are ions (electrolytic conduction)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-18a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"heat q = mcΔT vs q = mL","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:18#1","pe6-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:18","sample-cp:58"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"How much more heat is needed to melt the lead block completely?","choices":["5.5 × 10³ J","2.3 × 10⁴ J","9.6 × 10⁴ J","5.5 × 10⁶ J"],"ans":0,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Already at the melting point: ΔT = 0, so the c value is a decoy. q = 0.24 × 2.3 × 10⁴; 0.24 × 2.3 ≈ 0.55, so about 0.55 × 10⁴ J."},"exp":"The lead is already at its melting point, so there is **no temperature change** and the specific heat is not needed. **q = mL** = 0.240 kg × 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg ≈ **5.5 × 10³ J**.","how":{"ask":"Which heat formula applies when a solid changes phase at constant temperature?","know":"• **Phase change:** q = m·L (no ΔT); L_fusion for melting, L_vap for boiling.\n• **Temperature change:** q = m·c·ΔT, only when T changes.\n• Match units: L in J/kg needs m in kg (g ÷ 1000).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the block reached its melting point but was still entirely solid: no ΔT is left, so q = mL only, and the specific heat is a distractor.","hl":"reached its melting point but was still entirely solid"},{"text":"Convert the mass to kilograms, since L is per kilogram.","hl":"240 g","strike":["5.5 × 10⁶ J"],"calc":["240 g ÷ 1000 = 0.240 kg"]},{"text":"Multiply mass by the heat of fusion; neither copying L nor dividing by the mass gives heat.","strike":["2.3 × 10⁴ J","9.6 × 10⁴ J"],"calc":["q = 0.240 kg × 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg","q ≈ 0.55 × 10⁴ J = 5.5 × 10³ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.5 × 10³ J."}],"trap":"Adding an m·c·ΔT term; the solid is already at its melting point, so there is no warming step and c goes unused."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"reached its melting point but was still entirely solid","note":"no ΔT"},{"t":"240 g","note":"→ 0.240 kg"},{"t":"2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg","note":"L_fusion"},{"t":"128 J/kg·K","note":"distractor"}]},{"text":"**Pick the formula.** Melting at a fixed temperature uses q = mL; m·c·ΔT is for warming or cooling."},{"text":"**Also: units first.** L is per kilogram, so grams must become kilograms before multiplying.","calc":["240 g = 0.240 kg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heat for a phase change at constant T is q = m·L with m in kg; use m·c·ΔT only when the temperature moves."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-19a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"molar mass bookkeeping","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:19#1","pe6-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:19"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"Ignoring the carbon-14 tag, what is the sweetener’s approximate molar mass?","choices":["163 g/mol","197.5 g/mol","198.5 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Swap, don't rebuild: 180 − OH (17) + Cl (35.5) = 180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Start from glucose, **180 g/mol**. Take off one **OH (16 + 1 = 17)** and put on one **Cl (35.5)**: 180 − 17 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol** (formula C₆H₁₁ClO₅: 72 + 11 + 35.5 + 80 = 198.5).","how":{"ask":"Adjust the parent sugar's molar mass for the group that leaves and the atom that enters.","know":"• A substitution changes the molar mass by **(mass added) − (mass removed)**.\n• OH = 16 + 1 = **17 g/mol** (not 18: that is a whole water).\n• Cl ≈ **35.5 g/mol**.\n• Net change for OH → Cl: **+18.5 g/mol**.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the parent sugar and the swap: glucose, 180 g/mol, with one hydroxyl replaced by chlorine. Start from the parent molecule’s molar mass.","hl":"C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol","calc":["parent = 180 g/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract the hydroxyl group that leaves.","hl":"one hydroxyl group replaced","calc":["OH = 16 + 1 = 17","180 − 17 = 163 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the chlorine atom that takes its place.","hl":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","calc":["163 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol**"]},{"text":"163 stops after the subtraction, and 215.5 adds the chlorine without removing the OH.","strike":["163 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"197.5 subtracts 18, as if a whole water left, but only the OH (17) is replaced.","strike":["197.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 198.5 g/mol."}],"trap":"Subtracting 18 for the leaving group (197.5 g/mol): in a direct swap only OH (17) leaves, not H₂O."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"parent sugar"},{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"leaves: −17"},{"t":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","note":"enters: +35.5"}]},{"text":"**Start from the parent.** The new molecule is glucose with one change, so its molar mass is glucose's plus a correction.","calc":["M(new) = M(parent) − M(removed) + M(added)"]},{"text":"**Weigh the swapped pieces.** OH is one O (16) and one H (1); Cl is 35.5.","calc":["−17 + 35.5 = +18.5","180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same method works for any swap. Glucose with one OH replaced by ¹⁸F (mass 18) changes by −17 + 18 = +1."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a substituted molecule, molar mass = parent − group removed + group added; OH (17) → Cl (35.5) is a net +18.5."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-20a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleon counting (A = Z + N)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:20#2","pe6-cp:20#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:20"],"passage":"To follow a chlorinated sweetener through the body, a lab made glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol), and tagged a small share of the molecules with carbon-14. Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while ordinary carbon-12, which is stable, does not; both isotopes have six protons. Each decay is registered by a counter whose pulses travel through copper leads to a meter. The meter’s reference electrode connects to the sample cup through an agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The counter sits inside lead shielding that the lab recasts itself. In one batch, a 240 g lead block was heated in a graphite crucible until it reached its melting point but was still entirely solid; for lead, L_fusion = 2.3 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 128 J/kg·K.","q":"Compared with that of carbon-12, the neutron-to-proton ratio of carbon-14 is best described, along with what happens in its nucleus as it decays, as:","choices":["higher, and a neutron is converted into a proton.","higher, and a proton is converted into a neutron.","lower, and a neutron is converted into a proton.","lower, and a proton is converted into a neutron."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Heavier isotopes have more neutrons","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Isotopes share Z; only N differs. Heavier isotope → more neutrons → n:p up. β⁻ fixes a neutron excess by converting one neutron into a proton."},"exp":"Isotopes of carbon all have **6 protons**; carbon-14 has **8 neutrons** and carbon-12 has 6, so carbon-14’s **n : p ratio is higher**. It sheds the excess by **β⁻ decay**: a **neutron becomes a proton** plus an emitted electron (and an antineutrino), giving nitrogen-14 (7 p, 7 n). A proton-poor-in-neutrons isotope does the reverse: carbon-11 (6 p, 5 n) decays by positron emission, turning a proton into a neutron and becoming boron-11.","how":{"ask":"How many neutrons does each isotope have, and which particle change does β⁻ emission cause?","know":"• Isotopes: same Z (protons), different N (neutrons); N = A − Z.\n• **β⁻ decay**: n → p + e⁻; Z up 1, A same; fixes too many neutrons.\n• **β⁺ (positron) decay** or electron capture: p → n; Z down 1, A same; fixes too few neutrons.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says both isotopes have six protons, so they differ only in neutrons. Count neutrons for each.","hl":"six protons","calc":["carbon-12: 12 − 6 = 6 n → n:p = 1.0","carbon-14: 14 − 6 = 8 n → n:p ≈ 1.33"],"strike":["lower, and a neutron is converted into a proton.","lower, and a proton is converted into a neutron."]},{"text":"β⁻ emission ejects an electron from the nucleus: a neutron becomes a proton, so Z rises to 7 (nitrogen).","hl":"beta-minus emission","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + e⁻"],"strike":["higher, and a proton is converted into a neutron."]},{"text":"Also: positron emission is the opposite fix, for neutron-poor nuclei: carbon-11 (5 n) turns a proton into a neutron and becomes boron-11."},{"text":"Answer: higher n:p, and a neutron becomes a proton."}],"trap":"Mixing up β⁻ and β⁺: a negative electron leaving means the nucleus gained positive charge, so a neutron became a proton."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Carbon-14 decays","note":"6 p, 8 n"},{"t":"carbon-12, which is stable","note":"6 p, 6 n"},{"t":"beta-minus emission","note":"n → p"}]},{"text":"**Isotopes differ only in neutrons.** Same Z, so the heavier isotope has the higher n : p ratio."},{"text":"**Decay fixes the ratio.**","calc":["too many n → β⁻: n → p","too few n → β⁺ or capture: p → n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare isotopes by neutron count (N = A − Z); a neutron-rich nucleus decays by β⁻ (n → p, Z up 1), and a neutron-poor one by positron emission or capture (p → n, Z down 1)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P4"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-21a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structures","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:21#1","pe6-cp:21#2","pe6-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:21"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"Which two amino acids form the stabilizing salt bridge?","choices":["Lysine and glutamate","Arginine and glutamate","Lysine and aspartate","Histidine and glutamine"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Glutamine's amide can't salt-bridge","c":[3]},{"f":"Arginine ends in guanidinium, not NH₃⁺","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count CH₂ links: 4 then NH₃⁺ = lysine; 2 then COO⁻ = glutamate (aspartate has 1, arginine ends in guanidinium). Lys + Glu."},"exp":"**Four CH₂ then –NH₃⁺** is lysine (arginine ends in a guanidinium after three CH₂). **Two CH₂ then –COO⁻** is glutamate (aspartate has one CH₂; glutamine ends in –CONH₂). Lys⁺ and Glu⁻ form the salt bridge.","how":{"ask":"Name each side chain by its end group, then count its CH₂ groups.","know":"• **Lys**: –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺. **Arg**: –(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ (guanidinium).\n• **Asp**: –CH₂–COO⁻. **Glu**: –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻.\n• **Asn**: –CH₂–CONH₂. **Gln**: –(CH₂)₂–CONH₂.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the first side chain, –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺: four CH₂ and a plain ammonium end. Arginine would end in a guanidinium; histidine is a ring.","hl":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","calc":["(CH₂)₄ + NH₃⁺ → **lysine**","arginine: (CH₂)₃ + guanidinium","histidine: imidazole ring"],"strike":["Arginine and glutamate","Histidine and glutamine"]},{"text":"Second side chain: two CH₂ and a carboxylate. Aspartate has only one CH₂.","hl":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","calc":["(CH₂)₂ + COO⁻ → **glutamate**","aspartate: one CH₂","glutamine: –CONH₂, no charge"],"strike":["Lysine and aspartate"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and glutamate."}],"trap":"Aspartate and glutamate share the carboxylate end; only the CH₂ count tells them apart."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","note":"side chain 1"},{"t":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","note":"side chain 2"},{"t":"salt bridge","note":"+ meets −"}]},{"text":"**Name the end group first.** A lone –NH₃⁺ is lysine's; a carbon bonded to three N with a + charge is arginine's guanidinium.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc"],"calc":["–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ → Lys","–(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ → Arg"]},{"text":"**Then count the CH₂ groups** to tell close relatives apart.","calc":["–CH₂–COO⁻ → Asp","–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻ → Glu"]},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain amides: –CH₂–C(=O)NH₂ is asparagine, and one more CH₂ makes glutamine. Neither is charged."},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain carboxylates: aspartate (one CH₂) and glutamate (two CH₂) are the negative residues at pH 7."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the end group (ammonium, guanidinium, amide, carboxylate), then count CH₂ groups: Lys 4, Arg 3, Asp and Asn 1, Glu and Gln 2."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-23a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"H-bond donor vs acceptor","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:23"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"The labeled atom of FMN that can accept the arginine’s hydrogen bond but cannot donate one is:","fig":"fmn-sites","choices":["I","II","III","IV"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"N–H atoms can donate H-bonds","c":[0]},{"f":"Phosphate P has no lone pair","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Acceptor-only = lone pair, no H. Skip any N–H or O–H (donors) and any atom whose lone pair is busy; the ring N with a free in-plane pair is left."},"exp":"To **accept** a hydrogen bond an atom must be N, O or F **with an available lone pair**; to **donate** it needs an **H bonded to N, O or F**. Atom II is a ring nitrogen with **no H** and a lone pair pointing out in the ring plane: an acceptor only. Atom I is an N–H (a donor), atom III is a ring N whose lone pair belongs to the π system, and atom IV is a phosphorus with no lone pair. The same logic makes the C=O and carboxylate oxygens of a ligand such as pyruvate acceptors only.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a free lone pair but no attached H?","know":"• **Donor**: H on N, O or F. **Acceptor**: an N, O or F atom bearing a lone pair.\n• C=O oxygens, carboxylate O⁻, ether O and pyridine-like ring N (no H) are acceptors only.\n• An N whose lone pair is delocalized (amide N, pyrrole-like N) is a poor acceptor.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 says the arginine points an N–H toward the flavin: the arginine supplies the H, so the flavin atom must accept. It needs a lone pair, and the question says it has no H.","hl":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring"},{"text":"Check each labeled atom.","fig":"fmn-sites","calc":["I: N–H → donor","II: ring N, no H, free lone pair → acceptor only ✓","III: N bonded to three C, pair in the π system","IV: P, no lone pair"]},{"text":"Atom I can donate, and atoms III and IV have no usable lone pair.","strike":["I","III","IV"]},{"text":"Answer: atom II."}],"trap":"Picking the N–H because it \"looks like\" the binding site: an N–H is a donor, so it fails the \"cannot donate one\" condition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring","note":"arginine donates"},{"t":"accept the arginine’s hydrogen bond","note":"needs a lone pair"},{"t":"cannot donate one","note":"not a donor"}]},{"text":"**Donor vs acceptor.**","calc":["donor: N–H, O–H, F–H","acceptor: N, O or F bearing a lone pair","acceptor only: lone pair, no H"]},{"text":"**Common acceptor-only groups.** Carbonyl O, carboxylate O⁻, ether O, and a ring N with no H (pyridine-like)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom can accept but not donate a hydrogen bond when it is N, O or F with a free lone pair and no H attached, such as a C=O oxygen, a carboxylate oxygen or a pyridine-like ring N."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-24a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"catalytic residue roles (nucleophile/acid/base)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:24#1","pe6-cp:22#1","pe6-cp:22#2","pe6-cp:22#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:24","pe6-cp:22"],"passage":"A heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe binds the cofactor flavin mononucleotide (FMN), drawn with four atoms labeled I to IV, so tightly that only denaturing the protein releases it. In the binding pocket, an arginine side chain points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. The fold is held together partly by a salt bridge between the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻, and mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature. The enzyme is switched on when an aspartate side chain, modeled by a small compound with four sites labeled I to IV, attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, that same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. In the catalytic step, a glutamic acid residue that stays protonated in a nonpolar pocket hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group as a C–O bond breaks. It is then reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"What role does the catalytic glutamic acid most likely play?","choices":["General acid","General base","Proton relay","Nucleophile"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":1,"c":[2,3]},{"r":2,"c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask where the residue’s proton goes. It starts protonated and gives H⁺ to the leaving group, so it is a proton donor, a general acid."},"exp":"The glutamic acid starts **protonated** (a nonpolar pocket raises its pKa) and **donates its H⁺** to the oxygen of the departing group as the C–O bond breaks. A residue that gives a proton to a substrate is a **general acid catalyst**. A **proton shuttle** would first take a proton from one group of the substrate and then pass that same proton to another, as the threonine-like relays in some carboxylases do.","how":{"ask":"Does the residue give a proton, take one, or take one from one group and pass it to another?","know":"• **General acid**: donates H⁺ to the substrate (e.g. to a leaving group).\n• **General base**: removes H⁺ from the substrate.\n• **Proton shuttle**: accepts H⁺ from one group, then gives it to another group.\n• **Nucleophile**: forms a bond to an electrophilic atom.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 says the residue stays protonated and then hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group: it donates H⁺.","hl":"hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group","strike":["General base"]},{"text":"It is reprotonated by solvent water, not by the substrate, so it never moved a proton from one reacting group to another.","hl":"not by the substrate","strike":["Proton relay"]},{"text":"It makes no bond to the substrate carbon.","strike":["Nucleophile"]},{"text":"Answer: General acid."}],"trap":"Calling any residue that gains and loses a proton a \"shuttle\": a shuttle takes the proton from one reacting group and gives it to another."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"stays protonated","note":"starts as HA"},{"t":"hands its proton to the oxygen of the departing group","note":"donates H⁺"},{"t":"reprotonated by water from the solvent","note":"not a relay"}]},{"text":"**Follow the proton.**","calc":["gives H⁺ → general acid","takes H⁺ → general base","takes from A, gives to B → shuttle"]},{"text":"**pKa and environment.** A nonpolar pocket keeps a carboxylic acid protonated at a pH where it would usually be ionized, so it can donate H⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name a catalytic residue’s role by where its proton goes: donates H⁺ → general acid, removes H⁺ → general base, carries H⁺ from one reacting group to another → proton shuttle."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P5","mergedSrcs":["pe6-cp:22"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-22a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-25a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"peptide sequencing from fragments","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:25#1","pe6-cp:25#2","pe6-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:25","sample-cp:4"],"q":"Complete hydrolysis of an unknown tripeptide releases twice as much Phe as Ser. Partial hydrolysis gives the dipeptides Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser. What is the tripeptide's sequence?","choices":["Phe–Phe–Ser","Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe","Phe–Ser–Ser"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Dipeptides list neighbors, N to C","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Chain the dipeptides on their shared Phe: Phe–Phe then Phe–Ser overlap into Phe–Phe–Ser, which also matches 2 Phe : 1 Ser."},"exp":"Counts: **2 Phe + 1 Ser**. Each dipeptide is a pair of neighbors written N → C. Overlap them on the shared Phe: Phe–**Phe** + **Phe**–Ser → **Phe–Phe–Ser**, which releases exactly Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser.","how":{"ask":"Which order of two Phe and one Ser contains both Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser as neighbor pairs?","know":"• Complete hydrolysis breaks every peptide bond: it gives the **composition**.\n• Partial hydrolysis leaves short pieces that **keep their order** (N → C).\n• Overlap the pieces on a shared residue to build the chain, then check the counts.","steps":[{"text":"Composition: twice as much Phe as Ser in three residues means two Phe and one Ser, which rules out two Ser.","hl":"twice as much Phe as Ser","calc":["2 Phe + 1 Ser"],"strike":["Phe–Ser–Ser"]},{"text":"List the neighbor pairs each remaining order would release.","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser → **Phe–Phe**, **Phe–Ser**","Ser–Phe–Phe → Ser–Phe, Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe → Phe–Ser, Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Only one order gives both observed pieces: the others give Ser–Phe or lack Phe–Phe.","hl":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","strike":["Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Answer: Phe–Phe–Ser."}],"trap":"Ser–Phe is not the same piece as Phe–Ser; the order is fixed N → C, so a reversed pair is a different dipeptide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Complete hydrolysis","note":"all bonds cut: counts"},{"t":"twice as much Phe as Ser","note":"2 Phe, 1 Ser"},{"t":"Partial hydrolysis","note":"some bonds cut: neighbors"},{"t":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","note":"two neighbor pairs"}]},{"text":"**Each dipeptide shows two neighbors**, in N → C order.","calc":["Phe–Phe: a Phe right before a Phe","Phe–Ser: a Phe right before Ser"]},{"text":"**Assemble one chain that contains both**: overlap them on the shared Phe.","calc":["Phe–Phe","     Phe–Ser","→ Phe–Phe–Ser"]},{"text":"**Check against the counts.**","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser: 2 Phe, 1 Ser ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** get the composition from complete hydrolysis, overlap the partial pieces on shared residues, and keep only the order that explains every piece and the counts."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-26a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"molar volume 22.4 L at STP","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:26#1","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:26"],"q":"How many liters of O₂ at STP does the complete combustion of 29 g of butane, C₄H₁₀, consume?","choices":["11.2 L","44.8 L","72.8 L","145.6 L"],"ans":2,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"C₄H₁₀ + 6.5 O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O. 29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol; × 6.5 = 3.25 mol O₂; × 22.4 ≈ 73 L."},"exp":"Balance: **2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O**, so each butane needs **6.5 O₂**. Butane is 58 g/mol, so 29 g = **0.50 mol**. O₂ = 0.50 × 6.5 = **3.25 mol**, and at STP 3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**.","how":{"ask":"How many O₂ does one butane need once the equation is balanced, and how many moles of butane are there?","know":"• Balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O: C first, then H, then O (halves allowed, then double).\n• Moles of gas × 22.4 L/mol = volume at STP.\n• Molar mass of C₄H₁₀ = 4(12) + 10(1) = 58 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Balance the combustion.","hl":"butane, C₄H₁₀","calc":["C: 4 CO₂; H: 5 H₂O","O needed: 8 + 5 = 13 O atoms = 6.5 O₂","×2 → 2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O"]},{"text":"Convert the mass of butane to moles.","hl":"29 g","calc":["29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol"]},{"text":"Scale by the O₂ ratio and convert to liters.","calc":["0.50 × 13/2 = 3.25 mol O₂","3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**"]},{"text":"11.2 L skips the 6.5 ratio, 44.8 L is the CO₂, and 145.6 L uses 13 O₂ per butane.","strike":["11.2 L","44.8 L","145.6 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 72.8 L."}],"trap":"Reading the 13 in the doubled equation as O₂ per butane: it is O₂ per 2 butane."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"29 g","note":"0.50 mol butane"},{"t":"O₂ at STP","note":"22.4 L per mol"},{"t":"complete combustion","note":"CO₂ + H₂O"}]},{"text":"**Balance, then ratio.**","calc":["2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O","O₂ : butane = 13 : 2"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters.** At STP, every mole of an ideal gas fills 22.4 L."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for gas used in a combustion, balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ first, convert the fuel to moles, multiply by the O₂ : fuel ratio, then by 22.4 L/mol at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"¹H NMR splitting and integration","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:27#2","pe6-cp:27#1","pe6-cp:27#3","pe6-cp:27#4"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:27"],"q":"Four small oxygen-containing compounds are shown. Which one gives a ¹H NMR signal that is a doublet integrating to 6H?","choices":["2-Methyl-1-propanol","1-Propanol","Ethanol","tert-Butyl methyl ether"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-isobutanol","1-propanol","cpt-ethanol","cpt-pe6-mtbe"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"CH₃ beside CH₂: triplet","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A 6H doublet = an isopropyl pattern: two CH₃ on one CH. Only 2-methyl-1-propanol has (CH₃)₂CH–."},"exp":"**n + 1 rule:** a signal is split into n + 1 peaks by the n H on adjacent carbons. A doublet means **one neighbor H**. In 2-methyl-1-propanol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH, the two equivalent CH₃ groups (6 H) border a single CH, so they appear as a **6H doublet**. 1-Propanol's and ethanol's CH₃ are triplets (next to CH₂), and the ether gives only singlets.","how":{"ask":"Which compound has six equivalent H on carbons that border a carbon carrying exactly one H?","know":"• n + 1 rule: n H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Doublet → 1 neighbor H; triplet → 2; quartet → 3; singlet → 0.\n• A 6H signal = two equivalent CH₃ groups. A (CH₃)₃C– group sits on a carbon with no H, so its 9 H give a singlet.","steps":[{"text":"6 H means two equivalent CH₃ groups; a doublet means exactly one H next door.","hl":"doublet integrating to 6H"},{"text":"2-Methyl-1-propanol is (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH: each CH₃ borders the CH, which carries 1 H.","calc":["n = 1 → 1 + 1 = **2 peaks** (doublet)","2 × CH₃ = **6 H**"]},{"text":"1-Propanol's CH₃ borders a CH₂, and so does ethanol's.","calc":["n = 2 → 2 + 1 = 3 peaks (triplet), only 3 H"],"strike":["1-Propanol","Ethanol"]},{"text":"Also: in the ether the central carbon of (CH₃)₃C– has no H, so its 9 H have n = 0 and give a singlet; the OCH₃ is a singlet as well.","calc":["n = 0 → 0 + 1 = 1 peak (singlet)"],"strike":["tert-Butyl methyl ether"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methyl-1-propanol.","fig":"cpt-pe6-isobutanol"}],"trap":"Counting the H in the group itself instead of its neighbors: a CH₃ is split by the H next door, not by its own three."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doublet","note":"1 neighbor H"},{"t":"6H","note":"two equivalent CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Also: the n + 1 rule.** Count the H on the adjacent carbons (not the O–H) and add 1.","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Also:** a group whose neighbor carbon has no H is never split: the 9 H of a (CH₃)₃C– group show as one singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** splitting = n + 1 from the H on neighboring carbons, so a 6H doublet points to (CH₃)₂CH– and an unsplit 9H signal to (CH₃)₃C–."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-27b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"buoyancy / floating","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:28#1","pe6-cp:28#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:28"],"q":"A block floats in fresh water (1000 kg/m³) with 60% of its volume submerged, and in an oil with 80% submerged. What is the oil’s density?","choices":["600 kg/m³","750 kg/m³","800 kg/m³","1330 kg/m³"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Floating deeper means less dense liquid","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³. In oil, ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = 750 kg/m³: sinking deeper means a thinner liquid."},"exp":"A floating object displaces its own weight, so **fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid**. In water: ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = **600 kg/m³**. In oil: 0.80 = 600 ÷ ρ_oil, so ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**. The block rides lower in oil, which fits a liquid less dense than water.","how":{"ask":"What is the block’s density from the water data, and what oil density makes it 80% submerged?","know":"• Floating: buoyant force = weight, so ρ_liquid × V_sub = ρ_object × V.\n• Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Deeper float → less dense fluid; higher float → denser fluid. Fully submerged and level with the surface → ρ_liquid = ρ_object.","steps":[{"text":"Find the block’s density from the water float.","hl":"60% of its volume submerged","calc":["ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³"],"strike":["600 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Use the oil float to find the oil’s density.","hl":"80% submerged","calc":["ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**"]},{"text":"It floats lower in the oil, so the oil is less dense than water; 1330 flips the ratio and 800 copies the fraction.","strike":["1330 kg/m³","800 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Answer: 750 kg/m³."}],"trap":"Dividing the wrong way: sinking deeper means a thinner fluid, so the second density must come out below the first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1000 kg/m³","note":"reference fluid"},{"t":"60% of its volume submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ 1000"},{"t":"80% submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ ρ_oil"}]},{"text":"**Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid.** The same block has one density, so use the first fluid to get it."},{"text":"**Edges of the range.** Fully submerged at the surface → fluid density equals the object’s; the smallest fraction a scale shows → the densest fluid it can read."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a floating object, fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; find ρ_object from one fluid, then ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction for any other."}],"mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-28b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-29a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"protein charge vs pI (ion exchange)","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:29"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"Which prediction about protein X’s movement is correct?","choices":["At pH 5.5 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 7.5 it does not move","At pH 5.5 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 7.5 it does not move","At pH 5.5 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 7.5 it moves toward the anode","At pH 5.5 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 7.5 it moves toward the cathode"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"No net charge at the pI","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Below pI, net charge is positive","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with pI. Lower pH → extra H⁺ on the protein → positive → toward −. Equal → neutral → no movement."},"exp":"Below its pI a protein holds extra protons, so it carries a **net positive charge** and drifts toward the **negative electrode, the cathode**: at pH 5.5 (two units below the pI of 7.5) protein X heads to the cathode. At **pH 7.5 = pI** its net charge is **zero**, so it stays put. (At pH 9.5, above the pI, it would be net negative and move toward the anode.)","how":{"ask":"Is each buffer’s pH below, equal to or above the pI, and what net charge does that give?","know":"• At pH = pI, net charge = 0 → no migration.\n• pH < pI → net + → toward the cathode (−).\n• pH > pI → net − → toward the anode (+).\n• In electrophoresis the anode is +, the cathode −.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 gives the pI, 7.5. pH 5.5 lies under that value, so protein X carries net positive charge and drifts toward the negative electrode.","hl":"isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5","calc":["5.5 < 7.5 → net + → cathode"],"strike":["At pH 5.5 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 7.5 it does not move","At pH 5.5 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 7.5 it moves toward the cathode"]},{"text":"pH 7.5 equals the pI: no net charge, no movement.","calc":["7.5 = pI → net 0 → stays"],"strike":["At pH 5.5 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 7.5 it moves toward the anode"]},{"text":"Also: at pH 9.5 the protein is net negative and would move toward the anode."},{"text":"Answer: toward the cathode at pH 5.5, no movement at 7.5."}],"trap":"Mixing up the electrode names: in a gel the cathode is the negative electrode, where positive proteins go."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5","note":"net charge 0 at pH 7.5"},{"t":"pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5","note":"below, at, above pI"},{"t":"positive electrode","note":"the anode"}]},{"text":"**pH vs pI sets the sign.**","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH = pI → 0","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** A positive protein moves toward the cathode (−); a negative one toward the anode (+); a neutral one stays."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a protein is net positive below its pI, neutral at its pI and net negative above it, so it moves toward the cathode, stays put, or moves toward the anode."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-30a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"disulfide bonds (Cys oxidation)","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:30#1","pe6-cp:30#2","pe1-cp:22#1","pe1-cp:22#2","pe1-cp:22#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:30","pe1-cp:22"],"passage":"Protein X, a small basic enzyme with an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5, was labeled with iodine-131 so that it could be tracked in tissue. The iodine-131 nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state, which then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray that a scanner detects. To choose a running buffer, protein X was loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. During weeks of storage the protein slowly formed soluble aggregates, which ran as covalent dimers and trimers on SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent but only as monomers when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, was added. Mass spectrometry found no dityrosine or other radical cross-links. Finally, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence after four treatments: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 5%; plain buffer at room temperature, 3%.","q":"Which side chain forms the cross-links in the stored protein?","choices":["Cysteine","Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"],"choiceFigs":["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","threonine-sc","lysine-sc"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Thr and Lys contain no sulfur","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Methionine's sulfur can't form disulfides","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Undone by a reducing agent ⇒ S–S bond ⇒ needs two thiols. Only one drawn side chain ends in –SH."},"exp":"The links are **covalent** (they survive SDS) but **break with DTT**, a reducing agent that cuts **disulfide (S–S) bonds**. A disulfide forms when two **cysteine thiols (–SH)** are oxidized together, so cysteine forms the cross-links. Methionine’s sulfur has no H to lose, and radical-made links such as dityrosine, which join two tyrosine rings by a C–C bond, would not be reversed by DTT and were not found.","how":{"ask":"What kind of covalent bond does DTT break, and which side chain can make it?","know":"• **Disulfide**: Cys–SH + HS–Cys → Cys–S–S–Cys (oxidation); DTT or β-mercaptoethanol reduces it back.\n• **Dityrosine**: radicals oxidize tyrosine phenol rings, which join by a C–C bond; reducing agents do not undo it.\n• Methionine is a thioether (C–S–C): no S–H.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 says the links survive SDS, so they are covalent, and DTT, which breaks S–S bonds, erases them: they are disulfides.","hl":"which breaks S–S bonds"},{"text":"An S–S bond needs a thiol on each partner: only cysteine has –SH.","fig":"cysteine-sc","calc":["2 Cys–SH → Cys–S–S–Cys + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"Methionine has a thioether sulfur with no H; threonine’s –OH and lysine’s –NH₂ make no S–S bonds.","strike":["Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Also: radical damage joins two tyrosines into dityrosine, a C–C link that DTT cannot break; the passage rules it out.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dityrosine"},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Picking methionine because it contains sulfur: without an S–H it cannot form a disulfide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"covalent dimers and trimers","note":"bonds, not sticking"},{"t":"which breaks S–S bonds","note":"disulfides"},{"t":"no dityrosine","note":"radical link ruled out"}]},{"text":"**Two covalent cross-links.**","calc":["Cys–S–S–Cys: oxidation; DTT reverses","dityrosine (Tyr–Tyr C–C): radicals; not reversible"]},{"text":"**Sulfur alone is not enough.** A disulfide needs an –SH; methionine’s S is already bonded to two carbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent protein cross-link that a reducing agent such as DTT breaks is a disulfide between two cysteine thiols; a radical-made dityrosine link between two tyrosine rings is not reversed."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P6","mergedSrcs":["pe1-cp:22"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe1-22a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"gel electrophoresis reading","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:33#2","pe6-cp:33#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:33"],"passage":"To follow an 40 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"After the first trial, roughly what percent of the kinase stayed unlabeled?","choices":["About 25%","About 75%","About 100%","About 33%"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Unshifted, non-glowing band = unlabeled; shifted, glowing = labeled. Darkness 3 : 1 → unlabeled share 3 of 4 = 75%."},"exp":"Coomassie shows all protein; fluorescence shows dye. The band level with untreated kinase **stains but does not glow**: unlabeled. The shifted band that fluoresces is **labeled** protein. Darkness 3 : 1, so unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) ≈ **75%**.","how":{"ask":"Which stained band is the unlabeled protein, and what share of the total stain does it hold?","know":"• **Protein stain** (Coomassie) shows every protein band; **fluorescence** shows only dye.\n• A lane of untreated kinase marks where unlabeled protein runs.\n• Band darkness is proportional to amount, so a fraction = that band ÷ the sum of all protein bands.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 describes the first-trial gel: one stained band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. Assign each band by the two tests.","hl":"one band level with the untreated kinase","calc":["level with untreated: stain ✓, glow ✗ → **unlabeled**","shifted band: stain ✓, glow ✓ → **labeled**"]},{"text":"The labeled band both stains and glows, so some of the protein carries dye.","hl":"shifted band that fluoresces","strike":["About 100%"]},{"text":"Turn darkness into a share of the total. 25% is the labeled share, and 33% is the 1 : 3 ratio, not a share of all the protein.","hl":"one-third as dark","calc":["unlabeled : labeled = 3 : 1","unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) = **0.75 = 75%**","labeled = 1 ÷ 4 = 25%"],"strike":["About 25%","About 33%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 75% stayed unlabeled."}],"trap":"Reading 1 : 3 as one-third; a fraction divides by the total of both bands (4 parts), not by the other band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Coomassie-stained","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"level with the untreated kinase","note":"where unlabeled runs"},{"t":"shifted band that fluoresces","note":"carries dye"},{"t":"one-third as dark","note":"1 part vs 3 parts"}]},{"text":"**Compare the lanes.** The untreated-protein lane is the ruler: a sample band at the same height is protein with no dye on it."},{"text":"**Two tests per band.**","calc":["stain ✓ glow ✓ → labeled protein","stain ✓ glow ✗ → unlabeled protein","stain ✗ glow ✓ → free dye"]},{"text":"**Turn darkness into a share.**","calc":["unlabeled 3 parts + labeled 1 part = 4 parts","unlabeled = 3/4 = 75%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the unlabeled band by its match to the untreated lane (stains, no glow), then divide its darkness by the total of all protein bands."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7","mergedSrcs":[],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-33b"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"side-chain functional group reactivity","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:34#3","pe6-cp:34#2","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:34","pe3-cp:36"],"passage":"To follow an 40 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"Of the side chains shown, which is the strongest nucleophile toward the dye?","choices":["Lysine","Threonine","Glutamine","Glutamate"],"choiceFigs":["lysine-sc","threonine-sc","glutamine-sc","glutamate-sc"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Amides and carboxylates are poor nucleophiles","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Alcohol –OH is a weak nucleophile","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7 the strong side-chain nucleophiles are amine (Lys) and thiol (Cys). No Cys listed, so Lys; OH, amide and carboxylate are weak."},"exp":"Lysine's ε-amine (pKa ≈ 10.5) is mostly –NH₃⁺ at pH 7, but its small **neutral –NH₂ share** has a free lone pair and reacts fast. Thr's –OH, Gln's amide and Glu's resonance-stabilized carboxylate are **poor nucleophiles**. Cysteine's –SH/–S⁻ is the other strong nucleophile at pH 7.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a lone pair that is free (not delocalized, not tightly held) at pH 7?","know":"• Good nucleophiles at pH 7: **Lys –NH₂** (the unprotonated share) and **Cys –SH/–S⁻**.\n• Poor: alcohol O (Ser, Thr), amide N (Asn, Gln), carboxylate O (Asp, Glu).\n• Amide and carboxylate lone pairs are delocalized by resonance.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 1 says the dye is an electrophile and the kinase has no cysteine: the dye needs an electron-pair donor near neutral pH, and the thiol option is gone.","hl":"an electrophile that acylates","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Rule out the weak donors: Thr holds its O lone pairs tightly, and Gln and Glu spread theirs by resonance.","calc":["Thr: R–OH, weak O nucleophile","Gln: R–C(=O)NH₂, N lone pair in resonance","Glu: R–COO⁻, charge spread over two O"],"strike":["Threonine","Glutamine","Glutamate"]},{"text":"Lys: only a tiny share is neutral –NH₂ at pH 7, but that free amine reacts, and more deprotonates as it is used up.","calc":["share as –NH₂ = 1 ÷ (1 + 10^(10.5 − 7.4))","= 1 ÷ (1 + 1259) ≈ 0.08%"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine."}],"trap":"Picking Glu because it carries a full negative charge; charge alone doesn't make a good nucleophile when resonance spreads it over two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"an electrophile","note":"needs a lone-pair donor"},{"t":"pH 7.4","note":"near neutral: check protonation"},{"t":"strongest nucleophile","note":"most available lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Two strong nucleophiles at pH 7:** lysine's –NH₂ (amine) and cysteine's –SH (thiol, partly S⁻).","fig":["lysine-sc","cysteine-sc"]},{"text":"**Also:** Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7: alcohol oxygens hold their lone pairs tightly, and amides and carboxylates delocalize theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a labeling site, look for an available lone pair: the Lys amine and the Cys thiol react; O–H, amide and carboxylate side chains do not."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7","mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:36"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe6-34b","cpt-pe3-36a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-35a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:35#1","pe6-cp:35#2","pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2","pe3-cp:21#3","pe5-cp:24#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:35","pe3-cp:21","pe5-cp:24"],"passage":"To follow an 40 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"What mass of NaCl goes into the 200 mL of labeling buffer?","choices":["0.10 g","2.3 g","6.0 g","6000 g"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mol = 0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.10; × 58.5 g/mol ≈ 6 g. Check: 3% w/v = 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g in 200 mL."},"exp":"Moles = M × V = 0.51 mol/L × 0.200 L ≈ **0.102 mol**. NaCl’s molar mass is 23.0 + 35.5 = **58.5 g/mol**, so mass ≈ 0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**. The % (w/v) gives the same answer: 3 g per 100 mL × 200 mL = 6 g. (Normal saline, 0.9%, is 0.15 M.)","how":{"ask":"How many moles are in 200 mL at 0.51 M, and what do they weigh?","know":"• **mol = M × V(L)**; **mass = mol × molar mass**.\n• Normal saline = 0.9% (w/v) NaCl ≈ 0.15 M; % (w/v) = grams per 100 mL.\n• NaCl = 23.0 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the recipe: 0.51 M NaCl, 200 mL. Convert the volume and find moles.","hl":"200 mL of that buffer","calc":["200 mL = 0.200 L","0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.102 mol"],"strike":["6000 g"]},{"text":"Build the molar mass from both atoms, then convert to grams.","hl":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","calc":["58.5 g/mol","0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**"],"strike":["0.10 g","2.3 g"]},{"text":"Check with % (w/v): 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g per 200 mL.","hl":"3% (w/v)"},{"text":"Answer: about 6.0 g."}],"trap":"Multiplying molarity by milliliters: M × mL gives millimoles, so convert or the answer is off by 1000."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.51 M","note":"mol per liter"},{"t":"200 mL","note":"0.200 L"},{"t":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","note":"sum = 58.5"}]},{"text":"**Two steps.**","calc":["mol = M × V(L)","g = mol × molar mass"]},{"text":"**Saline anchors.** Normal saline is 0.9% (w/v), about 0.15 M NaCl, and 9 g per liter; % (w/v) means grams per 100 mL."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity × volume in liters × molar mass; for normal saline, 0.15 M NaCl is 0.9 g per 100 mL."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7","mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:21","pe5-cp:24"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-21a","cpt-pe5-24a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-36a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:36#1","pe6-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:36"],"passage":"To follow an 40 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"How many moles of kinase went into each labeling trial?","choices":["0.6 nmol","6 μmol","0.6 mmol","0.6 μmol"],"ans":3,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mg ÷ kDa gives μmol (10⁻³ g ÷ 10³ g/mol = 10⁻⁶ mol). 16 × 1.5 = 24 mg; 24 ÷ 40 = 0.6 → 0.6 μmol."},"exp":"**Mass = concentration × volume**: 16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg = 0.024 g. **40 kDa = 40 000 g/mol.** Moles = 0.024 g ÷ 40 000 g/mol = 6 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.6 μmol**.","how":{"ask":"Get the mass in grams, convert kDa to g/mol, then divide.","know":"• **mass = (mg/mL) × mL**, then mg ÷ 1000 = g.\n• **1 kDa = 1000 Da**, and Da per molecule = g/mol, so **kDa × 1000 = g/mol**.\n• **mol = g ÷ (g/mol)**; 10⁻⁶ mol = 1 μmol.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 gives the amount used: 1.5 mL at 16 mg/mL. Mass delivered = concentration × volume.","hl":"1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL","calc":["16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg","24 mg ÷ 1000 = 0.024 g"]},{"text":"Convert the size to a molar mass.","hl":"40 kDa","calc":["40 kDa × 1000 = 40 000 g/mol"]},{"text":"Divide grams by g/mol.","calc":["0.024 ÷ 40 000 = 6 × 10⁻⁷ mol","6 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.6 μmol**"]},{"text":"0.6 mmol skips the kDa conversion, 6 μmol uses 4000 g/mol, and 0.6 nmol divides by 1000 one time too many.","strike":["0.6 mmol","6 μmol","0.6 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.6 μmol."}],"trap":"Dividing by 40 instead of 40 000: kDa must become g/mol before you divide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 mL","note":"volume"},{"t":"16 mg/mL","note":"mass per volume"},{"t":"40 kDa","note":"molar mass, ×1000"}]},{"text":"**Mass from concentration.** A mg/mL concentration times a volume in mL gives mg directly.","calc":["16 × 1.5 = 24 mg = 2.4 × 10⁻² g"]},{"text":"**Moles from mass.** Divide by the molar mass in g/mol, after turning kDa into g/mol.","calc":["2.4 × 10⁻² g ÷ 4.0 × 10⁴ g/mol = 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** writing every quantity in powers of ten keeps the zeros honest: 10⁻² ÷ 10⁴ = 10⁻⁶, the micro range, as expected for milligrams of a large protein."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol of protein = (mg/mL × mL ÷ 1000) ÷ (kDa × 1000)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-37a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"experimental evidence logic","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:37#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:37"],"passage":"To follow an 40 kDa kinase inside cells, researchers tagged it with an NHS-ester dye, an electrophile that acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups; a maleimide dye, which alkylates –SH groups, labeled nothing, since the kinase has no cysteine. The four side chains most exposed on its surface are shown. For each trial, 1.5 mL of kinase at 16 mg/mL was stirred with a surplus of dye in a buffer of 0.51 M NaCl, about 3% (w/v), and 200 mL of that buffer was prepared (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). After a first trial at pH 7.4, the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated kinase, imaged under UV and then Coomassie-stained: the stain showed one band level with the untreated kinase and a shifted band that fluoresces, one-third as dark. After a second trial at pH 8.3, UV showed a bright shifted band and a glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band level with the untreated kinase had one-quarter of its intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank. Mass spectrometry placed the dye on a single lysine, and circular dichroism showed that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"On the kinase, where is the labeled lysine most likely located?","choices":["In the substrate cleft","On a distant surface loop","Inside the nonpolar core","Anywhere on the protein"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Charged lysines face water, not core","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Still folded yet fully dead: the tag must block function directly, so it sits in the substrate cleft. A distant loop wouldn't matter; the core is unreachable."},"exp":"A dye in water reaches exposed residues. A tag on an exposed patch **far from the catalytic pocket** leaves activity unchanged. A tag that **abolishes activity while the fold is intact** must sit where substrate binds, physically blocking it.","how":{"ask":"Activity is lost but the fold is intact: which spot must a bulky dye be blocking?","know":"• A water-soluble reagent labels **exposed** residues; buried ones are out of reach while the protein is folded.\n• Exposed sites far from the catalytic pocket: label tolerated, activity unchanged.\n• Label in or at the mouth of the catalytic pocket: substrate blocked, activity lost.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives two clues: the labeled kinase stays folded, yet all activity is gone.","hl":"stays folded but loses all activity"},{"text":"Folded means the core is closed to the dye, and a charged lysine sits at the surface anyway.","hl":"NHS-ester dye","strike":["Inside the nonpolar core"]},{"text":"A tag far from the catalytic pocket would leave activity intact, and most surface sites are like that.","strike":["On a distant surface loop","Anywhere on the protein"]},{"text":"Answer: In the substrate cleft, where the dye blocks substrate binding."}],"trap":"Assuming any label disturbs an enzyme; most surface labels are harmless, so a total loss of activity pinpoints the catalytic pocket."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"single lysine","note":"one exposed Lys tagged"},{"t":"stays folded","note":"core still closed"},{"t":"loses all activity","note":"catalytic pocket hit"}]},{"text":"**Where can a dye in water reach?** Only exposed residues; a folded protein keeps its core shut."},{"text":"**Activity lost + fold intact** → the dye sits where substrate binds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a water-borne label reaches only exposed residues; unchanged activity puts it far from the catalytic pocket, and lost activity with an intact fold puts it in the pocket."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P7"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-38a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"moles from molarity × volume","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:38#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:38","sample-cp:15"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"How many moles of chloride ion does each test cell receive from the stock?","choices":["21 μmol","42 μmol","42 mmol","42 nmol"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × mL = μmol: 3.5 × 6.0 = 21 μmol MgCl₂. Two Cl⁻ per formula unit → twice that."},"exp":"**mM × mL = μmol**: 3.5 mM × 6.0 mL = 21 μmol of MgCl₂. Each **MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻**, so n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 21 = **42 μmol** (4.2 × 10⁻⁵ mol).","how":{"ask":"Multiply concentration by volume with matched prefixes, then count chlorides per formula unit.","know":"• **n = M × V.** Prefix pairs: **M × L = mol**, **M × mL = mmol**, **mM × mL = μmol**.\n• Ions per formula unit come from the subscripts: MgCl₂ → 1 Mg²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the clue: each cell gets 6.0 mL of a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock. Multiply concentration by volume, keeping the prefixes.","hl":"3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock","calc":["3.5 mM × 6.0 mL = 21 μmol MgCl₂","check: 3.5 × 10⁻³ mol/L × 6.0 × 10⁻³ L = 2.1 × 10⁻⁵ mol"]},{"text":"Count chloride ions: the subscript 2 in MgCl₂ means two Cl⁻ per formula unit.","hl":"MgCl₂","calc":["2 × 21 μmol = **42 μmol Cl⁻**"]},{"text":"21 μmol is the salt, not the chloride; 42 mmol and 42 nmol misplace a factor of 1000.","strike":["21 μmol","42 mmol","42 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 42 μmol."}],"trap":"Writing mM × mL as mmol: two milli factors make micro."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 mL","note":"volume, 10⁻³ L"},{"t":"3.5 mM","note":"10⁻³ mol/L"},{"t":"chloride ion","note":"two per MgCl₂"}]},{"text":"**Milli × milli = micro.** 10⁻³ × 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶, so the product of mM and mL comes out in μmol.","calc":["3.5 × 6.0 = 21 → 21 μmol"]},{"text":"**Ions per unit.** Dissolving one MgCl₂ gives one Mg²⁺ and two Cl⁻.","calc":["n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 21 μmol = 42 μmol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = M × V with matched prefixes (mM × mL = μmol, M × mL = mmol), then multiply by the ions per formula unit."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-39a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mass percent composition","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:39#1","pe6-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:39"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"Approximately how much iron does one electrode’s coating contain?","choices":["1.7 mg","5.2 mg","7.2 mg","8.7 mg"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Magnetite = 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 mg. Fe fraction ≈ 168/232 ≈ 0.7, so iron ≈ 0.7 × 7.2 ≈ 5 mg (7.2 would be all of the oxide)."},"exp":"Magnetite in the coating: 0.60 × 12.0 mg = 7.2 mg. **Fe₃O₄ = 3(55.8) + 4(16.0) ≈ 231 g/mol**, and iron’s mass fraction is **167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72**. Iron = 0.72 × 7.2 mg ≈ **5.2 mg**.","how":{"ask":"Find the oxide's mass, then the iron fraction of the oxide, and multiply.","know":"• **Mass fraction of an element = (atoms of it × atomic mass) ÷ formula mass.**\n• Mass of the element = fraction × mass of the compound.\n• In a mixture, first find the compound's own mass: % × total.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 gives the clue: 12.0 mg of coating, 60% of it magnetite. First find the oxide’s own mass in the mixture.","hl":"12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite","calc":["0.60 × 12.0 mg = 7.2 mg Fe₃O₄"]},{"text":"Formula mass and iron’s share, from the atomic masses given.","hl":"Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol","calc":["3 × 55.8 ≈ 167; 4 × 16.0 = 64","167 + 64 ≈ 231 g/mol","167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"Multiply the fraction by the oxide’s mass.","calc":["0.72 × 7.2 mg ≈ **5.2 mg Fe**"]},{"text":"7.2 mg stops at the oxide, 8.7 mg skips the 60%, and 1.7 mg counts only one iron atom.","strike":["7.2 mg","8.7 mg","1.7 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.2 mg."}],"trap":"Counting one Fe atom per formula unit: the subscript 3 multiplies the iron's mass."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"12.0 mg","note":"whole coating"},{"t":"60% magnetite","note":"share that is oxide"},{"t":"Fe₃O₄","note":"three Fe per unit"}]},{"text":"**Peel the layers from outside in.** Coating → oxide (60%) → iron (its mass fraction in the oxide).","calc":["mass Fe = total × %oxide × (n·M_Fe ÷ M_oxide)"]},{"text":"**Mass fraction of the metal.** Count the metal atoms, multiply by the atomic mass, and divide by the formula mass.","calc":["(3 × 55.8) ÷ 231 ≈ 167 ÷ 231 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** element mass = (atoms × atomic mass ÷ formula mass) × compound mass, after taking the compound’s % of any mixture."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"metal ion coordination","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:42"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"Which form of free aspartate, shown, dominates at pH 1.2?","choices":["Cation (net +1)","Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-asp-plus1","cpt-pe6-asp-net0","cpt-pe6-asp-minus1","cpt-pe6-asp-minus2"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Very low pH protonates every carboxyl","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"pH below every pKa → every group keeps its proton. Carboxyls neutral, amine +1 → net +1, the cation."},"exp":"pH 1.2 is **below every pKa**, so every group keeps its H⁺: α-COOH (0), side-chain COOH (0), α-NH₃⁺ (+1) → **net +1**. With its side chain as neutral –COOH instead of –COO⁻, aspartate can no longer hold Ca²⁺, so the ion is released.","how":{"ask":"For each group, is pH 1.2 above or below its pKa? Then add up the charges.","know":"• **pH < pKa → protonated** (COOH neutral, NH₃⁺ +1); pH > pKa → deprotonated (COO⁻ −1, NH₂ neutral).\n• Metal ions are held by negative **–COO⁻**; protonating it to –COOH releases the metal.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 gives the stripping pH, 1.2, and the three pKa values. Compare 1.2 with each pKa.","hl":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","calc":["α-COOH: 1.2 < 1.9 → COOH (0)","side-chain COOH: 1.2 < 3.7 → COOH (0)","α-NH₃⁺: 1.2 < 9.6 → NH₃⁺ (+1)","net = 0 + 0 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"The matching drawing has two COOH groups and NH₃⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"Ionized carboxyls need pH above their pKa values (1.9 and 3.7), and a neutral amine needs pH above 9.6.","strike":["Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"]},{"text":"Answer: the cation, net charge +1; the side chain is neutral –COOH and lets go of Ca²⁺."}],"trap":"Stopping after the side chain: check the α-COOH too. pH 1.2 is even below its pKa of 1.9, so the zwitterion is not the main form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hold Ca²⁺","note":"held by –COO⁻"},{"t":"the calcium is released","note":"–COO⁻ picks up H⁺"},{"t":"pH 1.2","note":"very acidic"},{"t":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","note":"compare each"}]},{"text":"**Carboxylates bind metal ions.** At pH 7 the side chain is –COO⁻, and its negative oxygens grip Ca²⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-minus1"},{"text":"**Lower the pH below the side chain's pKa** and –COO⁻ picks up H⁺; the neutral –COOH cannot hold the metal, so the ion is released."},{"text":"**Then check every group** against its own pKa and add the charges.","calc":["below pKa → H⁺ on","above pKa → H⁺ off"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal-binding carboxylates release the metal once pH falls below their pKa; to find the dominant form, compare pH with each group's pKa and add the charges."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-43a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"electrolyte vs metallic conduction","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:43#1","pe6-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:43"],"passage":"An aluminum–air cell pairs an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces O₂ from the air: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, E°red = +0.40 V, and the full cell gives E°cell = 2.70 V. The two electrodes press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper soaked in concentrated KOH, and metal terminals connect them to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected. To slow corrosion of the aluminum, Mg²⁺ is added from a 3.5 mM MgCl₂ stock, and each test cell receives 6.0 mL of it. A rechargeable version adds a cathode coating: each electrode carries 12.0 mg of coating that is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 55.8 g/mol, O 16.0 g/mol), by mass. Lab tests show the coating stores 250 mAh per gram, where a milliamp-hour is the charge delivered by one milliamp flowing for one hour. The coating’s binder is a peptide whose aspartate side chains hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH, but when spent electrodes are stripped in acid at pH 1.2 the calcium is released (aspartate pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6).","q":"What is the function of the paper sheet itself?","choices":["It separates the electrodes","It conducts electrons through the cell","It stores the cell’s charge","It reacts at the cathode"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Electrolytes carry ions, not electrons","c":[1]},{"f":"Reactions occur at the electrodes","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tear it and the cell shorts out internally: the electrodes touch. So the sheet’s job is to keep them apart while its KOH lets ions pass."},"exp":"The porous sheet is the **separator**. It keeps the aluminum and the carbon cathode from touching, so electrons must take the **external circuit**, while the **KOH solution** soaked into it carries **OH⁻ and K⁺ ions** between the electrodes. If it tears, the electrodes touch, electrons flow straight across inside the cell (an internal short), and the cell heats and drains.","how":{"ask":"What goes wrong when the sheet tears, and which job does that reveal?","know":"• **Electrolyte**: carries ions between the electrodes (completes the internal circuit).\n• **External wire**: carries electrons from anode to cathode.\n• **Separator**: porous, electrically insulating; keeps electrodes apart but lets ions through.\n• Electrodes: where oxidation (anode) and reduction (cathode) happen.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 3 says a torn sheet makes the cell heat and drain with nothing connected: the electrodes touch and short out.","hl":"If the paper tears"},{"text":"So the sheet keeps the electrodes apart; its soaked-in KOH carries ions between them.","hl":"soaked in concentrated KOH"},{"text":"Electrons go through the external circuit, the energy is stored in the aluminum and O₂, and the cathode reaction is the reduction of O₂.","strike":["It conducts electrons through the cell","It stores the cell’s charge","It reacts at the cathode"]},{"text":"Answer: it separates the electrodes."}],"trap":"Thinking electrons cross the inside of the cell: if they could, the cell would short out; they must go around through the wire."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porous paper","note":"lets ions through"},{"t":"soaked in concentrated KOH","note":"the electrolyte"},{"t":"external circuit","note":"electron path"},{"t":"If the paper tears","note":"electrodes touch"}]},{"text":"**Four parts, four jobs.**","calc":["anode: oxidized","cathode: reduced","electrolyte: carries ions","wire: carries electrons"]},{"text":"**Why separate?** If the electrodes touched, electrons would flow directly between them, and no current would reach the outside circuit."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the solution between a cell’s electrodes is the electrolyte and carries ions, electrons travel only through the external wire, and a porous separator keeps the electrodes from touching."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P8"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-45a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide components","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:45#4","pe6-cp:45#1","pe6-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:45#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:45"],"q":"The deoxynucleotide dCTP is shown. How many carbon atoms does one molecule of dCTP contain?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dctp","choices":["8","9","11","12"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count by part: sugar 5 C + cytosine 4 C + phosphates 0 C = 9. Deoxy removes an O, never a carbon."},"exp":"Count by parts: **deoxyribose 5 C** (deoxy = one O missing, not a C) + **cytosine 4 C** + **three phosphates 0 C** = **9 C**.","how":{"ask":"Add the carbons of the sugar, the base and the phosphates.","know":"• **Sugar:** ribose and deoxyribose are both 5-carbon pentoses (C1′–C5′); 'deoxy' means one O fewer at C2′.\n• **Bases:** adenine, guanine and thymine have 5 C each; cytosine and uracil have 4 C.\n• **Phosphate:** PO₄ has no carbon, so one, two or three of them add 0 C.","steps":[{"text":"Split dCTP into its three parts: sugar, base, phosphates.","hl":"dCTP is shown"},{"text":"Sugar: deoxyribose keeps 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone.","calc":["deoxyribose: 5 C"],"strike":["8"]},{"text":"Base: cytosine's ring holds 4 C and 2 N, with an NH₂ and a C=O attached.","calc":["cytosine: 4 C, 3 N"],"strike":["11"]},{"text":"Phosphates: three PO₄ groups, no carbon.","calc":["3 × 0 = 0 C","5 + 4 + 0 = 9 C"],"strike":["12"]},{"text":"Answer: 9 carbon atoms."}],"trap":"Counting deoxyribose as 4 carbons because 'deoxy' sounds like something was removed; it lost an oxygen, not a carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"deoxynucleotide","note":"sugar + base + phosphate(s)"},{"t":"carbon atoms","note":"count C only"}]},{"text":"**Also: the sugar.** Deoxyribose has 5 C, the same as ribose; deoxy only means no O on C2′.","calc":["deoxyribose C₅H₁₀O₄: 5 C"]},{"text":"**Also: the bases.** Adenine and guanine each have 5 C (a six-ring fused to a five-ring that share 2 C); thymine has 5 C (4 ring C + a methyl); cytosine has 4 C."},{"text":"**Also: the phosphates** have no carbon, so dCMP, dCDP and dCTP all have the same 9 C."},{"text":"**Also: purines vs pyrimidines.** Adenine and guanine bring 5 C, one more than cytosine, so a purine nucleotide has one carbon more than the matching cytosine nucleotide."},{"text":"**Also:** count N the same way, by parts: sugar and phosphate have none, so every N sits in the base; dCTP has cytosine's 3 N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** atoms in a nucleotide = sugar + base + phosphate; sugar 5 C, adenine, guanine and thymine 5 C, cytosine and uracil 4 C, phosphate 0 C."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-46a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"heart wall thickness vs pressure","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:46"],"q":"The walls of the atria are much thinner than the walls of the ventricles. What best explains this difference?","choices":["The atria work against low pressure.","The atria move a larger stroke volume.","The atria receive venous return.","The atria hold oxygen-poor blood."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Left atrium holds oxygen-rich blood","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Muscle mass follows workload = pressure. Atria only top up the ventricles next door, so they need little force and little muscle."},"exp":"Muscle builds to match the **pressure a chamber must generate**. The atria only push blood a short way into the relaxed ventricles below them, at a few mmHg, so their walls are thin. The **left ventricle** must drive blood through the whole **systemic circulation** (about 120 mmHg) and is thickest; the **right ventricle** pumps only to the lungs (about 25 mmHg) and is in between.","how":{"ask":"What sets how much muscle a heart chamber needs?","know":"• Wall thickness ∝ the pressure a chamber must generate (Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure).\n• LV → systemic circuit, high resistance, ~120 mmHg; RV → pulmonary circuit, ~25 mmHg; atria → ventricles, a few mmHg.\n• Both sides pump the same volume per beat.","steps":[{"text":"Wall thickness follows pressure, not volume or blood type.","hl":"much thinner"},{"text":"Atria fill the adjacent ventricles at low pressure; no high-resistance circuit lies ahead of them.","calc":["atria: a few mmHg","RV: ~25 mmHg","LV: ~120 mmHg"]},{"text":"Stroke volume is not larger in the atria, receiving blood takes no force, and the left atrium carries oxygen-rich blood.","strike":["The atria move a larger stroke volume.","The atria receive venous return.","The atria hold oxygen-poor blood."]},{"text":"Also: the same rule explains why the left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right: it must overcome systemic resistance, while the right pumps only to the lungs."},{"text":"Answer: The atria work against low pressure."}],"trap":"Linking wall thickness to blood volume: both ventricles move the same volume each beat, yet their walls differ because their pressures differ."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"atria","note":"fill the ventricles"},{"t":"much thinner","note":"less force needed"},{"t":"ventricles","note":"pump into circuits"}]},{"text":"**Pressure sets muscle.**","calc":["LV: systemic, highest","RV: pulmonary, lower","atria: lowest"]},{"text":"**Same volume, different pressure.** Left and right ventricles eject equal volumes; only the resistance ahead of them differs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a heart chamber’s wall is as thick as the pressure it must generate: the left ventricle, which must drive blood around the whole body, is thickest; the atria, which only fill the ventricles, are thinnest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-47a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"unpaired electrons and spin","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:47#1","pe6-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:47"],"q":"A beam of ground-state nitrogen atoms (1s² 2s² 2p³) passes through a nonuniform magnetic field. What is the total electron spin of each atom, and how do the atoms respond to the field?","choices":["7/2, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic","½, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic","3/2, and the atoms are weakly repelled because they are diamagnetic","3/2, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Paired electron spins cancel out","c":[0]},{"f":"Unpaired electrons make atoms paramagnetic","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Paired electrons cancel; Hund's rule leaves N's three 2p electrons unpaired: 3 × ½ = 3/2. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic."},"exp":"By the **Pauli principle** the 1s and 2s pairs have **opposite spins** and cancel. **Hund's rule** spreads the three 2p electrons over the three 2p orbitals, all spin-up, so the total spin is **3 × ½ = 3/2**. Unpaired electrons make nitrogen **paramagnetic**: drawn toward the stronger field.","how":{"ask":"Pair the electrons orbital by orbital, add the leftover spins, then classify the magnetism.","know":"• **Pauli exclusion**: two electrons in one orbital must have opposite spins (+½ and −½), so a full orbital contributes **0**.\n• **Hund's rule**: in a partly filled subshell, electrons occupy separate orbitals with parallel spins first.\n• **Unpaired electrons → paramagnetic** (attracted); **all paired → diamagnetic** (weakly repelled).","steps":[{"text":"Read the configuration.","hl":"1s² 2s² 2p³","calc":["1s: 2 electrons, 2s: 2 electrons","2p: 3 electrons in 3 orbitals"]},{"text":"Pair the full orbitals, then add the 2p spins.","calc":["1s: +½ − ½ = 0; 2s: +½ − ½ = 0","2p: +½ +½ +½ = 3/2","total S = **3/2**"]},{"text":"Unpaired electrons mean the atom is paramagnetic.","strike":["3/2, and the atoms are weakly repelled because they are diamagnetic"]},{"text":"7/2 ignores Pauli pairing; ½ pairs two 2p electrons against Hund's rule.","strike":["7/2, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic","½, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic"]},{"text":"Answer: 3/2, and the atoms are attracted because they are paramagnetic."}],"trap":"Adding ½ for every electron: paired electrons in the same orbital always cancel."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state nitrogen atoms","note":"lowest-energy filling"},{"t":"1s² 2s² 2p³","note":"two full orbitals, a half-full p set"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"tests para- vs diamagnetism"}]},{"text":"**Pauli in one line.** No two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers, so two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.","calc":["full orbital: +½ + (−½) = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** an atom whose subshells are all filled, such as Be (1s² 2s²) or Ne, has total spin 0, so it is diamagnetic and is weakly pushed out of a strong field."},{"text":"**Also:** any unpaired electron (H, Li, N) leaves net spin, so the atom is paramagnetic and is drawn into the field."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** paired electrons cancel (Pauli); count only unpaired ones for the spin, and any unpaired electron means paramagnetic."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-49a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain classes","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:49#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:49"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"In wash water, what would the variant module most likely do?","choices":["Clump together in solution","Form extra disulfide bonds","Lose its catalytic activity","Bind more water molecules"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Val and Leu contain no sulfur","c":[1]},{"f":"Hydrophobic side chains don't bind water","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Polar OH on the surface → hydrophobic side chains on the surface: oily patches stick together, so the protein aggregates. Val/Leu add no S, so no disulfides."},"exp":"Surface Ser and Thr **–OH groups hydrogen-bond with water**, building the hydration shell around the protein. Swapping them for **hydrophobic** Val and Leu removes those H-bonds and exposes oily patches, which stick to one another: the variant **aggregates**.","how":{"ask":"What do surface –OH groups do with water, and what happens when oily side chains replace them?","know":"• Ser and Thr carry polar, uncharged –OH groups at pH 7: they donate and accept H-bonds with water.\n• Those H-bonds build a **hydration (solvation) shell** around the protein.\n• Exposed hydrophobic side chains (Val, Leu, Ile, Phe) pull together to avoid water → aggregation.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the swap: surface serines and threonines (polar –OH) replaced by valines and leucines (oily alkyl), all on the surface.","hl":"replaced by valines and leucines"},{"text":"No sulfur enters, so no new S–S bridges; and surface residues are not the catalytic pocket.","strike":["Form extra disulfide bonds","Lose its catalytic activity"]},{"text":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more.","strike":["Bind more water molecules"]},{"text":"Oily patches on neighboring molecules stick together (the hydrophobic effect).","calc":["fewer –OH···H₂O bonds → thinner water shell → molecules clump"]},{"text":"Answer: it clumps together in solution."}],"trap":"Assuming any big surface change must hit catalysis; the active site is a small pocket, while surface polarity governs how the protein mixes with water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"surface serines and threonines","note":"polar –OH groups"},{"t":"valines and leucines","note":"oily, no H-bonding"},{"t":"pocket and buried core unchanged","note":"activity site untouched"}]},{"text":"**What the –OH does.** Each Ser or Thr –OH hydrogen-bonds with water molecules, adding to the protein's hydration shell."},{"text":"**Charge check.** Ser and Thr are uncharged at pH 7 (their –OH pKa is about 16), so the swap changes polarity, not charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** polar surface groups (–OH, amides, charges) hold the water shell that keeps proteins dispersed; exposed hydrophobic groups make them aggregate."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-50a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"salt naming from neutralization","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:50#2","pe6-cp:50#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:50"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Which choice gives the correct name of the booster and the correct ion that acidifies the water?","choices":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates a proton to water","Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates a proton to water","Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates a proton to water","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates a proton to water"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Na⁺ has no proton to donate","c":[1]},{"f":"Sulfide is S²⁻, not SO₄-based","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"HSO₄⁻ = hydrogen sulfate, an acid (it still has an H to give). Na⁺ is a spectator, so the anion is the proton donor."},"exp":"HSO₄⁻ is the **hydrogen sulfate** ion (sulfate, SO₄²⁻, with one H left on it), so the salt is **sodium hydrogen sulfate**. That anion still has an acidic proton, **HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺**, which makes the solution strongly acidic. Na⁺ is a spectator. The same logic explains ammonium chloride: there the acidic ion is the cation, **NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺**, while Cl⁻ is a spectator.","how":{"ask":"What is the correct name of HSO₄⁻, and which of the two ions can donate a proton to water?","know":"• Naming: -ate = oxyanion (SO₄²⁻ sulfate), -ide = no oxygen (S²⁻ sulfide), \"hydrogen\" = an H still attached (HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate); NH₄⁺ = ammonium.\n• Ions of strong acids and bases (Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, NO₃⁻) do not change pH.\n• Acidic ions: NH₄⁺ (weak acid), HSO₄⁻ (fairly strong acid).","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says the solid gives Na⁺ and HSO₄⁻ and a very low pH. Name the anion: sulfate with one H attached is hydrogen sulfate, not sulfate and not sulfide.","hl":"HSO₄⁻ ions","strike":["Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates a proton to water","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates a proton to water"]},{"text":"Find the proton donor: HSO₄⁻ can give its H to water; Na⁺ cannot.","calc":["HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺"],"strike":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates a proton to water"]},{"text":"Also: in ammonium chloride the roles swap: NH₄⁺, a weak acid, gives the proton, and Cl⁻ is the spectator, so its solution is only slightly acidic.","calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium hydrogen sulfate, with HSO₄⁻ as the proton donor."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal cation makes a solution acidic: Na⁺ is a spectator; look for an ion that still carries a removable H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions","note":"name each ion"},{"t":"a pH of about 1.5","note":"acidic: one ion gives H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Name by the ion.**","calc":["NH₄⁺ ammonium","SO₄²⁻ sulfate","HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate","S²⁻ sulfide"]},{"text":"**Who gives the proton?** Only an ion that still holds an acidic H (NH₄⁺, HSO₄⁻); ions of strong acids and bases are spectators."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to explain a salt’s pH, name each ion and ask which can donate or accept a proton; NH₄⁺ (as in ammonium chloride) and HSO₄⁻ are acids, while Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are spectators."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"use a given formula","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:52"],"passage":"A detergent maker is engineering a small starch-binding module that anchors its wash enzymes to food stains. In the binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the face of a glucose ring, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows release, so the engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position. To help the module grip greasy stains, a variant was made with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines, leaving its pocket and buried core unchanged. The wash also contains an acidic booster, a white solid that dissolves to give Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers and, at 0.20 M, a pH of about 1.5. Binding is followed by NMR: in the spectrometer’s 11.7 T magnet, ¹H nuclei resonate at 500 MHz and ¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz, and absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s). Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B.","q":"Using the passage’s data, what is γ for ¹³C?","choices":["6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"r":3,"c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"units","how":"γ = 2πf/B = 6.28 × 1.25 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 7.9 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."},"exp":"From the passage, **γ = 2πf / B**. With f = 125 MHz = **1.25 × 10⁸ Hz** and B = 11.7 T: 2π × 1.25 × 10⁸ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s, and 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)** (that is, γ/2π ≈ 10.7 MHz/T).","how":{"ask":"What does the passage’s definition give when the frequency (in Hz) and the field are plugged in?","know":"• Translate the words into a formula first: γ = (2π × f) ÷ B.\n• Convert MHz to Hz; tesla stays as given.\n• Units follow the formula: rad/s per T.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 7 defines γ in words: two pi times the resonance frequency f, divided by the field B. Turn the definition into a formula.","hl":"two pi times the resonance frequency f","calc":["γ = 2πf / B"]},{"text":"Convert and substitute.","hl":"11.7 T magnet","calc":["f = 125 MHz = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz","2πf ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s","γ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)**"]},{"text":"Leaving MHz unconverted, multiplying by B, or dropping 2π each gives a wrong power or factor.","strike":["6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."}],"trap":"Using f instead of 2πf: the passage defines γ with the angular frequency."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"¹³C nuclei at 125 MHz","note":"f = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"11.7 T","note":"B"},{"t":"divided by the magnetic field B","note":"divide, don’t multiply"}]},{"text":"**Read the definition as an equation.** \"Two pi times f, divided by B\" is γ = 2πf/B."},{"text":"**Unit check.** Hz ÷ T → s⁻¹·T⁻¹; adding the radian from 2π gives rad/(s·T)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a passage defines a quantity in words, write it as a formula, convert every value to base units (MHz → Hz), and keep each constant such as 2π."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P9"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-53a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"ideal gas law PV = nRT","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:53"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"Which explanation is most consistent with the smaller hydrogen volume in the second run?","choices":["The same moles of hydrogen were at a higher pressure","Fewer moles of hydrogen were produced in the second run","The magnesium did not react in the second run","The hydrogen was at a lower temperature in the chamber"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Excess acid consumes all the magnesium","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Both runs were at room temperature","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"n same (identical strips, completion), T same (room temperature), P doubled → V halves (Boyle). Nothing else changed."},"exp":"Identical strips with excess acid going to completion make the **same moles of H₂** in both runs, and both runs are at room temperature. Only the **pressure** differs, and for a fixed amount of gas at constant T, **V ∝ 1/P** (Boyle’s law): doubling the pressure halves the volume. Likewise, at constant pressure, the same moles at a lower temperature would take up less volume (V ∝ T, Charles’s law).","how":{"ask":"Which of n, T and P differs between the two runs?","know":"• **PV = nRT**, so V = nRT/P.\n• Constant n and T: V ∝ 1/P (Boyle). Constant n and P: V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles).\n• Moles of gas formed are set by the limiting reagent, not by the conditions of collection.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 2 gives the amounts: identical strips, excess acid, run to completion, so both runs make the same moles of H₂.","hl":"Identical strips of magnesium","strike":["Fewer moles of hydrogen were produced in the second run"]},{"text":"Gas formed in both runs, and both were at room temperature.","hl":"both runs are done at room temperature","strike":["The magnesium did not react in the second run","The hydrogen was at a lower temperature in the chamber"]},{"text":"Only the pressure doubled, so the same gas fills about half the volume.","hl":"twice that pressure","calc":["V = nRT/P → P × 2 → V ÷ 2"]},{"text":"Also: the temperature version of the same idea: at constant pressure, the same moles of gas fill less volume when colder (V ∝ T)."},{"text":"Answer: the same moles of gas, compressed by the higher pressure."}],"trap":"Assuming a smaller gas volume means less gas formed: volume also depends on pressure and temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"go to completion","note":"same moles"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"same T"},{"t":"twice that pressure","note":"P doubled"}]},{"text":"**Hold the variables steady.** Check n, T and P one at a time; whichever differs explains the change.","calc":["V ∝ 1/P (n, T fixed)","V ∝ T (n, P fixed)"]},{"text":"**Amount vs volume.** The reaction fixes the moles; the conditions fix the volume those moles fill."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when the same reaction gives different gas volumes, use V = nRT/P: if n is fixed, the volume change comes from T (V ∝ T) or P (V ∝ 1/P)."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-54a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"phase diagram transitions","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe6-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:54"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"As compound R is compressed, which changes occur, in order?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]},"choices":["Condensation, then freezing","Deposition only","Freezing, then condensation","Condensation only"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Only liquids freeze","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Constant T = straight up the diagram. Above the triple-point temperature you cross gas→liquid, then liquid→solid: condensation, then freezing."},"exp":"Constant temperature = a **vertical** path at −28 °C. Going up, it first crosses the **liquid–gas line (≈ 2.1 atm): condensation**, then the **solid–liquid line (≈ 3.6 atm): freezing**, ending as a solid at 8 atm. It never touches the solid–gas line, which lies left of the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","how":{"ask":"Draw the path at −28 °C from 0.5 to 8 atm: which lines does it cross, in order?","know":"• On a phase diagram, **pressure is up, temperature is across**; constant T = a vertical path, constant P = a horizontal path.\n• Each boundary crossed is one phase change: liquid–gas = vaporization/condensation; solid–liquid = melting/freezing; solid–gas = sublimation/deposition.\n• Name the change from the region you leave to the region you enter.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 6 gives the path: a constant −28 °C, compressed from 0.5 to 8 atm. That is a vertical line on the diagram, starting in the gas region (low pressure, right of the triple point).","hl":"constant −28 °C"},{"text":"Move straight up. First boundary: liquid–gas, near 2.1 atm, so gas becomes liquid.","strike":["Deposition only"],"calc":["liquid–gas line at −28 °C: 2 + (2/20) × 1 ≈ 2.1 atm","gas → liquid = condensation"]},{"text":"Keep going: the solid–liquid line sits near 3.6 atm, below 8 atm, so the liquid becomes solid.","strike":["Condensation only","Freezing, then condensation"],"calc":["solid–liquid line at −28 °C: 2 + (2/5) × 4 ≈ 3.6 atm","3.6 atm < 8 atm → freezing"]},{"text":"Answer: condensation, then freezing."}],"trap":"Choosing deposition because the path starts as gas and ends as solid; at −28 °C, warmer than the triple point, a liquid region lies between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"constant −28 °C","note":"a vertical path"},{"t":"compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm","note":"move up"},{"t":"phase diagram","note":"read regions and lines"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** solid at upper left, liquid in the wedge, gas at lower right; the three lines meet at the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]}},{"text":"**Draw the path and list each line it crosses**, in order, naming the region it leaves and the one it enters.","calc":["gas → liquid (condensation) → solid (freezing)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a path held below the triple-point pressure, such as warming at 1 atm from −80 °C, crosses only the solid–gas line: sublimation, with no liquid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the path (vertical for constant T, horizontal for constant P) and name each boundary it crosses in order; sublimation or deposition happens only below the triple-point pressure."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"energy diagram Ea and ΔH","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:44#1","pe5-cp:44#2","pe5-cp:44#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:55","pe3-cp:14","pe5-cp:44"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"Suppose a catalyst lowered the diagram's peak to 170 kJ/mol. Which statement would then correctly describe nitroglycerin's decomposition?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy along the reaction coordinate for the decomposition","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"Decomposition","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,150],[1,150],[2,165],[3,210],[4,270],[5,300],[6,260],[7,170],[8,90],[9,45],[10,40]]}]},"choices":["Exothermic, with an activation energy of 20 kJ/mol","Endothermic, with an activation energy of 20 kJ/mol","Exothermic, with an activation energy of 170 kJ/mol","Exothermic, with an activation energy of 130 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"A catalyst never changes ΔH","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The ends do not move: right end below left → still exothermic. New Ea = new peak − reactants = 170 − 150 = 20 kJ/mol."},"exp":"A catalyst lowers only the peak; the reactants stay at **150 kJ/mol** and the products at **40 kJ/mol**, so ΔH = 40 − 150 = **−110 kJ/mol: still exothermic**. The new peak, 170 kJ/mol, sits **170 − 150 = 20 kJ/mol** above the reactants, so the catalyzed Ea is 20 kJ/mol. With so low a barrier the compound would no longer sit unchanged: it would decompose readily.","how":{"ask":"Which energies does a catalyst leave alone, and how high is the new peak above the reactants?","know":"• ΔH (or ΔG) = products − reactants: negative → exothermic (exergonic).\n• Forward Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy; reverse Ea = transition state − products.\n• A catalyst lowers the transition state only: Ea falls, ΔH stays the same.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 5 says nitroglycerin releases a great deal of heat once it reacts: the products lie well below the reactants. Then read the diagram.","hl":"release a great deal of heat and gas"},{"text":"Read the two ends of the curve; a catalyst does not move them.","calc":["reactants: 150 kJ/mol","products: 40 kJ/mol","ΔH = 40 − 150 = −110 → exothermic"],"strike":["Endothermic, with an activation energy of 20 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Measure the stem's new peak up from the reactants.","calc":["new peak: 170 kJ/mol","Ea = 170 − 150 = **20 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"170 measures from zero, and 130 is the new reverse barrier, measured from the products.","strike":["Exothermic, with an activation energy of 170 kJ/mol","Exothermic, with an activation energy of 130 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: exothermic, with Ea = 20 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading Ea as the peak's height on the axis: the barrier is measured from where the reactants start, and a catalyst moves only the peak."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"lowered the diagram's peak","note":"catalyst: Ea only"},{"t":"release a great deal of heat","note":"exothermic"},{"t":"energy diagram","note":"read ends and peak"}]},{"text":"**Two separate questions.**","calc":["thermodynamics: products vs reactants → ΔH","kinetics: peak vs reactants → Ea"]},{"text":"**Stable but explosive.** Uncatalyzed, a big negative ΔH sits behind a high Ea of 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/mol; a catalyst that cuts the barrier leaves the same energy release but lets the reaction start easily."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy diagram, ΔH is products minus reactants and the forward Ea is the peak minus reactants; a catalyst lowers the peak, so Ea drops while ΔH is unchanged."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10","mergedSrcs":["pe3-cp:14","pe5-cp:44"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe3-14a","cpt-pe5-44a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-56a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"mole fraction from coefficients","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:56#1","pe6-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:56"],"passage":"A teaching lab compares reactions and materials that involve gases. Identical strips of magnesium are dropped into excess hydrochloric acid, the hydrogen is collected in a syringe, and both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume. Baker’s ammonia, used to leaven thin cookies, decomposes completely when heated, NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g), and all of the gas is caught in a closed flask. Nitroglycerin, by contrast, can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate and release a great deal of heat and gas; its energy diagram is shown. Finally, compound R, a candidate refrigerant whose phase diagram is shown, is held as a gas at a constant −28 °C and compressed from 0.5 atm to 8 atm.","q":"Water vapor makes up what mole fraction of the gas from the baker’s ammonia?","choices":["0.25","0.33","0.67","1.0"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Solids don't count as gas moles","c":[0]},{"f":"Mixed gases: each fraction below 1","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count only gas coefficients: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, water is 1 of them → 1/3 ≈ 0.33. The solid reactant is not in the gas."},"exp":"Count gas moles from the coefficients: **1 NH₃ + 1 CO₂ + 1 H₂O = 3 mol**; the solid is not part of the gas. **x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.33**.","how":{"ask":"Divide water's coefficient by the sum of the gas coefficients only.","know":"• **Mole fraction x_i = n_i ÷ n_total**, always between 0 and 1.\n• For a complete reaction, coefficients give mole ratios.\n• **Count only (g) species**: solids and liquids are not in the gas mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Sentence 4 gives the equation: NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g). Pick out the gaseous products; the solid is not part of the gas.","hl":"NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g)","calc":["gas moles = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"Divide the water's share by the total.","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ **0.33**"]},{"text":"0.25 puts the solid in the total, 0.67 is the other two gases, and 1.0 forgets them entirely.","strike":["0.25","0.67","1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.33."}],"trap":"Adding the solid reactant's coefficient to the total: a gas mole fraction counts gases only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄HCO₃(s)","note":"solid: not in the gas"},{"t":"H₂O(g)","note":"1 mol of the gas asked"},{"t":"mole fraction","note":"part ÷ whole"}]},{"text":"**Coefficients are moles.** Per mole of solid that decomposes, the products are 1 mol each of NH₃, CO₂ and H₂O.","calc":["n(gas) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 mol"]},{"text":"**Part over whole.**","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1/3 ≈ 0.33","x(NH₃) + x(CO₂) = 2/3; all three sum to 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** leave every (s) and (l) out of the total; the phase labels decide what counts in a gas mixture."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mole fraction of a gaseous product = its coefficient ÷ the sum of the gas coefficients, ignoring solids and liquids."}],"pid":"pe6-cp:P10"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-57a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"half-life decay","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:57","pe4-cp:45","sample-cp:53"],"q":"Technetium-99m (half-life 6.0 h) arrives at a clinic with an activity of 112 mCi. Its activity 24 h later is:","choices":["3.5 mCi","7 mCi","14 mCi","28 mCi"],"ans":1,"cut":[],"fast":{"kind":"round","how":"24 ÷ 6.0 = 4 half-lives: 112 → 56 → 28 → 14 → 7 mCi."},"exp":"The number of half-lives is **n = 24 h ÷ 6.0 h = 4**. Activity halves each time: 112 → 56 → 28 → 14 → **7 mCi**, the same as 112 × (1/2)⁴ = 112 ÷ 16.","how":{"ask":"How many half-lives fit in 24 hours, and what is left after halving that many times?","know":"• n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.\n• A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ (the same rule for mass or number of nuclei).\n• After 1, 2, 3, 4 half-lives: ½, ¼, ⅛, 1/16 remain.","steps":[{"text":"Count half-lives.","hl":"half-life 6.0 h","calc":["24 h ÷ 6.0 h = **4**"]},{"text":"Halve four times.","hl":"112 mCi","calc":["112 → 56 → 28 → 14 → **7 mCi**","check: 112 × 1/16 = 7"]},{"text":"14 mCi stops one halving short, 3.5 mCi halves once too often, and 28 mCi divides by 4 instead of halving 4 times.","strike":["14 mCi","3.5 mCi","28 mCi"]},{"text":"Answer: 7 mCi."}],"trap":"Dividing the starting activity by the number of half-lives: decay is a repeated halving, (1/2)ⁿ, not 1/n."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"half-life 6.0 h","note":"one halving per 6 h"},{"t":"112 mCi","note":"starting activity"},{"t":"24 h later","note":"four half-lives"}]},{"text":"**Count, then halve.**","calc":["n = t ÷ t½","A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ"]},{"text":"**Fractions to know.** 1 half-life → ½, 2 → ¼, 3 → ⅛, 4 → 1/16, 5 → 1/32."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** divide the elapsed time by the half-life to get n, then multiply the starting activity by (1/2)ⁿ."}],"mergedSrcs":["pe4-cp:45"],"mergedFrom":["cpt-pe4-45a"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-58a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"radioactive decay A/Z changes","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:58","sample-cp:54"],"q":"Radon-222 (Z = 86), a gas that seeps into basements, is an alpha emitter. Given Po (Z = 84), At (Z = 85) and Ra (Z = 88), what is the daughter nucleus?","choices":["²¹⁸At","²²⁶Ra","²¹⁸Po","²²⁰Po"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Alpha lowers Z 2, A 4","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Alpha = lose 4 in mass, 2 in Z. 222 − 4 = 218; 86 − 2 = 84 = Po → polonium-218."},"exp":"An **alpha particle is ⁴₂He**, so **A falls by 4 and Z by 2**: A = 222 − 4 = 218, Z = 86 − 2 = 84 = **polonium**. ²²²Rn → **²¹⁸Po** + ⁴He.","how":{"ask":"Subtract 4 from A and 2 from Z, then name the element from the new Z.","know":"• **Alpha decay** emits ⁴₂He: **A − 4, Z − 2**.\n• Z alone fixes the element's identity.\n• Check: mass numbers and charges balance on both sides.","steps":[{"text":"Read the parent.","hl":"Radon-222 (Z = 86)","calc":["A = 222, Z = 86"]},{"text":"Remove a helium-4 nucleus.","calc":["A: 222 − 4 = 218","Z: 86 − 2 = 84 → Po"]},{"text":"Balance check.","calc":["222 = 218 + 4 ✓; 86 = 84 + 2 ✓"]},{"text":"Astatine drops Z by only 1, radium adds the alpha, and polonium-220 subtracts 2 from A instead of 4.","strike":["²¹⁸At","²²⁶Ra","²²⁰Po"]},{"text":"Answer: polonium-218."}],"trap":"Subtracting 2 from the mass number too: the alpha carries 2 protons but 4 nucleons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Radon-222","note":"A = 222"},{"t":"Z = 86","note":"protons"},{"t":"alpha emitter","note":"loses ⁴₂He"}]},{"text":"**What leaves.** An alpha particle is two protons and two neutrons.","calc":["ΔA = −4, ΔZ = −2"]},{"text":"**Name the daughter by Z.** The new proton count picks the element from the list.","calc":["Z = 84 → polonium; A = 218"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** alpha decay lowers A by 4 and Z by 2; the new Z names the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"Lewis structure and formal charge","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-cp:59#1","pe6-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:59"],"q":"In the Lewis structure of the nitrosonium ion, NO⁺, in which both atoms have complete octets, what are the bond order and the formal charge on oxygen?","choices":["3 and +1","3 and 0","2 and +1","2 and 0"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Double bond leaves N without octet","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = 10 valence e⁻, like CO and N₂: a triple bond plus a single lone pair on both N and O. O: 6 − (2 + 3) = +1."},"exp":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = **10 valence electrons**. Only **:N≡O:⁺** gives both atoms octets: a **triple bond** (6 shared electrons) plus a single lone pair on both N and O. Formal charges: **N = 5 − (2 + 3) = 0** and **O = 6 − (2 + 3) = +1**, so oxygen carries the positive charge (they sum to the ion’s +1).","how":{"ask":"How many valence electrons does NO⁺ have, which bond gives both atoms octets, and what does oxygen own in that structure?","know":"• Total valence electrons: add each atom’s, subtract one per + charge (add one per − charge).\n• Use multiple bonds until every atom has an octet.\n• **Formal charge = valence e⁻ − (lone-pair e⁻ + number of bonds)**; the charges add up to the ion’s charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons.","hl":"nitrosonium ion, NO⁺","calc":["N 5 + O 6 − 1 = 10 e⁻"]},{"text":"Place them: a double bond leaves N short; a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O gives both octets.","calc":[":N≡O: → 6 shared + 2 + 2 = 10 ✓"],"strike":["2 and +1","2 and 0"]},{"text":"Formal charge on oxygen in that structure.","calc":["O: 6 − (2 + 3) = **+1**","N: 5 − (2 + 3) = 0","sum = +1 ✓"],"strike":["3 and 0"]},{"text":"Answer: 3 and +1: oxygen’s formal charge is +1, and the bond is triple."}],"trap":"Putting the + charge on the less electronegative atom by instinct: formal charge comes from electron bookkeeping, and here it lands on oxygen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NO⁺","note":"10 valence e⁻"},{"t":"complete octets","note":"forces a triple bond"},{"t":"formal charge","note":"valence − owned"}]},{"text":"**Isoelectronic family.** N₂, CO, CN⁻ and NO⁺ all have 10 valence electrons and a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O."},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.**","calc":["owned = lone-pair e⁻ + one per bond","formal charge = valence − owned"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count valence electrons, add bonds until every atom has an octet, then assign formal charge = valence − (lone-pair electrons + bonds); in a 10-electron diatomic the bond is triple."}]}
];
